  The following allows us to switch between the different types of non empty
  bounded intervals.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.121.200>Let <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> the we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|]>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a-<frac|1|n>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\\<around*|[|b,\<infty\>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|]>=<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b|]>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>=\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|]>> then
      <math|a\<less\>x\<leqslant\>b> and as <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|b\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>> we have
      <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>> so it follows that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.8.020><around*|]|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>> or
      <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>> which by [corollary:
      <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (2)>] proves that
      <math|a\<less\>x\<leqslant\>b> hence <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|]>>.
      So it follows that <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|]|a,b|]>>
      which using [eq: <reference|eq 18.8.020>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>> then
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b> and as <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      we have <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>a> it follows that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.9.020><around*|[|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<less\>b\<Rightarrow\>a\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>\<wedge\>x\<less\>b>
      which by [corollary: <reference|complex Archimedean property
      consequence (2)>] proves that <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b> hence
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. So
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
      which using [eq: <reference|eq 18.9.020>] prove that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>> then
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b> and as <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      we have <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>a\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|]>>.
      Hence

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.10.021><around*|[|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|]>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<leqslant\>b> or
      <math|a\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>> which by [corollary: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (2)>] proves that
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x> giving <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>>. So we
      have <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|]>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|]>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 18.10.021>] gives

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>> then
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>b> so that
      <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>b\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>>. Which proves
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.11.021><around*|[|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>>. Using [corollary:
      <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (2)>] we have
      <math|x\<leqslant\>b> proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>>.
      Hence <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|]>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 18.11.021>] gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>> so that
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>b> hence
      <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.12.021><around*|[|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>\<Rightarrow\>a\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>\<wedge\>x\<less\>b+<frac|1|n>>.
      Using [corollary: <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence
      (2)>] we have <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>b> or
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>>. Hence
      <math|<rigid|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b+<frac|1|n>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|]>>>
      which together with [eq: <reference|eq 18.12.021>] proves\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>> then
      <math|x\<leqslant\>a> so that <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|x\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>>, hence it follows that
      <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.13.021><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|x\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>> which by [corollary: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (2)>] gives <math|x\<leqslant\>a> or
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>. Hence
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>
      which together with [eq: <reference|eq 18.13.021>] proves

      <\equation*>
        <big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>=<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>> then
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x> so that <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>x> giving
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>> proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.14.021><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<Rightarrow\>a\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>> which
      by [corollary: <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence
      (2)>] proves that <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>>.
      Hence <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>>
      which together with [eq: <reference|eq 18.14.021>] <math|>proves that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|]>> then
      <math|a\<less\>x\<leqslant\>b> and using [corollary: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|a\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<leqslant\>b>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.15.021><around*|]|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>>
      then there exists a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>\<Rightarrow\>a\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>b>.
      Hence <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|]>> proving that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>\<subseteq\><around*|]|a,b|]>>
      which by [eq: <reference|eq 18.15.021>] gives

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,b|]>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>> then
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b> and using [corollary: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>\<less\>b\<Rightarrow\>a\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>b-<frac|1|n>>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>. So\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.16.021><around*|\<nobracket\>|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>
      then <math|\<exists\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<Rightarrow\>a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>b-<frac|1|n>\<less\>b>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. Hence
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
      which combined with <reference|eq 18.16.021> gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>> then
      <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>b> and using [corollary: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>\<less\>b>
      hence <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>b-<frac|1|n>> proving that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>. So\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.17.021><around*|]|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>
      then <math|\<exists\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|a\<less\>x\<leqslant\>b-<frac|1|n>\<less\>b\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>>
      proving that <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|]|a,b|[>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 18.17.021>] gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>> then
      <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>b> and using \ [corollary: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|a\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<less\>b>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|[>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.18.021><around*|]|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|[><rsub|>>
      then <math|\<exists\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|a\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<less\>b\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>>
      proving that <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|]|a,b|[>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 18.18.021>] gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>> then
      <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>b> and using [corollary: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists
      <math|n<rsub|1>,n<rsub|2>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|a\<less\>a+<frac|1|n<rsub|1>>\<less\>x\<less\>x+<frac|1|n<rsub|2>>\<less\>b\<Rightarrow\>a+<frac|1|n<rsub|1>>\<less\>x\<less\>b-<frac|1|n<rsub|2>>>.
      Take <math|m=max<around*|(|n<rsub|1>,n<rsub|2>|)>> then
      <math|<frac|1|m>\<leqslant\><frac|1|n<rsub|1>>,<frac|1|n<rsub|2>>> so
      that <math|a+<frac|1|m>\<leqslant\>a+<frac|1|n<rsub|1>>\<less\>x\<less\>b-<frac|1|n<rsub|2>>\<leqslant\>b-<frac|1|m>>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b-<frac|1|m>|]>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.19.021><around*|]|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b-<frac|1|m>|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>
      then <math|\<exists\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<Rightarrow\>a\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b-<frac|1|n>\<less\>b>
      proving that <math|a\<less\>x\<less\>b> or
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>>. So
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|]|a,b|[>>
      which together with [eq: <reference|eq 18.19.021>] gives

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b-<frac|1|m>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>> then
      <math|x\<less\>a> and using [corollary: <reference|complex Archimedean
      property consequence (1)>] there exists a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such
      that <math|x+<frac|1|n>\<less\>a\<Rightarrow\>x\<less\>a-<frac|1|n>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a-<frac|1|n>|]>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.20.021><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a-<frac|1|n>|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a-<frac|1|n>|]>>
      then <math|\<exists\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a-<frac|1|n>|]>\<Rightarrow\>x\<leqslant\>a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>a>
      giving <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>> and proving that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\<subseteq\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>>.
      Using this together with [eq: <reference|eq 18.20.021>] proves

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a-<frac|1|n>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>> then
      <math|a\<less\>x> and using [corollary: <reference|complex Archimedean
      property consequence (1)>] there exists a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such
      that <math|a+<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.21.021><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>
      then <math|\<exists\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>\<Rightarrow\>a\<less\>a+<frac|1|n>\<leqslant\>x>
      hence <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>>. This proves that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>>,
      combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 18.21.021>] gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>+0><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>> then
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>b\<Rightarrow\>a\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|b\<leqslant\>x|)>>
      so that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>> and
      <math|x\<nin\><around*|[|b,\<infty\>|[>> proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.22.021><around*|[|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\\<around*|[|b,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\\<around*|[|b,\<infty\>|[>>
      then <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|b\<leqslant\>x|)>>
      or <math|a\<leqslant\>x*> and <math|x\<less\>b> proving that
      <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\\<around*|[|b,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 18.22.021>] proves that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|[>=<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\\<around*|[|b,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|]>> then
      <math|a\<less\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>b> then
      <math|\<neg\><around*|(|x\<leqslant\>a|)>\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>b> so
      that <math|x\<nin\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\<wedge\>x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,b|]>>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,b|[>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>.
      For the opposite inclusion let <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,b|]>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>
      then <math|x\<leqslant\>b> and <math|\<neg\><around*|(|x\<leqslant\>a|)>>
      proving that <math|x\<leqslant\>b> and <math|a\<less\>x> or
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|]>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,b|]>=<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b|]>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>> then
      <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>a\<less\>x> so that
      <math|x\<nleqslant\>a\<Rightarrow\>x\<nin\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>
      hence <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>, further if
      <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>> then
      <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>x\<nin\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\<Rightarrow\>x\<nleqslant\>a\<Rightarrow\>a\<less\>x>
      or <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>> hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>=\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove alternative forms for
  <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel algebra on R>We have the following alternative forms
    for <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|definition>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>>\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|C\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\|C<text|
      is closed in >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|}>|]>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra and
      closure>]>>>|<cell|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> then by [corollary:
    <reference|normed open set as union of intervals (1)>] there exist a
    countable family <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.76.200>\<forall\>i\<in\>I<text|
      ><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,\<beta\><rsub|i>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\><text|
      and >U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,\<beta\><rsub|i>|[>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>>
    which is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measure countable union is in sigma algebra>] that
    <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,\<beta\><rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|)>>.
    Given that <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> was chosen
    arbitrary it follows that <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>>.
    Using then [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.77.200>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|def>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|<around*|]|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>> then by
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>]
    <math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>>.
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|><math|<around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>>

      <item*|<math|<around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|><math|<around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>>
    </description>

    so that <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>>
    from which it follows by [definition: <reference|measure sigma algebra>]
    that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a,b-<frac|1|n>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>
    </equation*>

    <math|>. Hence we have <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>>
    a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>>. Using [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] it follows then that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.78.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|<around*|]|a,b|]>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>> then by
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>]
    <math|<around*|]|a,b|]>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>>.
    For <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|<around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>>

      <item*|<math|<around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|<around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|)>>
    </description>

    so that <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>>
    from which it follows by [definition: <reference|measure sigma algebra>]
    that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|]|a,b|]>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|n>,b|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Hence we have <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>>
    a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>> so that by [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.79.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>> then by
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>]
    <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>>.
    For <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>

      <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    </description>

    so that <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    form which it it follows by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra
    equivalences>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|n>|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Hence we have <math|><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>> so that by [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.80.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then by
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>]
    <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>>.
    As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|<around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>>
    is open it follows that <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]
    <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,b|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>>.
    Hence we have that <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.81.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|def>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    Combining [eqs: <reference|eq 20.77.200>, <reference|eq 20.78.200>,
    <reference|eq 20.79.200>, <reference|eq 20.80.200> and <reference|eq
    20.81.200>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.82.200>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]><rsub|>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then
    using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>] we have that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a,b|]>=<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>\\<around*|[|b,\<infty\>|[><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure algebra properties>, <reference|measure sigma
      algebra equivalences>]>>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation*>

    proving <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra. Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure
    generated sigma algebra>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.83.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>
    then using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>] we have that
    <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>.
    As <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]
    that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|><math|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.84.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>
    then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>]
    <math|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>=\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    so that <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>.
    Using <math|>[theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.85.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>
    then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.121.200>]
    <math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>>.
    As <math|<rigid|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]
    that <math|><math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a+<frac|1|n>|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation*>

    which by <math|>[theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]
    proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.86.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>
    then as <math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>> is open it follows that
    <math|><math|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>>
    and using [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.87.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.82.200>]>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation>

    Combining [eqs: <reference|eq 20.83.200>, <reference|eq 20.84.200>,
    <reference|eq 20.85.200>, <reference|eq 20.86.200> and <reference|eq
    20.87.200>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation*>

    Finally combining the above with [eq: <reference|eq 20.82.200>] proves
    that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Borel algebra on <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>>

  We look now at the Borel algebra on the set of extended real numbers
  <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure Borel algebra on the extended real numbers>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|\<rangle\>>>
    be the topological space consisting of the extended real numbers with the
    canonical topology <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>>
    [see definition: <reference|extended canonical topology>] then the Borel
    algebra on <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is noted as
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Just as in the real case we formulate some alternative definitions for the
  Borel algebra on the extended real numbers.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.150.227>We have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|\<infty\>|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then if
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>>
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>a-<frac|1|n>\<less\>a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>>
      so that <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>>,
      hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.109.227><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>>
      then <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a-<frac|1|1>,\<infty\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>a-1\<less\>x>.
      Assume that <math|x\<less\>a> then <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and using
      [theorem: <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>]
      there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x+<frac|1|n>\<less\>a> or <math|x\<less\>a-<frac|1|n>> so that
      <math|x\<nin\><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>> contradicting
      <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>>.
      Hence the assumption is wrong and we have
      <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>> so that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>> proving that
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>>,
      combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.109.227>] results in\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|n\<less\>\<infty\>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>> it follows that
      <math|\<infty\>\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>>
      so that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.110.227><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>>.
      Then <math|x\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>-\<infty\>\<less\>1\<less\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>>
      so that <math|x\<neq\>-\<infty\>>. Assume that <math|x\<neq\>\<infty\>>
      then <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and by [theorem: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<less\>n\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\>\<nin\><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>>
      contradicting <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>>,
      hence the assumption is wrong and we must have that
      <math|x=\<infty\>\<in\><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>>. So
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.110.227>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|\<infty\>|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|-\<infty\>\<leqslant\>-\<infty\>\<less\>-n> it follows that
      <math|-\<infty\>\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>>
      so that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.111.227><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>>.
      Then <math|x\<in\><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-1|[>\<Rightarrow\>x\<less\>-1\<less\>\<infty\>>
      so that <math|x\<nin\>\<infty\>>. Assume that <math|x\<neq\>-\<infty\>>
      then <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and by [theorem: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|-x\<less\>n\<Rightarrow\>-n\<less\>x>
      so that <math|x\<nin\><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>> contradicting
      <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>>,
      hence the assumption is wrong and we must have that
      <math|x=-\<infty\>\<in\><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>>. So
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>\<subseteq\><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.111.227>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|\<infty\>|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.151.227>For <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    we have the following

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have that
      <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have that
      <math|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,a|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have that
      <math|<around*|]|a,b|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> we have
      that <math|U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>,
      hence <rigid|<math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>>.
      As <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.150.227>]>><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|a-<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|[|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|]>\<wedge\>x\<nin\><around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<infty\>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>|)>\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|a\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<infty\>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>|)>\<wedge\><around*|(|x\<less\>a\<vee\>\<infty\>\<less\>x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|\<infty\>\<less\>x<text|
        is false>>>|<cell|-\<infty\>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>\<wedge\>x\<less\>a>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|-\<infty\>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>a>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|[|-\<infty\>,a|[>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,a|[>=<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>>
      which, as by (1) <math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>
      and <rigid|<math|\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>>
      is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, proves by [theorem: <reference|measure
      sigma algebra equivalences>]

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|-\<infty\>,a|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|b\<less\>a>>Then <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>

        <item*|<math|a\<leqslant\>b>>Then\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]><big|cap><around*|[|-\<infty\>,b|[>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>*\<wedge\>x\<in\><around*|[|-\<infty\>,b|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|a\<less\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>\<infty\>\<wedge\>-\<infty\>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>b>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|a\<less\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>b>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        proving that <math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]><big|cap><around*|[|-\<infty\>,b|[>>.
        As <math|><math|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>
        and by (2) <math|<rigid|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,b|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>>
        we have [theorem <math|><math|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]><big|cap><around*|[|-\<infty\>,b|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>.
        So by ]theorems: <reference|measure algebra equavalent definition>,
        <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|]|a,b|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      <item>Let <math|U> be a open set in <math|\<bbb-R\>> then by
      [corollary: <reference|normed open set as union of intervals (1)>]
      there exist a countable set <math|I> and family
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>>
      such that <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>.
      By (3) it follows that <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>
      so that using [theorem: <reference|measure countable union is in sigma
      algebra>] <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>.

      <item>Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.150.227>] we have that
      <math|<around*|{|\<infty\>|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>><around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>>
      which, as <math|<rigid|<around*|{|<around*|]|n,\<infty\>|]>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>>,
      \ proves by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.150.227>] we have that
      <math|<around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>><around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|[>>
      which, as by (2) \ <math|<rigid|<around*|{|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,-n|]>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>>>,
      \ proves by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.152.227><math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|definition>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|I\<in\><around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>
    then there exist a <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that
    <math|I=<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>>. By [definition: <reference|extended
    basis for the topology>] it follows that
    <math|I\<in\>\<cal-B\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>>,
    so that <math|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>.
    Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.112.227>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inclusion, let <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>>
    then by [theorem: <reference|extended canonical topologies on the real
    and extended real space>] we have that
    <math|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>. From
    <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>=\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}><big|cup><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>>
    it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.113.227>U=U<big|cap><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>=<around*|(|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)><big|cup><around*|(|U<big|cap><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>|)><big|cup><around*|(|U<big|cap><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Now as <math|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>
    we have by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.151.227>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.114.227>U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    Also <math|U<big|cap><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>> is either
    <math|\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    or <math|<around*|{|-\<infty\>|}><below|\<in\>|<text|[lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.151.227>]>>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.115.227>U<big|cap><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    Finally <math|U<big|cap><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>> is either
    <math|\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>
    or <math|<around*|{|\<infty\>|}><below|\<in\>|<text|[lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.151.227>]>><rigid|<around*|{|\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.116.227>U<big|cap><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    From \ [eqs: <reference|eq 20.113.227>, <reference|eq 20.114.227>,
    <reference|eq 20.115.227> and <reference|eq 20.116.227>] it follows from
    [theorems: <reference|measure algebra equavalent definition>,
    <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>] that
    <math|<rigid|U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>.
    Hence <math|<rigid|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>
    [a sigma algebra on <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>] so that
    <math|<rigid|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>,
    combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.112.227>] gives

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The relation between <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> and
  <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>> is expressed in
  the following theorem.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.153.227 ><math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>=<around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>\<equallim\><rsub|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>|}>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First we prove that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.117.227><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}><text|
      is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra on ><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
    </equation>

    <\proof>
      As <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>=\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.118.227><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<in\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>
      </equation>

      Let <math|U\<in\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>
      then <math|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      hence <math|\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|(|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      further

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\U|)><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and
        intersection>]>>*<around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\\U=\<bbb-R\>\\U<text|
        and >\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|(|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|class set difference and union ,
        intersection>]>><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>\\U|)><big|cup><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>\\\<bbb-R\>|)>=\<bbb-R\>\\U
      </equation*>

      so that <math|<around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\U|)><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>=\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|(|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      proving that <math|<around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\U|)>\<in\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>.
      In other words

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.119.227>\<forall\>U\<in\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}><text|
        we have ><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\U\<in\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|U<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>
      then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|U<rsub|i><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      so that <math|<rigid|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.120.227>\<forall\><around*|{|U<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}><text|
        ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Finally [eqs: <reference|eq 20.118.227>, <reference|eq 20.119.227> and
      <reference|eq 20.120.227>] proves [eq: <reference|eq 20.117.227>].
    </proof>

    Take <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>> then by
    [theorem: <reference|extended canonical topologies on the real and
    extended real space>] we have that <math|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|definition>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    proving that <math|U\<in\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    As <math|<around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>
    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.117.227>] it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.121.227>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><below|=|<text|definition>>\<sigma\><around*|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inclusion define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.122.227>S=<around*|{|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\|U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>
    </equation>

    then as <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
    we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.123.227>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|]theorem:
      <reference|extended canonical topologies on the real and extended real
      space>>><around*|{|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\|U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>|}>=S
    </equation>

    Further as <math|\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|extended canonical topologies on the real and extended real
    space>]>>\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
    we have for <math|U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
    that <math|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
    proving\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.124.227>S\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.125.227>S<text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra on
      >\<bbb-R\>
    </equation>

    <\proof>
      As <math|\<bbb-R\>=\<bbb-R\><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|\<bbb-R\>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended canonical topologies on the real and extended real
      space>]>>\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.126.227>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>
      </equation>

      If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then there exist a
      <math|U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      such that <math|A=U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>> so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-R\>\\A=\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|(|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|class set difference and union ,
        intersection>]>><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>\\U|)><big|cup><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>\\\<bbb-R\>|)>=\<bbb-R\>\\U
      </equation*>

      which, as <math|\<bbb-R\>,U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|measure algebra equavalent
      definition>]>>\<bbb-R\>\\U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>,
      proves that

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-R\>\\A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>
      </equation*>

      Hence <math|<around*|(|\<bbb-R\>\\A|)><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>=\<bbb-R\>\\A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      so that <math|\<bbb-R\>\\A\<in\>\<cal-S\>>. Summarized\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.127.227>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have
        >\<bbb-R\>\\A\<in\>S
      </equation>

      If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>S>
      then there exists a <math|<around*|{|U<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i>=U<rsub|i><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>>, then
      \ <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>|)>=<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>\<bbb-R\>>
      which, as <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>.
      proves that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>S>. In
      other words

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.128.227>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>S<text|
        we have ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>
      </equation>

      Finally [eqs: <reference|eq 20.126.227>, <reference|eq 20.127.227> and
      <reference|eq 20.128.227>] proves that <math|S> is a
      <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>> thus proving [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.125.227>].
    </proof>

    So we have proved that <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>\<subseteq\>S>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.123.227>] and <math|S> is a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>> from which it follows, using
    [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>], that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.129.227>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|definition>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>|]>\<subseteq\>S<below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.124.227>]>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\><around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>
    then <math|A\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> so that
    <math|A=A<big|cap><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<bar\>>=A<big|cap><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>,<around*|{|\<infty\>|}>|)>>
    giving\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.130.227>A=<around*|(|A<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cap><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cap><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    where\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|A<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.129.227>]>>A<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>

      <item><math|A<big|cap><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>> is either
      <math|\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      or <math|<around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      [as <math|-\<infty\>> is closed [theorem: <reference|extended closed
      sets>] and closed sets are part of <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and closure>]].\ 

      <item><math|A<big|cap><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>> is either
      <math|\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      or <math|<around*|{|\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      [as <math|\<infty\>> is closed by [theorem: <reference|extended closed
      sets>]and closed sets are part of <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
      by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and closure>]].
    </enumerate>

    From the above we conclude that <math|<around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.121.227>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>=<around*|{|U\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Summarizing the above two lemmas in a theorem we get:

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel algebra on the extended real numbers (1)>The Borel
    algebra on <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> satisfies\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|defintion>>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra and
      closure>]>>>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|C\|C is closed in
      \<cal-T\><rsub|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|U\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Further we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The first part of the theorem follows from [lemmas: <reference|lemma
    20.152.227>,<reference|lemma 20.153.227 >]. For the last part. Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> then
    <math|A\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> so
    that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>>
    and <math|A=A<big|cap>R\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> hence

    <\equation*>
      A\<in\><around*|{|U\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|U<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>.
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Borel algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>>

  Just as in for the real numbers and the extended real numbers we have many
  ways of generating the Borel algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure Borel algebra on R^n>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then the
    Borel algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> is noted as
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> so\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>n>|]>
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>> is the
    canonical topology on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> [see example:
    <reference|normed maximum norm R^n>]
  </definition>

  First we extend the concept of generalized intervals to intervals on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure order on R^n>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|x,y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then <math|x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>y> if
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>> further we have
    <math|x\<less\><rsub|n>y> if <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>y<rsub|i>>.

    <\note>
      If <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>y<rsub|i>> then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
      <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>y<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<neq\>y<rsub|i>>
      so that <math|x\<leqslant\>y\<wedge\>x\<neq\>y> which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        x\<less\><rsub|n>y\<Rightarrow\>x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>y\<wedge\>x\<neq\>y
      </equation*>

      However the opposite is not true. for example
      <math|<around*|(|1,2|)>\<leqslant\><rsub|2><around*|(|2,2|)>> and
      <math|<around*|(|1,2|)>\<neq\><around*|(|2,2|)>> but
      <math|<around*|(|1,2|)>\<nless\><rsub|2><around*|(|2,2|)>>. So this
      definition is different from the normal definition of a strict
      inequality.\ 
    </note>
  </definition>

  This relation makes <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<leqslant\><rsub|n>|\<rangle\>>>
  a partial ordered set.

  <\theorem>
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<leqslant\><rsub|n>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a partial ordered set.

    <\note>
      <math|\<leqslant\><rsub|n>> is however not a total order, for example
      for <math|<around*|(|1,2|)>>, <math|<around*|(|2,1|)>> we thave hat
      <math|<around*|(|1,2|)>\<nleqslant\><rsub|2><around*|(|2,1|)>> and
      <math|<around*|(|2,1|)>\<nleqslant\><rsub|2><around*|(|1,2|)>>.\ 
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We have

    <\description>
      <item*|reflexivity>If <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
      <math|x<rsub|i>=x<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>>
      proving that <math|x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>x>.

      <item*|anti-symmetry>If <math|x,y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> satisfies
      <math|x\<leqslant\>y\<wedge\>y\<leqslant\>x> then we have
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>>
      and <math|y<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>> so that
      <math|x<rsub|i>=y<rsub|i>> from which it follows that <math|x=y>.

      <item*|transitivity>If <math|x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>y> and
      <math|y\<leqslant\><rsub|n>z> then we have
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> that
      <math|x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>\<wedge\>y<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|i>>
      from which it follows that <math|x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>z>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  Using this order relation we can extend the concept of intervals to
  intervals in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure [a,b[ in R^n>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>,
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> such that <math|a\<leqslant\><rsub|n>b>
    then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|[|a,b|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<leqslant\><rsub|n>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>b|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|[|a,b|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<leqslant\><rsub|n>x\<wedge\>x\<less\><rsub|n>b|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|]|a,b|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<less\><rsub|n>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>b|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >a<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|]|a,b|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<less\><rsub|n>x\<wedge\>x\<less\><rsub|n>b|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >a<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|x\<leqslant\><rsub|n>a|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|]>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|]>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|x\<less\>a|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<leqslant\><rsub|n>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,\<infty\>|[>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,\<infty\>|[>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<less\><rsub|n>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >a<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,\<infty\>|[>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,\<infty\>|[>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </definition>

  To simply notation we have also the following definitions:

  <\definition>
    <label|interval power>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> and
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then we define the following sets

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|]><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|[><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|]><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|[><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[><rsup|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|a\<less\>x<rsub|i>|}>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      If we take <math|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> by
      <math|\<alpha\><rsub|i>=a> and <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>=b> then we have
      that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|[|a,b|]><rsup|n>=<around*|[|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>|]>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|[|a,b|[><rsup|n>=<around*|[|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>|[>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|]|a,b|]><rsup|n>=<around*|]|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>|]>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|]|a,b|[><rsup|n>=<around*|]|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>|[>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]><rsup|n>=<around*|]|\<infty\>,\<alpha\>|]>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsup|n>=<around*|]|\<infty\>,\<alpha\>|[>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[><rsup|n>=<around*|[|\<alpha\>,\<infty\>|[>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[><rsup|n>=<around*|]|\<alpha\>,\<infty\>|[>>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\proposition>
    <label|R^n is the union of ]-i,i[><math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we
    have <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    First if <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>>
    then <math|\<exists\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>> hence
    <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|<rigid|x<rsub|j>\<in\><around*|]|-i,i|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-i,i|]>>>
    so that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>> from which it follows
    that <math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>>.
    As further <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.84.300><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then
    <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|x<rsub|j>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><below|=|<text|[corollary:
    <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence
    (1)>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[>> so that there
    exist a <math|i<rsub|j>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|x<rsub|j>\<in\><around*|]|-i<rsub|j>,i<rsub|j>|[>>. Take
    <math|k=max<around*|(|<around*|{|i<rsub|j>\|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>>
    then we have <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> that
    <math|x<rsub|j>\<in\><around*|]|-i<rsub|j>,i<rsub|j>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|]|-k,k|[>>
    so that <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-k,k|[><rsup|n>>. Hence we have
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 21.84.300>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-R\><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We specify now the sufficient and necessary condition for a interval in
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> to be empty.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.129.200>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<emptyset\>\<Leftrightarrow\>\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      such that <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>>>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|]>=\<emptyset\>\<Leftrightarrow\>\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      such that <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>>>>

      <item><math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=\<emptyset\>\<Leftrightarrow\>\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      such that <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>>>>

      <item><math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=\<varnothing\>\<Leftrightarrow\>\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      such that <math|a<rsub|i>\<gtr\>b<rsub|i>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item> As <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
      we have

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<emptyset\>>
        then by [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
        <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=\<emptyset\>>. If
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> then by [theorem:
        <reference|complex densitiy theorem>] there exists a
        <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>x\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
        contradicting <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=\<emptyset\>>,
        so we must have <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>>.

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If there exists a
        <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> with
        \ <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>> then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>> we have
        <math|<rigid|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>>
        leading to the contradiction <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>> so
        <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=\<emptyset\>>. Hence using
        [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <rigid|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<varnothing\>>>.
      </description>

      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|<around*|]|a,b|]>=\<emptyset\>>
        then by [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
        <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>=\<emptyset\>>. If
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> then by [theorem:
        <reference|complex densitiy theorem>] there exists a
        <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>x\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>>
        contradicting <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>=\<emptyset\>>,
        so we must have <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>>.\ 

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If there exists a
        <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> with
        \ <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>> then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>> we have
        <math|<rigid|a<rsub|i>\<less\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>>
        leading to the contradiction <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>> so
        <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>=\<emptyset\>>. Hence using
        [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <rigid|<math|<around*|]|a,b|]>=\<varnothing\>>>.
      </description>

      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=\<emptyset\>>
        then by [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
        <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=\<emptyset\>>. If
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> then by [theorem:
        <reference|complex densitiy theorem>] there exists a
        <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>x\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
        contradicting <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=\<emptyset\>>,
        so we must have <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>>.

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If there exists a
        <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> with
        \ <math|a<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|i>> then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>> we have
        <math|<rigid|a<rsub|i>\<less\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>>>
        leading to the contradiction <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>> so
        <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=\<emptyset\>>. Hence using
        [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <rigid|<math|<around*|]|a,b|[>=\<varnothing\>>>.
      </description>

      <item>

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=\<emptyset\>>
        then by [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
        <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>=\<emptyset\>>. If
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>> then
        <math|a<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>> contradicting
        <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>=\<emptyset\>>, so we must
        have <math|a<rsub|i>\<gtr\>b<rsub|i>>.

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If there exists a
        <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> with
        \ <math|a<rsub|i>\<gtr\>b<rsub|i>> then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>> we have
        <math|<rigid|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>>>
        leading to the contradiction <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>> so
        <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>=\<emptyset\>>. Hence using
        [theorem <reference|choice product sets empty condition>]
        <rigid|<math|<around*|[|a,b|]>=\<varnothing\>>>.
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Next we introduct the classes of intervals in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> that
  can be used to generate the Borel algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure half open intervals in R^n>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>>=<around*|{|<around*|[|a,b|]>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<wedge\>a\<leqslant\>b|}>
    </equation*>

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,b|[>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<wedge\>a\<less\>b|}>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>>=<around*|{|<around*|]|a,b|]>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<wedge\>a\<less\>b|}>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>>=<around*|{|<around*|]|a,b|[>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<wedge\>a\<less\>b|}>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      If <math|a\<less\>b> then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> so that by [lemma: <reference|lemma
      20.129.200>]

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|[>,<around*|]|a,b|]>,<around*|]|a,b|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      Likewise if <math|a\<leqslant\>b> then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>> so that by [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.129.200>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure open closed rectangles>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we
    have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>>> we
      have that <math|R> is closed in <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>>> we
      have that <math|R> is compact in <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>>> we
      have that <math|R> is open in <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>
      [in other words <math|R\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>]
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>>> there
      exist a <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<leqslant\>b>.
      Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|[>> then
      <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
      <math|x<rsub|i>\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>>. Hence we have
      either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>>>Take then
        <math|\<varepsilon\>=a<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then
        <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>>
        we have

        <\equation*>
          y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>=a<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>
        </equation*>

        so that <math|y<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>> from which it follows that
        <math|y<rsub|i>\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i><rsub|>|]>>.
        Hence <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|]>> so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|]>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|b<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>>>Take then
        <math|\<varepsilon\>=x<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then
        <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>>
        we have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>=x<rsub|i>-b<rsub|i>
        </equation*>

        so that <math|b<rsub|i>\<less\>y<rsub|i>> from which if follows that
        <math|y<rsub|i>\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>>. Hence
        <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|]>> so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|]>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      So <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|]>> we
      found a <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|]>>,
      from this it follows that <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|[|a,b|]>>
      is open which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        R=<around*|[|a,b|]><text| is a closed set>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>>> there
      exists, <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>> such that
      <math|R=<around*|[|a,b|]>\<equallim\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|compact rectangles are compact>] it follows
      then that <math|R> is compact.

      <item>Let <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>>> then
      there exists <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<less\>b>
      such that <math|R=<around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>.
      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>> is open in
      <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> [see theorem: <reference|normed
      generalized intervals that are open>] it follows from [definition:
      <reference|topology box>] and [theorems: <reference|topology product
      topology and box topology are the same in the finite case>,
      <reference|normed maximum norm>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-R\>=<around*|]|a,b|[><text| is a open set in
        >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|x>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Later we have to define volumes [which are positive] for
  <math|n>-dimensional rectangles <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
  which will be defined as the product of the sides of this
  <math|n>-dimensial rectangle, for this we need the following lemma.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.158.223>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    then <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We prove this by induction, so let\ 

    <\equation*>
      S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      then ><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>x<rsub|o>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>If <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      then <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>x<rsub|i>=x<rsub|1>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      which proves that <math|1\<in\>S>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      then as <math|n\<in\>S> we have <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>,
      as also <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> it follows from
      [theorem: <reference|complex RC order properties>] that

      <\equation*>
        0\<less\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>x<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>x<rsub|n+1>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>x<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|n+1\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    </description>
  </proof>

  The following lemma allows us to define a function that maps a element of
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> to its endpoints. Using this
  we will be able to define the volume of elements of
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.132.200>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|I\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then there exists a
    <with|font-series|bold|unique> <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with
    <math|a\<less\>b> such that <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    If <math|I\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then by
    definition of <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> there exist a
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n >>with <math|a\<less\>b> such that
    <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>>. Assume that there exists also a
    <math|a<rprime|'>,b<rprime|'>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> such that
    <math|I=<around*|[|a<rprime|'>,b<rprime|'>|[>>. Then
    <math|<rigid|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rprime|'><rsub|i>,b<rprime|'><rsub|i>|[>>>
    and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>, hence
    using [theorem: <reference|choice product equality>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text| that
      >\<varnothing\>\<neq\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a<rprime|'><rsub|i>,b<rprime|'><rsub|i>|[>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then as
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<neq\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a<rprime|'><rsub|i>,b<rprime|'><rsub|i>|[>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|generalized intervals and
    boundaries>] that <math|a<rsub|i>=a<rprime|'><rsub|i>> and
    <math|b<rsub|i>=b<rprime|'><rsub|i>> so that
    <math|a=a<rprime|'>,b=b<rprime|'>> proving uniqueness.
  </proof>

  Thanks to the above lemmas the following functions are well defined.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure begin end><index|begin><index|end><index|<math|v<rsup|n><around*|(|I|)>>>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we define:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|begin:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>>
      by <math|begin<around*|(|I|)>=a> where <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>> and
      <math|a\<less\>b>

      <item><math|end:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>>
      by <math|end<around*|(|I|)>=b> where <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>> and
      <math|a\<less\>b>

      <item><math|v<rsup|n>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\><around*|]|0,\<infty\>|[>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      where <math|v<rsup|n><around*|(|I|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|begin<around*|(|I|)><rsub|i>-end<around*|(|I|)><rsub|i>|)>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As <math|begin<around*|(|I|)>=a\<less\>b=end<around*|(|I|)>> we have
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>

      <\equation*>
        begin<around*|(|I|)><rsub|i>\<less\>end<around*|(|I|)><rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>end<around*|(|I|)><rsub|i>-begin<around*|(|I|)><rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|>
        so that by <around*|[|lemma:<reference|lemma 20.158.223>|]>
      </equation*>

      <math|v<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> as is needed in
      (3).\ 
    </note>
  </definition>

  We introduce now the set of Dyadic cubes that are a countable subset of
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> and will be used later to
  express open sets as a countable union.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure Dyadic cubes><index|Dyadic interval><dueto|Dyadic
    Cubes>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> then
    we define <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>=<around*|{|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>\|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
    </equation*>

    Elements of <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> are called Dyadic cubes of
    order <math|m>. Further <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n>> defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-D\><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
    </equation*>

    is the set of all Dyadic cubes.

    <\note>
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have that
      <math|len<around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>|)>=<frac|1|2<rsup|m>>>
      which proves that all sides of <math|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>>
      are equal, hence the name Dyadic <with|font-series|bold|cube>.
    </note>

    <\note>
      As <math|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>> we have that
      <math|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>\<in\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>>
      so that Dyadic cubes are not empty.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.136.200>Let <math|k,l\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> with
    <math|k\<leqslant\>l>, <math|r,q\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>> then for
    <math|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|]>>,
    <math|<around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>> we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>\<neq\>\<emptyset\>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>=\<emptyset\>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|[>\<nsubseteq\><around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    in other words. We have either <math|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>=\<emptyset\>>
    or <math|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>
    but not both.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|l>>|[>> and
    <math|J=<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>. As
    <math|k\<leqslant\>l> we have <math|0\<leqslant\>l-k=n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>.
    Next

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|J>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|k>>\<cdot\><frac|2<rsup|l-k>|2<rsup|l-k>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|k>>\<cdot\><frac|2<rsup|l-k>|2<rsup|l-k>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r\<cdot\>2<rsup|l-k>|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r\<cdot\>2<rsup|l-k>+2<rsup|l-k>|2<rsup|l>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|r\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>|[><eq-number><label|eq
      18.39.022>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Assume that <math|I<big|cap>J\<neq\>\<emptyset\>> so that
    <math|\<exists\>x\<in\>I<big|cap>J> with
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 18.40.022><frac|q<rsub|i>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|q<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|l>>\<wedge\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>
    </equation>

    If now <math|<frac|q<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>>
    then <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|q<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>>
    giving the contradiction <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>> so we must
    have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 18.41.022><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>\<less\><frac|q<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|l>>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|<frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|<rsup|n>>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\><frac|q<rsub|i>|2<rsup|l>>>
    then <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\><frac|q<rsub|i>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>>
    giving the contradiction <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>> again so we
    must have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 18.42.022><frac|q<rsub|i>|2<rsup|l>>\<less\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>
    </equation>

    Multiplying <reference|eq 18.41.022> and <reference|eq 18.42.022> by
    <math|2<rsup|l>> gives <math|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>\<less\>q<rsub|i>+1<text|
    and >q<rsub|i>\<less\>r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>>. As
    <math|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>,q<rsub|i>+1,r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>
    [as <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>] we have by [theorem:
    <reference|complex x\<less\>y=\<gtr\>x+1\<less\>=y>] that
    <math|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|i>> and
    <math|q<rsub|i>+1\<leqslant\>r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>>..
    Dividing by <math|2<rsup|l>> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 18.43.022>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      ><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\><frac|q<rsub|i>|2<rsup|l>>\<wedge\><frac|q<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>
    </equation>

    Next if <math|x\<in\>I> then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|<frac|q<rsub|i>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|q<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|l>>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|<reference|eq
    18.43.022>>><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|r<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>>
    so that <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|[|<frac|r\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r\<exterior\>2<rsup|n>+2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|l>>|[>>>
    proving using [eq: <reference|eq 18.39.022>] that <math|x\<in\>J>. Hence
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <text|If >I<big|cap>J\<neq\>\<emptyset\><text| then >I\<subseteq\>J
    </equation*>

    Finally if <math|I<big|cap>J=\<emptyset\>> then if <math|I\<subseteq\>J>
    we have <math|\<emptyset\>=I<big|cap>J=I> a contradiction as
    <math|<frac|q|2<rsup|l>>\<in\>I>. Hence we have either
    <math|I<big|cap>J=\<emptyset\>> or <math|I\<subseteq\>J> but not both.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Dyadic cubes properties>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> and
      <math|\<forall\>R,Q\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> with
      <math|R\<neq\>Q> we have <math|R<big|cap>Q=\<varnothing\>>

      <item>Let <math|k,l\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> with <math|k\<leqslant\>l>
      then if <math|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|l>> and
      <math|Q\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|k>> we have that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<big|cap>Q\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|R\<subseteq\>Q>>|<row|<cell|R<big|cap>Q=\<varnothing\>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|R\<nsubseteq\>Q>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item><math|\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> we have that
      <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>=<big|sqcup><rsub|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>R>
      [here <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>R> is a
      disjoint union because of (1)].

      <item><math|\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> and
      <math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> we have that
      <math|v<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n\<cdot\>m>>\<less\>1>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> and
      <math|R,Q\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> with <math|R\<neq\>Q>. Then
      there exist <math|r,z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>> such that

      <\equation*>
        R=<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>|[><text|
        and >Q=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>
      </equation*>

      As <math|R\<neq\>Q> there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      such that <math|<around*|[|<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|r<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>\<neq\><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>>,
      hence we must have that <math|r<rsub|i>\<neq\>z<rsub|i>>. We can always
      assume that <math|r<rsub|i>\<less\>z<rsub|i>> [if not exchange <math|R>
      and <math|Q>] so that <math|r<rsub|i>+1\<leqslant\>z<rsub|i>> [as
      <math|r<rsub|i>,z<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> we can use \ [theorem:
      <reference|complex x\<less\>y=\<gtr\>x+1\<less\>=y>]]. Assume that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|r<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>>
      then <math|<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>\<leqslant\>x\<less\><frac|r<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>\<leqslant\><frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>\<leqslant\>x\<less\><frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>>
      leading to the contradiction <math|x\<less\>x> hence we must have that
      <math|<around*|[|<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|r<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>=\<varnothing\>>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|choice product sets empty condition>] it
      follows that

      <\equation*>
        <big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|r<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>|[><big|cap><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      or\ 

      <\equation*>
        R<big|cap>Q=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|k,l\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> with <math|k\<leqslant\>l>
      and <math|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|l>>,
      <math|Q\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|k>> then there exists
      <math|r,q\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>> with
      <math|R=<around*|[|<frac|r|2<rsup|l>>,<frac|r+1|2<rsup|l>>|[>>,
      <math|Q=<around*|[|<frac|q|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|q+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>. Using
      [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.136.200>] we have then that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <text|If <math|R<big|cap>Q\<neq\>\<varnothing\><text| then
        <math|R\<subseteq\>Q>>>>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <text|If >R<big|cap>Q=\<varnothing\><text| then >R\<nsubseteq\>Q
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
      then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
      <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. Using [theorem: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
      <math|z<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> such that
      <math|z<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<cdot\>2<rsup|m>\<less\>z<rsub|i>+1>,
      hence <math|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>>.
      So if we take <math|z=<around*|(|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|m>|)>> then
      <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>
      proving that <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>R>.
      As <math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>
      <math|R\<subseteq\>R<rsup|n>> we have proved that\ 

      <\equation*>
        R<rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>R
      </equation*>

      Finally by (1) we have that <math|\<forall\>R,R<rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>
      with <math|R\<neq\>R<rprime|'>> we have
      <math|R<big|cap>R<rprime|'>=\<varnothing\>> so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        R<rsup|n>=<big|sqcup><rsub|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>R
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><rsub|m>>
      then <math|\<exists\>z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>> such that
      <math|R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        v<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<frac|z<rsub|i>+1-z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|m>>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><frac|1|2<rsup|m>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n\<cdot\>m>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  One of the reasons that we introduce the Dyadic cubes as a subset of
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> is that
  <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> is a denumerable set in contrast with
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> which we need giving the
  definition of a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra. This is proved in the next lemma.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.138.200>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>
      <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> is denumerable.

      <item><math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n>> is denumerable
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \;

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> then as <math|\<bbb-Z\>> is
      denumerable [see theorem: <reference|complex embedding>] we have by
      [theorem: <reference|denumerable product of finite family of
      denumerable sets is denumerable (1)>] that <math|\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>> is
      also denumerable hence there exist a bijection\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>
      </equation*>

      \ Define now <math|\<beta\>:\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>
      by <math|\<beta\><around*|(|z|)>=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>>
      then <math|\<beta\>> is a bijection.

      <\description>
        <item*|injectivity>If <math|\<beta\><around*|(|z|)>=\<beta\><around*|(|w|)>>
        then <math|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>=<around*|[|<frac|w|2<rsup|m>>,<frac|w+1|2<rsup|m>>|[>>.
        Using the fact that a Dyadic cube is not empty together with [lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.132.200>] gives
        <math|<frac|z|2<rsup|m>>=<frac|w|2<rsup|m>>> proving that <math|z=w>.

        <item*|surjectivity>This follows from the definition of
        <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>.
      </description>

      So <math|\<beta\>\<circ\>\<alpha\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>
      is a bijection between <math|\<bbb-N\>> and
      <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> which proves that
      <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>> is denumerable.

      <item>Using [theorem: <reference|denumerable union of a denumerable
      family of denumerable sets is denumerable>] it follows that
      <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>>
      is denumerable.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We are now finally ready to prove that every non empty open set on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> can be written as a denumerable union of pairwise
  disjoint Dyadic cubes.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.139.200>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>> the canonical
    topology on <math|\<bbb-R\>> [see example: <reference|normed maximum norm
    R^n>] then if <math|\<varnothing\>\<neq\>U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>
    there exist a <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint family
    ><math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n>>
    such that <math|U=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|\<varnothing\>\<neq\>U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>
    be a non empty open set in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>. Given
    <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.93.200>\<cal-S\><rsub|m>=<around*|{|D\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>\|D\<subseteq\>U|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>
    </equation>

    and recursively\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.94.200>\<cal-T\><rsub|m>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|0><text|
      if >m=0>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|m>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,m-1|}><text|
      we have >\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|i><text| that
      >R<big|cap>D=\<varnothing\>|}><text| if >m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>>>>
    </equation>

    Using the above definitions we have the following inclusions

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.95.200>\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0><text| we have
      >\<cal-T\><rsub|m>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|m>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n>
    </equation>

    Further we define

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.96.200>\<cal-T\>=<big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-T\><rsub|m>
    </equation>

    If <math|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>> then there exist a
    <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> such that
    <math|D\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> proving by
    [eq: <reference|eq 20.93.200>] that <math|D\<subseteq\>U>, hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.97.200><big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D\<subseteq\>U
    </equation>

    Proving the opposite inclusion is a little more difficult. Let
    <math|x\<in\>U> then there exist a <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>U
    </equation*>

    Using the Archimedean property of the real numbers [see theorem:
    <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist
    <math|n<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.98.200>0\<less\><frac|1|n<rsub|x>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure Dyadic cubes properties> (3)] there
    exist a <math|D<rsub|x>\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|n<rsub|x>>> such that
    <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|x>>. Hence there exist a
    <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>> such that

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|x>=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>|[>\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|n<rsub|x>><text|
      such that >x\<in\>D<rsub|x>
    </equation*>

    from which it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.99.200>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have ><frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|y\<in\>D<rsub|x>> then <math|y\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>|[>>
    so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.100.200>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have ><frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>
    </equation>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.100.200>>>>|<cell|<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>-x<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.99.200>]>>>|<cell|<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>-<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.98.200>>>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>|<row|<cell|x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|i>>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.99.200>]>>>|<cell|<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>-y<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.100.200>>>>|<cell|<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>-<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.98.200>>>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|<around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>. Hence
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|y-x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>=max<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>
    so that <math|y\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>>.
    In conclusion\ 

    <\equation*>
      x\<in\>D<rsub|x>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>U
    </equation*>

    As <math|D<rsub|x>\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|n<rsub|x>>> it follows
    from the above and [eq: <reference|eq 20.93.200>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.101.200>x\<in\>D<rsub|x>\<in\>S<rsub|n<rsub|x>>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.102.200>\<cal-K\><rsub|x>=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|x>|}>\|\<exists\>D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>\|x\<in\>D|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|x><rsub|>|}>
    </equation>

    Then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.101.200>]
    <math|n<rsub|x>\<in\>\<cal-K\><rsub|x>> so that
    <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|x>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>, as further
    <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|x>> is finite it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.103.200>m<rsub|x>=min<around*|(|\<cal-K\><rsub|x>|)><text|
      exist>
    </equation>

    For <math|m<rsub|x>\<in\>\<cal-K\><rsub|x>\<subseteq\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|x>|}>>
    we have now two possible cases:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|m<rsub|x>=0>>Then <math|\<exists\>D\<in\>S<rsub|0>> such
      that <math|x\<in\>D>. As by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.94.200>,
      <reference|eq 20.96.200>] <math|S<rsub|0>=\<cal-T\><rsub|0>> and
      <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|0>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\>> we that
      <math|x\<in\>D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>. In other words
      <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D>.

      <item*|<math|m<rsub|x>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|x>|}>>>Then
      <math|\<exists\>D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|m<rsub|x>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.93.200>]>>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|m<rsub|x>>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>D> and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|x>-1|}>>
      we have that <math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> that
      <math|x\<nin\>R>. Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|x>-1|}>>
      and assume that <math|\<exists\>R\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|i>>
      such that <math|R<big|cap>D\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> then by [theorem:
      <reference|measure Dyadic cubes properties> (2)] and the fact that
      <math|i\<leqslant\>m<rsub|x>> it follows that <math|D\<subseteq\>R>, as
      <math|x\<in\>D> it follows that <math|x\<in\>R> contradicing
      <math|x\<nin\>R> hence the assumption is wrong and we must have that
      <math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> we have that
      <math|R<big|cap>D=\<varnothing\>>. As this is true
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|x>-1|}>> it
      follows from [definition: <reference|eq 20.94.200>] that
      <math|D\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|m<rsub|x>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.96.200>]>>\<cal-T\>>. In other words we have
      <math|x\<in\>D\<in\>\<cal-T\>> which proves that
      <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D>.
    </description>

    So in all cases we have that <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D>
    which as <math|x\<in\>U> was chosen arbitrary proves that
    <math|<rigid|U\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D>>. Combining
    this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.96.200>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.104.200>U=<big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D
    </equation>

    Next we must prove that <math|<big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D> is a
    pairwise disjoint union. Let <math|D,E\<in\>\<cal-T\>=<big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-T\><rsub|m>>
    with <math|D\<neq\>E> then there exist <math|k,l\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>
    such that <math|D\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|k>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|k>>
    and <math|E\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|l>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n><rsub|l>>.
    For <math|k,l> we have the following possible cases:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|k=l>>Then using [theorem: <reference|measure Dyadic cubes
      properties> (1)] it follows that <math|D<big|cap>E=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item*|<math|k\<less\>l>>Then using [eq: <reference|eq 20.94.200>] that
      <math|D<big|cap>E=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item*|<math|l\<less\>k>>Then using [eq: <reference|eq 20.94.200>] that
      <math|D<big|cap>E=\<varnothing\>>.
    </description>

    combining the above with [eq: <reference|eq 20.104.200>] gives then\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.105.200>\<forall\>D,E\<in\>\<cal-T\><text| with
      >D\<neq\>E<text| we have >D<big|cap>E=\<varnothing\>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that <math|\<cal-T\>> is denumerable, first we prove that
    <math|\<cal-T\>> is not finite (hence by definition infinite). Assume
    that <math|\<cal-T\>> is finite. As <math|U\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> we must
    have that <math|\<cal-T\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> [because
    <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<varnothing\>>D=\<varnothing\>>] so that
    <math|m=min<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|begin<around*|(|D|)>|)><rsub|1>\|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>|}>|)>>
    exist. Hence there exist a <math|R=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-T\>> such
    that <math|a<rsub|1>=m>. As <math|a\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>=R> and by [eq:
    <reference|eq 20.104.200>] <math|R\<subseteq\>U> it follows that
    <math|a\<in\>U>. Hence there exist a <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    such that <math|a\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|a,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>U>.
    Define <math|e> by <math|<around*|(|e|)><rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1<text|
    i=1>>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>>
    then for <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around*|(|a-<around*|(|a-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>e|)>|)><rsub|i>|\|>=<around*|\||<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>|\|>=<around*|\||<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1<text|
      if >i=1>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    so that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|a-<around*|(|a-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>e|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    Hence <math|a-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>e\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|a,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>U>
    then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.104.200>] there exist a
    <math|D<rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-T\>> such that
    <math|a-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>e\<in\>D<rprime|'>>. As
    <math|D<rprime|'>=<around*|[|begin<around*|(|D<rprime|'>|)>,end<around*|(|D<rprime|'>|)>|[>>
    it follows that <math|begin<around*|(|D<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>a-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>e>
    so that <math|<around*|(|begin<around*|(|D<rprime|'>|)>|)><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around*|(|a-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>e|)><rsub|1>=a<rsub|1>-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\>1=m-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
    As <math|m=min<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|begin<around*|(|D|)>|)><rsub|1>\|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>|}>|)>>
    <math|<rigid|m\<leqslant\><around*|(|begin<around*|(|D<rprime|'>|)>|)><rsub|1>>>
    which leads to the contradiction <math|m\<leqslant\>m-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>m>,
    so the assumption is wrong and we must have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.106.200>\<cal-T\><text| is not finite or
      <math|\<cal-T\><text| is infinite>>>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<cal-D\><rsup|n>> is denumerable [see theorem: <reference|lemma
    20.138.200>] and <math|\<cal-T\><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.95.200>]>>\<cal-D\><rsup|n>> it follows from the above
    and [theorem: <reference|denumerable every subset is finite
    denumerable.>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-T\><text| is denumerable>
    </equation*>

    So there exist a bijection <math|\<beta\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\>\<cal-T\>>.
    Define then <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n>>
    by <math|D<rsub|i>=\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family union of family set and
      surjections>]>><big|cup><rsub|D\<in\>\<cal-T\>>D=U
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then as
    <math|\<beta\>> is a bijection, hence injective, it follows that
    <math|D<rsub|i>\<neq\>D<rsub|j>>, so that using [eq: <reference|eq
    20.105.200>] we have <math|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      U=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.141.200>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> with
    <math|R\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> then there exist a sequence
    <math|<around*|{|U<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>
    of open sets such that <math|R=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> there exists a
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<leqslant\>b> such that
    <math|R=<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>.
    Define then <math|<around*|{|U<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> by
    <math|U<rsub|i>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>,b<rsub|j>|[>>.
    As \ <math|<around*|]|a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>
    [see theorem: <reference|normed basis of T\|\|>] and
    <math|T<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>> is the product
    topology based on <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> [see
    <reference|normed maximum norm R^n>] it follows that
    <math|<around*|{|U<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>.
    Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|x\<in\>R> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> that
    <math|x<rsub|j>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|j>\<less\>b<rsub|j>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>\<less\>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|j>\<less\>b<rsub|j>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|j>\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>,b<rsub|j>|[>>,
    proving using [theorem: <reference|choice product inclusion (1)>] that
    <math|R\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>>. Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.107.200>R\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inequality let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>>
    then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have <math|x\<in\>U<rsub|i>> so
    that <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|x<rsub|j>\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>\<less\>x<rsub|j>\<less\>b<rsub|j>>.
    Assume that <math|\<exists\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
    <math|x<rsub|j>\<less\>a<rsub|j>> then using [theorem: <reference|complex
    Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists a
    <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x<rsub|j>\<less\>x<rsub|j>+<frac|1|i>\<less\>a<rsub|j>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|j>\<less\>a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>>
    contradicting the fact that <math|a<rsub|j>-<frac|1|i>\<less\>x<rsub|j>>.
    Hence <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|j>\<less\>b<rsub|j>> proving that
    <math|x\<in\>R> so that <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>R>.
    This together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.107.200>] proves

    <\equation*>
      R=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We are ready now to prove a alternative definition for the Borel algebra on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel algebras on R^n equivalences>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|C\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|C<text|
      is close in >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|}>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and closure>] we have

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|C\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|C<text|
      is close in >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|}>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra and
      closure>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|def>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
    </equation*>

    so we only have to prove that <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>.
    Let <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>
    then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|U=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|U=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>.

      <item*|<math|U\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then using [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.139.200>] there exist a sequence
      <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      such that <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>> which as
      <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>> is
      a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra proves that
      <math|U\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    </description>

    Hence we have <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    which by [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.108.200>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inclusion let <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.141.200>] there exist a
    <math|<around*|{|U<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>
    such that <math|R=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|i>>. As
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>>
    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-alebra we have by [theorem: <reference|measure
    sigma algebra equivalences>] that <math|R\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>>.
    Hence <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>>
    which by [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] gives
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.108.200>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|def>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure halfo open spaces>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then for
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> and <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we
    define <math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    by

    <\equation*>
      <around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsub|i>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a|}>
    </equation*>

    The set <math|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsub|i>> is called a
    <with|font-series|bold|lower half open space>. The set of all lower half
    spaces is noted as <math|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-H\><rsup|n>=<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<wedge\>a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  It turns out that every lower half space is a open set.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.183.240>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    <math|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>
    [the canonical topology on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>]
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|H\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsup|n>> then there exists a
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> and a <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    such that <math|H=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a|}>>.
    Take <math|x\<in\>H> then <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a> so that
    <math|\<varepsilon\>=a-x<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. Let
    <math|y\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\><around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>|\|>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>=a-x<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    proving that <math|y<rsub|i>\<less\>a> so that <math|y\<in\>H>. Hence
    <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|max>><around*|(|x,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<subseteq\>H>
    which proves that <math|H> is open.
  </proof>

  Next we show that every half open interval can be written as a finite
  intersection of differences of lower half open spaces.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.184.240>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then
    <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>|)>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>> then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>>
    or <math|\<neg\><around*|(|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>|)>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>>
    so that <math|x\<nin\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\<wedge\>x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.152.241><around*|[|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inclusion, let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>|)>>
    then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|x\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>>
    hence <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>|)>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<wedge\>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>>
    proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. So
    <math|><math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.152.241>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel algebra on R^n (equivalences 2)>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>|]>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|H\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsup|n>> then <math|H> is open [see lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.183.240>], so that
    <math|H\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure Borel algebra on R^n (equivalences
    1)>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>, which proves that
    \ <math|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>,
    so using [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.153.241>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then
    <math|\<exists\>a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> such that
    <math|R=<around*|[|a,b|[>>, using the previous lemma [see lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.184.240>] we have then that
    <math|R=<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,b<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>\\<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a<rsub|i>|[><rsub|i>|)><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>,<reference|measure algebra
    equavalent definition>]>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>|]>>. So
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>|]>>
    which as <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure Borel algebra on R^n (equivalences
    1)>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> proves that
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>|]>>.
    Combining this with <reference|eq 20.153.241>] gives

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel algebra on R^n rectangles>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>>>
    then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|<around*|[||]>><rsup|n>>>By
      [theorem: <reference|measure open closed rectangles>] <math|R> is a
      closed set so that by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and
      closure>] <math|R\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>.

      <item*|<math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|<around*|]||[>><rsup|n><rsub|>>>By
      [theorem: <reference|measure open closed rectangles>] <math|R> is a
      open set so that <math|R\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>.

      <item*|<math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>By
      [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebras on R^n equivalences>] it
      follows that <math|R\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>.

      <item*|<math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|<around*|]||]>><rsup|n>>>Then there
      exists <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<less\>b> such
      that <math|R=<around*|]|a,b|]>>. As is already proved we have that
      <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
      and <math|<around*|]|a,b+1|[>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>,
      hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|[|a,b|]><big|cap><around*|]|a,b+1|[>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
      </equation*>

      Now\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]><big|cap><around*|]|a,b+1|[>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
        x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>+1|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>\<wedge\>a<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>+1>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >a<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|]>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        R=<around*|]|a,b|]>=<around*|[|a,b|]><big|cap><around*|]|a,b+1|]>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <section|Measures>

  <subsection|Measure definition and properties>

  We turn our attention now on associating with every set in a measure
  algebra a non negative number that represent the \<#2018\>size\<#2018\> or
  \<#2018\>volume\<#2018\> of this set. This leads to the study of set
  functions that satisfies the additive properties expected of a
  \<#2019\>volume'. Thanks to the extra properties of a
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra we have the following equivalently statements
  about additivity defined in [definitions: <reference|measure additive set
  function>, <reference|measure sub-additive set function>,
  <reference|measure countable additive set function> and <reference|measure
  countable sub-additive set function>]

  <\theorem>
    Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable
    space [see definition: <reference|measure measurable space>] and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a set
    function then we have the following :\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|finite additive
      ><math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> for every <with|font-series|bold|pairwise
      disjoint> families <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      where <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|finite sub-additive>
      <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> for every
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|countable additive
      ><math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> for all <with|font-series|bold|pairwise
      disjoint> <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|countable sub-additive>
      <math|if\<Leftrightarrow\>> for all
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>This follows from the fact that
      <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have, as <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      and the definition of additivity [see definition: <reference|measure
      additive set function>].

      <item>This follows from the fact that
      <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have, as <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      and the definition of sub-additivity [see definition:
      <reference|measure sub-additive set function>].

      <item>This follows from the fact that
      <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have, as <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and the
      definition of countable additivity [see definition: <reference|measure
      countable additive set function>].

      <item>This follows from the fact that
      <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have, as <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and the
      definition of countable sub-additivity [see definition:
      <reference|measure countable sub-additive set function>].
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Using mathematical induction it is easy to specify a neccesary and
  sufficient condition for finite additivity and finite sub additivity.

  <\lemma>
    <label|label 20.147.200>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a set function then we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\>> is finate additive <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>>
      <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|A<big|cap>B=\<emptyset\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is finite sub additive <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>>
      \ <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \;

    <\enumerate>
      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|A<big|cap>B=\<emptyset\>> then
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2|}>>>
        defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A if
        i=1>>|<row|<cell|B if i=2>>>>>>is pairwise disjoint and
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>.
        So by assumption we have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|2>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let

        <\equation*>
          \<cal-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>\|<text|<math|If
          >><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
          is a pairwise disjoint family then we have that
          ><rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|}>
        </equation*>

        then we have:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let
          <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
          then <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
          proving that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>

          <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let
          <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
          be pairwise disjoint. Take <math|<rigid|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>>
          and <math|B=A<rsub|n+1>>, then <math|A<big|cup>B=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
          and <rigid|<math|A<big|cap>B=<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|i+1>|)>=\<emptyset\>>>.
          Hence we have

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          proving that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
        </description>

        Using mathematical induction we have that <math|\<bbb-N\>=\<cal-S\>>.
        So <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
        pairwise disjoint we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      </description>

      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2|}>>>
        defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A if
        i=1>>|<row|<cell|B if i=2>>>>>>satisfies
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>.
        So by assumption we have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|2>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let

        <\equation*>
          \<cal-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>\|<text|\<forall\>><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
          we have >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|}>
        </equation*>

        then:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let
          <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
          then <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
          proving that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>

          <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let
          <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
          pairwise disjoint. Take <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>
          and <math|B=A<rsub|n+1>>, then <math|A<big|cup>B=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
          . Hence we have

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><rsub|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          proving that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
        </description>

        Using mathematical induction we have that <math|\<bbb-N\>=\<cal-S\>>.
        So <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
        \ we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure countable additivity implies additivity>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then if <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    satisfies:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is countable additive
    </enumerate>

    then <math|\<mu\>> is additive.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    This was actually proved in [theorem: <reference|measure countable
    additivity implies additivity>].
  </proof>

  We are ready now to define a measure on a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra which is
  a countable set function that associate with every set in the
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra a non negative number which represents the
  \<#2018\>size\<#2018\> or \<#2018\>volume\<#2018\> of this set.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure measure definition>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> is a
    <with|font-series|bold|measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>> if\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item>For every <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint>
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      [in other word <math|\<mu\>> is countable additive].
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  A trivial example of a measure is the following.

  <\example>
    Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable
    space then <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    defined by <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> is a measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\>>.
  </example>

  <\proof>
    We have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item>If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>0\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|extended infinite sum of zero
      elements>]>>0=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure measure space><index|measure
    space><index|<math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>><dueto|Measure
    Space>A measure space is a triple <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    where <math|X> is a non empty set, <math| \<cal-A\>> is a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> is a
    measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measure space properties (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we
      have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B>
      and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|A\<subseteq\>B> then
      <math|A<big|cap><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<varnothing\>> and
      <math|B=A<big|cup><around*|(|B\\A|)>> so that by [theorem:
      <reference|measure countable additivity implies additivity>] we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>
      </equation*>

      Further as <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>> we have by
      [theorem: <reference|extended 0\<less\>=y and z=x+y then x\<less\>=z>]
      that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.

      <item>If <math|A\<subseteq\>B> and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      then as <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> we have that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, further by (1)

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>
      </equation*>

      so that by [theorem: <reference|extended x+z=y+z=\<gtr\>x-y>]
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  As sequences of pairwise disjoint sets in a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra plays
  a important role in the definition of a measure the following theorem will
  be usefull.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure countable union pairwise>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    then there exist a <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint family>
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.161.201>] we have for
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> defined by

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1><text| if
      >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}>>A<rsub|j><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| with >i\<neq\>j<text| then
      >B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i=1>>Then <math|B<rsub|i>=B<rsub|1>=A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item*|<math|1\<less\>i>>Then by [theorems: <reference|measure sigma
      algebra equivalences>, <reference|measure algebra properties>]
      <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>\\<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}>>A<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    </description>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measure space properties (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then for every <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure countable union pairwise>] there
    exists a <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint >family
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.113.201>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text| and
      ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>] on the
    above we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.109.300>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    So\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.113.201>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.109.300>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem: <reference|extended
      infinite sum properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|measure measure space properties (4)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then for every
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\><text|
      if >i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>\<varnothing\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<equallim\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family properties (2)>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended series and index
      translation>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  Once we have defined a measure space we can define <math|\<sigma\>>-finite
  sets and <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measures.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure sigma finite set><index|<math|\<sigma\>>-finite set>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|A\<subseteq\>X> is <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<sigma\>>-finite
    under <math|\<mu\>>> if there exist a
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that
    <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>.
  </definition>

  We can find two equivalent definitions for <math|\<sigma\>>-finite sets.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure sigma finite disjoint>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|A\<subseteq\>X> then we have:\ 

    <\equation*>
      A<text| is >\<sigma\><text|-finite under >\<mu\><text|
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >\<exists\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      pairwise disjoint with >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text| such that
      >A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|A> is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite
      under <math|\<mu\>> there exist a <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      such that <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>> with
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Define now

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >A<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|B<rsub|i><text| if
        >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}><text|
        if >i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1|}>>B<rsub|i>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.161.201>] we have that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| is pairwise
        disjoint and ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=A
      </equation*>

      Further, as <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>>, it follows from [theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>] that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>This is trivial.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure sigma finite increasing>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|A\<subseteq\>X> then we have:\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|A<text| is >\<sigma\><text|-finite
      under >\<mu\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Updownarrow\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<exists\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i+1>|)>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
      such that >A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|A> is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite
      there exist a <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      such that <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>. Define
      now\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >A<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>B<rsub|j><below|\<in\>|<text|[theore:
        <reference|measure countable union is in sigma algebra>]>>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.102.202>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1><text|
        and ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=A
      </equation*>

      Finally we have also\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[corollary:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (4)>]>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|j>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>This is trivial
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure sigma finite measure>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then a measure <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<sigma\>>-finite measure> if <math|X>
    is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite. In other words <math|\<mu\>> is
    <math|\<sigma\>>-finite if there exists a
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that
    <math|X=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>.
  </definition>

  We look now at new ways of defining new measures based on existing
  measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure sum and scalar product of measures>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measureable space
    then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      and <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      are measures on <math|\<cal-A\>> then

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> and
      <math|\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      is a measure and <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|B|]>>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|B|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>
      </equation*>

      is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.\ 

      <\note>
        As <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
        <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that <math|\<mu\><rsub|B>> is
        well defined.
      </note>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>First we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=0+0=0
      </equation*>

      Secondly if <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      is pairwise disjoint then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended infinite sum properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>> is a measure.

      <item>First we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>0=0
      </equation*>

      Secondly if <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      is pairwise disjoint then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended infinite sum properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is a measure.

      <item>First we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|B|]>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\><big|cap>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
      </equation*>

      Secondly if <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      is pairwise disjoint then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|B|]>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|B|]>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure on sub sigma algebra>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and let <math|\<cal-B\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X>
    such that <math|\<cal-B\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> then
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-B\>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a measure space where <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>>> is the
    restriction of <math|\<mu\>> to <math|\<cal-B\>> defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>>:\<cal-B\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      where >\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<cal-B\>> is already a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> we
    only have to proof that <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>>> is a measure on
    <math|\<cal-B\>>.\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<cal-B\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> we
      have <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=0>

      <item>If <math|><math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      is a pairwise disjoint family then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-B\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measure on A\|B>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>:\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
    </equation*>

    then we have that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|A<big|cap>B\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>

      <item><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra on
      >A>

      <item><math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>> is a measure on
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>> making <math|<around*|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>|\<rangle\>>>
      a measure space
    </enumerate>

    Measure spaces of the form <math|<around*|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>|\<rangle\>>>
    are called <with|font-series|bold|measure subspaces of
    ><math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>

    <\note>
      If <math|A=X> then <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|X>=<around*|{|X<big|cap>B\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>=\<cal-A\>>
      so that <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|X>>=\<mu\>>, in other
      words we have that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|X>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|X>>|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure sub measure space>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|B<big|cap>A\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}><text|
      and >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\>><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra>
    </equation*>

    As additional <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> it follows
    from the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measure on sub sigma
    algebra>] that <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>> is a measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>.
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure finite measure><index|finite measure>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|\<mu\>> is a <with|font-series|bold|finite measure> if
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  </definition>

  We have the following equivalent definition of a finite measure.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measure finite>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><text| is a finite measure >\<Leftrightarrow\><text|
      >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then
      <math|A\<subseteq\>X> so that by [theorem: <reference|measure measure
      space properties (1)>] <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> we have as
      <math|X\<in\>\<cal-A\>> that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measure space properties (3)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      satisfies <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> then

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      satisfies <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|\<exists\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> then

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1><text| if
        >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then we may always
      assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange <math|i> and
      <math|j>]. Assume that <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>> then
      we have for <math|i> either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|i=1>>Then <math|1=i\<less\>j\<Rightarrow\>i\<leqslant\>j-1\<wedge\>j\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
        so that <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>=B<rsub|1>=A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j-1>>
        and <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>> we have
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j-1>> and <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|j-1>> a
        contradiction..\ 

        <item*|<math|1\<less\>i>>Then <math|1\<less\>i\<less\>j\<Rightarrow\>i\<leqslant\>j-1\<wedge\>j\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
        so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j-1>> and
        <rigid|<math|x\<in\>B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>>> so that
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j-1>> and <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j-1>> a
        contradiction.
      </description>

      so the assumption must be wrong proving that
      <math|B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>. Hence

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.114.201>\<forall\>i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation>

      Next if <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have that
      <math|B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
      if >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.115.201><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n><text|
        >
      </equation>

      Further if <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n>> then
      <math|n\<in\><around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      so that <rigid|<math|m=min<around*|(|<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>>
      exists. We have now two cases to consider for <math|m>:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|m=1>>then <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>=B<rsub|1>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>

        <item*|<math|1\<less\>m>>then <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|m-1>> and
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|m>> so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|m>\\A<rsub|m-1>=B<rsub|m>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>
      </description>

      proving that <math|A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>
      which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.115.201>] proves that
      <math|A<rsub|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.116.201>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >A<rsub|n>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      From the above it follows that <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|n>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>
      proving that <math|<rigid|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|n>>>,
      futher <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
      if >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>
      so that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>.
      Hence we have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.117.201><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Next we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure countable additivity implies
        additivity>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.116.201>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      So that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i+1>|)>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
      sequence exist>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>First we consider the cases where <math|k=1>. Then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>> so that by [theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>]
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.118.118.201>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      Define <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> by
      <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>>. Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
      for <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>> we have
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>\<wedge\>x\<nin\>A<rsub|i>\<supseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>>
      so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i+1>=B<rsub|i+1>>. Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.119.201>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i+1>
      </equation>

      So using (1) it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.120.201>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Now\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family de Morgan>]>>>|<cell|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
        <reference|family de Morgan>]>>>|<cell|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i><eq-number><label|eq
        20.121.201>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>> it follows
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.122.201>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      and <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.123.201>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      Next\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.121.201>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.123.201>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.120.201>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.118.118.201>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<below|lim<rsub|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>|><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorems:
        <reference|extended limit properties>, <reference|extended limit
        constant sequence>]]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\>*<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\>*<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      hence we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.124.201>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text|
        with >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text| and
        >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Next we proof the general case where <math|1\<less\>k>. Define
      <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      by <math|C<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>> then we have as
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|1>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1+<around*|(|k-1|)>>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Further <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have that
      <math|C<rsub|i+1>=A<rsub|<around*|(|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>|)>+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=C<rsub|i>>.
      So all conditions of [eq: <reference|eq 20.124.201>] are satisfied and
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      As <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>A<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit and index
      translation>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>A<rsub|i>> so that
      we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.126.201.1>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrowlim\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>. Then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have, as
      <math|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> [because <math|1\<less\>k>],
      that <math|<rigid|x\<in\>A<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=C<rsub|i>>> hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.126.201.2><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>>. For
      <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k-1|}>>>Then
        <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|1>=A<rsub|1+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=A<rsub|k><below|\<subseteq\>|i\<leqslant\>k-1\<less\>k>A<rsub|i>>
        so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>.>

        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>Then
        <math|i-<around*|(|k-1|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> so that
        <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i-<around*|(|k-1|)>>=A<rsub|<around*|(|i-<around*|(|k-1|)>|)>+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=A<rsub|i>>
        so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>
      </description>

      as <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> was choosen arbitrary it follows that
      <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>, hence
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.126.201.2>] proves that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.127.201.1><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      So combining [eq: <reference|eq 20.126.201.1>] and [eq: <reference|eq
      20.127.201.1>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves the case <math|1\<less\>k>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  The above theorem has the following inverse which provides ways to prove if
  a set function is actually a mesure.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measure conditions>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a set
    funtion that satisfies:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>.

      <item>If <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>,<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      is a <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint family> then

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      that satisfies <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> we have that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and \ <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      then <math|\<mu\>> is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      that satisfies <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>> we have
      that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and \ <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=0>
      then <math|\<mu\>> is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|o>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be a pairwise disjoint family. Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| by
        >B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.102.202>] we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.131.202><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i+1>
      </equation>

      Then by the hypothesis

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.132.202><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>B<rsub|i><text|
        exist and >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Next\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.131.202>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.132.202>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|hypothesis>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving countable additivity. Hence <math|\<mu\>> is a measure on
      <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|o>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be a pairwise disjoint family. Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| by
        >B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure countable union is in sigma algebra>]>>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|B<rsub|i+1>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|j>=B<rsub|i>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>> then
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|1>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j><rsub|>>
      so that <math|\<exists\>k\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|k>>, as
      <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>> we must have
      that <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|k>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>>
      so that <math|\<exists\>l\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|l>>. Hence it follows that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|k><big|cap>A<rsub|l>\<equallim\><rsub|k\<neq\>l>\<varnothing\>>
      leading to the contradiction <math|x\<in\>\<varnothing\>>. Hence we
      have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>=<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>>.
      Assume <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>>>
      then there exist a <math|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>> and a
      <math|l<rigid|\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|k>\<wedge\>x\<in\>A<rsub|l>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\>A<rsub|k><big|cap>A<rsub|l><below|=|k\<neq\>l>\<varnothing\>>
      leading to the contradiction <math|x\<in\>\<varnothing\>>. Hence we
      must have that <math|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>=\<varnothing\>>.
      By the hypothesis we have then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
        is finite additive>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
        is finite additive>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.140.210.1>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      By [definition: <reference|extended infinite sum>]\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.141.210.2><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)><text|
        exist and ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>
      </equation>

      and by the hypothesis\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.142.210.3><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0
      </equation>

      Next we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit constant
        sequence>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.140.210.1>>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>\<wedge\><text|[eqs:
        <reference|eq 20.141.210.2>, <reference|eq
        20.142.210.3>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>+<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
        <reference|eq 20.141.210.2>, <reference|eq
        20.142.210.3>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      So <math|\<mu\>> is countable additive proving that <math|\<mu\>> is a
      measure.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Carathéodory Theorem>

  The Carathéodory theorem provides a general method for constructing a
  measure. Given a set <math|X> and a non negative set function on
  <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> the Carathéodory theorem allows us to
  reduce <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> to a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
  <math|X> such that the restriction of the set function to this
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra is a measure on this <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra. The
  idea is that <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is in general to big to be
  used as a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on which we can define a measure. As the
  restriction of the set function is a measure it must satisfies some extra
  conditions. For example every <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra contains
  <math|\<varnothing\>> and a measure must map <math|\<varnothing\>> to
  <math|0> so the set function on <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> must also
  map <math|\<varnothing\>> to <math|0>. This is the idea of a outer-measure.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure outer measure><index|outer
    measure><index|<math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>><dueto|Outer Measure>Let
    <math|X> be a set then <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|outer measure >on <math|X> if\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item>For <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> with
      <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we have <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>
      [in other words <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>> is monoton]

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      we have <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      [in otherwords <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>>> is countable sub-additive]
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  It is easy to prove that a outer measure is finitely sub-additive.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure outer measure is finite subadditive>Let <math|X> be a set
    and <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a outer measure on <math|X>, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    then <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Define <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    by <math|C<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i><text| if
    >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\><text| if
    >i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>> then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text|
      is a outer measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended series and index
      translation>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|\<infty\>>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended infinite sum of zero
      elements>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  We need now to reduce <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> to a
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra in such a way that the restriction of the
  outer-measure to this <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra becomes a measure. This is
  the idea of <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>-measurable sets.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure measurable sets><index|measurable
    sets><index|<math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>><dueto|<math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>-measurable
    sets>Let <math|X> be a set and <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a outer measure on <math|X> then <math|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    is <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>-measurable if

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text| we have
      >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>
    </equation*>

    The set of all <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>-measurable sets in
    <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is noted as
    <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>, hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
      we have >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Using the properties of a outer-measure we can simplify the above
  definition.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measurable set equivalences>Let <math|X> be a set and
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      A<text| is >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text|-measurable
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
      we have >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence we have the following alternative definition for
    <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
      we have >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>|}>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>This is trivial as
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>>
      implies <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      then as <math|B=<around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)><big|cup><around*|(|B\\A|)>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measure outer measure is finite
      subadditive>] that <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>>>,
      combining this with the hypothesis it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure measurable sets properties>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a outer measure and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> then

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=0> then
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|X\\A|)>=0> then
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> then as
      <math|B<big|cap>A\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=0>
      so that <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>=0>, further
      as <math|B\\A\<subseteq\>B> we have
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>.
      Hence it follows that <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>

      proving by the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measure measurable
      set equivalences>] that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.

      <item>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> then as
      <math|B\\A\<subseteq\>X\\A> we have
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<subseteq\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|X\\A|)>=0>
      so that <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>=0>, further as
      <math|B<big|cap>A\<subseteq\>B> we have
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>.
      Hence it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>

      proving by the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measure measurable
      set equivalences>] that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We prove now that <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>> is a
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra and the restriction of <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  on <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>> is a measure on
  <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Caratheodory><dueto|Carathéodory Theorem>Let <math|X> be a
    set and <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a outer measure on <math|X> then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>> is a
      <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X>.

      <item><math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>:\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      is a measure on <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>X|)>+0=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>X|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>X|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|B\\X|)>
      </equation*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.163\<point\>202>X\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>> then for
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap><around*|(|X\\A|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<around*|(|X\\A|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|B\<subseteq\>X\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and intersection>
        (6)]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap><around*|(|X\\A|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|B\<subseteq\>X\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and intersection>
        (8)]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that <math|X\\A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.164.202>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text|
        we have >X\\A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>> then
      for <math|C\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)><big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)><big|cap>A|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class intersection, union, inclusion>
        (7)]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>\\A|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class intersection, union, inclusion>
        (6)]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|A\\A|)><big|cup><around*|(|B\\A|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|B\\A|)>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.165.202>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|B\\A|)>|)>
      </equation>

      So we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C\\<around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.165.202>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|B\\A|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C\\<around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class set difference and union ,
        intersection>(1)]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap><around*|(|B\\A|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|C\\A|)>\\B|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class intersection, union,
        inclusion>(7)]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|C\\A|)><big|cap>B|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|C\\A|)>\\B|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|B\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|C\\A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|C|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|A<big|cup>B> is <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>-measurable.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.166.202>\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text|
        we have that >A<big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text|
        [in other words >\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text| is
        >\<cup\><text|-stable]>
      </equation>

      From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.163\<point\>202>, <reference|eq 20.164.202>
      and <reference|eq 20.166.202>] and [theorem: <reference|measure algebra
      equavalent definition>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.167.202>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text|
        is a algebra>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
      <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint> and
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>. We show then by mathematical
      induction that <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.168.202>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      <\proof>
        Define\ 

        <\equation*>
          S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|}>
        </equation*>

        then we have:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>As <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
          we have\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A<rsub|1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|[|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|]>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          which proves that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>

          <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>If
          <math|x\<in\>B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>> then <math|x\<in\>B> and
          <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n+1>> then <math|<rigid|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
          we have by pairwise disjointedness <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|i>> so that
          <math|x\<nin\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>
          or <rigid|<math|x\<in\><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>>>.
          Hence <math|B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>\<subseteq\><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>\<subseteq\>B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>>.
          Summarized\ 

          <\equation>
            <label|eq 20.169.202><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>=B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>
          </equation>

          As <math|A<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
          we have\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\A<rsub|n+1>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
            <reference|eq 20.169.202>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\A<rsub|n+1>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
            <reference|class set difference and union ,
            intersection>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>A<rsub|n+1>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<around*|(|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\<nobracket\>>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          proving that\ 

          <\equation>
            <label|eq 20.170.202>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<around*|(|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\<nobracket\>>|)>
          </equation>

          Finally\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|n\<in\>S>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
            <reference|eq 20.170.202>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|n+1>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<around*|(|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\<nobracket\>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<asymp\>1><rsup|n+1>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<around*|(|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\<nobracket\>>|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          which proves that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
        </description>

        Mathematical induction proves then [eq: <reference|eq 20.168.202>].
      </proof>

      Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have
      <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>
      so that <math|B\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>
      hence <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>.
      So\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.168.202>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.171.202>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Next\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\><text|>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>\<wedge\><text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.171.202>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit constant
        sequence>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
      and <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.172.202><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation>

      Hence we have for <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
      and <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|><around*|(|B<big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text|
        is a outer measure>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.172.202>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      So by [theorem: <reference|measure measurable set equivalences>] it
      follows that <math|><math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>
      is <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>>>-measureable. Hence we have proved that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.173.202>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text|
        pairwise disjoint we have ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>> is a algebra [see
      eq: <reference|eq 20.167.202>] we can use the alternative definition
      for a <math|\<sigma\>>-algbra [see theorem: <reference|measure sigma
      algebra equivalences>] and the above to prove that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text| is a
        >\<sigma\><text|-algebra>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
      a pairwise disjoint family then for
      <math|B=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.172.202>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As further by the sub-additivity of <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|*\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      it follows that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|*\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      which together with <math|><math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>
      proves

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><text|
        is a measure on >\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Uniqueness of measure extensions>

  We will use the Carathéodory theorem to extend set functions defined on a
  subset <math|\<cal-G\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> to a measure on
  <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>>. In this section we will examine
  under which conditions this extensions is unique. To simplify our proofs we
  first introduce the concept of Dynkin systems.

  <subsubsection|Dynkin systems>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure Dynkin system><index|Dynkim
    system><index|<math|\<frak-D\>>><dueto|Dynkin System>Let <math|X> be a
    set then <math|\<frak-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is a
    <with|font-series|bold|Dynkin system >on <math|X> if\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|X\<in\>\<frak-D\>>

      <item>If <math|D\<in\>\<frak-D\>> then <math|X\\D\<in\>\<frak-D\>>

      <item>If <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\>>
      is a <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint family >then
      <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>\<in\>\<frak-D\>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      Using [definition: <reference|measure sigma algebra>] every
      <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra is automatical a Dynkin system however the
      opposite is in general not true.
    </note>
  </definition>

  Some of the properties of a Dynkin system are similar to the properties of
  a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Dynkin system properties>Let <math|X> be a set and
    <math|\<frak-D\>> is a Dynkin system on <math|X> then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<frak-D\>>

      <item>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
      <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\>>
      pairwise disjoint then <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>D<rsub|i>\<in\>\<frak-D\>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|X\<in\>\<frak-D\>> we have that
      <math|\<varnothing\>=X\\X\<in\>\<frak-D\>>

      <item>Define <math|<around*|{|E<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\>>
      by <math|E<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>|<row|<cell|D<rsub|i><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>>. Let
      <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then we have either

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|i\<leqslant\>n\<wedge\>j\<leqslant\>n>>Then
        <math|E<rsub|i><big|cap>E<rsub|j>=D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>

        <item*|<math|n\<less\>i\<wedge\>j\<leqslant\>n>>Then
        <math|E<rsub|i><big|cap>E<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>

        <item*|<math|i\<leqslant\>n\<wedge\>n\<less\>j>>Then
        <math|E<rsub|i><big|cap>E<rsub|j>=D<rsub|i><big|cap>\<varnothing\>=\<varnothing\>>

        <item*|<math|n\<less\>i\<wedge\>n\<less\>j>>Then
        <math|E<rsub|i><big|cap>E<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\><big|cap>\<varnothing\>=\<varnothing\>>
      </description>

      which proves that <math|<around*|{|E<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
      is pairwise disjoint. So\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<frak-D\>
      </equation*>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>D<rsub|i>>
      then <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|i>=E<rsub|i>> so that
      <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>>. If
      <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>> then there
      exist a <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<in\>E<rsub|i>>, as
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      <math|E<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>> we must have that
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> so that
      <math|E<rsub|i>=D<rsub|i>> proving that
      <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>D<rsub|i>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>D<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<frak-D\>
      </equation*>

      \;
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\example>
    <label|measure Dynkin system P(X)>Let <math|X> be a set then
    <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is a Dynkin system on <math|X>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    \ As <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
    <math|X> [see example: <reference|measure P(X) is a sigma algebra>] and
    every <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra is a Dynkin system it follows that
    <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is a Dynkin system on <math|X>.
  </proof>

  Given a set of subsets of <math|X> we can, just like with
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras, find the smallest Dynkin system that contains
  the set of subsets.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.172.1>Let <math|X> be a set and <math|\<cal-X\>> a non
    empty set of Dynkin systems on <math|X> then
    <math|<big|cap><rsub|\<frak-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<frak-D\>> is a Dynkin
    system on <math|X>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<cal-X\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> there exist a
    <math|\<frak-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>> with
    <math|\<frak-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> so that we have by
    [theorem: <reference|class general intersection>] that
    <math|<big|cap><rsub|<math-tt|>\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|cap><rsub|<math-tt|>\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation*>

    \ <math|>As <math|\<forall\>\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<frak-D\>> <math|\<cal-D\>>
    is a Dynkin system on <math|X> we have <math|X\<in\>\<cal-D\>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      X\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|A\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>> then
    for <math|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>> we have <math|A\<in\>\<cal-D\>> so
    that <math|X\\A\<in\>\<cal-D\>> which as <math|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>
    was chosen arbitrary proves that <math|X\\A\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>>.
    Hence, as <math|A\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>>
    was chosen arbitrary, we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\><text|
      we have >X\\A\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>
    </equation*>

    If <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>>
    is pairwise disjoint then for <math|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>> we have
    <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>
    so that <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>
    which as <math|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>> was chosen arbitrary proves that
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i><rsub|>\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>>.
    Hence, as <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>>
    was chosen arbitrary, we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <text|For every pairwise disjoint family
      ><around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\><text|
      we have ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<cal-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<cal-D\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Dynkin system generated>Let <math|X> be a set
    <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> then there exist a
    <with|font-series|bold|unique> Dynkin system
    <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-A\>|]>> on <math|X> that satsifies:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>

      <item>If <math|\<frak-D\><rprime|'>> is another Dynkin system with
      <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rprime|'>> then
      <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rprime|'>>

      <item><math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>
    </enumerate>

    This unique smallest Dynkin system that contains <math|\<cal-D\>> is
    called the Dynkin system generated by <math|\<cal-D\>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Define <math|\<cal-X\>=<around*|{|\<frak-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<frak-D\><text|
      is a Dynkin system on >X<text| and >\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\>|}>>
      then as <math|\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is a Dynkin system [see
      example: <reference|measure Dynkin system P(X)>] and
      <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> it follows that
      <math|\<cal-X\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>. Hence by [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.172.1>] it follows that, if we take
      <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>=<big|cap><rsub|\<frak-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<frak-D\>>,
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><text| is a Dynkin system on >X
      </equation*>

      If <math|D\<in\>\<cal-D\>> then, as
      <math|\<forall\>\<frak-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>> we have by defintion of
      <math|\<cal-X\>> that <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\>>, it
      follows that <math|D\<in\>\<frak-D\>>, hence
      <math|D\<in\><big|cap><rsub|\<frak-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<frak-D\>>. So
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-D\>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|\<frak-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<frak-D\>=\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Assume that <math|\<frak-D\><rprime|'>> is another Dynkin system
      on <math|X> such that <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rprime|'>>
      then <math|\<frak-D\><rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-X\>> so that by [theorem:
      <reference|class general intersection>]

      <\equation*>
        \<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>=<big|cap><rsub|\<frak-D\>\<in\>\<cal-X\>>\<frak-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rprime|'>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>,
      <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra
      hence a Dynkin system on <math|X> it follows from (2) that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We show now the sufficient and necessary condition for a Dynkin system to
  be a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Dynkin system condition for sigma algebra>Let <math|X> be
    a set and <math|\<frak-D\>> a Dynkin system on <math|X> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-D\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<frak-D\><text| we have
      >A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<frak-D\><text| [in other words >\<frak-D\><text| is
      >\<cap\><text|-stable]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<frak-D\>> then, as
      <math|\<frak-D\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra, it follows from
      [theorems: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>,
      <reference|measure algebra equavalent definition>] <math|>that
      <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>As <math|\<frak-D\>> is a Dynkin system and
      <math|\<cap\>>-stable we have already that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.133.202>X\<in\>\<frak-D\><text|,
        >\<forall\>D\<in\>\<frak-D\><text| we have
        >X\\D\<in\>\<frak-D\><infix-and>\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<frak-D\><text|
        we have >A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<frak-D\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\>>.
      Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|E<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| by
        >E<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsub|1><text| if
        >i=1>>|<row|<cell|D<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}>><around*|(|X\\D<rsub|j>|)>|)><text|
        if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>\<infty\>|}>>>>>><below|\<in\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.133.202>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure union intersection stable>]>>\<frak-D\>
      </equation*>

      Then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.134.202>E<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>D<rsub|i><text|
        if >i=1>>|<row|<cell|D<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}>><around*|(|X\\D<rsub|j>|)>|)>\<subseteq\>D<rsub|i><text|
        if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>\<subseteq\>D<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.135.202><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>> then there
      exist a <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|k>>.
      Define

      <\equation*>
        S=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>\|x\<in\>D<rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>
      </equation*>

      then <math|k\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>S\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> and <math|S>
      is finite so that <math|m=min<around*|(|S|)>> exists. For <math|m> we
      have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|m=1>>Then <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|1>=E<rsub|1>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>>

        <item*|<math|1\<less\>m>>Then <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|m>> and
        <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>>
        <math|x\<nin\>D<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\>X\\D<rsub|i>> so that

        <\equation*>
          x\<in\>D<rsub|m><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|(|X\\D<rsub|i>|)>|)>=E<rsub|m>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      Hence <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>>
      and we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.135.202>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.136.202><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Let <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then we may always
      assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange <math|i> and
      <math|j>]. Assume that <math|x\<in\>E<rsub|i><big|cap>E<rsub|j>> then
      <math|x\<in\>E<rsub|i><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.134.202>]>>D<rsub|i>> and \ <math|<rigid|x\<in\>E<rsub|j>=D<rsub|j><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,j-1|}>><around*|(|X\\D<rsub|k>|)>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|X\\D<rsub|i>|)>>\<Rightarrow\>x\<nin\>D<rsub|i>>
      so that we reach the contradiction <rigid|<math|x\<in\>D<rsub|i>> and
      <math|x\<nin\>D<rsub|i>>>. Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| with >i\<neq\>j<text| we have
        >E<rsub|i><big|cap>E<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      So by the definition of a Dynkin system it follows that

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.136.202>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<frak-D\>
      </equation*>

      Finally by the above, [eq: <reference|eq 20.133.202>] and [definition:
      <reference|measure sigma algebra>] it follows that <math|\<frak-D\>> is
      a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Dynkin system condition for sigma algebra (1)>If
    <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|\<cal-A\>|)>> is such that
    <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<frak-D\>> <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-D\>>
    [in other words <math|\<frak-D\>> is <math|\<cap\>>-stable] then
    <math|\<delta\><around*|(|\<cal-D\>|)>=\<sigma\><around*|(|\<cal-D\>|)>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure Dynkin system generated>] it follows
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.137.202>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inclusion. Defiine for every
    <math|D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.138.202>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>=<around*|{|E\<subseteq\>X\|E<big|cap>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>|}>
    </equation>

    As <math|X\<subseteq\>X> and <math|X<big|cap>D=D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.139.202>X\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>
    </equation>

    Let <math|E\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>> then <math|X\\E\<subseteq\>X> and\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X\\<around*|(|<around*|(|E<big|cap>D|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\D|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class set difference and union ,
      intersection>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|X\\<around*|(|E<big|cap>D|)>|)><big|cap><around*|(|X\\<around*|(|X\\D|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class inclusion and union and
      intersection>]>\<wedge\>D\<subseteq\>X>>|<cell|<around*|(|X\\<around*|(|E<big|cap>D|)>|)><big|cap>D>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class set difference and union ,
      intersection>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|X\\E|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\D|)>|)><big|cap>D>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class class commutative,idempotent,associative,distributivity>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|X\\E|)><big|cap>D|)><big|cup><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\D|)><big|cap>D|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|X\\E|)><big|cap>D|)><big|cup>\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|X\\E|)><big|cap>D>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Further <math|<around*|(|E<big|cap>D|)><big|cap><around*|(|X\\D|)>=\<varnothing\>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.140.202><around*|(|X\\E|)><big|cap>D=X\\<around*|(|<around*|(|E<big|cap>D|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|X\\D|)>|)><text|
      [union is disjoint union]>
    </equation>

    As <math|E\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>> we have by [eq: <reference|eq
    20.138.202>] that <math|E<big|cap>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>,
    further as <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> is a Dynkin system and
    <math|D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> it follows that
    <math|X\\D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> so that
    <math|<around*|(|E<big|cap>D|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|X\\D|)>\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>
    and finally that\ 

    <\equation*>
      X\\<around*|(|<around*|(|E<big|cap>D|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|X\\D|)>|)>\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Hence by [eq: <reference|eq 20.140.202>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.141.202>\<forall\>E\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D><text| that
      >X\\E\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>>
    be pairwise disjoint then <math|\<forall\>i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with
    <math|i\<neq\>j> we have <rigid|<math|<around*|(|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D|)><big|cap><around*|(|D<rsub|j><big|cap>D|)>\<subseteq\>D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>>,
    further <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have by the definition of
    <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|D>> that <rigid|<math|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>>.
    Hence <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>
    is pairwise disjoint, so that, as <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>
    is a Dynkin system, we have \ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>D=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D|)>\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>
    </equation*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.142.202><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>
    </equation>

    From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.139.202>. <reference|eq 20.141.202> and
    <reference|eq 20.142.202>] it follows that <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|D>> is a
    Dynkin system on <math|X>, hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.143.202>\<forall\>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><text|
      we have that >\<frak-D\><rsub|D><text| is a Dynkin system>
    </equation>

    Let <math|G\<in\>\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>
    then by the hypothesis we have <math|\<forall\>D\<in\>\<cal-D\>> that
    <math|G<big|cap>D\<in\>\<cal-D\>\<Rightarrow\>D\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|G>>,
    hence <math|\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|G>>. As
    <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|G>> is a Dynkin system and
    <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> is the smallest Dynkin system
    containing <math|\<cal-D\>> it follows that
    <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|G>>.
    Hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.144.202>\<forall\>G\<in\>\<cal-D\><text| we have
      >\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|G>
    </equation>

    Let <math|G\<in\>\<cal-D\>> and <math|D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.144.202>]>>\<frak-D\><rsub|G>> then by the definition of
    <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|G>> we have <math|G<big|cap>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>
    so that <math|G\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>>. In other words
    <math|\<forall\>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> we have
    <math|\<forall\>G\<in\>\<cal-D\>> that <math|G\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>>
    proving that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><text| we have
      >\<cal-D\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> is the smallest Dynkin system
    containg <math|\<cal-D\>> and <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|D>> is a Dynkin
    system [see eq: <reference|eq 20.143.202>] it follows from the above that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>D\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><text| that
      >\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|D>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> then by the above
    <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|B>>
    and, as <math|A\<in\>\<delta\><around*|(|\<cal-D\>|)>>,
    \ <math|A\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|B>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.138.202>]>>A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>>.
    Hence we have

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><text| we have
      <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]>> where
      >\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><text| is a Dynkin system>
    </equation*>

    \ Hence by [theorem: <reference|measure Dynkin system condition for sigma
    algebra>] it follows finally that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-D\>|]><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove that every set function on
  <math|\<cal-G\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> has a unique extension
  to a measure on <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>> if certain
  conditions are met.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure uniqueness of extension of a measure>Let <math|X> be a
    set, <math|\<cal-G\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> such that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-G\>> we have
      <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-G\>> [in other words <math|\<cal-G\>> is
      <math|\<cap\>>-stable]

      <item><math|\<exists\><around*|{|G<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-G\>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|G<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>G<rsub|i+1><text|>> and
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>G<rsub|i>=X>
    </enumerate>

    and <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>> are measures on
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>> such that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <with|item-nr|2|<item>><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-G\>>
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>

      <with|item-nr|3|<item>><math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>
      \<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> [hence as
      <math|G<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-G\>> we have also
      <math|\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>]
    </enumerate>

    then we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<nu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.145.202>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>\|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>
    </equation>

    We prove then that <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|n>> is a Dynkin system that
    contains <math|\<cal-G\>> for every <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. Take
    \ <math|G\<in\>\<cal-G\>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|\<cal-G\>|)>>
    then <math|G,G<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-G\>> so that by (1)
    <math|G<rsub|n><big|cap>G\<in\>\<cal-G\>>, as <math|\<mu\>>,
    <math|\<nu\>> are the same on <math|\<cal-G\>> we have
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>G|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>G|)>>.
    Hence we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.146.202>\<cal-G\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    As <math|X\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>X|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|G<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-G\>>\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)><below|=\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>X|)>|>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.147.202>X\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>> then we have
    <math|G<rsub|n>\\<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|class set difference and union ,
    intersection>]>><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\G<rsub|n>|)><big|cup><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\A|)>=\<varnothing\><big|cup><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\A|)>=G<rsub|n>\\A>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.148.202>G<rsub|n>\\<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>=G<rsub|n>\\A
    </equation>

    Further <math|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure measure space properties
    (1)>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|(4)>>\<infty\>>
    and <math|\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure measure space properties
    (1)>]>>\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|(4)>>\<infty\>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.149.202>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>\<wedge\>\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)>-\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>
    </equation>

    hence\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap><around*|(|X\\A|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class inclusion and union and
      intersection>(8)]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.148.202>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.149.202>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)>-\<nu\>*<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|G<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-G\>>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>|)>-\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.149.202>]>>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.148.202>]>>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class inclusion and union and
      intersection>(8)]>>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap><around*|(|X\\A|)>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    proving that <math|<around*|(|X\\A|)>\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>>, further
    as <math|A\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>>
    we have <math|X\\A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>>. Hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.150.202>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n><text| we have
      >X\\A\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>>
    be a pairwise disjoint family. Then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    with <math|i\<neq\>j> we have <math|<rigid|<around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|j>|)>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>
    so that <math|<around*|{|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
    is pairwise disjoint. Hence, as <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>> are measures, it
    follows that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>
    </equation*>

    which, as <math|G<rsub|n><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>>
    and <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>>,
    proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.151.202>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n><text|
      we have ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|>\<in\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    By [eqs: <reference|eq 20.147.202>, <reference|eq 20.150.202> and
    <reference|eq 20.151.202>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.152.202>\<frak-D\><rsub|n><text| is a Dynkin system>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-G\>> we have by (1) that
    <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-G\>> we have by [theorem: <reference|measure
    Dynkin system condition for sigma algebra (1)>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.153.202>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>
    </equation>

    As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.146.202>]
    <math|\<cal-G\>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n>> and
    <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|n>> is a Dynkin system it follows that

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]><below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.153.202>]>>\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<frak-D\><rsub|n><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.145.202>]>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Hence we have <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
    >\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>=\<frak-D\><rsub|n>> so that using the
    definition of <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> [see eq: <reference|eq
    20.145.202>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.154.202>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]> that
      \<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|n><big|cap>A|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>> then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A\<subseteq\>G<rsub|i+1><big|cap>A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>>
    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.173.1>A=A<big|cap>X=A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>G<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>
    </equation>

    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>] we
    have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.155.202><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|)><below|\<equallim\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.173.1>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.156.202><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|)><below|\<equallim\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.173.1>]>>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    Hence we have for <math|A\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-G\>|]>> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.155.202>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.154.202>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.156.202>]>>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<nu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Constructing measures and <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras><label|Constructing
  measures and sigma alegebra>

  <subsubsection|Constructing outer measures>

  Let <math|X> be a set then given <math|\<cal-G\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
  we want to extend a function <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-G\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
  to a measure on a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra containing <math|\<cal-G\>>. To
  use the Carathéodory theorem for this task we have to construct a outer
  measure out of <math|\<mu\>>. This is the subject of this section. First we
  need some definitions that specifies the minmum requirements for this task.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure proto-ring><index|proto-ring><dueto|Proto-Ring>Let
    <math|X> be a set then <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|proto-ring> on <math|X> if\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<varnothing\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure proto-measure><index|proto-measure><dueto|Proto-Measure>Let
    <math|X> be a set then <math|\<cal-S\>> a proto-ring on <math|X> then a
    function <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|proto-measure >on <math|\<cal-S\>> if\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Next we shown how to construct a outer-measure from a proto-measure.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure outer measure construction>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a proto-ring and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    proto-measure on <math|\<cal-S\>> then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)><text|
        if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text|
        if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=\<varnothing\>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      where\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=<around*|{|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>\|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|}>
      </equation*>

      is a outer measure.\ 

      <item>If additional we have that\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>S> that
        <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-S\>>

        <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B>
        we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>

        <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
        with ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><text|
        we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>
      </enumerate>

      then

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      <label|note 20.198.210>The outer measure defined in (1) is not
      neccesary a extension of <math|\<mu\>>, for that we need the extra
      conditions 2.a, 2.b, 2.c.
    </note>

    <\note>
      If <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>\<neq\>0> then
      <math|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|extended sup and inf exist>]
      <math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>>
      exist. Further as <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<b-S\><around*|[|A|]>>
      <math|0\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      we have that <math|0\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>>.
      This proves that <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>> is well defined.\ 
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Define <math|<around*|{|N<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      by <math|N<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then
      <math|\<varnothing\>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>N<rsub|i>>
      so that <math|<around*|{|N<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|\<varnothing\>|]>>,
      hence <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|\<varnothing\>|]>\<neq\>\<oslash\>>. So
      <math|0=<big|sum><rsub|I=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|i>|)>\<in\><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>>,
      hence

      <\equation*>
        0\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>0
      </equation*>

      proving that <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>=0>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.157.202>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
      </equation>

      Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> with
      <math|A\<subseteq\>B>. Then for <math|S<around*|[|B|]>> we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|S<around*|[|B|]>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
        <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|[|A|]>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|[|B|]>>

        <item*|<math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Let
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>>
        then <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>S>
        and <math|B\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>
        hence, as <math|A\<subseteq\>B>, we have
        <math|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> so that
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>>.
        Hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<varnothing\>\<neq\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>\<subseteq\>S<around*|[|A|]>
        </equation*>

        From the above it follows that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>,
        </equation*>

        so that by [theorem: <reference|order sup,inf and inclusion>]\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|[|A|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|[|B|]>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </description>

      To summarize\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.158.202>\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
        with >A\<subseteq\>B<text| we have
        >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|T<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      then we have two cases to consider:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<exists\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
        >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>=\<infty\>>>Then
        <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>.

        <item*|<math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>Then
        we must have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
        <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|T<rsub|i>|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> so that

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|T<rsub|i>|]>|}>|)>
        </equation*>

        Take <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. Given
        <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have, as
        <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
        that <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n>>>>,
        so as <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|T<rsub|n>|]>|}>|)>>,
        there exist a <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|n,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|T<rsub|n>|]><text|
        [so that ><rigid|T<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|n,i>>>]
        that satisfies <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|I<rsub|n,i>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n>>>.
        Hence we have

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.159.202>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|I<rsub|n,i>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|n>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n>><text|
          and >T<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|n,i>
        </equation>

        As <math|\<bbb-N\>\<times\>\<bbb-N\>> is denumerable [see theorem:
        <reference|denumerable product of denumerable sets is denumerable>]
        there exist a bijection <math|\<beta\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-N\>\<times\>\<bbb-N\>>.
        Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>> then
        <math|\<exists\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>T<rsub|k><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.159.202>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|k,i>>, hence
        <math|\<exists\>l\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>I<rsub|k,l>>. As <math|<around*|(|k,l|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<times\>\<bbb-N\>>
        there exist a <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
        <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>=<around*|(|k,l|)>> so that
        <math|x\<in\>I<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>.
        To summarize we have proved that

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.160.202><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><text|
          so that ><around*|{|I<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|]>
        </equation>

        Hence <math|><math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
        so that

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|]>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.160.202>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|I<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
          <reference|extended denumerable sum of denumerable
          sums>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|I<rsub|i,j>|)>|)><rsub|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.159.202>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|extended infinite sum
          properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|extended infinite sum
          properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|i>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><frac|1|2<rsup|i>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|extended lim inf lim sup in the set of real numbers
          (1)>]>\<wedge\><text|[examle: <reference|series power series in
          R>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><frac|<frac|1|2>|1-<frac|1|2>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>+\<varepsilon\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        Hence we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+> that
          \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>+\<varepsilon\>
        </equation*>

        which, as <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> is choosen
        arbitrary, proves by [theorem: <reference|extended
        x\<less\>=x+e=\<gtr\>x\<less\>=e>] that

        <\equation*>
          \ \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>.
        </equation*>
      </description>

      \;

      To summarize we have in all cases that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.161.202>\<forall\><around*|{|T<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
        we have >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Taking in account the definition of a outer measure and [eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.157.202>, <reference|eq 20.158.202> and <reference|eq
      20.161.202>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text| is a outer measure on >X
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then for
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<text|
      if >i=1>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> we
      have <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> so that
      <math|A\<in\>S<around*|[|A|]>\<Rightarrow\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
      and

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended series and index
        translation>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=2><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=2><rsup|\<infty\>>0=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>S<around*|[|A|]>|}>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>S<around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.177.204>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation>

      For the opposite inequality, let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>>
      then <math|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> so
      that <math|<rigid|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>=A\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>.
      By (2.a) we have <math|<around*|{|A<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>S>
      and further

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<around*|(|2.c|)>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|(2.b)>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Hence <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is a lower bound of
      <math|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>S<around*|[|A|]>|}>,therefore
      \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.177.204>] gives finally\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  As is mentioned in [note: <reference|note 20.198.210>] if we want that the
  above theorem extend a proto-measure to a outer measure we need extra
  conditions on the proto-ring and the proto-measure. This motivates the
  definition of a semi-ring and a pre-measure below.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure semi-ring><index|semi-ring><dueto|Semi-Ring>Let <math|X>
    be a set then <math|S\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is a
    <with|font-series|bold|semi-ring> on <math|X> if

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> we have
      <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> [in other words <math|\<cal-S\>> is
      <math|<big|cap>>-stable]

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> there exist a finite pairwise
      disjoint family <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that

      <\equation*>
        A\\B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As by (2) <math|\<cal-S\>> is <math|\<cap\>>-stable it follows from
      [lemma: <reference|measure union intersection stable>] that
      <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have that
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
    </note>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> it follows from the definition
      of a proto-ring [see definition: <reference|measure proto-ring>] that
      every semi-ring is a proto-ring.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure pre-measure><index|pre-measure><dueto|Pre-Measure>Let
    <math|X> be a set, <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> then a set
    function <math|<rigid|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>>
    is a pre-measure if

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item>For every pairwise disjoint family
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      such that <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
      we have that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      In other words <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      is countable addive on <math|\<cal-A\>>]
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0> it follows from the
      definition of a proto-measure [see definition: <reference|measure
      proto-measure>] that that a pre-measure on a semi-ring is a
      proto-measure on a proto-ring.
    </note>
  </definition>

  The above definitions do not exactly cover the extra conditions <math|1.a>,
  <math|2.b> and <math|2.c> in [theorem: <reference|measure outer measure
  construction>]. The next theorems and lemmas will ensure that these
  conditions will be satisfied by semi-rings and pre-measures.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.204.217>Let <math|X> be a set, <math|\<cal-S\>> a
    semi-ring on <math|X>, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
    and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then there exist a pairwise disjoint family
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
    <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We use induction to prove this. Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and define

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|A>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|If
      <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      then there exist a pairwise disjoint
      ><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      such that >A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    then we have:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>S<rsub|A>>>If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      then <math|A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>=A\\A<rsub|1>>.
      As <math|\<cal-S\>> is a semi-ring it follows that there exist a
      pairwise disjoint family <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that

      <\equation*>
        A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>=A\\A<rsub|1>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|A>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|A>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      then as <math|n\<in\>S> there exist a
      <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      pairwise disjoint such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.188.217>A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Further we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>>|<cell|=>|<cell|A\\<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class set difference and union ,
        intersection>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>\\A<rsub|n+1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.188.217>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>\\A<rsub|n+1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family properties (3)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|B<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|n+1>|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.189.217>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      If <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
      then we have <math|<around*|(|B<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|n+1>|)><big|cap><around*|(|B<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|n+1>|)>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.190.217><around*|{|B<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|n+1>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> then as <math|\<cal-S\>>
      is a semi-ring and <math|B<rsub|i>,A<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> there
      exist a pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>.,n<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      such that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.191.217.1>B<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|n+1>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>C<rsub|i,j>
      </equation>

      Hence using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.110.214>]\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<label|eq
        20.191.217><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|B<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|n+1>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>C<rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|1>,\<sigma\><rsub|2>><eq-number>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      and\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.192.217><around*|{|C<rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|1>,\<sigma\><rsub|2>>|}><rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      As <math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>
      is finite [see theorems: <reference|product of finite sets>,
      <reference|finite union of finite sets is finite>] there exist a
      bijection such that <math|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>\<rightarrow\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>.
      Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
      then, as <math|\<beta\>> is injective, we have
      <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>. For
      <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>,
      <math|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>=\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then
        we must have that <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>
        so that

        <\equation*>
          C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>=C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>=\<varnothing\>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then
        we have\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>|}>>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,k>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>|}>>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>,k>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varnothing\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </description>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.193.217><around*|{|C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Further

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.189.217>]>>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|B<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.191.217>]>>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<sigma\>\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>C<rsub|\<sigma\><rsub|1>,\<sigma\><rsub|2>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union of family set and
        surjections>]>>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure pre-measure is additive>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    pre-measure then <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>S>
    pairwise disjoint, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
    we have

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    In other words a pre-measure is additive.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As for a pre-measure we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> is
    countable additive it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure countable
    additivity implies additivity>] that <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is additive. In other words <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>S>
    pairwise disjoint, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure additive function monotone>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a additive
    set function [see definition: <reference|measure additive set function>]
    then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <with|font-series|bold|pairwise disjoint> such
      that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A>
      we have

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we
      have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have by [lemma: <reference|lemma
      20.204.217>] that there exist a pairwise disjoint
      <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.195.217>A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>!,\<ldots\>,m>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
        by >D<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|i-n><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-S\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
      then we can always assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange
      <math|i> and <math|j>]. For <math|i> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then for <math|j> we
        have either:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then
          <math|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j><below|=|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
          is pairwise disjoint>>\<varnothing\>>

          <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>Then\ 

          <\equation*>
            D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j-n><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
            <reference|eq 20.195.217>]>>A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|A\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<varnothing\>
          </equation*>
        </description>

        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>Then as
        <math|i\<less\>j> we have <math|j\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=B<rsub|i-n><big|cap>B<rsub|j-n>\<equallim\><rsub|i-n\<neq\>j-n>\<varnothing\>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.196.217><around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Further as <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A>
      we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class disjoint unions>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.195.217>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>D<rsub|i+n>|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union of family set and
        surjections>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>D<rsub|i>|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family properties (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>D<rsub|i><eq-number><label|eq
        20.197.217>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      So as <math|><math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>D<rsub|i>=A\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
      we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.197.217>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
        is additive>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+m>\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|n+m>\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|n+m>\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that by [theorem: <reference|extended 0\<less\>=y and z=x+y then
      x\<less\>=z>] we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B>. Take
      then <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      by <math|A<rsub|i>=A> then we have <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>=A\<subseteq\>B>,
      hence by (1) it follows that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|measure pre-measure monotonity>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    pre-measure on <math|\<cal-S\>> [see definition: <reference|measure
    pre-measure>] then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> pairwise disjoint such that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A>
      we have

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we
      have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    As a pre-measure is additive [see theorem: <reference|measure pre-measure
    is additive>] this follows from the previous theorem [theorem:
    <reference|measure additive function monotone>].
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure pre-measure is countable sub-additive>Let <math|X> be a
    set, <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    pre-measure then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      with ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we
      have >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    In other words <math|\<mu\>> is countable sub-addtive.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
    be a sequence such that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| by
      >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1><text| if
      >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}>>A<rsub|j><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.161.201>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.165.204><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i><text|
      and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Note that the sets <math|B<rsub|i>> are not elements of <math|\<cal-S\>>,
    however we will show that each <math|B<rsub|i>> is a finite pairwise
    disjoint union of sets in <math|\<cal-S\>> so that we can write
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> as countable pairwise
    disjoint union of elements of <math|\<cal-S\>>, allowing use to apply
    countable additivity. Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i=1>>Define <math|n<rsub|1>=1> and
      <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|1,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|1>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      by <math|C<rsub|1,1>=A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then
      <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|1,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|1>|}>>\<subseteq\>S>
      is pairwise disjoint and <math|B<rsub|1>=A<rsub|1>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|1>|}>>C<rsub|1,j>>

      <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>By \ [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.204.217>] there exist a pairwise disjoint family
      <math|<rigid|<around*|{|C<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>>
      such that <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>\\<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}>>A<rsub|j>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>C<rsub|i,j>>.
    </description>

    Hence we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.203.218>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >\<exists\><around*|{|C<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      pairwise disjoint such that >B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>C<rsub|i,j>
    </equation>

    By [corollary: <reference|union of {i}x{1,..,ni}>] it follows that
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>
    is denumerable, hence there exist a bijection

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    Using the above and [eq: <reference|eq 20.203.218>] allows us to use
    [corollary: <reference|corollary 20.111.218>] giving

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.204.218><around*|{|C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.165.204>]>><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>
    </equation>

    So we have, as <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>,
    that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.204.218>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
      is a pre-measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended denumerable sum of finite
      sums>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n<rsub|i>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i,j>|)>|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.205.218>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>C<rsub|i,j><below|=|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.203.218>]>>B<rsub|i><rsub|>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> it
    follows from [corollary: <reference|measure pre-measure monotonity>(1)]
    and [eq: <reference|eq 20.204.218>] that
    <math|><math|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n<rsub|i>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i,j>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>,
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.205.218>] and [theorem:
    <reference|extended infinite sum properties>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>A<rsub|j>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We can now combine the above theorems to prove a version of [theorem:
  <reference|measure outer measure construction>] for semi-rings and
  pre-measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure outer measure construction from pre-measure>Let <math|X>
    be a set, <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a
    semi-ring and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a pre-measure on <math|\<cal-S\>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)><text|
      if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text|
      if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=\<varnothing\>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=<around*|{|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>\|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    is a outer measure and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text| is a extension of >\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure [see definition: <reference|measure
    pre-measure>] we have that \ <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>
    hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><text| is a proto-measure on >\<cal-S\>
    </equation*>

    Using [theorems: <reference|measure pre-measure monotonity>,
    <reference|measure pre-measure is countable sub-additive>] it follows
    that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\><text|
      with >A\<subseteq\>B<text| we have >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>S<text|
      such that ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><text|
      we have >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    and as <math|\<cal-S\>> is a semi-ring we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have >A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-S\>
    </equation*>

    So all conditions for [theorem: <reference|measure outer measure
    construction>] are satisfies so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text| is a outer measure on >S<text| and
      >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  In the above proof we use the fact that a pre-measure is countable
  sub-additive [see theorem <reference|measure pre-measure is countable
  sub-additive>] but never the countable additivity that is part of the
  definition of a pre-measure. So why do we define a pre-measure to be
  countable additive and not just countable sub-additive? The answer lies in
  proving the existence of a <with|font-series|bold|unique>
  <with|font-series|bold|extension> of a pre-measure on a semi-ring
  <math|\<cal-S\>> to a measure on <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
  To extend the pre-measure <math|\<mu\>> on <math|\<cal-S\>> we first use
  the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measure outer measure
  construction>] to create a outer-measure <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>> that
  extend <math|\<mu\>>, next we use the Carathéodory theorem [theorem:
  <reference|measure Caratheodory>] to find a measure on
  <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>. However to make sure
  that the constructed measure is also a measure on
  <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> we still have to prove that
  <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
  and this is where we need additivity of <math|\<mu\>>. The countable
  additivity of a pre-measure ensures additional the additivity and countable
  sub-additivity of the pre-measure. Another way to look at this is the
  following, if <math|\<mu\>> on <math|S> is extended to a countable additive
  measure on <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then we must have, as
  <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>, that
  <math|\<mu\>> is also countable additive.\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure extending pre-measure to a measure>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    pre-measure on <math|\<cal-S\>> and define

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
      \ by \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text|
      if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=<around*|{|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>\|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
      we have >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text| is a outer measure>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]><text| is a
      >\<sigma\><text|-algebra on >X
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\><text| [in
      other words >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><text| extends >\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>:\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and by [theorem: <reference|measure on sub sigma algebra>]

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>,<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      and ><around*|\<langle\>|X,\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<comma\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      are measure spaces>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
    </equation*>

    If additional there exist a <math|<around*|{|G<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|G<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>G<rsub|i+1>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    and <math|X=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>G<rsub|i>> then if
    <math|\<nu\>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a another measure on <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> such that
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
    </equation*>

    In other words the extension of <math|\<mu\>> to a measure
    <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>>
    on <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is unique.

    <\note>
      We need additivity on <math|\<mu\>> to prove that
      <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
      which is essential for the last part of the theorem.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure outer measure construction from
    pre-measure>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.190.210>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)><text|
      if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text|
      if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=\<varnothing\>>>>>>
    </equation>

    where\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.191.210>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=<around*|{|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>\|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation>

    is a outer measure and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.192.210>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)><text| hence
      ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>> is a outer measure we can use the
    Carathéodory [theorem: <reference|measure Caratheodory>] so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
      we have >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.193.210><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>:\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on the >\<sigma\><text|-algebra
      >\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
    </equation>

    so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.194.210><around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>,<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      a measure space>
    </equation>

    Next we have to prove that <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>.
    Let <math|R,T\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then as <math|\<cal-S\>> is a semi-ring
    there exist a <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|S<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
    pairwise disjoint such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.195.210>T\\R=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>S<rsub|i><text|
      and >R<big|cap>T\<in\>\<cal-S\>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      by >D<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>|<row|<cell|R<big|cap>T<text| if
      >i=m+1>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-S\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m+1|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
    then we may always assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange
    <math|i> and <math|j>], so that we have the following possibilities for
    <math|j>:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|j=m+1>>Then, as <math|i\<less\>j>,
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> so that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>>|<cell|=>|<cell|S<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>S<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.195.210>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|T\\R|)><big|cap><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|<around*|(|T\\R|)><big|cap>R>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varnothing\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      henve we have <math|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>Then, as
      <math|i\<less\>j>, <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> so that
      <math|D<rsub|i><big|cap>D<rsub|j>=S<rsub|i><big|cap>S<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation*>

    Further\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|T\\R|)><big|cup><around*|(|T<big|cap>R|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>S<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|T<big|cap>R|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>D<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>D<rsub|m+1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m+1|}>>D<rsub|i>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.196.210><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m+1|}>>D<rsub|i>=T\<in\>\<cal-S\>
    </equation>

    As by [theorem: <reference|measure pre-measure is additive>]
    <math|\<mu\>> is additive on <math|S> we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|T|)>>|<cell|<below|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.196.210>]>>|>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m+1|}>>D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
      is additive>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m+1>\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|m+1>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.197.210>\<mu\><around*|(|T|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Further as a outer measure is sub-additive [see definition:
    <reference|measure outer measure> and theorem: <reference|measure
    countable sub-additivity implies sub-additivity>] we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T\\R|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.195.210>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>S<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|sub-additivity
      of \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|S<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.192.210>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.197.210>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|T|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    To summarize we have proved the following:\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.198.210>\<forall\>R,S\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have that
      >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|R<big|cap>T|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|T\\R|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|T|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and take <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>.
    For <math|B> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<star\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>

      <item*|<math|S<around*|[|B|]>\<neq\>0>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>>
      then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended infinite sum properties>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended infinite sum properties>] and [eq: <reference|eq
        20.198.210>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.199.210>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|><math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>>
      we have <math|B\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>
      so that

      <\equation*>
        B\\A\<subseteq\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)><text|
        and >B<big|cap>A\<subseteq\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>> is a outer measure hence monotone by
      definition [see definition: <reference|measure outer measure>(2)] it
      follows from the above that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.200.210>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>|)><text|
        and >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|)>
      </equation>

      Further using the sub-additivity of a outer measure [see definition:
      <reference|measure outer measure>(3)] we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)><text|
        and >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>
      </equation*>

      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.200.210>] gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)><text|
        and >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.199.210>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      So <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>>
      is a lower bound for <math|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>|}>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|B|]>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.190.210>]>>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have that <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>>
    which by [theorem: <reference|measure measurable set equivalences>] means
    that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>. As
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> was chosen arbitrary we conclude that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.201.210>\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>> is a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure
    generated sigma algebra>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.202.210>\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>
    </equation>

    From the above, [eq: <reference|eq 20.193.210>] and \ [theorem:
    <reference|measure on sub sigma algebra>] it follows that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|function restricted function properties>
      (3)]>><around*|(|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|(|\<cal-S\>|)>><text|
      is a measure on >\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation*>

    making\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|X,\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>,<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      a measure space>
    </equation*>

    Finally assume additional that there exist a
    <math|<around*|{|G<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|G<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>G<rsub|i+1>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|G<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Let <math|\<nu\>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    be a measure such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> we have
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>> then as
    <rigid|<math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.192.210>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>> it follows that
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
    Hence by [theorem: <reference|measure uniqueness of extension of a
    measure>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>=\<nu\>
    </equation*>

    proving uniqueness.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Contents and pre-measures>

  So given a pre-measure <math|\<mu\>> on a semi-ring <math|\<cal-S\>> we can
  extend <math|\<mu\>> to a measure on <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
  and from there to a measure on <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>. In
  the next section we show a way of proving that a additive set-function on a
  ring [see definition: <reference|measure ring>] can be extended [if certain
  conditions are satisfied] to a pre-measure. First we show that every ring
  \ is actually a semi-ring.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure ring is a semi-ring>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-R\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a ring [see
    definition: <reference|measure ring>] \ then <math|\<cal-R\>> is a
    semi-ring [see definition: <reference|measure semi-ring>].
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<cal-R\>> is a ring we have by [theorem: <reference|measure
    ring properties>] that

    <\equation*>
      \<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><text| and
      >\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><text| we have >A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-R\>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> then by the definition of a ring
    [see definition: <reference|measure ring>] <math|A\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. So
    if we define <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
    by <math|A<rsub|1>=A\\B> we have that
    <math|A\\B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>. Hence we
    have proved that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><text| \ >\<exists\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
      pairwise disjoint such that >A\\B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we define what a <with|font-series|bold|content> is.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure content>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a semi-ring then
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> is a
    <with|font-series|bold|content> on <math|\<cal-S\>> if\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> pairwise disjoint such that
      <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
      we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>.
    </enumerate>

    A content is <with|font-series|bold|finite > if
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

    <\note>
      If <math|\<cal-S\>> is a ring then (2) can be replaced by\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|,
        >n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      because [theorem: <reference|measure ring properties>] ensures that
      \ <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\lemma>
    <label|measure pre-measure is a content>Let <math|X> be a set
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a semi-ring then a
    pre-measure <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a content
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    From the definition of a pre-measure [see definition: <reference|measure
    pre-measure>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    Further <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,<math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    pairwise disjoint such that <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|measure pre-measure is additive>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    \;
  </proof>

  We have a simpler equivalent definition of a content on a ring.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure alternative definition for a content>Let <math|X> be a
    set, <math|\<cal-R\>> a ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-R\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a function
    such that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0> [in other words
    <math|\<mu\>> is a proto-measure] then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><text| is a content >\<Leftrightarrow\><text|
      >\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><text| with
      >A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> with
      <math|A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>> and define
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      by <math|A<rsub|1>=A> and <math|A<rsub|2>=B>, then
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>> is
      pairwise disjoint and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|2>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>We prove this by induction so let

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|>\|<text|if
        ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
        is a pairwise disjoint family of sets in >\<cal-R\> then
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|}>
      </equation*>

      then we have\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>If
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>>
        is a pairwise disjoint family of sets in <math|\<cal-R\>> then

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
        </equation*>

        proving that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

        <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>If
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>>
        is a pairwise disjoint family of sets in <math|\<cal-R\>> then we
        have <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|sqcup>A<rsub|n+1>>
        so that

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|sqcup>A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        \ proving that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
      </description>
    </description>
  </proof>

  A content has the following properties:\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure content properties>Let <math|X> be a set, <math|\<cal-R\>>
    a ring on <math|X> and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-R\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a content on <math|\<cal-R\>> then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we
      have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
      [<math|\<mu\>> is monotone].

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B>
      and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>.>

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      [sub-additivity]

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      pairwise disjoint such that <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>
      we have <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First as <math|\<cal-R\>> is a ring we have that
    <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> <math|B\\A\<in\>\<cal-R\>>, further

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|A<big|cup><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class disjoint unions>]>>A<big|cup>B> with
      <math|A<big|cap><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class disjoint unions>]>>\<varnothing\>> and <math|\<mu\>>
      is a content we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.206.211>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>
      </equation>

      Further <math|B\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|class
      disjoint unions>]>><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><big|cup><around*|(|B\\A|)>>
      with <math|<around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><big|cap><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class disjoint unions>]>>\<varnothing\>> so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.207.211>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>
      </equation>

      For <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>As
        <math|\<mu\>> is non negative we can sum [eq: <reference|eq
        20.206.211>] and [eq: <reference|eq 20.207.211>] giving

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>
        </equation*>

        and we can use [theorem: <reference|extended x+z=y+z=\<gtr\>x-y>] to
        get\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<infty\>>>Then

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.206.211>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<infty\>=\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        and

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
          20.207.211>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>+\<infty\>=\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>=\<infty\>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>=\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      So in all cases we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B> then
      <math|A<big|cup>B=B> so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.208.211>\<mu\>*<around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.206.211>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>
      </equation>

      then by [theorem: <reference|extended 0\<less\>=y and z=x+y then
      x\<less\>=z>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we have by [eq: <reference|eq
      20.208.211>] that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>>
      so as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> we can safely add
      <math|-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> to both sides giving\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|B|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      and define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1><text| if
        >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i-1|}>>A<rsub|j>>>>>><below|\<in\>|\<cal-R\><text|
        is a ring and [theorem: <reference|measure ring
        properties>]>>\<cal-R\>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.209.211><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><below|\<Rightarrow\>|<around*|(|2|)>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.161.201>] we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.210.211><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint and ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.210.211>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|measure content>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.209.211>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      \ 

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      be a pairwise disjoint sequence of sets in <math|\<cal-R\>> such that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. Let
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have
      <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.211.211><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|measure content>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<around*|(|2|)>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Hence <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>
      is a upper bound of <math|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|extended infinite sum>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    \;
  </proof>

  Similar to [theorems: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>,
  <reference|measure measure conditions>] we have the following neccesary and
  sufficient conditions for a content to be a pre-measure.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure pre-measure equivalences>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-R\>\<subseteq\>X> a ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-R\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a content
    then for the following statements:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> and
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>> we have
      <math|<below|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>> we have
      \ <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>.
    </enumerate>

    we have <math|<around*|(|1|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|2|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|3|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|4|)>>,
    Further if <math|\<mu\>> is finite then
    <math|<around*|(|1|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|2|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|3|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|4|)>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ First as <math|\<cal-R\>> is a ring we have by [theorem:
    <reference|measure ring properties>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.225.219>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
      ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\><text|
      and ><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>
    </equation>

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|(|1|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|2|)>>>Let<math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      be such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> and
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1><text| if
        >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>><below|\<in\>|\<cal-R\><text|
        is a ring>>\<cal-R\>
      </equation*>

      Then by the above definition we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.206.210>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Let <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>. We may always
      assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange <math|i> and
      <math|j>], then for <math|i> we have either

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|i=1>>Then <math|1\<less\>j> so that
        <math|B<rsub|1>=A<rsub|1>> and <math|B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>>.
        As <math|1\<less\>j\<Rightarrow\>1\<leqslant\>j-1> we have
        <math|A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j-1>> so that by [theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and intersection>]
        <math|B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|1>=A<rsub|j>\\B<rsub|1>>.
        Hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|1><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\\B<rsub|1>|)>=\<varnothing\>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>Then
        <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> so that
        <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1>\<wedge\>B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>>.
        As <math|i\<less\>j\<Rightarrow\>i\<leqslant\>j-1> we have
        <math|B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j-1>> so that
        \ by [theorem: <reference|class inclusion and union and
        intersection>] <math|B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j>\\B<rsub|i>>.
        Hence

        <\equation*>
          B<rsub|j><big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\\B<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>B<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      So it follows that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.207.210><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      we have <math|B<rsub|i><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.206.210>]>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>> from which it follows
      that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.208.210><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n>> then <math|n\<in\><around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>>
      a finite set so that <math|j=min<around*|(|<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>
      exists. For <math|j> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|j=1>>Then <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>=B<rsub|1>> so that
        <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>

        <item*|<math|1\<less\>j>>Then <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j>> and
        <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|j-1>> so that
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>=B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>
      </description>

      hence <math|A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.208.210>] we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.209.210>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >A<rsub|n>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>> then
      <math|\<exists\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n>> and
      by [eq: <reference|eq 20.209.210>] <math|\<exists\>m\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-N\>>
      such that <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|m>>, hence
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>.
      As also <math|B<rsub|n><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.206.210>]>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n><rsub|>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.210.210><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure hence countable additive we have that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|extended infinite sum>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure pre-measure is
        additive>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.209.210>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|<around*|(|2|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|1|)>>>As for a
      content <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0> we only have to
      prove countable additivity. Let <math|<rigid|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>>
      be a pairwise disjoint sequence of sets in <math|\<cal-R\>> such that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>. Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>
      </equation*>

      Then we have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.211.210>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>A<rsub|i+1>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i+1|}>>A<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i+1>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> then
      <math|\<exists\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>,
      hence <math|<rigid|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>>,
      as also <math|B<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>>
      it follows that <math|<rigid|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.212.210><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>
      </equation>

      Using (2) together with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.211.210>] it follows
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.213.210>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)><below|=|<around*|(|2|)>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>
      </equation>

      A <math|\<mu\>> is a content hence additive we have
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>>
      so that by the above\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|extended infinite sum>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Finally using [eq: <reference|eq 20.212.210>] it follows that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>
      so that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves \ countable additivity.

      <item*|<math|<around*|(|2|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|3|)>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i><below|\<in\>|\<cal-R\><text| is a
        ring>>\<cal-R\>
      </equation*>

      Then we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.220.212.1>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and
        intersection>]>>A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i+1>=B<rsub|i+1>
      </equation>

      and\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.220.212>A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family de Morgan>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<cal-R\>> is a ring and <math|A<rsub|1>,<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>
      we have that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>.<math|>
      Hence we can apply (2) \ \ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.221.212>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|2|)>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measure content properties> (2)]
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
      further we have <math|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure content properties>(3)] we
      have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.222.212>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
        >\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      and

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.223.212>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.220.212>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      So\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
        <reference|eq 20.221.212>, <reference|eq
        20.223.212>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq;
        <reference|eq 20.222.212>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim<rsub|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>|><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      we can safely subtract <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> from both sides
      from the above equation to get <math|-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>|\<nobracket\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      from which it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|<around*|(|3|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|4|)>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      be such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
      then by (3) we have

      <\equation*>
        0=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|<around*|(|4|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|3|)>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      be such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. Take
      <math|A=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>> and
      define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>\\A<below|\<in\>|\<cal-R\><text| is a
        ring>>\<cal-R\>
      </equation*>

      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
        >B<rsub|i+1>=A<rsub|i+1>\\A\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\\A=B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Further we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<varnothing\>=A\\A=<around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\A\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family properties (3)>]>><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Hence using (4) we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.225.212>0=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|A=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure content properties>(2)]
      that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
      hence we have by [theorem: <reference|measure content properties>(3)]
      that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.226.212>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation>

      Hence we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.225.212>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.226.212>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    Now for the last part we have to prove that if <math|\<mu\>> is finite
    then <math|<around*|(|4|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|2|)>> [because then
    from <math|<around*|(|1|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|2|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|3|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|4|)>>
    it follows that <math|<around*|(|1|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|2|)>\<Leftrightarrow\><around*|(|3|)>\<Leftrightarrow\>4>].\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|(|4|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|2|)>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
      is such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> and
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. Take
      <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> and define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=A\\A<rsub|i><below|\<in\>|\<cal-R\><text| is a
        ring>>\<cal-R\>
      </equation*>

      Then we have

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
        >B<rsub|i+1>=A\\A<rsub|i+1><below|\<subseteq\><rsub|>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and
        intersection>]>>A\\A<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i><text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><below|\<less\>|\<mu\><text| is
        finite>>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Further we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<varnothing\>=A\\A=A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family properties (3)>]>><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A\\A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      So using (4) we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.227.212><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A\\A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0
      </equation>

      Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then, as
      \ <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=A>
      and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><below|\<less\>|\<mu\><text| is
      finite>>\<infty\>>, it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure
      content properties>(3)] that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.227.212>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A\\A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|\<mu\>> is finite it follows from the above that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  The above equivalences helps us to determine that a content on a ring is a
  pre-measure. If we start with a ring based on a topological space then we
  can make use of the properties of the topological space to help us to prove
  that a content is a pre-measure. For this we introduce approximation by
  compact classes. First we recap the definition of a compact class [see
  definition: <reference|compact class>].

  <\definition>
    Let <math|X> be a set then <math|\<cal-C\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|compact class >if
    <math|\<forall\><around*|{|K<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-C\>>
    with <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>K<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>
    there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><space|1em>such that
    <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>K<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>.
  </definition>

  We have then the following theorem that can be used to determine if a
  content is a pre-measure.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure compact class approximation>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-R\>> a ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:X\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    <with|font-series|bold|finite >content. If there exist a compact class
    <math|\<cal-C\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-R\>> and
    <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> we have that
    <math|\<exists\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-C\>> and
    <math|\<exists\>R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|A\\R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    then <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure. In other words if we can
    'approximate' <math|\<cal-R\>> by a complex class <math|\<cal-C\>> then
    <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure.\ 
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
    be such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.228.212>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text| and
      ><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation>

    \ As <math|\<mu\>> is finite we have automatically\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.229.212>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    So if we prove that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>
    then it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure pre-measure
    equivalences>] that <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure. Let
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    then by the assumption there exist a <math|K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-C\>>
    and a <math|R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\>> such that
    <math|R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n>>>.
    Hence there exist a <math|<around*|{|K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-C\>>
    and <math|<around*|{|R<rsub|n,e>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.230.212>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n><text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|A\\R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n>>
    </equation>

    Then we have <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n><below|=|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.228.212>]>>\<varnothing\>> so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    By the definition of a compact class there exist a
    <math|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>=\<varnothing\>>.
    As <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.230.212>]>><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>>
    it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.231.212><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>K<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>> then
    <math|A<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.228.212>]>>A<rsub|n>> so that
    <math|A<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
    proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.232.212>A<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>A<rsub|n>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>A<rsub|n>>.
    Assume that <math|x\<nin\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|n>\\R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|)>>
    then <rigid|<math|\<forall\>m\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>>
    we have <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|m>\\R<rsub|m,\<varepsilon\>>>, as
    <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|m>>,
    it follows that <math|x\<in\>R<rsub|m,\<varepsilon\>>> [otherwise
    <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|m>\\R<rsub|m,\<varepsilon\>>>]. So
    <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>>
    contradicting [eq: <reference|eq 20.231.212>]. Hence the assumption is
    wrong and we must have that <math|<rigid|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|n>\\R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|)>>>
    which proves that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.233.212>A<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.232.212>]>><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|n>\\R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|)><below|\<in\>|\<cal-R\><text|
      is a ring>>\<cal-R\>
    </equation>

    Hence using the above we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure content properties>(2)]>\<wedge\><around*|[|eq:<text|<reference|eq
      20.233.212>>|]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|n>\\R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure content properties>(5)]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>\\R<rsub|n,\<varepsilon\>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.230.212>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|N><around*|(|<frac|1|2>|)><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|sum of powers lemma>]>>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><around*|(|<frac|1|2>|)>\<cdot\><frac|1-<around*|(|<frac|1|2>|)><rsup|n>|1-<frac|1|2>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><around*|(|1-<frac|1|2<rsup|n>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Let <math|n\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    then we have have <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>-0|\|><below|=|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.228.212>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    To summarize we have proved that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text| there exist a
      <math|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
      >\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      we have ><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>-0|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|<below|lim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>=0>.
    Using the fact that <math|\<mu\>> is finite and [theorem:
    <reference|extended lim inf lim sup in the set of real numbers (1)>] it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    So using [theorem: <reference|measure pre-measure equivalences>] it
    follows that <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure.
  </proof>

  The above theorem is especially usefull if we have a ring on a topological
  Hausdorff space because every set of compact sets is a compact class [see
  theorem: <reference|compact class and sets of compact sets>]. This is
  expressed in the following theorem.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure compact class approximation (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>> be a Hausdorff
    topological space,, <math|\<cal-R\>> a ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:X\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    <with|font-series|bold|finite >content. If
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-R\>> and
    <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> we have that
    <math|\<exists\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> compact and
    <math|\<exists\>R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|A\\R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    then <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure.\ 
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|\<cal-C\>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|A<text| is
    compact>|}>> is the set of compact subsets of <math|X> then by [theorem:
    <reference|compact class and sets of compact sets>] <math|\<cal-C\>> is a
    compact class. The proof follows then from [theorem: <reference|measure
    compact class approximation>].
  </proof>

  Proving that <math|\<cal-R\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is a ring
  is not always possible, especially proving that
  <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\>> we have that
  <math|A\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\>>. For example the difference of two half-open
  intervals in <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> is not always a
  half-open interval, hence <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> is not a
  ring, however it will be proved later that
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> is a semi-ring on <math|\<bbb-R\>>.
  Luckely it turns out that a semi-ring with a content on it can easely be
  extended to a ring with a content on it. First we show how we can extend a
  semi-ring to a smallest ring containing the semi-ring.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure generated ring><index|<math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>>Let
    <math|X> be a set, <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a
    semi-ring on <math|X> then <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is
    defined by

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>=<around*|{|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>S<text|
      is pairwise disjoint>|}>
    </equation*>

    So <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is the set of all finite
    pairwise disjoint unions of sets in <math|\<cal-S\>>.

    <\note>
      <label|eq 21.160.300>As for <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> we have by
      definition of a semi-ring that <math|\<exists\><around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> pairwise disjoint such that
      <math|A\\B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>>
      it follows that

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\><text| we have >A\\B\<in\>\<cal-S\>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  To prove that <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is a ring we have to
  prove that <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> and
  <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> that
  <math|<rigid|A<big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<wedge\>A\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>>.
  We start with proving that <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
  <math|A<big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> using the
  following two lemma's.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.225.220>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a semi-ring on
    <math|X>, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
    <math|B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      A<big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> there exist a pairwise
    disjoint <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>><text|
      by >C<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|B<rsub|i>\\A<text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|A<text| if >i=n+1>>>>>
    </equation*>

    Then if <math|x\<in\>A<big|cup>B> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|n+1>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>>

      <item*|<math|x\<nin\>A>>Then we must have that <math|x\<in\>B> so that
      there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that

      <\equation*>
        x\<in\>B<rsub|i>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|x\<in\>\<nin\>A>x\<in\>B<rsub|i>\\A=C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><rsub|><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    proving that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.248.220>A<big|cup>B\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>>
    then there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> such that
    <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i>>. For <math|i> we have either\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then
      <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i>\\A\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>=B\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B>

      <item*|<math|i=n+1>>Then <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|n+1>=A\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B>
    </description>

    hence <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.248.220>] results in\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.249.220>A<big|cup>B=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
    then for <math|i> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then for <math|j> we
      have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then
        <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=<around*|(|B<rsub|i>\\A|)><big|cap><around*|(|B<rsub|j>\\A|)>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>

        <item*|<math|j=n+1>>Then <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=<around*|(|B<rsub|i>\\A|)><big|cap>A=\<varnothing\>>
      </description>

      <item*|<math|i=n+1>>Then as <math|i\<neq\>j> we have that
      <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> so that
      <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=A<big|cap><around*|(|B<rsub|j>\\A|)>=\<varnothing\>>
    </description>

    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.250.220><around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint so that >A<big|cup>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>As
      <math|A,B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and <math|S> is a semi-ring there
      exist a pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      such that <math|C<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i>\\A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>D<rsub|i,j>.>

      <item*|<math|i=n+1>>Take <math|n<rsub|1>=1> and
      <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|1,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      by <math|D<rsub|1,1>=A> so that <math|C<rsub|n+1>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>D<rsub|1,j>>
    </description>

    Summarized we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.251.220>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}><text|
      >\<exists\><around*|{|D<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      with >C<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>D<rsub|i,j>
    </equation>

    As <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>>
    is finite by [theorems: <reference|product of finite sets>,
    <reference|finite union of finite sets is finite>] there exists a
    bijection\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.252.220>\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>\<rightarrow\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation>

    Using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.250.220>, <reference|eq 20.251.220> and
    <reference|eq 20.252.220>] allows us to apply [theorem:
    <reference|corollary 20.111.218>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|D<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>2>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>C<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>D<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>
    </equation*>

    Hence using [eq: <reference|eq 20.250.220>] it follows that
    <math|A<big|cup>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>D<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      A<big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|\<cal-R\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We use the above lemma in a proof by induction to prove that that
  <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is <math|\<cup\>>-stable.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.226.220>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a semi-ring on
    <math|X> and <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then
    <math|A<big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsub|B>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      pairwise disjoint then ><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<b-S\><rsub|B>>>If
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      is pairwise disjoint then, as <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, we have
      by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.225.220>] that
      <math|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B=A<rsub|1><big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
      which proves that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|B>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|B>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|B>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      be a pairwise disjoint family of sets in <math|\<cal-S\>>. As
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      is pairwise disjoint and <math|n\<in\>S<rsub|B>> we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Hence, as <math|A<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, we have by the previous
      lemma [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.225.220>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        A<rsub|n+1><big|cup><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
      </equation*>

      which as <math|A<rsub|n+1><big|cup><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B|)>=<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B>
      proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B
      </equation*>

      so that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
    </description>

    Mathematical induction proves then that
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|B>=\<bbb-N\>>. Finally if
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then there exist a
    <math|<rigid|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>>
    such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>,
    hence, as <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>=\<cal-S\><rsub|B>>, it follows that
    <math|<rigid|A<big|cup>B=<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>>.
  </proof>

  Next we prove that <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
  that <math|A\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|S|]>>, we do this in 2 sets.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.228.220>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a semi-ring on
    <math|X>, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> and
    <math|B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then <math|A\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We use mathematical induction in the proof. Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|B>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      pairwise disjoint then ><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>S<rsub|B>>>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      pairwise disjoint then <math|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B=A<rsub|1>\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|S|]>>
      [using the note in the definition of
      <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> see [definition:
      <reference|measure generated ring>]], hence <math|1\<in\>S<rsub|B>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>S<rsub|B>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S<rsub|B>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      pairwise disjoint. Then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|sqcup>A<rsub|n+1>|)>\\B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|*<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>\\B|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.253.220>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|n\<in\>S<rsub|B>> we have that
      <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
      Further using the fact that <math|A<rsub|n+1>,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> it
      follows from the note in \ [definition: <reference|measure generated
      ring>] that <math|A<rsub|n+1>\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
      Hence using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.226.220>] that
      <math|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B|)><big|cup><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>\\B|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
      So by [eq: <reference|eq 20.253.220>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|B>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|B>>.
    </description>

    By mathematical induction it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|B>=\<bbb-N\>
    </equation*>

    Finally let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then there
    exist a pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
    such that <math|<rigid|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>>.
    As <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>=S<rsub|B>> we have that
    <math|A\\B=<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.229.220>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a semi-ring on
    <math|X> then <math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
    we have that <math|A\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|A>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint then >A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>S<rsub|A>>>Let <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then by the previous lemma [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.228.220>] we have that
      <math|<rigid|A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>B<rsub|i>=A\\B<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>>
      which proves that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|A>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>S<rsub|A>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S<rsub|A>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      be pairwise disjoint. Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>B<rsub|i>>|<cell|=>|<cell|A\\<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|sqcup>B<rsub|n+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class set difference and union ,
        intersection>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>\\B<rsub|n+1>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|n\<in\>S<rsub|A>> we have that
      <math|><math|A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
      so that by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.228.220>]
      <math|<rigid|<around*|(|A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>\\B<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>>.
      Hence <math|A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
      which proves that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|A>>.
    </description>

    Mathematical induction proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsub|A>=\<bbb-N\>
    </equation*>

    Finally if <math|B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then by
    definition of <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> there exist a
    pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>.
    As <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>=\<cal-S\><rsub|A>>
    <math|A\\B=A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove that <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is a
  ring more specific\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure semi-ring to ring>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> a semi-ring on
    <math|X> then <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is a ring on
    <math|X> such that:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|S|]>>

      <item>If <math|\<cal-T\>> is another ring on <math|X> with
      <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\>> then
      <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
    </enumerate>

    In other words the set of all disjoint finite unions of sets in
    <math|\<cal-S\>> is the smallest ring covering <math|\<cal-S\>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and define
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
    by <math|A<rsub|1>=A> then <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>>
    is pairwise disjoint and <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>,
    hence <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.259.220>\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<cal-S\>> is a semi-ring we have
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> so by the above we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.260.220>\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then by [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.226.220>] and [lemma:<reference|lemma 20.229.220>] we
    have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.261.220>A<big|cup>B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]><text|
      and >A\\B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation>

    So by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.259.220>, <reference|eq 20.260.220> and
    <reference|eq 20.261.220>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]><text| is a ring on >X
    </equation*>

    It remains to prove that <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is the
    smallest ring containing <math|\<cal-S\>>. So let <math|\<cal-T\>> be
    another ring such that <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\>>. If
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then then there exist a
    pairwise disjoint finite family <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\>>
    such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>.>
    As <math|\<cal-T\>> is a ring it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measure ring properties>] that
    <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-T\>>
    which proves that

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we want to extend a content on a semi-ring <math|\<cal-S\>> to a
  content on the ring <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure content extension>Let <math|X> be a set, <math|\<cal-S\>>
    a semi-ring on <math|X> and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-S\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a content on <math|\<cal-S\>> then

    <\enumerate>
      <item>There exist a content <math|\<nu\>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      such that <math|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\>> [in other words
      <math|\<nu\>> is a extension of <math|\<mu\>>]

      <item>If <math|\<omega\>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      is another content such that <math|\<omega\><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\>>
      then <math|\<omega\>=\<nu\>> [in other words the extension is unique].

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>> is finite then <math|\<nu\>> is finite.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>First we proof existence. Let
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then there exist a
      pairwise disjoint finite family <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|A=\<sqcup\><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>.
      As <math|\<nu\>> will be a content on
      <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> we must have that
      <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<Sigma\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>.
      So it make sense to define\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        by >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <text|where ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|,>
        n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| is pairwise disjoint and
        >A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Of course this only works if <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>> is
      independent of the chosen family <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>.
      So assume that for <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> we
      have two pairwise disjoint families
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Then we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A=A<rsub|i>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family distributivity>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|\<nobracket\>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended sum of sums>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family distributivity>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|\<nu\>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      is well defined. Next we prove that <math|\<nu\>> is a extension of
      <math|\<cal-S\>>. Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> then if we define
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
      by <math|A<rsub|1>=A> then <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>>
      is clearly disjoint and <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>=A>
      so that <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>
      proving that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.257.216>\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\>
      </equation>

      Next we use [theorem: <reference|measure alternative definition for a
      content>] to prove that <math|\<nu\>> is a content on
      <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>. As
      <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> we have by [eq: <reference|eq
      20.257.216>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.258.216>\<nu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
      </equation>

      Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> such that
      <math|A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>> then there exist pairwise disjoint
      families <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>,
      <math|B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>. Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
        by >C<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|i-n><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
      then we may assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange <math|i>
      and <math|j>], for <math|i> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then for <math|j> we
        have either:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then
          <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>\<equallim\><rsub|i\<neq\>j>\<varnothing\>>.

          <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>Then
          <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i-n><big|cap>B<rsub|j-n>\<subseteq\>A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>>.
        </description>

        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>Then
        <math|j\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>
        <rigid|<math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i-n><big|cap>B<rsub|j-n>\<subseteq\>A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>>>
      </description>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.259.216><around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>A<big|cup>B> then either
      <math|x\<in\>A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      with >x\<in\>A<rsub|i>=C<rsub|i>> or
      <math|x\<in\>B=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>
      with <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>=C<rsub|i+n>>, hence
      <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>C<rsub|i>>,
      proving that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.260.216>A<big|sqcup>B\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>C<rsub|i>.
      </equation>

      For <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>> we have either
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then
      <math|C<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B> or
      <math|<rigid|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>> then
      <math|C<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i-n>\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B>, hence in all cases
      <math|>we have <math|C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B>. This proves
      that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B>
      which combines with [eq: <reference|eq 20.260.216>] gives\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.261.216>A<big|sqcup>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>C<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Hence we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|def>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+m>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|sum associativity simple>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|n+m>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|n+m>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i-n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|sum change start index>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|B|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.262.216>\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]><text|
        with >A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\><text| we have
        >\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation>

      Using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.258.216>, <reference|eq 20.262.216>] we
      can apply ]theorem: <reference|measure alternative definition for a
      content>] to prove that

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        is a content on >\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|\<omega\>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      be another content on <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|S|]>> such that
      <math|\<omega\><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\>>. Let
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then there exist a
      pairwise disjoint family <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<omega\><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\>>|<cell|\<omega\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<omega\><text|
        is a content>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<omega\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<omega\><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|def
        of >\<nu\>>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<omega\>=\<nu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then there
      exist a finite pairwise disjoint family
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>.
      Then <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      [because <math|\<mu\>> is assumed to be finite].
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We can now rephrase [theorem: <reference|measure compact class
  approximation>] to work with semi-rings instead of rings.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure compact class approximation (2)>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> and
    <math|\<mu\>:X\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    <with|font-series|bold|finite >content. If there exist a compact class
    <math|\<cal-C\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> such that

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|K<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-C\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>K<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-C\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> we have that
      <math|\<exists\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-C\>> and
      <math|\<exists\>A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text|
        and >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><text| is a pre-measure>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure content extension>] there exist a
    <with|font-series|bold|finite> content
    <math|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|S|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    on the ring <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.273.220><around*|(|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-S\>>=\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> then by definition
    there exist a pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>.
    Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then by the hypothesis
    there exists <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
    and <math|<around*|{|K<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-C\>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.263.216>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n>>
    </equation>

    Define

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.264.216>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>K<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-C\><text|
      [by (1)]>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.265.216>A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]><text|
      [because >\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]><text|
      and >\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]><text| is a ring hence
      >\<cup\><text|-stable]>
    </equation>

    then we have

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.263.216>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>K<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>=K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>K<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.263.216>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>=A
    </equation*>

    so that we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.266.216>A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]><text|,
      >K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-C\><text| and
      >A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A
    </equation>

    Next\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A\\A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|j,\<varepsilon\>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family properties (3)>]>>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|j,\<varepsilon\>>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family de Morgan>]>>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|j,\<varepsilon\>>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Further if <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> with
    <math|i\<neq\>j> then <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j,\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>.
    To summarize we have that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.267.216>A\\A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)><text|
      and ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    As <math|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is a <with|font-series|bold|finite>
    content on the ring <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>,
    <math|A\\A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
    and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>
    [because <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>> is a ring and
    <math|A,A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,A<rsub|i>,A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>]
    we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A\\A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|><around*|[|theorem:<reference|measure
      content properties><around*|(|2|)>|]>>>|<cell|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure content properties>(3)]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      extends >\<mu\>\<wedge\>A<rsub|i>,A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i,\<varepsilon\>>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.263.216>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<frac|1|2>|)><rsup|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|sum of powers lemma>]>>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><around*|(|<frac|1|2>|)>\<cdot\><frac|1-<around*|(|<frac|1|2>|)><rsup|n>|1-<frac|1|2>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><around*|(|1-<around*|(|<frac|1|2>|)><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.268.216><wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A\\A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.266.216>] and [eq: <reference|eq 20.268.216>]
    allows us to apply [theorem: <reference|measure compact class
    approximation>] proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| is a pre-measure on
      >\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<mu\>> is a content\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.274.220>\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>
    be pairwise disjoint such that <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
    then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.273.220>]>>>|<cell|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is a pre-measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.273.220>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.274.220>] proves that
    <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure.
  </proof>

  If we deal with topological spaces then we have a even simpler test to see
  if the extension of a content on a semi-ring <math|\<cal-S\>> to the ring
  <math|\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>>.

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure compact class approximation (3)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>> be a Hausdorff
    topological space, <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring on <math|X> and
    <math|<rigid|\<mu\>:X\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>> a
    <with|font-series|bold|finite >content. If
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
    <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> there exists a
    compact <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> and a
    <rigid|<math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    then

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>:\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a pre-measure> on \<cal-S\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First using [theorem: <reference|compact class and sets of compact sets>]
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.269.216>\<cal-C\>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|A<text|
      is compact>|}><text| is a compact class>
    </equation>

    Further:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|K<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-C\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have by [theorem: <reference|compact finite
      union of compact sets>] that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>K<rsub|i>>
      is compact hence <math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>K<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-C\>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> we have by the
      hypothesis that <math|\<exists\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-C\>>
      and <math|\<exists\>A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\>> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text|
        and >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    So using the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measure compact class
    approximation (2)>] we have that <math|\<mu\>> is a pre-measure.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Using the Carathéodory theorem on <math|\<bbb-R\>>>

  We use now the theory developped in the previous sections to construct a
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra and a measure on <math|\<bbb-R\>>. The procedure
  that we will follow is the following:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>Prove that <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=<around*|{|<around*|[|a,b|[>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\><around*|[|a,b|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    [see theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on R>] is semi-ring.

    <item>Prove that <math|len:\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    defined by <math|len<around*|(|I|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|b-a<text|
    if <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>>>|<row|<cell|0<text|
    if >I=\<varnothing\>>>>>>> [the function that calculates the lenght of a
    half-open interval] is a finite content on the semi-ring
    <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

    <item>Use [theorem: <reference|measure extending pre-measure to a
    measure>] a derivate of the Carathéodore Theorem to extend the
    pre-measure to a measure [the Lebesgue measure] \ on a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra [the Lebesgue algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>>] that
    contains <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and whose restriction on <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> is also
    a measure that we also call the Lebesgue measure.\ 
  </enumerate>

  <\definition>
    The set <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>
    is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=<around*|{|<around*|[|a,b|[>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure semi-ring on the set of real numbers
    (1)><math|S<rsub|<around*|[||[>>=\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    and <math|<around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}><big|cap>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=\<varnothing\>>
    in other words\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
    <reference|measure bounded intervals>]>><around*|{|<around*|[|a,b|[>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\><around*|[|a,b|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|I\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then there exists
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>>. For
    <math|a,b> we have either

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|a\<less\>b>>Then <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
      so that <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>

      <item*|<math|b\<leqslant\>a>>Then <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<varnothing\>>
      so that <math|I=\<varnothing\>\<in\><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    </description>

    hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.275.220>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
    </equation>

    If <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then either we have:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>Then
      <math|\<exists\>a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> [with
      <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>] such that
      <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>> so that <math|I\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

      <item*|<math|I\<in\><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>>Then
      <math|I=\<varnothing\>=<around*|[|0,0|[>> so that
      <math|I\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    </description>

    which proves that <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.275.220>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|<around*|[||[>>=\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cap><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then <math|I=\<varnothing\>> and there exists a <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    such that <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> which is a
    contradiction. Hence

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cap><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure semi-ring on the set of real
    numbers><em|<math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> is semi-ring>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ First note that by [theorem: <reference|measure semi-ring on the set of
    real numbers (1)>] that

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=<around*|{|<around*|[|a,b|[>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>=\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
    </equation*>

    Further we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>

      <item>If <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>>, <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<varnothing\>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
        <math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\><big|cap>\<varnothing\>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
        <math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap>\<varnothing\>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<varnothing\>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>Then
        <math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>Then
        for <math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>> we have either

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
          <math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>

          <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then
          for <math|a> we have either:

          <\description>
            <item*|<math|a\<leqslant\>c>>For <math|b> we have either:\ 

            <\description>
              <item*|<math|b\<leqslant\>d>>Let
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,b|[>> then
              <math|a\<leqslant\>c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<leqslant\>d> so
              that <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\<wedge\>y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
              hence <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>>,
              proving that\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|c,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>
              </equation*>

              Let <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>>
              then <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\<wedge\>y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
              so that

              <\equation*>
                a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d\<Rightarrow\>c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b
              </equation*>

              proving that <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,b|[>> so that
              <math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|c,b|[>>.
              Hence

              <\equation*>
                \<varnothing\>\<neq\><around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|c,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
              </equation*>

              <item*|<math|d\<less\>b>>Let <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
              then <math|a\<leqslant\>c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d\<less\>b> so
              that <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>> hence
              <rigid|<math|[c,d[\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>> so that

              <\equation*>
                [a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
              </equation*>
            </description>

            <item*|<math|c\<less\>a>>For <math|b> we have either:\ 

            <\description>
              <item*|<math|b\<leqslant\>d>>Let
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>> then
              <math|c\<less\>a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<leqslant\>d> so that
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>> hence
              <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|c,d|[>> so that
              \ 

              <\equation*>
                [a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>S<rsub|<around*|[||[>>
              </equation*>

              <item*|<math|d\<less\>b>>Let <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,d|[>>
              then <math|c\<less\>a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d\<less\>b> so that
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>> hence
              <math|y\<in\>[a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>>, proving that\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,d|[>\<subseteq\>[a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>
              </equation*>

              Let <math|y\<in\>[a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>> then
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\<wedge\>y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
              so that\ 

              <\equation*>
                a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d\<Rightarrow\>a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d
              </equation*>

              proving that <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,d|[>> or
              <math|[a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,d|[>>.
              Hence\ 

              <\equation*>
                [a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|a,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
              </equation*>
            </description>
          </description>
        </description>
      </description>

      so in all cases we have that

      <\equation*>
        [a,b[<big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>>,<math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<varnothing\>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
        <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>\\\<varnothing\>=\<varnothing\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
        where <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
        is defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
        <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[>\\\<varnothing\>=<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
        where <math|<rigid|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>
        is defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=<around*|[|a,b|[>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=\<varnothing\>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>Then
        <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
        where <math|<rigid|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>
        is defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<wedge\><around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>Then
        for <math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>> we have
        either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
          <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
          where <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
          is defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=<around*|[|a,b|[>>.

          <item*|<math|<around*|[|a,b|[><big|cap><around*|[|c,d|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>As
          <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>\<neq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
          we have\ 

          <\equation>
            <label|eq 20.202.204>a\<less\>b\<wedge\>c\<less\>d
          </equation>

          further there exist a <math|x> such that
          <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b\<wedge\>c\<leqslant\>x\<less\>d>,
          hence

          <\equation>
            <label|eq 20.203.204>a\<less\>d\<wedge\>c\<less\>b
          </equation>

          For <math|a,c> we have either:

          <\description>
            <item*|<math|a\<leqslant\>c>>Then for <math|b,d> we have either:\ 

            <\description>
              <item*|<math|b\<leqslant\>d>>Let
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,c|[>> then
              <math|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>c<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
              <reference|eq 20.203.204>]>>b> so that
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. Assume that
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>> then <math|c\<leqslant\>y>
              contradicting <math|y\<less\>c> hence
              <math|y\<nin\><around*|[|c,d|[>> so that
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>>. So we have\ 

              <\equation>
                <label|eq 20.204.204><around*|[|a,c|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>
              </equation>

              On the other hand if <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>>
              we have <math|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<leqslant\>d> and
              <math|\<neg\><around*|(|c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d|)>\<Rightarrow\>y\<less\>c\<vee\>d\<leqslant\>y>.
              Assume that <math|d\<leqslant\>y> then, as also
              <math|y\<less\>b\<leqslant\>d\<Rightarrow\>y\<less\>d>, we
              would have a contradiction so that we must have
              <math|d\<nleqslant\>y> so that <math|y\<less\>c>. Hence we have
              <math|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>c> which proves that
              <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,c|[>>,
              combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.204.204>] proves

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|a,c|[>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
              </equation*>

              So if we define <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
              by <math|A<rsub|1>=<around*|[|a,c|[>> we have\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>
              </equation*>

              <item*|<math|d\<less\>b>>Let
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,c|[><big|cap><around*|[|d,b|[>> then
              <math|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>c\<wedge\>d\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b>,
              as by [eq: <reference|eq 20.202.204>] <math|c\<less\>d> we
              reach the contradictib <math|y\<less\>y> so that\ 

              <\equation>
                <label|eq 20.205.204><around*|[|a,c|[><big|cap><around*|[|d,b|[>=\<varnothing\>
              </equation>

              Let <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,c|[><big|cup><around*|[|d,b|[>>
              then by the above we have either:

              <\description>
                <item*|<math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,c|[>>>Then
                <math|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>c<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
                <reference|eq 20.203.204>]>>b> so that
                <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. Assume that
                <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>> then
                <math|c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d> contradicting
                <math|y\<less\>c> hence we have
                <math|y\<nin\><around*|[|c,d|[>> so

                <\equation*>
                  y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|]>
                </equation*>

                <item*|<math|y\<in\><around*|[|d,b|[>>>Then
                <math|a<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
                20.203.204>]>>d\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b> so that
                <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. Assume that
                <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>> then
                <math|c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d> contradicting
                <math|d\<leqslant\>y>, hence we must have
                <math|y\<nin\><around*|[|c,d|[>> or\ 

                <\equation*>
                  y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>
                </equation*>
              </description>

              as in all cases we have <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>>
              it follows that\ 

              <\equation>
                <label|eq 20.206.204><around*|[|a,c|[><big|cup><around*|[|d,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>
              </equation>

              On the other hand if <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>>
              then

              <\eqnarray*>
                <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|c\<leqslant\>y\<wedge\>y\<less\>d|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\><around*|(|y\<less\>c\<vee\>d\<leqslant\>y|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|*>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>y\<less\>c|)>\<vee\><around*|(|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b<rsup|>\<wedge\>d\<leqslant\>y|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
              </eqnarray*>

              so that <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,c|[>\<vee\>y\<in\><around*|[|d,b|[>\<Rightarrow\>y\<in\><around*|[|a,c|[><big|cup><around*|[|d,b|[>>
              which proves that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,c|[><big|cup><around*|[|d,b|[>>.
              Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.206.204>]\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|a,c|[><big|cup><around*|[|d,b|[>
              </equation*>

              Further if <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,c|[><big|cap><around*|[|d,b|[>>
              then <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<less\>c\<wedge\>d\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b>
              so that <math|d\<less\>c> wich contradicts with [eq:
              <reference|eq 20.202.204>]. Hence we have\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,c|[><big|cap><around*|[|d,b|[>=\<varnothing\>
              </equation*>

              Hence if we define <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
              by <rigid|<math|A<rsub|1>=<around*|[|a,c|[>>,
              <math|A<rsub|2>=<around*|[|d,b|[>>> then
              <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>>
              is pairwise disjoint and\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>A<rsub|i>
              </equation*>
            </description>

            <item*|<math|c\<less\>a>>Then for <math|b> we have either:\ 

            <\description>
              <item*|<math|b\<leqslant\>d>>If <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
              then <math|c\<less\>a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<leqslant\>d> so
              that <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>>, hence
              <math|<rigid|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>>
              so that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=\<varnothing\>>.
              Hence if we define <math|<rigid|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>
              by <math|A<rsub|1>=\<varnothing\>> it follows that\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>
              </equation*>

              <item*|<math|d\<less\>b>>Let <math|y\<in\><around*|[|d,b|[>>
              then <math|a<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
              20.203.204>]>>d\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b> so that
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. Assume that
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|c,d|[>> then
              <math|c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d> contradicting
              <math|d\<leqslant\>y>. Hence
              <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>> which proves
              that\ 

              <\equation>
                <label|eq 20.207.204><around*|[|d,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|]>
              </equation>

              If <math|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|]>> then

              <\eqnarray*>
                <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|c\<leqslant\>y\<less\>d|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\><around*|(|y\<less\>c\<vee\>d\<leqslant\>y|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>y\<less\>c|)>\<vee\><around*|(|a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>d\<leqslant\>y|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|c\<less\>a>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|c\<less\>a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>y\<less\>c|)>\<vee\><around*|(|c\<less\>a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>d\<leqslant\>y|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|c\<nless\>c>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|c\<less\>a\<leqslant\>y\<less\>b\<wedge\>d\<leqslant\>y>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
              </eqnarray*>

              so that <math|y\<in\><around*|[|d,b|[>>. Hence
              <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|d,b|[>>
              which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.207.204>] gives\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<around*|[|d,b|[>
              </equation*>

              Define <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
              by <math|A<rsub|1>=<around*|[|d,b|[>> then\ 

              <\equation*>
                <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>
              </equation*>
            </description>
          </description>
        </description>

        So in all cases we found a pairwise disjoint family
        <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
        such that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|[|a,b|[>\\<around*|[|c,d|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>A<rsub|i>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </enumerate>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure generated sigma algebra on R>For
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    where <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=<around*|{|<around*|[|a,b|[>\|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
    such that ><around*|[|a,b|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>> we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation*>

    so that using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on R>]\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|C\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\|C<text|
      is closed in >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|}>|]>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra and
      closure>]>>>|<cell|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|[|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>]
    and the fact that <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.284.221>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then either
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    or <math|A=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    which proves that <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>.
    Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] and
    the fact that <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows that
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.284.221>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure begin end (R)>We define\ 

    <\equation*>
      begin:\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| by
      >begin<around*|(|I|)>=a<text| where >I=<around*|[|a,b|[>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      end:\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| by
      >end<around*|(|I|)>=b<text| where >I=<around*|[|a,b|[>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      If <math|\<varnothing\>\<neq\>I=<around*|[|a,b|[>=<around*|[|c,d|[>>
      then by [theorem: <reference|generalized intervals and boundaries>(7)]
      we have <math|a=c\<wedge\>b=d> so these functions are well defined.
    </note>

    <\note>
      As <math|I\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> we have always that
      <math|begin<around*|(|I|)>\<less\>end<around*|(|I|)>>
    </note>
  </definition>

  Using the above two functions we can now define the length function.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure length definition>We define

    <\equation*>
      len:\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >len<around*|(|I|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >I=\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|end<around*|(|I|)>-begin<around*|(|I|)><text|
      if >I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    In other words the function <math|len> calculates the length of a
    interval.
  </definition>

  To simplify the proof that <math|len> is a content we need to order the
  half open intervals based on their boundary points. We define now a total
  order on <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> the set of half-open
  intervals.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure order on half open intervals>The relation
    <math|\<preccurlyeq\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<times\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[><text| if
      >a\<less\>c\<vee\><around*|(|a=c\<wedge\>b\<leqslant\>d|)>
    </equation*>

    is a total order on <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> making
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>,\<preccurlyeq\>|\<rangle\>>>
    a totally ordered set.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We have

    <\description>
      <item*|reflexivity>Let <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      then as <math|a=a> and <math|b=b\<Rightarrow\>b\<leqslant\>b> it
      follows that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
      proving reflectivity.

      <item*|anti-symmetry>Let <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>,<around*|[|c,d|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      be such that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
      and <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>. As
      <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[>> we have
      either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|a\<less\>c>>Then as <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
        we have either <math|c=a> or <math|c\<less\>a> which both give a
        contradiction, so this cases never occurs.

        <item*|<math|a=c>>Then from <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
        it follows that <math|b\<leqslant\>d> and from
        <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a,b|[>> it follows
        that <math|d\<leqslant\>b>. Hence we have <math|a=c\<wedge\>b=d> so
        that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<around*|[|c,d|[>>
      </description>

      <item*|transitivity>Assume that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
      and <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>. As
      <math|><math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[>> we have
      either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|a\<less\>c>>Then as <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>
        we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|c\<less\>e>>Then <math|a\<less\>e> so that
          <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>

          <item*|<math|c=e>>Then <math|a\<less\>e> so that
          \ <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>
        </description>

        <item*|<math|a=c>>Then as <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
        we must have that

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 21.259.300>b\<leqslant\>d
        </equation>

        As <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>> we have
        either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|c\<less\>e>>Then <math|a\<less\>e> so that
          <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>.

          <item*|<math|c=e>>Then as <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>
          we have that <math|b<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
          21.259.300>]>>d\<leqslant\>f> which combined with <math|a=c=e>
          proves that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>.
        </description>
      </description>

      So in all cases we have <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|e,f|[>>
      proving transitivity.

      <item*|totally>If <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>,<around*|[|c,d|[>> then, as
      <math|a,b,c,d\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<leqslant\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is totally ordered, we have for <math|a,c> either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|a\<less\>c>>Then <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>

        <item*|<math|a=c>>Then as <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<leqslant\>|\<rangle\>>>
        is totally ordered we have for <math|b,d> either:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|b\<less\>d>>Then <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|c,d|]>>

          <item*|<math|d\<leqslant\>b>>Then
          <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
        </description>

        <item*|<math|c\<less\>a>>Then <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
      </description>
    </description>
  </proof>

  So we can sort half open intervals which will be used in the next theorem.

  TODO

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure length of union of pairwise half opem intervals>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    pairwise disjoint such that

    <\equation*>
      <big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
    </equation*>

    then there exist a bijection <math|\<sigma\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}><text| we have
      >b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>=a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|n=1>>Take <math|\<sigma\>=Id<rsub|<around*|{|1|}>>:<around*|{|1|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|1|}>>
      then <math|\<sigma\>> is a bijection, further as
      <math|<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>=\<varnothing\>> we have vacuously
      that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>
      <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>=a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>>,
      finally <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|1>,b<rsub|1>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>|[>>.\ 

      <item*|<math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>As
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>,\<preccurlyeq\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is totally ordered there exist by [corollary: <reference|ordering of a
      finite family (2)>] a bijection <math|<rigid|\<sigma\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.215.205>\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        with <math|i\<leqslant\>j> we have
        ><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>,\<beta\><rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>|[>
      </equation>

      Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> with
      <math|i\<leqslant\>j> then as <math|><math|<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>\<preccurlyeq\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>,\<beta\><rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>|[>>
      we have either <math|<rigid|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<less\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>>>
      or <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>=a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>>
      so that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.216.205>\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        with >i\<leqslant\>j<text| we have
        >a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>><text|>
      </equation>

      Futher by [theorem: <reference|measure pairwise disjoint sets and
      bijections>] we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.217.206><around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Assume that there exist a <math|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>
      such that <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>>.
      Then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.216.205>]
      \ <math|<rigid|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>>>
      from which it follows that <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>|[>>
      contradicting <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>|[>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.217.206>]>>\<varnothing\>>. Hence the assumtion is
      wrong and we conclude that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.217.205>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}><text|
        we have >b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>
      </equation>

      As <math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      there exist a <math|c,d\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with <math|c\<less\>d> such
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.218.205><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union of family set and
        surjections>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|c,d|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>> then by [eq: <reference|eq
      20.217.205>] we have <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>>
      and as <math|i\<leqslant\>n-1\<less\>n\<Rightarrow\>i+1\<leqslant\>n>
      it follows from [eq: <reference|eq 20.216.205>] that
      <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>>
      so that <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>>,
      hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.219.205>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        we have <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>>>
      </equation>

      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then for
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>>
      we have <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.216.205>]>>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.219.205>]>>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>><rsub|>> so
      that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>>
      hence <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>>.
      So\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.220.205><around*|[|c,d|[>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.218.205>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>,>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>
      </equation>

      As <math|<around*|[|c,d|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|generalized intervals and
      boundaries>(9)] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.221.205>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>\<leqslant\>c\<wedge\>d\<leqslant\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>
      </equation>

      As <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.218.205>]>><around*|[|c,d|]>> it follows that
      <math|c\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>> which combined
      with [eq: <reference|eq 20.221.205>] proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.222.205>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>=c
      </equation>

      As <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[><below|=|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.218.205>]><around*|[|c,d|[>> we have
      <math|c\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>\<less\>d>. Assume
      that <math|d\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>> then
      <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>\<less\>d\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>>,
      proving that <math|d\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|c,d|[>>
      leading to the contradiction <math|d\<less\>d>. Hence we must have
      <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>\<leqslant\>d> which combined
      with [eq: <reference|eq 20.221.205>] proves\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.223.203>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>=d
      </equation>

      Summarizing [eqs: <reference|eq 20.218.205>, <reference|eq 20.222.205>
      and <reference|eq 20.223.203>] we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.224.205><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>
      </equation>

      Assume now that there exist a <math|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>
      such that <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<neq\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>>.
      If <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>>
      then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.217.205>]
      <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>>
      and we reach the contradiction <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>\<less\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>>
      so we must have that <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>>
      which as <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<neq\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>>
      proves that <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<less\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>>.
      Hence there exist a <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.225.205>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<less\>x\<less\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>><text|
        so that >x\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>|[><text|
        and >x\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>|[>
      </equation>

      If <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k-1|}>> then
      <math|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.217.205>]>>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.216.205>]>>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<less\>x>
      so that <math|x\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>>.
      Further if <math|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then
      <math|x\<less\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>>
      so that <math|x\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>>.
      From this we conclude that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|x\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>>
      or\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.226.205>x\<nin\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>
      </equation>

      As <math|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.216.205>]>>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>\<less\>x>
      and <math|x\<less\>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|k+1|)>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.216.205>]>>a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>\<less\>b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>>
      so that <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>>>
      which contradicts [eq: <reference|eq 20.226.205>]. Hence the assumption
      is false and we must have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}><text| that
        >b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>=a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove that <math|len> is a content on the semi-ring
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

  TODO

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.246.220>The length function
    <math|len:\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    defined by <math|len<around*|(|I|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0<text|
    if >I=\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|end<around*|(|I|)>-begin<around*|(|I|)><text|
    if >I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>>>>> [see definition:
    <reference|measure length definition>] is a finite content on the
    semi-ring <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First by definition we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.295.220>len<around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Second <math|\<forall\>I\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><text| we
    have >len<around*|(|I|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0\<less\>\<infty\><text|
    if >I=\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|end<around*|(|I|)>-begin<around*|(|I|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
    if >I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>>>>> which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.296.220>len<text| is a finite>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that <math|len> is additive on
    <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>. Let
    <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> pairwise disjoint such that
    <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    then we have for <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|n=1>>Then <math|len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>|)>=len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>I<rsub|i>|)>=len<around*|(|I<rsub|1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>

      <item*|<math|1\<less\>n>>Then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      there exists <math|a<rsub|i>>, <math|b<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with
      <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> such that
      <math|I<rsub|i>=<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>. Using [theorem:
      <reference|measure length of union of pairwise half opem intervals>]
      there exist a bijection <math|\<sigma\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.299.220><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>
      </equation>

      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.298.220>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}><text|
        >b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>=a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>
      </equation>

      Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|sum commutativity>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|I<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|1\<less\>n>>|<cell|<around*|(|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n-1><around*|(|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.298.220>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n-1><around*|(|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i+1|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|sum of differences>]>>>|<cell|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>+a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>-a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|len<around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|1|)>>,b<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|n|)>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.299.220>]>>>|<cell|len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    To summarize\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.301.221>\<forall\><around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><text|
      pairwise disjoint with ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><text|
      \ >len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    <label|eq 20.297.220>Next we extend the above to
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>. Let
    <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.
    Define <math|N=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|I<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then for <math|N> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|N=\<varnothing\>>>Then\ 

      <\equation*>
        len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>|)>=len<around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|N\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then as
      <math|\<varnothing\>\<neq\>N> is finite there exist a
      <math|m\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> and a bijection
      <math|<rigid|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\<rightarrow\>N>>.
      Further we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union intersection and empty
        set>]>>>|<cell|len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>N>I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union of family set and
        surjections>]>>>|<cell|len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>I<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.301.221>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>len<around*|(|I<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>len<around*|(|I<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|sum bijection on index>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>N>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>N>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\N>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>N>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\N>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.300.220>Let <around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><text|,
      >n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| pairwise disjoint with
      ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><text|
      then ><rigid|len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>
    </equation>

    So using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.295.220>, <reference|eq 20.296.220> and
    <reference|eq 20.300.220>] it follows that <math|len> is a finite content
    on the semi-ring <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.
  </proof>

  Next we prove that <math|len> is a pre-measure on
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.248.220>The function <math|len:\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    [see definition: <reference|measure length definition>] is a pre-measure
    on the semi-ring <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Consider the topological space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Let <math|A\<in\>S<rsub|<around*|[||[>>> and
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then for <math|A> we have
    either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|A=\<varnothing\>>>Take then
      <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      and <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<varnothing\>>. Then
      <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> is compact by [example:
      <reference|compact empty set>], <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A>
      and <math|len<around*|(|A|)>-len<around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>=0-0=0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.

      <item*|<math|A\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then there exists
      <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with <math|a\<less\>b> such that
      <math|A=<around*|[|a,b|[>>. Define then
      <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<around*|[|a,b-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|[>>
      and <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<around*|[|a,b-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|]>>.
      Then by the Heine Borel theorem ]theorem: <reference|compact Heine
      Borel (1)>] <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> is compact. Further
      <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A>
      and <math|len<around*|(|A|)>-len<around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>=len<around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>-len<around*|(|<around*|[|a,b-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|[>|)>=b-a-<around*|(|b-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>-a|)>=<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>
    </description>

    Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure compact class approximation
    (3)>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <wide|len|\<wide-bar\>><text| is a pre-measure on
      >\<cal-R\><around*|[|\<cal-S\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we use a derivate of the Carathéodry theorem [theorem:
  <reference|measure extending pre-measure to a measure>] to extend
  <math|len> to the Lebesgue measure on Lebesgue measurable sets in
  <math|\<bbb-R\>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Lebesgue measure space on R>If we define

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >\<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text|
      if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|}>|)>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=<around*|{|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>><text| is a outer measure>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>\|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)><text|
      we have >\<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<lambda\>|\<wide-bar\>>=<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-L\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>>:\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\>=<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>=len
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|\<mu\>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is another measure such that <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|S<rsub|<around*|[||[>>>=len>
    then

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<lambda\>
    </equation*>

    We have then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,<wide|\<lambda\>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a measure space called the Lebesgue measure space> on \<bbb-R\>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| is called the set of Lebesgue
      measurable sets>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<lambda\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| the Lebesgue measure>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As by [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra on R>]
      <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
      we have also that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\>=<around*|(|<wide|\<lambda\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        is a measure on >\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      So\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<lambda\>|\<rangle\>><text|
        is a measure space called the Borel measure space on >\<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>

      where\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| is called the set of Borel
        sets on >\<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\><text| is called the Borel measure on >\<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.248.220>] we have that

    <\equation*>
      len<text| is a pre-measure on the semi-ring
      >\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
    </equation*>

    Define <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|-i,i|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    then we have

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      ><around*|[|-i,i|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-<around*|(|i+1|)>,i+1|[><text|
      and >len<around*|(|<around*|[|-i,i|[>|)>=2\<cdot\>i\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    If <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then by [theorem: <reference|complex
    Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist
    <math|n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|-n\<less\>x\<less\>m>. Let
    <math|i=max<around*|(|n,m|)>> then <math|-i\<leqslant\>-n\<less\>x\<less\>m\<less\>i>
    so that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|-i,i|[>> from which it follows that
    <math|\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|[>>.
    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|<around*|[|-i,i|[>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    we have <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|[>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-R\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|[>
    </equation*>

    The proof follows then from applying [theorem: <reference|measure
    extending pre-measure to a measure>].\ 
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Product of semi-rings>

  If we want to use the Carathéodory theorem to create a measure on
  <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> we have to start with a
  semi-ring, as <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
  it seems sensible to prove that <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
  is a semi-ring on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>. First note that
  <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
  can be written as <math|R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>>
  where <math|I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> which suggest
  the following definition that extends this note to the more general cases
  of a product of a finite number of semi-rings.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure product of two semi-rings>Let
    <math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    a finite family such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|i>|)>> then
    <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
    is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      In the particular cases where <math|n=2> we note
      <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>S<rsub|i>> as
      <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>. In other words\ 

      <\equation*>
        S<rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>=<around*|{|A\<cdot\>B\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<wedge\>B\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>|}>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  Next we will prove using mathematical induction that
  <math|><math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
  is a semi-ring on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,|}>>X<rsub|i>>.
  The next lemma will be used in the base case and the induction step.

  <\lemma>
    <label|label 20.252.221>Let <math|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|2>> be two sets and
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|2>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|2>|)>> be
    semi-rings on <math|X<rsub|1>> and <math|X<rsub|2>> respectively then
    \ <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>S<rsub|2>> is a semi-ring on
    <math|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>>, <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|2>> are semi-rings we
    have that <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>S<rsub|1>\<wedge\>\<varnothing\>\<in\>S<rsub|2>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.311.221>\<varnothing\>=\<varnothing\>\<cdot\>\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Let <math|R,S\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>> then there
    exists <math|A,C\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>> and
    <math|B,D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      R=A\<cdot\>B<text| and >S=C\<cdot\>D
    </equation*>

    Then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<big|cap>S>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A\<cdot\>B|)><big|cap><around*|(|C\<cdot\>D|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|finite product properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    given that <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>,\<cal-S\><rsub|2>> are semi-rings it
    follows that <math|A<big|cap>C\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<wedge\>B<big|cap>D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>
    so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.312.221>R<big|cap>S\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>*\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure A.B minus C.D>] we have that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R\\S>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A\<cdot\>B|)>\\<around*|(|C\<cdot\>D|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)><big|cup><around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)><big|cup><around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    where\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|(|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>|)>=\<varnothing\>>

      <item><math|<around*|(|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>|)>=\<varnothing\>>

      <item><math|<around*|(|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>|)>=\<varnothing\>>
    </enumerate>

    So if we define <math|<around*|{|R<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>>>
    by\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|1>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|2>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|3>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    then we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.313.221><around*|{|R<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>><text|
      is a pairwise disjoint and >R\\S=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2.3|}>>R<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Now for <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i=1>>As <math|A,C\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>> a semi ring
      there exist a pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.314.221>A\\C=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      likewise as <math|B,D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>> there exist a pairwise
      disjoint <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.315.221>B\\D=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      so that we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|1>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|finite product properties
        (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\<cdot\>B<rsub|j>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      By [theorem: <reference|product of finite sets>]
      <math|<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>>
      is finite so there exist a bijection\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<sigma\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>
      </equation*>

      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        R<rsub|1>=<big|cup><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\<cdot\>B<rsub|j>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union of family set and
        surjections>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>\<cdot\>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)>
      </equation*>

      Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|R<rsub|1,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>><text|
        by >R<rsub|1,i>=A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>\<cdot\>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>
      </equation*>

      then we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>>
      that, as <math|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>>,
      <math|B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>
      so that <math|R<rsub|1,i>=A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>\<cdot\>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>\<in\>S<rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|R<rsub|1,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2><text|
        and >R<rsub|1>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>>R<rsub|1,i><rsub|>
      </equation*>

      Further if <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>> with
      <math|i\<neq\>j> we have, as <math|\<sigma\>> is a bijection, that
      <math|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)>\<neq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)>>, for
      <math|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>,
      <math|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>> we have either

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>=\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then
        <math|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>\<neq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>
        so that

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|1,i><big|cap>R<rsub|1,j>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>\<cdot\>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>\<cdot\>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>|)>\<cdot\>\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varnothing\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item*|<math|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>\<neq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|1,i><big|cap>R<rsub|1,j>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>\<cdot\>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>\<cdot\>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>A<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varnothing\>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varnothing\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </description>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|R<rsub|1,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2><text|
        is pairwise disjoint and >R<rsub|1>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|1>|}>>R<rsub|1,i><rsub|>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|i=2>>Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|2>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A\\C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.314.221>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|finite product properties
        (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|1>|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>>R<rsub|2,i>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      where\ 

      <\equation*>
        N<rsub|2>=m<rsub|1><text| and ><around*|{|R<rsub|2,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>><text|
        is defined by >R<rsub|2,i>=A<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>> then, as
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>>,
      <math|B,D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>\<Rightarrow\>B<big|cap>D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>,
      <math|R<rsub|2,i>=A<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|R<rsub|2,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>>\<subseteq\>S<rsub|1>\<odot\>S<rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      Further if <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>> with
      <math|i\<neq\>j> then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        R<rsub|2,i><big|cap>R<rsub|2,j>=<around*|(|A<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>|)>=<around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>=\<varnothing\>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<big|cap>D|)>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      So we have proved that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|R<rsub|2,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2><text|
        is pairwise disjoint and >R<rsub|2>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>>R<rsub|2,i>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|i=3>>Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|3>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B\\D|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.315.221>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|finite product properties
        (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|2>|}>><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|3>|}>>D<rsub|3,i>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      where\ 

      <\equation*>
        N<rsub|3>=m<rsub|2><text| and ><around*|{|R<rsub|3,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|3>|}>><text|
        is defined by >R<rsub|3,i>=<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|3>|}>> then, as
      <math|B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>,
      <math|A,C\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<Rightarrow\>A<big|cap>C\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>>,
      <math|R<rsub|3,i>=<around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>*\<cdot\>B<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|R<rsub|3,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>>\<subseteq\>S<rsub|1>\<odot\>S<rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      Further if <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|2>|}>> with
      <math|i\<neq\>j> then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        R<rsub|3,i><big|cap>R<rsub|3,j>=<around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\>B<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\>B<rsub|j>|)>=<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>=<around*|(|A<big|cap>C|)>\<cdot\>\<varnothing\>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      So we have proved that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|R<rsub|3,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|3>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2><text|
        is pairwise disjoint and >R<rsub|3>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|3>|}>>R<rsub|3,i>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    To summarize the above, we have a pairwise disjoint
    <math|<around*|{|R<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>>> such that

    <\equation*>
      R\\S\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.313.221>[>><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>>R<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>> there exist a pairwised
    disjoint <math|<around*|{|R<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>S<rsub|2>>
    such\ 

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|i>|}>>R<rsub|i,j>
    </equation*>

    Further as <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<cdot\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|i>|}>>
    is finite by [theorems: <reference|product of finite sets>,
    <reference|finite union of finite sets is finite>] there exist a
    bijection\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>\<rightarrow\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2,3|}>><around*|{|i|}>\<cdot\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    \ Hence we can apply ]corollary: <reference|corollary 20.111.218>] to get
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.316.221><around*|{|R<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and >R\\S=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,N|}>>R<rsub|\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<sigma\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>
    </equation>

    Finally from [eqs: <reference|eq 20.311.221>, <reference|eq 20.312.221>
    and the above eq: <reference|eq 20.316.221>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2><text| is a semi-ring on
      <math|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  If we want to use the above lemma in the proof by mathematical induction
  that <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i><rsub|>>
  is a semi-ring we have a small problem:
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>X<rsub|i>> is
  not equal to <math|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>X<rsub|n+1>>.
  Luckely we can prove that <math|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>X<rsub|n+1>>
  is bijective to <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>X<rsub|i>>.
  So it makes sense to see how a bijection can be used to generate a
  semi-ring on the image of the bijection based on a semi-ring on the domain
  of the bijection.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure semi-ring bijection>Let <math|X,Y> be sets,
    <math|\<beta\>:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a bijection and
    <math|\<cal-S\>\<subseteq\>X> a semi-ring on <math|X> then
    <math|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>> is a
    semi-ring on <math|Y>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<beta\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<varnothing\>> it follows
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.304.221>\<varnothing\>\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>
    </equation>

    Let <math|R,S\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>>
    there exist <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> such that
    <math|R=\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>>, <math|S=\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>>. Using
    [theorem: <reference|function function and intersection and union>(4)]
    and the fact that <math|\<beta\>> is a bijection hence injective we have
    that

    <\equation*>
      R<big|cap>S=\<beta\><around*|(|A|)><big|cap>\<beta\><around*|(|B|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|function function and intersection and
      union>(4)]>>\<beta\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<cal-S\>> is a semi-ring <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> so
    that <math|\<beta\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>>
    which combined with the above proves that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.305.221>\<forall\>R,S\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}><text|
      we have >R<big|cap>S\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|function properties (1)>(5)] and the fact that
    <math|\<beta\>> is injective it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.306.221>R\\S=\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\\\<beta\><around*|(|B|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|function properties (1)>(5)]>>\<beta\><around*|(|A\\B|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and <math|\<cal-S\>> is a semi-ring there
    exist a pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.307.221>A\\B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text| with
    <math|i\<neq\>j> then <math|\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|function function and intersection and
    union>(4)]>>\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>|)>=\<beta\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<varnothing\>>>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.308.221>\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    Further\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.309.221><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family image and preimage>]>>\<beta\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.307.221>]>>\<beta\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.306.221>]>>R\\S
    </equation>

    Define <math|<around*|{|R<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>>
    by <math|R<rsub|i>=\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>> then [eqs:
    <reference|eq 20.308.221>, <reference|eq 20.309.221>] can be summarized
    as\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.310.221>\<forall\>R,S\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}><text|
      >\<exists\><around*|{|R<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}><text|
      such that >R\\S=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>R<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    By [eqs: <reference|eq 20.304.221>, <reference|eq 20.305.221> and
    <reference|eq 20.310.221>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}><text| is a
      semi-ring on >Y
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we construct a bijection between <math|><math|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>X<rsub|n+1>>
  and <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>X<rsub|i>>.\ 

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.256.220>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>>
    a finite family such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|i>|)>> then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\>:<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>X<rsub|n+1>\<rightarrow\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>X<rsub|i><text|
        defined by <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)><rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x<rsub|i><text|
        if >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|y<text| if
        >i=n+1>>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      is a bijection\ 

      <item><math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>=<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      Be carefull in the notation used in <math|<around*|(|2|)>>
      <math|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>> is the image of the set <math|B> in the
      domain <math|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>X<rsub|n+1>>
      of the bijection <math|\<beta\>>. So
      <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
      is not equal to <math|\<beta\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|)>>
      which is by the way not well defined.\ 
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>We have:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|injectivity>Assume that <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)>=\<beta\><around*|(|u,v|)>>
        then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          x<rsub|i>=\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)><rsub|i>=\<beta\><around*|(|u,v|)><rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>
        </equation*>

        so that <math|x=u> and <math|y=\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)><rsub|n+1>=\<beta\><around*|(|u,v|)><rsub|n+1>=v>.
        So <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>=<around*|(|u,v|)>> which proves
        injectivity.

        <item*|surjectivity>Let <math|u\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|[|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|]>>X<rsub|i>>
        and define <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>
        by <math|x<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>> and <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|n+1>> by
        <math|y=u<rsub|n+1>> then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>
        we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)><rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i><text|
          if >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|u<rsub|n+1><text|
          if >i=n+1>>>>>=u<rsub|i>
        </equation*>

        proving that <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)>=u>
      </description>

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>>
      be a family of sets such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<in\>S<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>.
      Let <rigid|<math|u\<in\>\<beta\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>|)>>>
      then <math|u=\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)>> where
      <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>A<rsub|i>> and <math|y\<in\>A<rsub|n+1>>. So\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}><text|
        >\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)><rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x<rsub|i>\<in\>A<rsub|i><text|
        if >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|y\<in\>A<rsub|i><text|
        if >i=n+1>>>>>\<in\>A<rsub|>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|u\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>>,
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.317.222>\<beta\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>|)>\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      If <math|u\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
      then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> we have
      <math|u<rsub|i>\<in\>A<rsub|i>>. Define
      <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>
      by <math|x<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i>\<in\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|y=u<rsub|n+1>\<in\>A<rsub|n<rsub|1>>> then
      <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>>.
      further\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x<rsub|i>=u<rsub|i><text|
        if >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|y=u<rsub|n+1><text|
        if >i=n+1>>>>>=u<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      proving that <math|u=\<beta\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>\<beta\>><math|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>|)>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<beta\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>|)>
      </equation*>

      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.317.222>] proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.318.222><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>=\<beta\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>|)>
      </equation>

      Let now <math|A\<in\><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>S<rsub|i>>
      then <math|A=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A>
      where <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>. Then
      <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
      and <math|A<rsub|n+1>\<in\>S<rsub|n+1>> so that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n+1>
      </equation*>

      Hence as

      <\equation*>
        A=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.318.222>]>><rsub|<text|>>\<beta\><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>|)>|)>
      </equation*>

      it follows that <math|A\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.319.222><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>S<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>
      </equation>

      On the other hand if <math|B\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>>
      then there exists a <math|<rigid|A\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>>>
      such that <math|B=\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>>. As
      <math|A\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S>
      there exist a <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> and a
      <math|A<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n+1>> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        B=\<beta\><around*|(|A|)>=\<beta\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>A<rsub|n+1>|)>
      </equation*>

      Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.318.222>] on the above proves that that\ 

      <\equation*>
        B=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      hence <math|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>\<subseteq\><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.319.222>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>S<rsub|i>=<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        \;
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to use mathematical induction to prove that
  <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>S<rsub|i>> is a
  semi-ring on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure finite product of semi-rings>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    a finite family such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> is a semi-ring on <math|X<rsub|i>> then for
    <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
    defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    we have that

    <\equation*>
      <big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>S<rsub|i><text| is a
      semi-ring on ><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    \;
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We prove this by mathematical induction, so define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
      is a family such that >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      <verbatim|>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i><text| is a semi-ring on >X<rsub|i><text|
      then ><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>S<rsub|i><text|
      is a semi-ring on ><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>>
      be such that <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>> is a semi-ring on
      <math|X<rsub|1>>. Define

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>X<rsub|i><text|
        where >\<beta\><around*|(|x|)> is defined by
        \<beta\><around*|(|x|)><rsub|1>=x<text| [in other words
        <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|x|)>>>
      </equation*>

      then we have:

      <\description>
        <item*|injectivity>If <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x|)>=\<beta\><around*|(|y|)>>
        then <math|x=\<beta\><around*|(|x|)><rsub|1>=\<beta\><around*|(|y|)><rsub|1>=y>.

        <item*|surjectivity>If <math|y\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>X<rsub|i>>
        then for <math|x=y<rsub|1>> we have
        <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>> that
        <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x|)><rsub|1>=x=y<rsub|1>> so that
        <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x|)>=y>.
      </description>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\><text| is a bijection>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|A\<in\><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>S<rsub|i>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1|}><text|
      >A<rsub|i>\<in\>S<rsub|i>|}>> then <math|A=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
      where <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>>. If
      <math|x\<in\>A=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>> then
      <math|x<rsub|1>\<in\>A<rsub|i>> so that
      <math|x=\<beta\><around*|(|x<rsub|1>|)>\<in\>\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>>
      and if <math|x\<in\>\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>> then there exist a
      <math|a\<in\>A<rsub|1>> such that <math|x=\<beta\><around*|(|a|)>=<around*|(|a|)>>
      hence <math|x<rsub|1>=\<beta\><around*|(|a|)><rsub|1>=a\<in\>A<rsub|i>>
      so that <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>=A>,
      proving that <math|A=\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>|}>>.
      Hence we have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.320.226><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>S<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>|}>
      </equation>

      For the opposite inclusion, let <math|A\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>|}>>
      then there exist a <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\>S<rsub|1>> such that
      <math|A=\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>>. If
      <math|x\<in\>A=\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>> then there exist a
      <math|a\<in\>A<rsub|1>> such that <math|x=\<beta\><around*|(|a|)>> so
      that <math|<rigid|x<rsub|1>=\<beta\><around*|(|a|)><rsub|1>=a\<in\>A<rsub|1>>>
      hence <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
      and if <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
      then <math|x<rsub|1>\<in\>A<rsub|1>> so that
      <math|x=\<beta\><around*|(|x<rsub|1>|)>\<in\>\<beta\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>,
      proving that <math|A=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>S<rsub|i>>.
      Hence we have that <math|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>|}>\<subseteq\><big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>S<rsub|i>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.320.226>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>S<rsub|i>=<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|1>|}>
      </equation*>

      Finally as <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>> is a semi-ring it follows from
      [lemma: <reference|measure semi-ring bijection>] that
      <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> is a
      semi-ring which proves that <math|1\<in\>S>.

      <item*|<math|2\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>If <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>>
      is such that <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|1>> is a semi-ring on
      <math|X<rsub|1>> and <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|2>> is a semi-ring on
      <math|X<rsub|2>> then by [theorem: <reference|measure product of two
      semi-rings>] <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>=\<cal-S\><rsub|1>\<odot\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>
      is a semi-ring on <math|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>X<rsub|i>>.
      Hence <math|2\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>>
      be a finite family such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>
      we have that <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> is a semi-ring on
      <math|X<rsub|i>>. As <math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>> it follows that
      <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
      is a semi-ring on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>.
      As also <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|n+1>> is a semi-ring on <math|X<rsub|n+1>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure product of two
      semi-rings>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1><text|
        is a semi-ring on ><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>X<rsub|n+1>
      </equation*>

      Using the previous lemma [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.256.220>] there
      exist a bijection\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\>:<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>X<rsub|n+1>\<rightarrow\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>X<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      such that

      <\equation*>
        <big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>=<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>
      </equation*>

      By [theorem: <reference|measure semi-ring bijection>] it follows then
      that <math|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\><around*|(|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|)>\<odot\>S<rsub|n+1>|}>>
      is a semi-ring on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>X<rsub|i>>.
      Hence <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
      is a semi-ring on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>X<rsub|i>>
      proving that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
    </description>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|measure finite product of semi-rings (1)>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|X> a set and <math|\<cal-S\>> a semi-ring
    on <math|X> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsup|n>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>A<rsub|i>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\>|}>
    </equation*>

    is a semi-ring on <math|X<rsup|n>>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Define <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>.,n|}>>>
    by <math|X<rsub|i>=X> and <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|i>=\<cal-S\>>. Then by the
    previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measure finite product of
    semi-rings>] we have that <math|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>
    is a semi-ring on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>.
    Now by definition we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      X<rsup|n>\<equallim\><rsub|definition><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|odot><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<cal-S\><rsub|i>=\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-S\><rsup|n>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that <math|\<cal-S\><rsup|n>> is a semi-ring on <math|X<rsup|n>>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Semi-ring on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>>

  We are now ready to prove that <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
  is a semi-ring.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure R^n alternative definition>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}><below|=|def><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cap><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}><below|=|def><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then by
    [definition: <reference|measure half open intervals in R^n>] there exists
    <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with
    <math|a\<less\>b\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> such that

    <\equation*>
      R=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|measure [a,b[ in R^n>]>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> hence
    <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    it follows that <math|R\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>> proving that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.320.224>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|R\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>> then there exists a
    <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> such that
    <math|R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>>..
    Hence <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> there exists
    <math|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> such that
    <math|I<rsub|i>=<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>. Define
    <math|a=<around*|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>,
    <math|<around*|(|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    then <math|a\<less\>b> and <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
    <reference|measure [a,b[ in R^n>]>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>=R>
    from which it follows that <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>.
    So <math|><math|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>,
    combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.320.224>] gives\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.321.224>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>
    </equation>

    Let <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then there
    exists <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    with <math|I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> [so that
    <math|I<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>] such that
    <math|R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>>.
    Applying [theorem: <reference|product product is not empty>] it follows
    that <math|R\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.322.224>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cap><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation>

    If <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>Then by
      [eq: <reference|eq 20.321.224>] <math|R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>>
      where <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      so that <math|R\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>>.

      <item*|<math|R=\<varnothing\>>>Define
      <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
      by <math|I<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      then <math|\<varnothing\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|product of a empty set is
      empty>]>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>>
      so that <rigid|<math|R\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>>>.
    </description>

    hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.323.224>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>
    </equation>

    Let <math|R\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>> then
    <math|R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>>
    where <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>. Then we have
    either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|I<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then by [theorem:
      <reference|measure semi-ring on the set of real numbers (1)>] it
      follows that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I>
      <math|I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> so that
      <math|<rigid|I<rsub|i>=<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>>>,
      hence for <math|a=<around*|(|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|n>|)>,b=<around*|(|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      we have <math|a\<less\>b> so that

      <\equation*>
        R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
      <math|I<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>>Then

      <\equation*>
        R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|product of a empty set is
        empty>]>>\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cap><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    proving that <math|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cap><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.323.224>] proves finally that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  A direct consequence of the above theorem is the following

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure semi-ring on R^n>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    is a semi-ring on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure R^n alternative definition>]>>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>I<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >I<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>=<around*|(|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|)><rsup|n>
    </equation*>

    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure finite product of semi-rings
    (1)>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}><text|
      is a semi-ring>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel algebra on R^n (equivalences 1)>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|C\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|C<text|
      is closed in >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsup|n>>|}>|]>=\<beta\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    By [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebras on R^n equivalences>] we
    only have to prove that <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>>.
    As <math|<rigid|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>>>
    and <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>>
    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.325.226>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then either <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsub|n>|]>>
    or <math|A=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsub|n>|]>>
    so that <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>,
    as <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>
    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] that
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.325.226>] proves\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Content on the semi-ring
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure volume function><index|<math|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>>><dueto|Content
    of a rectangle>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we define

    <\equation*>
      vol<rsup|n>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0<text|
      if >R=\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|v<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)><text| if
      >R\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    where <math|v<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is defined by <math|v<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|begin<around*|(|R|)><rsub|i>-end<around*|(|R|)><rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    [see definition: <reference|measure begin end>].

    <\note>
      Using the definition it follows that <math|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>>
      is finite and\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}><text|
        we have >vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>=0\<Leftrightarrow\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  Next it remains to prove that <math|vol<rsup|n>> is actual a content on
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>,
  the most difficult part is proving additivity of <math|vol<rsup|n>>. To
  illustrate how to prove this consider the following <math|2>-dimesional
  case [see figure: ] where

  <\equation*>
    R=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,4|}>>R<rsub|i>
  </equation*>

  then we have to prove that\ 

  <\equation*>
    vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|4>vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>
  </equation*>

  <\big-figure|<with|gr-mode|<tuple|edit|text-at>|gr-frame|<tuple|scale|1cm|<tuple|0.5gw|0.449999gh>>|gr-geometry|<tuple|geometry|1par|0.6par>|gr-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|5>|gr-edit-grid-aspect|<tuple|<tuple|axes|none>|<tuple|1|none>|<tuple|10|none>>|gr-edit-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-edit-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|5>|gr-auto-crop|true|<graphics||<line|<point|-6|3>|<point|5.0|3.0>|<point|5.0|-3.0>|<point|-6.0|-3.0>|<point|-6.0|3.0>>|<line|<point|-6|-1>|<point|1.0|-1.0>|<point|1.0|-3.0>>|<line|<point|1|3>|<point|1.0|-1.0>>|<line|<point|1|0>|<point|5.0|0.0>>|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|2>>|<point|3.04691427437492|0.958575869824051>>|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|1>>|<point|-3.06547|0.799031>>|<line|<point|-1.5|-1>|<point|-1.5|-3.0>>|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|3>>|<point|-3.5|-2.0>>|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|4>>|<point|-0.240086624568957|-2.0>>|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|5>>|<point|3|-1.5>>>>>
    \;
  </big-figure>

  To do this we divide <math|R<rsub|1>>, <math|R<rsub|2>>,
  <math|R<rsub|3>,R<rsub|4>> and <math|R<rsub|5>> in sub-cubes by
  partitioning their sides in sub intervals.\ 

  <\big-figure|<with|gr-mode|<tuple|edit|text-at>|gr-frame|<tuple|scale|1cm|<tuple|0.5gw|0.340001gh>>|gr-geometry|<tuple|geometry|1par|0.6par>|gr-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|5>|gr-edit-grid-aspect|<tuple|<tuple|axes|none>|<tuple|1|none>|<tuple|10|none>>|gr-edit-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-edit-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|5>|gr-color|blue|gr-dash-style-unit|10ln|gr-auto-crop|true|<graphics||<with|color|blue|dash-style-unit|10ln|<line|<point|-6|3>|<point|5.0|3.0>|<point|5.0|-3.0>|<point|-6.0|-3.0>|<point|-6.0|3.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style-unit|10ln|<line|<point|-6|-1>|<point|1.0|-1.0>|<point|1.0|-3.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style-unit|10ln|<line|<point|1|3>|<point|1.0|-1.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style-unit|10ln|<line|<point|1|0>|<point|5.0|0.0>>>|<line|<point|-1.5|-1>|<point|-1.5|-3.0>>|<with|color|blue|<line|<point|1|0>|<point|-6.0|0.0>>>|<with|color|blue|<line|<point|-1.5|-1>|<point|-1.5|3.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style-unit|10ln|<line|<point|1|-1>|<point|5.0|-1.0>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|1,1>>|<point|-3.89805|1.57003>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|1,2>>|<point|-0.628638|1.65126>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|1,3>>|<point|-3.87775|-0.440353>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|1,4>>|<point|-0.48649|-0.420046>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|2,1>>|<point|2.51893438285488|1.67156700621775>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|2,2>>|<point|2.53924|-0.501273>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|3,1>>|<point|-3.85744|-1.92276>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|4,1>>|<point|-0.425569|-1.90245>>>|<with|color|blue|<text-at|<math|R<rsub|5,1>>|<point|2.64077589628258|-1.80091612647176>>>>>>
    \;
  </big-figure>

  so that\ 

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|1>>|<cell|=>|<cell|R<rsub|1,1>+R<rsub|1,2>+R<rsub|1,3>+R<rsub|1,4>>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|2>>|<cell|=>|<cell|R<rsub|2,1>+R<rsub|2,2>>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|3>>|<cell|=>|<cell|R<rsub|3,1>>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|4>>|<cell|=>|<cell|R<rsub|4,1>>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|5>>|<cell|=>|<cell|R<rsub|5,1>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  then we prove that

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,2>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,3>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,4>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|2,1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|2,2>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|3,1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|3,1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|4,1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|5,1>|)>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  and apply associativity to get\ 

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,2>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,3>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1,4>|)>|)>+<around*|(|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|2,1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|2,2>|)>|)>+<around*|(|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|3,1>|)>|)>+<around*|(|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|3,1>|)>|)>+<around*|(|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|4,1>|)>|)>+<around*|(|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|5,1>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|1>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|2>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|3>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|4>|)>+vol<rsup|2><around*|(|R<rsub|5>|)>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  which proves additivity. First we show that every increasing finite family
  of real numbers can be used to construct a pairwise disjoint union of half
  open intervals that is itself a half open interval. This will be used later
  to turn a union of half open intervals that is itself a half open interval
  in a disjoint union of half open intervals.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.287.224>Let <math|n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with
    <math|n\<leqslant\>m>, <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>> we have
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i+1>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|n>,a<rsub|m>|[>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
    then we may assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange <math|i>
    and <math|j>]. Assume that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[><big|cap><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,a<rsub|j+1>|[>>
    then <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>a<rsub|i+1><below|\<leqslant\>|i\<less\>j\<Rightarrow\>i+1\<leqslant\>j>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>a<rsub|j+1>>
    leading to the contradiction <math|x\<less\>x>, hence the assumption is
    wrong proving that <math|><math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[><big|cap><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,a<rsub|j+1>|[>=\<varnothing\>>.
    So

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|n>,a<rsub|m>|[>> then
    <math|a<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>a<rsub|m>> so that
    <math|n\<in\><around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m|}>\|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x|}>>
    a finite set. Hence <math|<rigid|M=max<around*|(|<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m|}>\|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x|}>|)>>>
    exist. As <math|x\<less\>a<rsub|m>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|m>\<nleqslant\>x>
    we have <math|M\<neq\>m> so that <rigid|<math|M\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>\<Rightarrow\>M+1\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>.
    As <math|M=max<around*|(|<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m|}>\|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x|}>|)>>
    it follows that <math|a<rsub|M>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>a<rsub|M+1>> so that
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|M>,a<rsub|M+1>|[>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>>.
    Hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.327.224><around*|[|a<rsub|n>,a<rsub|m>|[>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>>
    then there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>> such that
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>a<rsub|i+1>>. As
    <math|n\<leqslant\>i> and <math|i\<leqslant\>m-1\<less\>m> we have by the
    hypothesis that <math|a<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>\<wedge\>a<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>>
    so that <math|a<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x\<less\>a<rsub|m>> or
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|n>,a<rsub|m>|[>>. Hence
    <math|><math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a<rsub|n>,a<rsub|m>|[>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.327.224>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|n>,a<rsub|m>|[>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we show that a half open interval that is a union of half open
  subintevals is the disjoint union of sub intervals that are based on the
  boundary points of the half open subintervalas. For example:

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|[|-4,2|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|-3,2|[><big|cup><around*|[|-1,1|[><big|cup><around*|[|-4,0|[><big|cup><around*|[|0,2|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|-4,-3|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|-3,-1|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|-1,0|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|0,1|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|1,2|[>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  to do thist we sort the boundary points
  <math|<around*|{|-3,2,-1,1,-4,0,2|}>> giving the sorted set

  <\equation*>
    <around*|{|-4,-3,-1,0,1,2|}>
  </equation*>

  and use the the previous lemma to create the final list of intervals

  <\equation*>
    <around*|[|-4,-3|[>,<around*|[|-3,-1|[>,<around*|[|-1,0|[>,<around*|[|0,1|[>,<around*|[|1,2|[>
  </equation*>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.268.224>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    such that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[>>
    then if we take\ 

    <\equation*>
      A=<around*|{|\<alpha\><rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}><big|cup><around*|{|b<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>
    </equation*>

    \ there exists a <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1|}>> and a bijection
    <math|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\<rightarrow\>A> such that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<beta\><around*|(|1|)>=a\<wedge\>\<beta\><around*|(|m|)>=b>

      <item><math|\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> with
      <math|i\<less\>j> we have <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<less\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x,y\<in\>A> with <math|x\<less\>y> we have
      <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|y|)>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
      <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|j+1|)>|[>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>-1|}>>>
      is pairwise disjoint and <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>-1|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|j+1|)>|[>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|<around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|i+1|)>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>>>
      is pairwise disjoint and <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|i+1|)>|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Define <math|A=<around*|{|a<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}><big|cup><around*|{|b<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>>.
    As <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|1>,b<rsub|1>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> we have
    that <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.328.224>a\<less\>b
    </equation>

    Assume that <math|a\<nin\>A>. As <math|a\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
    there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
    <math|<rigid|a\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<Rightarrowlim\>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|a\<nin\>A\<Rightarrow\>a\<neq\>a<rsub|i>>a<rsub|i>\<less\>a\<less\>b<rsub|i>>>.
    Using the density of the real numbers [theorem: <reference|complex
    densitiy theorem>] there exist a <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>t\<less\>a\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>t\<nin\><around*|[|a,b|[>\<wedge\>t\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
    a contradiction. So the assumption is wrong and we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.329.224>a\<in\>A
    </equation>

    Assume that <math|b\<nin\>A>. Given <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    we have <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|[>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|generalized intervals and boundaries>]
    <math|b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|b\<nin\>A\<Rightarrow\>b\<neq\>b<rsub|i>>b<rsub|i>\<less\>b>.
    As also <math|a\<less\>b> it follows that
    <math|<rigid|max<around*|(|<around*|{|a|}><big|cup><around*|{|b<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>\<less\>b>>.
    Hence using the density of the real numbers [theorem: <reference|complex
    densitiy theorem>] there exists a <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with
    <math|<rigid|max<around*|(|<around*|{|a|}><big|cup><around*|{|b<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>\<less\>t\<less\>b>>.
    Hence <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|t\<nin\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<wedge\>a\<less\>t\<less\>b>.
    So <rigid|<math|t\<nin\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<wedge\>t\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>>
    again a contradiction. Hence the assumption is wrong and we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.330.224>b\<in\>A
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a,b|[>>
    it follows from theorem: <reference|generalized intervals and
    boundaries>] that <math|a\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b>
    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      a=min<around*|(|A|)>\<wedge\>b=max<around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    Using [theorems: <reference|union of two finite sets is finite>,
    <reference|image of a finite set>] it follows that <math|A> is finite and
    as <math|a,b\<in\>A> <math|card<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, hence
    using [theorem: <reference|sorting of a finite set>] it follows that
    there exist a bijection \ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.331.224>\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\<rightarrow\>A<text|
      where >m=card<around*|(|A|)><text| such that
      >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}><text|
      >\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<less\>\<beta\><around*|(|i+1|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> we have
    <math|\<beta\><around*|(|1|)>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>> and
    <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><around*|(|m|)>> it
    follows that <math|\<beta\><around*|(|1|)>=min<around*|(|A|)>=a\<wedge\>\<beta\><around*|(|m|)>=max<around*|(|A|)>=b>,
    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.332.224>a=\<beta\><around*|(|1|)>\<wedge\>b=\<beta\><around*|(|m|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x,y\<in\>A> such that <math|x\<less\>y>. If
    <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>>
    it follows that <math|y=\<beta\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|y|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>|)>=x>
    contradicting <math|x\<leqslant\>y>. Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.333.224>\<forall\>x,y\<in\>A<text| with >x\<less\>y<text|
      we have >\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then
    <math|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>\<in\>A> and <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>>
    [as <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>]
    so that by the above <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>>.
    In other words

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.334.224>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then for
    <math|j\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>-1|}>>
    we have <math|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>\<less\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>> so
    that by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.287.224>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.335.224><around*|{|<around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|j+1|)>|[>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint >
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.336.224><big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>-1|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|j+1|)>|[>=<around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i>|)>|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i>|)>|)>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>
    </equation>

    Using \ [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.287.224>] on [eq: <reference|eq
    20.331.224>] gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.337.224><around*|{|<around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|i-1|)>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.338.224><text| ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|i+1|)>|[>=<around*|[|\<beta\><around*|(|1|)>,\<beta\><around*|(|n|)>|[><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.332.224>]>><around*|[|a,b|[><rsub|>
    </equation>

    So <math|<around*|(|1|)>> is proved by [eq: <reference|eq 20.332.224>],
    <math|<around*|(|2|)>> is proved by [eq: <reference|eq 20.331.224>],
    <math|<around*|(|3|)>> is proved by [eq: <reference|eq 20.333.224>], (4)
    is proved by [eq: <reference|eq 20.334.224>], (5) by [eqs: <reference|eq
    20.335.224>, <reference|eq 20.336.224>] and finally (6) by [eqs:
    <reference|eq 20.337.224>, <reference|eq 20.338.224>].
  </proof>

  Next we show how the volume of a half open rectangle partioned by its sides
  can be calculated. Giving the follow example of a partioned rectangle
  <math|R>

  <small-figure|<with|gr-mode|<tuple|edit|text-at>|gr-frame|<tuple|scale|1cm|<tuple|0.5gw|0.490001gh>>|gr-geometry|<tuple|geometry|1par|0.6par>|gr-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|2>|gr-edit-grid-aspect|<tuple|<tuple|axes|none>|<tuple|1|none>|<tuple|10|none>>|gr-edit-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-edit-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|2>|gr-auto-crop|true|<graphics||<line|<point|-5|2.5>|<point|5.0|2.5>|<point|5.0|-2.5>|<point|-5.0|-2.5>|<point|-5.0|2.5>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|2,1>>|<point|-5.0|2.8>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|2,2>>|<point|-2.0|2.8>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|2,3>>|<point|3.0|2.8>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|2,4>>|<point|4.6|2.8>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-4.31911|2.82663>|<point|-2.11755853948935|2.82663050668078>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-1.2|2.8>|<point|2.8|2.8>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|3.68089|2.82663>|<point|4.4|2.8>>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|2>>|<point|-5.0|3.2>>|<text-at|<math|b<rsub|2>>|<point|4.8|3.2>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-4.4|3.2>|<point|4.6|3.2>>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|1,1>>|<point|-5.8|2.4>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|1,2>>|<point|-5.8|0.8>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-5.6|0.6>|<point|-5.6|-2.2>>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|1,3>>|<point|-5.8|-2.6>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|1>>|<point|-6.4|2.4>>|<text-at|<math|b<rsub|3>>|<point|-6.4|-2.6>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-6.23647|2.23183>|<point|-6.25400185209684|-2.23746527318428>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-5.6|2.2>|<point|-5.6|1.2>>>|<line|<point|-2|2.4>|<point|-2.0|-2.5>>|<line|<point|3.2|2.5>|<point|3.2|-2.5>>|<line|<point|-5|1>|<point|5.0|1.0>>|<text-at||<point|3.68089|2.82663>>>>|>

  we have then

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|a<rsub|1>,b<rsub|1>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2>,b<rsub|2>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|a<rsub|1,1>,a<rsub|1,2>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2,1>,a<rsub|2,2>|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|a<rsub|1,1>,a<rsub|1,2>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2,2>,a<rsub|2,3>|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|a<rsub|1,1>,a<rsub|1,2>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2,3>,a<rsub|2,4>|[><big|sqcup><rigid|<around*|[|a<rsub|1,2>,a<rsub|1,3>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2,1>,a<rsub|2,2>|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|a<rsub|1,2>,a<rsub|1,3>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2,2>,a<rsub|2,3>|[><big|sqcup><around*|[|a<rsub|1,2>,a<rsub|1,3>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2,3>,a<rsub|2,4>|[>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|1,1|)>,<around*|(|1,2|)>,<around*|(|1,3|)>,<around*|(|2,1|)>,<around*|(|2,2|)>,<around*|(|2,3|)>|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|1,\<rho\><rsub|1>>,a<rsub|1,\<rho\><rsub|1>+1>|[>\<cdot\><around*|[|a<rsub|2,\<rho\><rsub|2>>,a<rsub|2,\<rho\><rsub|2>+1>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  and\ 

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|b<rsub|1>-a<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|b<rsub|2>-a<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|a<rsub|1,2>-a<rsub|1,1>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|a<rsub|2,2>-a<rsub|2,1>|)>+<around*|(|a<rsub|1,2>-a<rsub|1,1>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|a<rsub|2,3>-a<rsub|2,2>|)>+<around*|(|a<rsub|1,2>-a<rsub|1,1>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|a<rsub|2,4>-a<rsub|2,3>|)>+<around*|(|a<rsub|1,3>-a<rsub|1,2>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|a<rsub|2,2>-a<rsub|2,1>|)>+<around*|(|a<rsub|1,3>-a<rsub|1,2>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|a<rsub|2,3>-a<rsub|2,2>|)>+<around*|(|a<rsub|1,3>-a<rsub|1,2>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|a<rsub|2,4>-a<rsub|2,3>|)><rigid|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|1,1|)>,<around*|(|1,2|)>,<around*|(|1,3|)>,<around*|(|2,1|)>,<around*|(|2,2|)>,<around*|(|2,3|)>|}>><around*|(|a<rsub|1,\<rho\><rsub|1>+1>-a<rsub|1,\<rho\><rsub|1>>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|a<rsub|2,\<rho\><rsub|2>+1>-a<rsub|2,\<rho\><rsub|2>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>-a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>|)>|)>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.269.224>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> there
    exists <math|<rigid|n<rsub|i>,m<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> with
    <math|n<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|i>> and a
    <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><text|
    such that <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>>>>
    <math|a<rsub|i,j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i,j+1>> and
    <rigid|<math|a<rsub|i,n<rsub|i>>=a<rsub|i>*\<wedge\>a<rsub|i,m<rsub|i>>=b<rsub|i>>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|}><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>-a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>|)>|)>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Assume that <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
    <math|n<rsub|i>=m<rsub|i>> then <math|a<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i,n<rsub|i>>=a<rsub|o,m<rsub|i>>=b<rsub|i>>
    contradicting <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> [because
    <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>], hence a
    assumption is false and we have\ 

    <\equation>
      \<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text| we have
      >n<rsub|i>\<less\>m<rsub|i><text| or >n<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|i>-1
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then, as
    <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>>
    <math|a<rsub|i,j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i,j+1>> it follows form [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.287.224>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.343.224><around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,a<rsub|i,j+1>|[>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint >
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.344.224><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|i,n<rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,m<rsub|i>>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><rsub|><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,a<rsub|i,j+1>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
    then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>, hence by the
    above there exist a <math|\<rho\><rsub|i>\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>>
    such that <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>>.
    So for <math|\<rho\>=<around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<rho\><rsub|n>|)>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>>
    it follows that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>>
    proving that <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>>.
    Hence we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.345.224><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>>
    then there exist a <math|<rigid|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>>>
    such that <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>>.
    Hence <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.344.224>]>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>> proving
    that <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>.
    So it follows that <math|<big|cup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.345.224>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.3463224><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>=<big|cup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<rho\>,\<sigma\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>>
    with <math|\<rho\>=\<sigma\>> then there exist a
    <math|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
    <math|\<rho\><around*|(|l|)>\<neq\>\<sigma\><around*|(|k|)>>. Assume that
    that there exist a <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>>>
    then <math|x<rsub|k>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|k,\<rho\><rsub|k>>,a<rsub|k,\<rho\><rsub|k>+1>|[><big|cap><around*|[|a<rsub|k,\<sigma\><rsub|k>>,a<rsub|k,\<sigma\><rsub|k>+1>|[>\<equallim\><rsub|\<rho\><rsub|k>\<neq\>\<sigma\><rsub|k>>\<varnothing\>>
    which is a contradiction, hence the assumption is wrong and we must have
    that <math|<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>=\<varnothing\>>.
    So we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.347.224><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|}><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    Further we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|measure length definition>]>>>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.343.224>, <reference|eq
      20.344.224>]>>>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>len<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,a<rsub|i,j+1>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.246.220>]>>>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>|}>>len<around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,a<rsub|i,j+1>|]>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|sum distributivity (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>|}>><rsup|><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>len<around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>,a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>|[>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|n<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|i>|}>><rsup|><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>+1>-a<rsub|i,\<rho\><rsub|i>>|)>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves the last part of the theorem.
  </proof>

  The following lemma shows that if the union of half open rectangles is a
  half open rectangle then each side of the of the open rectangle is a union
  of the corresponding sides of the subrectangles. For example in the
  following <math|2>-dimesional we have that\ 

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|[|a<rsub|1>,b<rsub|1>|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|a<rsub|1,1>,b<rsub|1,1>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|2,1>,b<rsub|2,1>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|3,1>,b<rsub|3,1>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|4,1>,b<rsub|5,1>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|5,1>,b<rsub|5,1>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,5|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,1>,b<rsub|i,1>|[>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|[|a<rsub|2>,b<rsub|2>|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|a<rsub|1,2>,b<rsub|1,2>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|2,2>,b<rsub|2,2>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|3,2>,b<rsub|3,2>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|<around*|\<nobracket\>|4,2|]>>,b<rsub|4,2>|[><big|cup><around*|[|a<rsub|5,2>,b<rsub|5,2>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,b|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,2>,b<rsub|i,2>|[>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

