7|0.33184>|<point|-6.85399854478106|-4.83724699034264>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-4.57255|-2.23254>|<point|-2.85292366715174|-2.20632689509194>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-1.10006|-2.21225>|<point|1.38244146051065|-2.19096441328218>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-6.0|-3.11357024621859>|<point|-6.0|-4.91183529816433>>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|1,1>>|<point|-6.1848229316833|0.5>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-6.0|0.263254726937795>|<point|-6.05028337305644|-2.0>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|2.5|-0.186824>|<point|2.5|-2.14453776405074>>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|5,2>>|<point|2.22596480024957|-3.5>>|<text-at|<math|b<rsub|5,1>>|<point|1.5|-5.17238006527566>>|<text-at|<math|a<rsub|5,1>>|<point|1.38786459253038|-3.0>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-2.5|-3.22740810796519>|<point|-2.5|-4.81846511912186>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|1.63062|-3.21362>|<point|1.63062243683027|-4.81786611985712>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|3|-3.37898>|<point|4.5|-3.39803122996072>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-4.81911|1.02664>|<point|1.28682346708628|1.0>>>>>>
    \;

    \;
  </small-figure>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.270.225>Let <math|n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and let
    <math|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
    </equation*>

    then <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>. If
    <math|t\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>>
    then there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> such that
    <math|t\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>>. As
    <math|><math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
    it follows from [theorems: <reference|product product is not empty>,
    <reference|product projection is surjective>] that the projection
    function <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>:<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>>
    is a surjection hence there exists a <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>>
    such that <math|x<rsub|j>=\<pi\><rsub|j><around*|(|x|)>=t>. As

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>
    </equation*>

    we have that <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>>
    so that <math|t=\<pi\><rsub|j><around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.345.225><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|t\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>>. As
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
    so that by \ [theorems: <reference|product product is not empty>,
    <reference|product projection is surjective>] the projection function
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>:<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>>
    is a surjection, hence there exist a <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|)>>
    such that <math|t=\<pi\><rsub|j><around*|(|x|)>>. Then there exists a
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> such that
    <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|m,j>,b<rsub|m,j>|[>>.
    So it follows that <math|<rigid|t=\<pi\><rsub|j><around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|m,j>,b<rsub|m,j>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>>>
    which proves that <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.345.225>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We show now that the volume of the pairwise distinct union of half open non
  empty rectangles is equal to the sum of the volumes of thise reactangles if
  this distinct union is also a half open rectangle.\ 

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.271.225>Let <math|n,> <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><rsub|>>
    pairwise disjoint such that <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>-a<rsub|i,j>|)>|)>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First using the previous lemma [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.270.225>] we
    have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.346.225>\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      ><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>. Using [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.268.224>] there exists a
    <math|n<rsub|j>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.347.225>\<beta\><rsub|j>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|a<rsub|i,j>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}>|}><big|cup><around*|{|b<rsub|i,j>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}>|}>
    </equation>

    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.348.225>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|1|)>=a<rsub|j>\<wedge\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|n<rsub|j>|)>=b<rsub|j>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.349.225>\<forall\>k,l\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}><text|
      with >k\<less\>l<text| we have >\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|k|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|l|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.350.225>\<forall\>x,y\<in\><around*|{|a<rsub|i,j>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}>|}><big|cup><around*|{|b<rsub|i,j>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}>|}><text|
      with >x\<less\>y<text| we have ><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.351.225>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
      we have ><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.352.225>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
      \ <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>=>><big|sqcup><rsub|l\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|l|)>,\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|l+1|)>|[>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.353.225><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><rsub|i><around*|(|i|)>,\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|i+1|)>|[>
    </equation>

    Because of [eqs: <reference|eq 20.348.225> and <reference|eq 20.349.225>]
    we can apply the previous lemma [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.269.224>]
    giving\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.354.225><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>-\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>|[>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.355.225><big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|j>-a<rsub|j>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>-\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>|)>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>>. As
    <math|a<rsub|i,j>=\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>|)>>
    and <math|b<rsub|i,j>=\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>|)>>
    and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>>
    we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.349.225>] that
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|i|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|i+1|)>>
    we can use [ [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.269.224>] again to get

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><around*|[|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>,\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>+1|[>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq
      20.356.225>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>-a<rsub|i,j>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>-\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|j|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq
      20.357.225>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    To relate [eq: <reference|eq 20.354.225>] to [eq: <reference|eq
    20.357.225>] we prove now the following:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.358.225><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>
    </equation>

    <\proof>
      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> and
      <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>. From [eq: <reference|eq
      20.347.225>] it follows that <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|j>|}>>,
      further by [eq: <reference|eq 20.351.225>] we have
      <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1>
      so that <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|j>-1|}>>.
      Applying [theorem: <reference|product inclusion>] gives us then
      <math|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>>
      hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.359.225><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>|)>\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>
      </equation>

      For the opposite inclusion let <math|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>>.
      For <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>> we
      have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.349.225>] that
      <math|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>\<less\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>>
      so that <math|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>\<in\><around*|[|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>,\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>|[>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|1><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsub|n><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|n>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>,\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>,\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.354.225>]>>>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,b<rsub|j>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|hypothesis>>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.360.225>\<exists\>k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
        such that ><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|1><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsub|n><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|n>|)>|)>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|k,j>,b<rsub|k,j>|[>
      </equation>

      Assume now that <math|\<exists\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      such that <math|b<rsub|k,j>\<less\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>>
      then <math|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.360.225>]>>b<rsub|k,j>\<less\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>>
      so that by [eq: <reference|eq 20.350.225>]
      <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>|)>\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>>,
      hence <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>\<less\>\<rho\><rsub|j>+1>
      from which it follows that <math|0\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>-1\<less\>1>
      which, as <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>>
      is a whole number [because <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>|}>>],
      is impossible. Hence the assumption is wrong and we must have that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.361.225>\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|k,j>
      </equation>

      So given <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we hav
      <math|a<rsub|k,j><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.360.225>]>>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.349.225>]>>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.361.225>>>b<rsub|k,j>>. applying then [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.350.225>] proves that
      <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|j>\<less\>\<rho\><rsub|j>+1\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>>,
      hence <math|<rigid|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>->1>
      proving that <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>-1|}>>
      from which it follows that <rigid|<math|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>-1|}>>>.
      This prove that <math|>

      <\equation*>
        <big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.359.225>] results in\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.362.225><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>|)>
      </equation>

      To finish the prove for [eq: <reference|eq 20.358.225>] we must prove
      that it is a pairwise disjoint union. So let
      <math|k,l\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> with <math|k\<neq\>l> and
      assume that

      <\equation*>
        \<exists\>r\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>-1|}><big|cap><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|l,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|l,j>|)>-1|}>
      </equation*>

      then <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>-1\<wedge\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|l,j>|)>\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|l,j>|)>-1
      </equation*>

      or

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>\<leqslant\>r\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>\<wedge\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|l,j>|)>\<leqslant\>r\<less\><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|l,j>|)>
      </equation*>

      applying then [eq: <reference|eq 20.349.225>] gives

      <\equation*>
        a<rsub|k,j>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|r|)>\<less\>b<rsub|k,j>\<wedge\>a<rsub|l,j>\<leqslant\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|r|)>\<less\>b<rsub|l,j><text|
        or >\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|r|)>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|k,j>,b<rsub|k,j>|[>\<wedge\>\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|r|)>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|l,j>,b<rsub|l,j>|[>
      </equation*>

      proving that <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|1><around*|(|r|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsub|n>*<around*|(|r|)>|)>\<in\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|k,i>,b<rsub|k,i>|[>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|l,i>,b<rsub|l,i>|[>|)>>
      which as by the hypothesis <math|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|1><around*|(|r|)>,\<ldots\>,\<beta\><rsub|n>*<around*|(|r|)>|)>\<in\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|k,i>,b<rsub|k,i>|[>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|l,i>,b<rsub|l,i>|[>|)>=\<varnothing\>>
      is a contradiction hence we must have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|k,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|k,j>|)>-1|}><big|cap><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|l,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|l,j>|)>-1|}>
      </equation*>

      the above together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.362.225>] proves finally
      [eq: <reference|eq 20.358.225>].\ 
    </proof>

    Finally we are able to finish the prove of the lemma.

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.355.225>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|j>-1|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>-\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.358.225>]>>>|<row|<cell|<text|[theorem: <reference|sum
      over disjoint subsets (1)>]>>>>>>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|a<rsub|i,j>|)>,\<ldots\>,<around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>|)>-1|}>><around*|(|\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>+1|)>-\<beta\><rsub|j><around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|j>|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.357.225>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>-a<rsub|i,j>|)>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  Now we are finally ready to prove that <math|vol<rsup|n>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
  is a finite content.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure content on R^n>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    <math|vol<rsup|n>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    [see definition: <reference|measure volume function>] is a finite content
    on the semi-ring <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> [see theorem: <reference|measure semi-ring on
    R^n>]
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First from the definition of <math|vol<rsup|n>> [see definition:
    <reference|measure volume function>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.361.226>vol<rsup|n><text| is finite>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.362.226>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<around*|{|R<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    be pairwise disjoint such that <math|<rigid|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>>
    then we have two possibilities for <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>>\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      we must have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> that
      <math|R<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>> so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>|)>=vol<rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then
      for <math|R=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>>
      there exist a <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.363.226>R=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
      </equation>

      Then there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> such that
      <math|R<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> so that
      <math|<rigid|I=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\|R<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>
      hence there exist a <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>\<rightarrow\>I
      </equation*>

      Then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>
      <math|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>> with
      <math|i\<neq\>j> we have, as <math|\<beta\>> is injective, that
      <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>> so that
      <math|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>><big|cap>R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>=\<varnothing\>>.
      So that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.364.226><around*|{|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Let <math|R=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>>
      then we have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.365.226>R=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union intersection and empty
        set>]>><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>R<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family union of family set and
        surjections>]>><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>k|}>>R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>
      </equation>

      Further as <math|<around*|{|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
      we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>> that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.366.226>R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>=\<Pi\><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\>n.
      </equation>

      So that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.367.226><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.363.226>]>><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,b<rsub|i,j>|[>|)>
      </equation>

      Then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|begin<around*|(|R|)><rsub|i>=end<around*|(|R|)><rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<tabular*|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.367.226>]>>>|<row|<cell|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.271.225>]>>>>>>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i,j>-a<rsub|i,j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.366.226>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n><around*|(|begin<around*|(|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>|)><rsub|j>-end<around*|(|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>|)><rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1.,\<ldots\>,k|}>>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|sum bijection on index>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>+0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|i\<nin\>I=R<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\\I>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>R<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|R<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      which finished the proof of the theorem.
    </description>
  </proof>

  Next we use [theorem: <reference|measure compact class approximation (3)>]
  to prove that <math|vol<rsup|n>> is actually a pre-measure on the semi-ring
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>.
  To do this we need the following lemma that will allows us to 'aproximate'
  a half open rectangle by a compact set.\ 

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.272.226>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and
    <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    then <math|\<exists\><around*|{|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    with <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>> such that
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>\<less\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<varepsilon\>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We use induction on <math|n>. Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    and define

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      then >\<exists\><around*|{|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      with >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >0\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i><text| such that
      ><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>\<less\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<varepsilon\>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>If
      <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      then <math|x<rsub|1>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>, define
      <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|1>=min<around*|(|<frac|x<rsub|1>|2>,<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      then <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|x<rsub|1>|2>\<less\>x<rsub|1>>
      and <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>x<rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|1><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>=x<rsub|1>-<around*|(|x<rsub|1>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|1>|)>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>><space|1em>so
      that <math|<rigid|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>x<rsub|i>\<less\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|1><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<varepsilon\>>>
      proving that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>Assume
      that <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      then as <math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>> there exists
      <math|<around*|{|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      with <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>> and
      <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>\<less\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2\<cdot\>x<rsub|n+1>>>
      so that as <math|0\<less\>x<rsub|n+1>> we have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 15.81.439>x<rsub|n+1>\<cdot\><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|n+1>\<cdot\><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation>

      Take now <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|n+1>=min<around*|(|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4\<cdot\><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>>,<frac|x<rsub|n+1>|2>|)>>
      then <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|n+1>\<leqslant\><frac|x<rsub|n+1>|2>\<less\>x<rsub|n+1>>
      and <rigid|<math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|n+1>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>.
      Now

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>x<rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|x<rsub|n+1>\<cdot\><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>-<around*|(|x<rsub|n+1>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|n+1>|)>\<cdot\><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x<rsub|n+1>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|n+1>\<cdot\><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|x<rsub|n+1>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 15.81.439>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|n+1\<in\>S<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  Now we can prove that the volume function is a pre-measure.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure volume is a pre-measure>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    the function <math|vol<rsup|n>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a finite pre-measure on the semi-ring
    <math|<rigid|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Consider the topological space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>|\<rangle\>>>
    based on the canonical topology <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>
    [see theorem: <reference|normed maximum norm R^n>]. Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then for <math|A> we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|A=\<varnothing\>>>Take then
      <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<varnothing\>> and
      <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<varnothing\>>. Then
      <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> is compact by [example:
      <reference|compact empty set>], <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A>
      and <math|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>-vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>=0-0=0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.

      <item*|<math|A\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then there exiss
      <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<less\>b> such that
      <math|A=<around*|[|a,b|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>.
      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      we can use [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.272.226>] there exists a
      <math|<around*|{|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.369.226>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >0\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i><text| and
        ><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<varepsilon\>
      </equation>

      From the above it follows that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>> so that

      <\equation*>
        A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|[>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
      </equation*>

      Further by [theorem: <reference|compact rectangles are compact>]

      <\equation*>
        K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a,b<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|]><text|
        is compact>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|]>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|product inclusion>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A
      </equation*>

      Further\ 

      <\equation*>
        vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>-vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>-\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.369.226>]>>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we found a <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    and a compact set <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> such that
    <math|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A>
    and <math|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>-vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    Finally using [theorem: <reference|measure compact class approximation
    (3)>] it follows that

    <\equation*>
      vol<rsup|n>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a pre-measure on >\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
    </equation*>

    which is finite by [theorem: <reference|measure content on R^n>].
  </proof>

  Next we use a version of the Carathéodry theorem [theorem:
  <reference|measure extending pre-measure to a measure>] to extend
  <math|vol<rsup|n>> to the Lebesgue measure on Lebesgue measurable sets in
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Lebesgue measure space on R^n>If we define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by ><around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text|
      if >\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=\<varnothing\>>>|<row|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|\<nobracket\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>|)>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><around*|[|A|]>=<around*|{|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\|A\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><text| is a outer
      measure>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><rsup|><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)><text|
      we have ><around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<wide-bar\>>=<around*|(|<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<star\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-L\><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)>>:\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n>=<around*|(|<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<star\>>|)><rsub|\|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><rsup|\<star\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>=vol<rsup|n>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|\<mu\>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is another measure such that <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>=vol<rsup|n>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>

    We have then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a measure space called the Lebesgue mesaure space> on
      \<bbb-R\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text| is called the set of
      Lebesgue measurable sets in >\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<wide-bar\>><text| is called the Lebesgue
      measure on >\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on R^n (equivalences
      1)>] <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>>
      we have also\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\><rsup|n>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        is a measure on >\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<rangle\>><text|
        is a measure space called the Borel measure space on
        >\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\><rsup|n><text| is called the Borel measure on
        >\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure volume is a pre-measure>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.371.226>vol<rsup|n>:\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|sqcup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a pre-measure on >\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>
    </equation>

    Define now <math|<around*|{|G<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    by <math|G<rsub|i>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|-i,i|[>>.
    Then as <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|<around*|[|-i,i|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-<around*|(|i+1|)>,i+1|[>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|product inclusion>] that
    <math|G<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>G<rsub|i+1>>, hence we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.372.226>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >G<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>G<rsub|i+1><text| and as >vol<rsup|n><text| is
      finite that >vol<rsup|n><around*|(|G<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have by [theorem:
    <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] that there
    exists <math|n<rsub|i>,m<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|-n<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>m<rsub|i>>. Take
    <math|k=max<around*|(|<around*|{|n<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}><big|cup><around*|{|m<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|-k\<less\>n<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>m<rsub|i>\<less\>k> so
    that <math|x\<in\>G<rsub|k>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>G<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.273.226>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>G<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    The proof follows then from [eqs: <reference|eq 20.371.226>,
    <reference|eq 20.372.226>, <reference|eq 20.273.226>] and [theorem:
    <reference|measure extending pre-measure to a measure>]
  </proof>

  We have the following relation between the Lebesgue measure of half open
  rectangles and closed rectangles.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Lebesgue measure of half-open closed rectangles>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with
    <math|a\<leqslant\>b> then <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|]>|)>>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on R^n rectangles>]
      <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>,<around*|[|a,b|]>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
      so that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>> and
      <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|]>|)>> are well
      defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<leqslant\>b>. If
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>> then <math|\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we
    have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> that
    <math|<rigid|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>>>
    so that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|>,b+<frac|1|m>|[>> which proves
    that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.350.293><around*|[|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>>
    then <math|\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>>. Assume that
    <math|x\<nin\><around*|[|a,b|]>> then there exist a
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<less\>a\<vee\>b<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>>. As
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>>
    we must have that <math|b<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>>, using [theorem:
    <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
    <math|m<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|b<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m<rsub|i>>\<less\>x<rsub|i>>
    from which it follows that <math|x\<nin\><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m<rsub|i>>|[>>
    which contradicts <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>>,
    hence the assumption is false and we must have that
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>>. So <math|<big|cap><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|]>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 21.350.293>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.351.293><around*|[|a,b|]>=<big|cap><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
    R^n>]>>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>-a<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.352.293>\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|A,b+<frac|1|m>|]>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m+1>|[>> then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m+1>\<less\>b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>>
    so that <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>> hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.353.293>\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m+1>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>
    </equation>

    So the conditions for [theorem: <reference|measure measure space
    properties (3)>] are satisfied by [eqs: <reference|eq 21.352.293>,
    <reference|eq 21.353.293>] so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>|)>=<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>|)>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.351.293>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b+<frac|1|m>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>-a<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|limit of a finite product of
      sequences>]>>>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>-a<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  TODO

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Lebesgue measure of half-open open intervals>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with
    <math|a\<leqslant\>b> then <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|]|a,b|[>|)>>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on R^n rectangles>]
      <math|<around*|[|a,b|[>,<around*|]|a,b|[>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
      so that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>> and
      <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|]|a,b|[>|)>> are well
      defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<leqslant\>b>. If
    <math|x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>> then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    we have <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>>, using
    [theorem: <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there
    exist a <math|m<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m<rsub|i>>\<less\>x<rsub|i>>, take
    <math|m\<in\>max<around*|(|<around*|{|m<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>>
    then <math|a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m<rsub|i>>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>,b<rsub|i>|[>>.
    Hence <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
    which proves that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|]|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>>
    then <math|\<exists\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>>, hence
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|]|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|a,b|[>>
    we conclude that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.354.296><around*|]|a,b|[>=<big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>> then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m+1>\<less\>a<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m+1>,b|[>>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.355.296>\<forall\>m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m+1>,b|[>
    </equation>

    So the conditions for [theorem: <reference|measure measure space
    properties (3)>] are satisfied by [eqs: <reference|eq 21.354.296>,
    <reference|eq 21.355.296>] which gives us that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>|)>=<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>|)>
    </equation*>

    hence we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|]|a,b|[>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.354.296>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a+<frac|1|m>,b|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>-a<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|limit of a finite product of
      sequences>]>>>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|<below|lim|m\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>+<frac|1|m>-a<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Completeness of measures>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure null set><index|<math|\<mu\><text|-null
    set>>><index|<math|\<mu\><text|-negligible set>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> is
    <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<mu\>>-negligible> [sometimes also called
    <math|\<mu\>>-null] if there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> such that <math|B\<subseteq\>A>. The set
    of all <math|\<mu\>>-negligible sets is noted as
    <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<exists\>B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<varnothing\><text| such that
      >A\<subseteq\>B|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.298.246>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|I> a countable set [i.e. finite or
    denumerable] and <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    a countable family of sets such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I>
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0> then
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>=0>

    <\note>
      [theorem: <reference|measure countable union is in sigma algebra>]
      ensures that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so
      that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>> is
      well defined.
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|I> is countable we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|I<text| is finite>>>Then there exist a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection
      <math|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\>I>. Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        <math|by >>B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>><text|
        if >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\><text|
        if >i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
        >\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>> then there exist a
      <math|i\<in\>I> such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>. As <math|\<beta\>>
      is a bijection there exist a <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      such that <math|i=\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>> hence
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>=B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>
      </equation*>

      On the other hand if <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>>
      then <math|\<exists\>j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|j>>. We must have that
      <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> for if
      <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then
      <math|B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>, hence
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>>
      which proves that <math|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>>.
      Combining this result with the above gives

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Using the above we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i><rsub|>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves, as <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>>,
      that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>=0
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|I> is denumerable>Then there exist a bijection
      <math|\<beta\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\>I>. Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>
      </equation*>

      then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>> then there exist a
      <math|i\<in\>I> such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>, as <math|\<beta\>>
      is a bijection there exist a <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|i=\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>, hence
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>=B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>
      </equation*>

      On the other hand if <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>>
      then there exist a <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>I>A<rsub|j>>
      which proves that <math|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>I>A<rsub|j>>.
      Combining this with the above proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|j>
      </equation*>

      Using the above we have \ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves, as <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>>,
      that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>=0
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  We have the following properties for <math|\<mu\>>-negligible sets.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure null set properties>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable
    space then we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>

      <item>If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> then for every
      <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have <math|B\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>

      <item>If <math|I> is countable, <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
      a countable family of <math|\<mu\>>-negligible sets then we have that

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>\ 

        <item>If <math|I\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> then
        <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
      </enumerate>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>This is trivial as <math|\<varnothing\>\<subseteq\>\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>.

      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> and
      <math|B\<subseteq\>A>. As <math|A\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> there
      exists a <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|C|)>=0>
      such that <math|A\<subseteq\>C> which as <math|B\<subseteq\>A> proves
      that <math|B\<subseteq\>C> so that <math|B\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>.

      <item>Assume that <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
      then there exists a <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I>
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0> and
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>>. Hence
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>B<rsub|i>>
      and using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.298.246>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>B<rsub|i>|)>=0<rsup|>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>
      </equation*>

      Finally if <math|I\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> then there exist a
      <math|k\<in\>I> so that <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
      it follows from <math|<around*|(|2|)>> that
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure complete measure><dueto|Complete Measures>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|complete> if
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> we
    have <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> with
    <math|B\<subseteq\>A> that <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Every measure space
    with a complete measure is called a <with|font-series|bold|complete
    measure space>.
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure complete measure characterization>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><text| is complete >\<Leftrightarrow\><text|
      >\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
      then there exists a <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=0> such that <math|A\<subseteq\>B>. As
      <math|\<mu\>> is complete, <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=0> and <math|A\<subseteq\>B> it follows
      that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Hence
      <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|A>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> then for
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we
      have by definition of <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> that
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> hence, as
      <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>, it follows that
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> which proves that <math|\<mu\>> is complete.
    </description>
  </proof>

  We prove now that every measure space constructed via the Carathéodory is a
  complete measure space.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Caratheodory produces complete measure space>Let <math|X>
    be a set and <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>:\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|outer measure> then the measure space

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>,<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      [see theorem: <reference|measure Caratheodory>]>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|measure measurable sets>]>><around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<forall\>B\<in\>P<around*|(|X|)><text|
      we have >\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B\\A|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    is a complete measure space.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>>>
    then there exists a <math|B\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
    with <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>><around*|(|B|)>=0>
    such that <math|A\<subseteq\>B>. Hence by the definition of a outer
    measure [see definition: <reference|measure outer measure>] we have that
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|B|)>=0>
    which proves that <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|A|)>=0>. Applying
    then [theorem: <reference|measure measurable sets properties>] proves
    that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>. Hence
    <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>
    which by the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measure complete
    measure characterization>] proves that
    <math|><math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>,<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>|]>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a complete measure space.
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|measure Lebesgue measure spaces are complete>We have that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,<below|<wide|\<lambda\>|\<wide-bar\>>|>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a complete measure space>>.

      <item>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a complete measure space
    </enumerate>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>This follows from the previous theorem [theorem:
      <reference|measure Caratheodory produces complete measure space>] and
      the definition of <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,<wide|\<lambda\>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>>>
      [see theorem: <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on R>].

      <item>This follows from the previous theorem \ [theorem:
      <reference|measure Caratheodory produces complete measure space>] and
      the definition of <rigid|<math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>>>>
      [see theorem: <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on R^n>].
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Sometimes we have to deal with arbitrary subsets of sets with measure zero,
  it is then desirable that there exists a extension of measure space to a
  bigger measure space in which these sets are elements of the bigger
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra. To do this we introduce the concept of the
  <with|font-series|bold|completion> of a measure space.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure completion of a measure
    space><index|<math|\<cal-A\>\<mu\>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then the <with|font-series|bold|completion >of <math|\<cal-A\>>
    noted as <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>\|\<exists\>E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      such that >E\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>F<text| and
      >\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0|}>
    </equation*>

    elements of <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> are called
    <math|\<mu\>>-measurable sets.

    <\note>
      As by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>] a
      <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra is a algebra if follows from [theorem:
      <reference|measure algebra properties>] that
      <math|\<forall\>E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\>> <math|\<mu\>\\F\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so
      the above is well defined.
    </note>
  </definition>

  Next we define a set function of <math|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>:\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>,
  first we need a little lemma needed for the existence of such a function.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.286.226>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space <math|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> and
    <math|E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that <math|E\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>F>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| with
      >B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\><text| [so that
      >sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text| exists]>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|E\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|E\<subseteq\>A> it
    follows that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
    with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>> hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.374.226><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.375.226>\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation*>
      <text|>
    </equation*>

    As <math|E\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>F> we have <math|E\<subseteq\>F> so
    that by [theorem: <reference|class disjoint unions>]
    <math|F=<around*|(|F\\E|)><big|sqcup>E> hence
    <rigid|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>=0+\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>>>
    proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.376.226>\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>
    </equation>

    If <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> then
    <math|B\<subseteq\>F> so that by [theorem: <reference|measure measure
    space properties (1)>] <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>>
    hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>
    </equation*>

    which combining with [eq: <reference|eq 20.375.226>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure completing of a measure space>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then if we define\ 

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>:\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by ><wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    \ then we have

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>> <text|we have if
      >E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| satsifies >E\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>F<text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0<text| then
      >\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>,<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a measure space >
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=<around*|(|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-A\>>
    </equation*>

    In other words the measure space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>,<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a extension of the measure space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>.
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>,<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is called the <with|font-series|bold|completion of the measure space>
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>

    <\note>
      <math|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is welll defined because if
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> then there exists
      <math|E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|E\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>F> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0> so that by [lemma: <reference|lemma
      20.286.226>] <math|><math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> then if
    <math|E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\>> is such that <math|E\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>F>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0> it follows from [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.286.226>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.377.226>\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>=<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> contains
    <math|\<cal-A\>> and is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra. Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and take <math|E=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>\R
    <math|F=A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then <math|E=\<varnothing\>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>A=F>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>
    so that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>>. Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.378.226>\<cal-A\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> hence using the above it follows
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.379.226>\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> then there exists
    <math|E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that <math|E\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>F>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0>. As <math|\<cal-A\>> is a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra we have that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.380.226>X\\E,X\\F\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    further applying [theorem: <reference|class inclusion and union and
    intersection>] gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.381.226>X\\F\<subseteq\>X\\A\<subseteq\>X\\E
    </equation>

    Next\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|X\\E|)>\\<around*|(|X\\F|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X\\E\<wedge\>x\<nin\>X\\F>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>x\<nin\>E|)>\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>x\<nin\>F|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>x\<nin\>E|)>\<wedge\><around*|(|x\<nin\>X\<vee\>x\<in\>F|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>x\<nin\>E\<wedge\>x\<nin\>X|)>\<vee\><around*|(|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>x\<nin\>E\<wedge\>x\<in\>F|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>x\<nin\>E\<wedge\>x\<in\>F>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|F\<subseteq\>X>>|<cell|x\<nin\>E\<wedge\>x\<in\>F>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|F\\E>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that <math|<around*|(|X\\E|)>\\<around*|(|X\\F|)>=F\\E> hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.382.226>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\E|)>\\<around*|(|X\\F|)>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0
    </equation>

    From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.380.226>, <reference|eq 20.381.226> and
    <reference|eq 20.382.226>] it follows that
    <math|X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.383.226>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>><text| we
      have >X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
    then there exists <math|<around*|{|E<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|E<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|i>> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|i>\\E<rsub|i>|)>=0>. Then we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.384.226><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|i><text|
      and, as >\<cal-A\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra,
      ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>,<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|i>|)>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>|)>>
    then <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|i>> and
    <math|x\<nin\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>>, hence there
    exist a <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<in\>F<rsub|i>> and
    <math|\<forall\>j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|x\<nin\>E<rsub|j>> in particular
    <math|x\<nin\>E<rsub|i>>, so <math|x\<in\>F<rsub|i>\\E<rsub|i>>. So
    <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|i>|)>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>|)>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|F<rsub|i>\\E<rsub|i>|)>>
    where as <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra
    <math|F<rsub|i>\\E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.385.226>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|i>|)>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|F<rsub|i>\\E<rsub|i>|)>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (2)>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|i>\\E<rsub|i>|)>=0
    </equation>

    From [eq: <reference|eq 20.384.226>] and [eq: <reference|eq 20.385.226>]
    it follows that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.386.226>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>><text|
      we have ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>
    </equation>

    From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.379.226>, <reference|eq 20.383.226> and
    <reference|eq 20.386.226>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.387.226>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>><text| is a
      >\<sigma\><text|-algebra>
    </equation>

    Now as <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<subseteq\>\<varnothing\>\<subseteq\>\<varnothing\>>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>\\\<varnothing\>|)>=0> it
    follows from [eq: <reference|eq 20.377.226>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.388.226><wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    If additional <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
    is pairwise disjoint then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|E<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|i>> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|i>\\E<rsub|i>|)>=0>. Let
    <rigid|<math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then
    <math|<rigid|E<rsub|i><big|cap>E<rsub|j>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>>
    which proves that <math|<around*|{|E<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
    is pairwise disjoint. So

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.384.226>, <reference|eq 20.385.226>, <reference|eq
      20.377.226>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
      is a measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.377.226>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    The above together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.388.226>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.389.226><wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| is a measure so
      that ><around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>,<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a measure space>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then as <math|A\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>A>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>
    we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.377.226>] that
    <math|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>,
    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.390.226><around*|(|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)><rsub|\|A>=\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>>> then there
    exist a <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> with
    <math|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|B|)>=0> such that
    <math|A\<subseteq\>B>. As <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>> there
    exists <math|E,F\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|E\<subseteq\>B\<subseteq\>F>
    and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F\\E|)>=0>, hence by [eq: <reference|eq
    20.377.226>] <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|B|)>=0>.
    Next as <math|\<varnothing\>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>B\<subseteq\>F> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F\\\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=0> it
    follows that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>>. This proves that
    <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure complete measure
    characterization>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\<mu\>>,<wide|\<mu\>|\<wide-bar\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is complete>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <section|Measurable Functions>

  <subsection|Measurable function definition>

  We define now the functions that can be integrated.

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function><index|measurable
    function><index|<math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable><dueto|Measurable
    Function>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>> be two measurable
    spaces and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then a function\ 

    <\equation*>
      f:X\<rightarrow\>Y
    </equation*>

    is <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable> if
    <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> we have that
    <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. The set of
    <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable function graphs is noted as
    <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>Y<rsup|X>\|f:X\<rightarrow\>Y<text|
      is >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      A set can have many <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras defined on it, so the
      same function can at the same time be measurable and not measurable,
      depending on the <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras used.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\example>
    <label|measurable function constant function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>> be measurable spaces
    then <math|C<rsub|y>:X\<rightarrow\>Y> defined by
    <math|C<rsub|y><around*|(|x|)>=y> [the constant function] is
    <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable. In other words
    <math|C<rsub|y>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>>.
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then we have
    <math|C<rsub|y><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>> which proves
    that <math|C<rsub|y>> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function composition>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>>,<math|<around*|\<langle\>|Z,\<cal-C\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be measurable spaces, <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a
    <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable function and
    <math|g:Y\<rightarrow\>Z> a <math|\<cal-B\>,\<cal-C\>>-measurable
    function then\ 

    <\equation*>
      g\<circ\>f:X\<rightarrow\>Z
    </equation*>

    is a <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-C\>> measurable function.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|B\<in\>\<cal-C\>> then, as <math|g> is
    <math|\<cal-B\>,\<cal-C\>>-measurable,
    <math|g<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\>>. As <math|f> is
    <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable it follows that
    <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|g<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    So, as <math|<around*|(|g\<circ\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|partial function image preimage of
    compositions>]>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|g<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>> it
    follows that <rigid|<math|<around*|(|g\<circ\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>.
    Hence <math|g\<circ\>f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-C\>>-measurable.\ 
  </proof>

  The following theorem shows that a restriction measurable function is
  measurable.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function restriction (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>,<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be measurable spaces, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable
    function then <math|f<rsub|\|A>:A\<rightarrow\>Y> is
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable. In other words if
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>> then
    <rigid|<math|f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then, as <math|f> is
    <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable, we have that
    <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that
    <math|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\><around*|{|A<big|cap>B\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>=\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>.
    So <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|function restricted function
    properties>]>>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
    which proves that <math|f> is \ <rigid|<math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable>.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function restriction (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>> be measurable spaces,
    <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|A\<subseteq\>B> and
    <math|f:B\<rightarrow\>Y> a <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable
    function then <math|f<rsub|\|A>:A\<rightarrow\>Y> is a
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|A>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable function. In other words if
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>>
    then <math|f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f:B\<rightarrow\>Y> is <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function restriction
    (1)>] it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.415.264>f<rsub|\|A>:A\<rightarrow\>Y<text| is
      ><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>|)><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>
    </equation>

    Let <math|C\<in\><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>|)><rsub|\|A>\<equallim\><rsub|def><around*|{|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>\|C\<subseteq\>A|}>>
    then <math|C\<subseteq\>A> and <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>=<around*|{|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|C\<subseteq\>B|}>>
    so that <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|C\<subseteq\>A> which proves
    that <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>. Hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.416.264><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>|)><rsub|\|A>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>
    </equation>

    Let <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|C\<subseteq\>A|}>>
    then <math|C\<subseteq\>A> and <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, as
    <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we have <math|C\<subseteq\>B> so that
    <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>>, which as <math|C\<subseteq\>A> proves
    that <math|C\<in\><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|B>|)><rsub|\|A>>.\ 
  </proof>

  Next we show the measurability of a extension of a function

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function extension (1)>Let <math|X,Y> be sets,
    <math|A\<subseteq\>X>, <math|y\<in\>Y> and <math|f:A\<rightarrow\>Y> a
    function then we define\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>:X\<rightarrow\>Y<text| by
      >f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|0>X><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|y<text| if >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|f<rsub|X\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
      so that <math|f<rsub|X\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>=f>
    </note>

    <\note>
      Let <math|x\<in\>A> then <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>f<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>>
      so that <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsub|\|A>=f<rsub|\|A>>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function extension (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>> be measurable spaces,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f:A\<rightarrow\>Y> a function then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<text| is >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><text| is
      >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>
    </equation*>

    In other words we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>\<Leftrightarrow\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,Y,\<cal-B\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then we have
      either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|y\<in\>B>>Then for <math|t\<in\><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
        we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|t\<in\>A>>Then <math|f<around*|(|t|)>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|t|)>\<in\>B>
          so that <math|t\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>> so that
          <math|t\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\A|)>>

          <item*|<math|t\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|t\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\A|)>>
        </description>

        hence we have\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.415.260><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\A|)>
        </equation>

        Let <math|t\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\A|)>>
        then we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|t\<in\>A>>Then as <math|t\<nin\>X\\A> we have
          <math|t\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>> so that
          <math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|t|)>=f<around*|(|t|)>\<in\>B>
          which proves that <math|t\<in\><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>

          <item*|<math|t\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|><math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|t|)>=y\<in\>B>
          so that <math|t\<in\><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
        </description>

        Hence we have <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\A|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
        which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.415.260>] proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\A|)>
        </equation*>

        As <math|f<text| is >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>
        > <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and as
        <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have <math|X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that
        <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\A|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
        which proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|y\<in\>Y\\B>>Let <math|t\<in\><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>.
        If <math|t\<in\>X\\A> then we have
        <math|y=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|t|)>\<in\>B>
        contradicting <math|y\<in\>Y\\B> hence <math|t\<in\>A>. Further
        <math|f<around*|(|t|)>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|t|)>\<in\>B\<Rightarrow\>t\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
        which proves that

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.416.260><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>
        </equation>

        Let <math|t\<in\>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>> then
        <math|t\<in\>A> and <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<in\>B> so that
        <math|><math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><around*|(|t|)>\<equallim\><rsub|t\<in\>A>f<around*|(|t|)>\<in\>B>
        which proves that <math|t\<in\><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>.
        Hence <math|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
        which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.416.260>] proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>
        </equation*>

        As <math|f> <math|<text| is >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>>
        we have that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that
        <math|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> which
        proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      Hence <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> we have
      <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><text| is
        >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>As <math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X><text|
      is >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><text|>>-measurable it follows from using
      [theorem: <reference|measurable function restriction (1)>] that
      <math|f\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition: <reference|measurable
      function extension (1)>]>><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|y>X>|)><rsub|\|A>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable.
    </description>
  </proof>

  We have the following more specialized cases of measurable functions.

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function Borel>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|\<rangle\>>> be two
    topological spaces then a <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>-measurable
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>Y> function is called a
    <with|font-series|bold|Borel measurable> function.
  </definition>

  <\example>
    A <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
    function <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is a Borel
    measurable function.
  </example>

  <\example>
    A a <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
    funciton <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is a Borel
    measurable function.
  </example>

  For integration we are only interested in functions to
  <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> so we use some simplified notations
  for these functions.

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function (1)><index|<math|\<cal-A\><text|-measurable
    function>>><index|<math|<wide|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>|]>|\<wide-bar\>>>><index|<math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>|]>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then a\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|-measurable
      function >f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
    </equation*>

    is called a <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable> function.
    Further we define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\><rsup|>|\<bar\>><rsup|X>\|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  For non negative measurable functions we have the following definitions

  <\definition>
    <label|non negative measurable function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then we define

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    where <math|><math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
    <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    is defined in [definition: <reference|measurable function (1)>]

    <\note>
      As <math|<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      and <math|<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      we have also\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function real or extended>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable then
      <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
      [in other words \ <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
      and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable. In other
      words if <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on the extended real
      numbers (1)>] <math|B<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      hence, as <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable,
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      Further

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function function and intersection and
        union>]>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cap>X=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, which proves
      that <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable.

      <item>As <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> it follows that
      <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is a well defined function. Let
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>B\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      then, as <math|B<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>=B>, we have by [theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra on the extended real numbers (1)>]
      that <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      hence, as <math|<rigid|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>>
      is <rigid|<math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable>,
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> which proves that
      <math|f:A\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Next we define Lebesgue measurable functions.

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function (4)>A <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
    function <math|f:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is
    called a Lebesgue measurable function and the set of
    <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
    function is noted as <math|\<cal-L\>>. So

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\>=<around*|{|f\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><rsup|\<bbb-R\>>\|f:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is >\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|R|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|-measurable>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function (5)><index|Lebesgue measurable
    function><index|<math|\<cal-L\><rsup|n>>>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    a <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
    function <math|f:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    is called a Lebesgue measurable function and the set of
    <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
    function is noted as <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|n>>. So

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><rsup|n>=<around*|{|f\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><rsup|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>\|f:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is >\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|R|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|-measurable>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  We show now how we can extend a measurable extended real valued function to
  a measurable function.

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function extension definition><index|<math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>>>Let
    <math|X>,Y be sets, <math|A\<subseteq\>X> and <math|f:A\<rightarrow\>Y> a
    function then we define

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by >f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|0>X><text| [see
      definition: <reference|measurable function extension (1)>]>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A> we have
      <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>f<around*|(|x|)>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsub|\|A>=f>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function extension (3)>Let <math|X,Y> be sets,
    <math|A\<subseteq\>X>, <math|B\<subseteq\>A> and
    <math|f:A\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a function then we
    have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>B>>Then, as <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have that
      <math|x\<in\>A>. Hence

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>B>>|<cell|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>B>>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A\<uparrow\>X>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\B>>Then we have either\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<nin\>B>>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A\<uparrow\>X>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<nin\>B>>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A\<uparrow\>X>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function extension (4)>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|A\<subseteq\>X> and <math|f:A\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a function then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>.

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=0\<cdot\>0=0=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>.
    </description>

    proving that in all cases <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>
    from which it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function extension>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable
    spacea, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f:A\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a function then we have

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Leftrightarrow\><text|
      >f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows that <math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|>X>\<equallim\><rsub|def>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function extension (2)>]
      <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable,
      hence <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function extension (2)>]
      <math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\><rsup|0>X>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable,
      hence <math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  In the case of generated <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras there \ is a easier way
  to check if a function is measurable.

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function and generated sigma algebra>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|Y> a set, <math|\<cal-B\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|Y|)>> so
    that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a measurable space and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a function then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<text| is >\<cal-A\>,\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]><text|-measurable
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-B\><text| we have
      >f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then as
      <math|\<cal-B\>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]>> we have
      that <math|B\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]>>. Hence, as <math|f>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]>>-measurable, it
      follows that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>First we prove that
      <math|\<cal-H\>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|Y|)>\|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>
      is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra containing <math|\<cal-B\>>. Let
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then by the hypothesis
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that
      <math|\<cal-B\>\<in\>\<cal-H\>>, hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.413.227>\<cal-B\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-H\>
      </equation>

      As <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|Y|)>=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have also

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.414.227>Y\<in\>\<cal-H\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-H\>> then <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      so that <math|<rigid|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|Y\\B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|function properties (1)>><around*|(|4|]>>X\\f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>.
      From this it follows that <math|Y\\B\<in\>\<cal-H\>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.415.227>\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-H\><text| we have that
        >Y\\B\<in\>\<cal-H\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-H\>>
      then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. As
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family image and preimage>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>
      and <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows that
      <math|><math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      so that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-H\>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.416.227>\<forall\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-H\><text|
        we have that ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-H\>
      </equation>

      From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.413.227>, <reference|eq 20.414.227>,
      <reference|eq 20.415.227> and <reference|eq 20.416.227>] it follows
      that <math|\<cal-H\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra containing
      <math|\<cal-B\>> hence, using [theorem: <reference|measure generated
      sigma algebra>], it follows that <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-H\>>.
      So <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]>> we have
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-H\>> meaning that
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> which proves that
      <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-B\>|]>>-measurable.
    </description>
  </proof>

  Most books on mesure theory give a alternative definition for
  <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable functions based on the following definitions.

  <\definition>
    <label|measurable function comparisation definition>Let <math|X> be a set
    then we define

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>,<math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      then given <math|x\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> we define\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,x|[>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,x|]>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|x\<less\>f<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|x\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f=x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|x|}>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|x=f<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f=g|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>>,
      <math|g:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> then given <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we
      define

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,x|[>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,x|]>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|[>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|x\<less\>f<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|x,\<infty\>|[>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|x\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f=x|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|x|}>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|x=f<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f=g|}>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.309.227>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> and
    <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>>

      <item><math|X\\<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>=<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>>

      <item><math|X\\<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>=<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then if
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>> we have that
      <math|x\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>x-<frac|1|n>\<less\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<Rightarrow\>t\<in\><around*|{|x-<frac|1|n>\<less\>f|}>>
      proving\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.417.227><around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>
      </equation>

      If <math|t\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|x-<frac|1|n>\<less\>f<around*|(|t|)>>. Assume that
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x> then by [theorem: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|f<around*|(|t|)>+<frac|1|n>\<less\>x\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x-<frac|1|n>>
      a contradiction, so we must have <math|x\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|t|)>>
      or <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>>. So
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.417.227>] proves\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>
      </equation*>

      <item>

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X\\<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>>|<cell|=>|<cell|X\\f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function properties (1)>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|[|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,x|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then if
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>> we have that
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>>
      or <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>>. So\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.418.227><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>
      </equation>

      Let <math|t\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>>
      then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>>. Assume that
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x> then by [theorem: <reference|complex
      Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x+<frac|1|n>\<less\>f<around*|(|t|)>>
      a contradiction hence <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x> or
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>>. Combining this with [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.418.227>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>
      </equation*>

      <item>\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X\\<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>>|<cell|=>|<cell|X\\f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,x|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|-\<infty\>,x|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function properties (1)>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|[|-\<infty\>,x|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<less\>f|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to state the following equivalences for
  <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurability [which is used in many books about measure
  theory].

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function alternative (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable set
    and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a function then
    we have the following equivalences:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>f is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable [or equivalently
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>]

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      <math|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      <math|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      <math|<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      <math|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<Rightarrow\>2>>Take <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|]>\<in\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra on the extended real numbers
      (1)>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>> and we
      have, as <math|f> is <rigid|<math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable>, that
      <math|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item*|<math|2\<Rightarrow\>1>>Let <math|B\<in\><around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>
      then <math|B=<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|]>> where <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>.
      hence

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|x,\<infty\>|]>|)>=<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>.
      </equation*>

      So using [theorem: \ <reference|measurable function and generated sigma
      algebra>] together with <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<around*|]|a,\<infty\>|]>\|a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra on the extended real numbers
      (1)>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>> it follows
      that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
      or in other words <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.

      <item*|<math|2\<Rightarrow\>3>>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then using
      (2) we have <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
      <math|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, hence by
      [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>] it follows
      that <math|<rigid|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>.
      Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.309.227>]
      <rigid|<math|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x-<frac|1|n>|}>>>
      proving that <math|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

      <item*|<math|3\<Rightarrow\>4>>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.309.227>]>>X\\<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      [as <math|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>].\ 

      <item*|<math|4\<Rightarrow\>5>>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then by (4)
      we have that <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
      <math|<around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, hence by
      [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>] it follows
      that <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.309.227>]
      <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>><around*|{|f\<less\>x+<frac|1|n>|}>>>
      proving that <math|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

      <item*|<math|5\<Rightarrow\>2>>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.309.227>]>>X\\<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Measurable function properties>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function and continuity>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|\<rangle\>>> be topological
    spaces and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a continuous function then <math|f>
    is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>-measurable.
    In other words continuous functions are Borel measurable.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>> then as <math|f> is continuous we have
    <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|U|)>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|X>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function and generated sigma
    algebra>] we have that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>-measurable.
  </proof>

  Note that although the composition of measurable functions is measurable
  this is not the case for Lebesgue measurable functions. For example if
  <math|f:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> and
  <math|g:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> are Lebesgue
  measurable functions such that <math|f<around*|(|\<bbb-R\>|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
  then by [theorem: <reference|measurable function real or extended>]
  <math|f:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is
  <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
  and <math|g> is <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>\Umeasurable
  but not <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable,
  so we can not apply the composition theorem. If however <math|g> is
  <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
  then <math|g\<circ\>f> is by the previous theorem
  <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable
  hence Lebesgue measurable.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function comparisation>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and let <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> and
    <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> be
    <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable functions then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|f=g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|f\<neq\>g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item>If <math|x\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> then
      <math|<around*|{|f=x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>> then
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|t|)>>. Using the density of
      the rational numbers in the extended real numbers [see theorem:
      <reference|extended density>] there exist a <math|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>>
      such that <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<less\>g<around*|(|t|)>>, so
      that <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>q|}>> and
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>>, proving
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>>
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.419.227><around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>|)>
      </equation>

      If <math|t\<in\><big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>|)>>
      then there exist a <math|q\<in\>Q> such that
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>>,
      hence <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|t|)>>
      proving that <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>>. Hence
      <math|><math|<rigid|<big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.419.227>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>=<big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] we
      have that <rigid|<math|\<forall\>q\<in\>Q>
      <math|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}>,<around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>.
      As <math|\<bbb-Q\>> is denumerable [see theorem: <reference|complex
      embedding>] it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure countable
      union is in sigma algebra>] that <rigid|<math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>q|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      <item>We have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\><around*|(|X\\<around*|{|g\<less\>f|}>|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|t|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|t|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|X\\<around*|{|g\<less\>f|}>=<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>>.
      Using <math|<around*|(|1|)>> we have
      <math|<around*|{|g\<less\>f|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> hence
      <math|X\\<around*|{|g\<less\>f|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> which proves that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      <item>We have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>\\<around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>\<wedge\>t\<nin\><around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|t|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|t|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>f<around*|(|t|)>=g<around*|(|t|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f=g|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|<around*|{|f=g|}>=<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>g|}>\\<around*|{|f\<gtr\>g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      [using (1), (2) and [theorems: <reference|measure algebra equavalent
      definition>, <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>]

      <item>We have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\>X\\<around*|{|f=g|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>\<neg\><around*|(|f<around*|(|t|)>=g<around*|(|t|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|t|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<neq\>g|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|<around*|{|f\<neq\>g|}>=X\\<around*|{|f=g|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      [using (3)]

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> then as
      <math|<around*|{|x|}>> is closed [see theorem: <reference|extended
      closed sets>], we have by <reference|measure Borel algebra and
      closure>] that <math|<around*|{|x|}>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      which as <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable proves that
      <math|<around*|{|f=x|}>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|x|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  If the sum of two mesurable functions is well defined then the sum is also
  measurable.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function sum (finite)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f+g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| defined by
      ><around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    is well defined then

    <\equation*>
      f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      If <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> then
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>\<wedge\><around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>

      so that in this case we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>

    <\note>
      If <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>\<times\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>\<subseteq\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>\<wedge\><around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
      </equation*>

      so that in this case we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<forall\>t\<in\>X> we have <math|<around*|(|f<around*|(|t|)>,g<around*|(|t|)>|)>\<in\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
    it follows that <math|f+g> is well defined. Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>.
    If <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f+g\<less\>x|}>> we have
    <math|f<around*|(|t|)>+g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x> so that by [theorem:
    <reference|extended x+y\<less\>z=\<gtr\>x\<less\>z-y>
    <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x-g<around*|(|t|)>>. Using the density of
    <math|\<bbb-Q\>> in <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> [see theorem:
    <reference|extended density>] there exists a <math|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>> such
    that <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<less\>x-g<around*|(|t|)>>, hence
    <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<wedge\>q\<less\>x-g<around*|(|t|)><below|\<Rightarrow\>|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|extended x\<less\>y-z=\<gtr\>z\<less\>y-x>]>>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x-q>
    which proves

    <\equation*>
      ft\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.421.227><around*|{|f+g\<less\>x|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>|)>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inclusion. Let <math|t\<in\><big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>|)>>
    then there exist a <math|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>> such that
    <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>q|}>\<wedge\>t\<in\><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>>
    from which it follows that <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x-q>.
    As <math|<rigid|<around*|(|f<around*|(|t|)>,g<around*|(|t|)>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>>
    and <math|><math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x-q\<less\>\<infty\>>
    we have the following possible cases for
    <math|f<around*|(|t|)>,g<around*|(|t|)>>:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|t|)>=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>=-\<infty\>>>Then
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>+g<around*|(|t|)>=-\<infty\>\<less\>x>.

      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>=-\<infty\>>>Then
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>+g<around*|(|t|)>=-\<infty\>\<less\>x>.

      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|t|)>=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
      <math|f<around*|(|t|)>+g<around*|(|t|)>=-\<infty\>\<less\>x>.

      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
      as <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x-q>
      we have <math|f<around*|(|t|)>+g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>q+<around*|(|x-q|)>=x>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have <math|f<around*|(|t|)>+g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>
    from which it follows that <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f+g\<less\>x|}>>.
    Hence we have <math|<big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|{|f+g\<less\>x|}>>.
    Combining this \ [eq: <reference|eq 20.421.227>] results in\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|f+g\<less\>x|}>=<big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>> then as <math|f,g> are
    <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable
    function alternative (1)>] that <rigid|<math|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}>,<around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
    from which we conclude that ><around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>.
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure countable union is in sigma algebra>]
    and the fact that <math|\<bbb-Q\>> is countable [see theorem:
    <reference|complex embedding>] it follows that
    <rigid|<math|<big|cup><rsub|q\<in\>\<bbb-Q\>><around*|(|<around*|{|f\<less\>q|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x-q|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>
    hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|f+g\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    As <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> was choosen arbitrary it follows from the
    above and [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] that
    <math|f+g> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.
  </proof>

  Next we extend the above two theorems to finite sums of
  <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable functions.

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function sum finite>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| and ><rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    such <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have either
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|\<infty\>\<circ\>|}>>
    or <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>>
    so that function\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by ><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    is well defined then

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We use induction to prove this, so define

    <\equation*>
      S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      then ><rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>
      which proves that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      hence <math|1\<in\>S>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|f<rsub|n+1>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.422.228><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|n+1>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      As <math|n\<in\>S> we have <math|><math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      which, as <math|f<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      proves, using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>],
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|n+1>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.422.228>] proves that
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that <math|n+1\<in\>S>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  Having covered the case of sums of measurable functions we consider now the
  multiplication of a function by a scalar.

  \;

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function product with scalar>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then <math|\<alpha\><rigid|\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    where <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f> is defined pointwise by
    \ <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
    by ><around*|(|\<alpha\>*\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>.\ 

    <\note>
      If additional <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      so that <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>,
      hence <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      In other words if <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have 3 cases to consider.

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<alpha\>=0>>Then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=0\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=0>
      so that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f=C<rsub|0>>, hence using [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function constant function>] it follows that
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item*|<math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>>>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<less\>x|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\><frac|x|\<alpha\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\><frac|x|\<alpha\>>|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<less\>x|}>=<around*|{|f\<less\><frac|x|\<alpha\>>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      [using theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]].
      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] again
      we have then that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.

      <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<less\>x|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f*<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\><frac|x|\<alpha\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<gtr\><frac|x|\<alpha\>>|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<less\>x|}>=<around*|{|f\<gtr\><frac|x|\<alpha\>>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      [using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]].
      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] again
      we have then that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.
    </description>

    so in all cases we have that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f> is
    <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable, hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function properties (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      is such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,-g<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<otimes\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>
      </equation*>

      so that the following function is well defined\ 

      <\equation*>
        f-g:A\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| is defined by
        ><around*|(|f-g|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|f+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      then\ 

      <\equation*>
        f-g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|2>:A\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| defined
        by >f<rsup|2><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>
      </equation*>

      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. In other words
      <math|f<rsup|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      where

      <\equation*>
        f\<cdot\>g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| defined
        by ><around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then we have that

      <\equation*>
        D=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      and for\ 

      <\equation*>
        <frac|f|g>:D\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| defined
        by ><frac|f|g><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|f<around*|(|x|)>|g<around*|(|x|)>>
      </equation*>

      we have that <math|<frac|f|g>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable function product with
      scalar>] that <math|<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)> it
      follows that\ 

      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>+<around*|(|<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>-g<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|f-g|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      we have that <math|f+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g=f-g>. Using [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function product with scalar>]

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g\<in\>f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|X|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      Hence, using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>]
      <math|f-g=f+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<leqslant\>0>>If <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f<rsup|2>\<less\>x|}>>
        we have <math|0\<less\><around*|(|f<around*|(|t|)>|)><rsup|2>=f<rsup|2><around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<leqslant\>0>
        leading to the contradiction <math|0\<less\>0>. Hence
        <math|<around*|{|f<rsup|2>\<less\>x|}>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

        <item*|<math|0\<less\>x>>Then

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f<rsup|2>\<less\>x|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsup|2><around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|complex square root properties
          (1)>]>>>|<cell|-<sqrt|x>\<less\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\><sqrt|x>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<gtr\>-<sqrt|x>|}>\<wedge\>t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\><sqrt|x>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<gtr\>-<sqrt|x>|}><big|cap><around*|{|f\<less\><sqrt|x>|}>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        proving that <math|<around*|{|f<rsup|2>\<less\>x|}>=<around*|{|f\<gtr\>-<sqrt|x>|}><big|cap><around*|{|f\<less\><sqrt|x>|}>>.
        As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative
        (1)>] that <math|><math|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>-<sqrt|x>|}>,<around*|{|f\<less\><sqrt|x>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|{|f\<gtr\>-<sqrt|x>|}><big|cap><around*|{|f\<less\><sqrt|x>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
        hence <math|<around*|{|f<rsup|2>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      </description>

      As in all cases <math|<around*|{|f<rsup|2>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]
      that <math|f<rsup|2>> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] that
      <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>. Applying
      (2) it follows that <math|f<rsup|2>>,<math|g<rsup|2>>,<math|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Using (1) twice we have that <math|<rigid|<around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|2>-f<rsup|2>|)>-g<rsup|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function product with
      scalar>]

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.424.228><frac|1|2>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|2>-f<rsup|2>|)>-g<rsup|2>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Finally if <math|x\<in\>X> then

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<frac|1|2>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|2>-f<rsup|2>-g<rsup|2>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|2>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|)><rsup|2>-f<rsup|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>-g<rsup|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|2>\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rsup|2><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|2><around*|(|x|)>+2\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|2><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsup|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that that <math|f\<cdot\>g=<frac|1|2>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|2>-f<rsup|2>|)>-g<rsup|2>|)>>
      and by [eq: <reference|eq 20.424.228>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|D=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>
      then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\>D>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>X\<wedge\><around*|(|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0\<vee\>0\<less\>g<around*|(|t|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|)>\<vee\><around*|(|t\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>0\<less\>g<around*|(|t|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|g\<less\>0|}>\<vee\>t\<in\><around*|{|g\<gtr\>0|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|g\<less\>0|}><big|cup><around*|{|g\<gtr\>0|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that <math|D=<around*|{|g\<less\>0|}><big|cup><around*|{|g\<gtr\>0|}>>.
      As <math|g> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable we have by [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] that
      <math|<around*|{|g\<less\>0|}>,<around*|{|g\<gtr\>0|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.245.228>D=<around*|{|x\<in\>A\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>=<around*|{|g\<less\>0|}><big|cup><around*|{|g\<gtr\>0|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>.\ 

      <\equation*>
        t\<in\><around*|(|<math|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      then we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>Then
        <math|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0\<wedge\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>>,
        so that <math|t\<in\>X\<wedge\><frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x>,
        proving as <math|g<around*|(|t|)>\<neq\>0>, that
        <math|t\<in\><around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>>then
        <math|t\<in\>X\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0\<wedge\>f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>>
        so that <math|t\<in\>X\<wedge\><frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x>
        proving as <math|g<around*|(|t|)>\<neq\>0>, that
        \ <math|t\<in\><around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>>.
      </description>

      So we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.426.228><around*|(|<math|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>
      </equation>

      For the opposite inclusion, let <math|t\<in\><around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>>
      then <math|g<around*|(|t|)>\<neq\>0> and
      <math|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x> so we must
      consider the following cases for <math|g<around*|(|t|)>\<neq\>0>:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0>>then
        <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>> or
        <math|t\<in\><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>

        <item*|<math|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0>>then
        <math|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>> or
        <math|t\<in\><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>
      </description>

      proving that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>\<subseteq\><around*|(|<math|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>|)><big|cup><rigid|<around*|(|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>A\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>|)>>
      </equation*>

      So combining this with <reference|eq 20.426.228> proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 18.325.036.7><around*|(|<math|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>|)>=<around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>
      </equation>

      Now as <math|g> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable it follows from
      <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] that
      <math|<around*|{|g\<gtr\>0|}>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>0|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      and

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|g\<less\>0|}>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>0|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
        .
      </equation*>

      As <math|f,g> are <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable and <math|x\<cdot\>g> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable [using theorem: <reference|measurable
      function product with scalar>] it follows from <reference|measurable
      function comparisation>] that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g|}>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <rigid|<around*|{|f\<less\>x\<cdot\>g|}>=<around*|{|t\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<cdot\>g<around*|(|t|)>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      </equation*>

      Using the above in [eq: <reference|eq 18.325.036.7>] proves then that
      <math|<around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      and as <math|<rigid|<around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>\<subseteq\>D>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|{|t\<in\>D\|<frac|f<around*|(|t|)>|g<around*|(|t|)>>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D>>
      Finally using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative
      (1)>] we get finally that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <frac|f|g>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function min and max>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f\<vee\>g:X\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> defined
      by <math|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>
      is a <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable function. In other words
      <math|f\<vee\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item><math|f\<wedge\>g:X\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>defined
      by <math|<around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>
      is a <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable function. In other words
      <math|f\<wedge\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)>\<less\>x|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)><around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|max<around*|(|f<around*|(|t|)>,g<around*|(|t|)>|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>\<wedge\>t\<in\><around*|{|g\<less\>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\>x|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)>\<less\>x|}>=<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<less\>x|}>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] and
      the fact that <math|f,g> are <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable it follows
      that <math|<around*|{|f\<less\>x|}>,<around*|{|g\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      from which it follows, using the above, that
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      Applying [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]
      again proves that <math|f\<vee\>g> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>\<gtr\>x|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)><around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|min<around*|(|f<around*|(|t|)>,g<around*|(|t|)>|)>\<gtr\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<wedge\>g<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>\<vee\>t\<in\><around*|{|g\<gtr\>x|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>x|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)>\<gtr\>x|}>=<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}><big|cap><around*|{|g\<gtr\>x|}>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] and
      the fact that <math|f,g> are <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable it follows
      that <math|<around*|{|f\<gtr\>x|}>,<around*|{|g\<gtr\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      from which it follows, using the above, that
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)>\<gtr\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      Applying [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]
      again proves that <math|f\<gtr\>g> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Using mathematical induction we can extend the above to a finite family of
  <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable functions.\ 

  <\corollary>
    <label|measurable function min and max (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    then

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<big|vee><rsub|i=1<rsup|>><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>:A\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by <math|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurble. In other words
      <math|><math|<big|vee><rsub|i=1<rsup|>><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item><math|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>:A\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by <math|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. In other words
      <math|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>.>
    </enumerate>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    We use recursion to prove this.\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let\ 

      <\equation*>
        S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
        ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        then ><big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>
      </equation*>

      then we have:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        then <math|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>
        so that <math|<rigid|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>,
        hence <math|1\<in\>S>

        <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Let
        <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[corollary:
          <reference|order max min S plus
          element>]>>>|<cell|max<around*|(|max<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<vee\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        \ <math|> so that

        <\equation*>
          <big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<vee\>f<rsub|n+1>
        </equation*>

        As <math|n\<in\>S> we have that <math|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
        hence, using [theorem: <reference|measurable function min and max>]
        it follows that <math|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
        proving <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
      </description>

      <item>Let\ 

      <\equation*>
        S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
        ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        then ><big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>
      </equation*>

      then we have:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        then <math|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>
        so that <math|<rigid|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>,
        hence <math|1\<in\>S>

        <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Let
        <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|min<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[corollary:
          <reference|order max min S plus
          element>]>>>|<cell|min<around*|(|min<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|min<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<wedge\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        \ <math|> so that

        <\equation*>
          <big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<wedge\>f<rsub|n+1>
        </equation*>

        As <math|n\<in\>S> we have that <math|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
        hence, using [theorem: <reference|measurable function min and max>]
        it follows that <math|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
        proving <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We now look at the measurability of the
  <math|sup,inf,<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>sup> and
  <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>inf> and limit of a sequence of
  measurable functions.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function sup inf limit>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    a sequence of <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable functions then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The function\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|sup<rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\><text|>|\<wide-bar\>>
        <text|defined by ><around*|(|<below|sup|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>|>
      </equation*>

      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. In other words
      <math|<below|sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item>The function\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|inf<rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\><text|>|\<wide-bar\>>
        <text|defined by ><around*|(|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>|>
      </equation*>

      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. In other words
      <math|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item>The function

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        defined by ><around*|(|<below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
        f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. In other words <math|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item>The function

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        defined by ><around*|(|<below|lim sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
        f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. In other words <math|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item>Let <math|D=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>> [this is
      the set where by [definition: <reference|extended limit>]
      <math|<below|limit|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>
      exist so <math|D=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist>|}>>] then\ 

      <\equation*>
        D\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      and for the function\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:D\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        defined by ><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      we have <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      <math|>

      <item>If <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> that
      <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>
      then we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        defined by ><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      is well defined and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=<below|sup|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. If
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|sup|k\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>>
      then <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>x>.
      So <math|<rigid|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>
      we have <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x\<Rightarrow\>t\<in\><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.428.228><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|sup|k\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>
      </equation>

      If <math|t\<in\><big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>>
      then we have <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      that <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<Rightarrow\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x>.
      So <math|x> is a upper bound for <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>>
      from which it follows that <math|<rigid|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>x>>
      or <math|t\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|sup|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>>
      proving that <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<subseteq\>><math|<around*|{|<around*|(|<below|sup|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.428.228>] gives\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.429.228><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|sup|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>=<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,n|}>> <math|f<rsub|n>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable it follows from [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] that
      <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Using
      [theorem: <reference|measure sigma algebra equivalences>] we have that
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      or, using [eq: <reference|eq 20.429.228>], that
      <math|><math|<around*|{|<around*|(|<below|sup|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      Applying then [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative
      (1)>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|sup|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n> is
        \<cal-A\><text|-measurable>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. If
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>x|}>>
      then <math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>\<less\>x>.
      So <math|<rigid|\<exists\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>
      such that <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x\<Rightarrow\>t\<in\><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.430.228><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>x|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>
      </equation>

      If <math|t\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>>
      then <math|\<exists\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> such
      that <math|t\<in\><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>\<Rightarrow\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>,
      hence <math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|t|)>\<less\>x>,
      proving that <math|t\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>x|}>>.
      So <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>x|}>>
      which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.430.228>] gives

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.431.228><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>x|}>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,n|}>> <math|f<rsub|n>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable it follows from [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] that
      <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, applying then
      [definition: <reference|measure sigma algebra>] proves that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|{|f<rsub|n>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      or using [eq: <reference|eq 20.431.228>] that
      <math|><math|<around*|{|<around*|(|<below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      Using then [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|inf|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n> is
        \<cal-A\><text|-measurable>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>A> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<below|lim
        inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim
        inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|extended lim inf and lim
        sup>]>>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>\|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|<below|inf|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|sup|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<around*|(|<below|inf|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<below|sup|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<below|inf|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.432.228><below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
        f<rsub|n>=<below|sup|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<below|inf|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Now <math|\<forall\>l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> we have
      by (2) that <math|<below|inf|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. So applying (1) it follows that
      <math|><math|<below|sup|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<below|inf|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. Hence using [eq: <reference|eq
      20.432.228>] it follows that <math|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>A> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<below|lim
        sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim
        sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|extended lim inf and lim
        sup>]>>>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>\|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|<below|sup|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|inf|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<around*|(|<below|sup|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<below|inf|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<below|sup|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.433.228><below|lim sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
        f<rsub|n>=<below|inf|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<below|sup|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Now <math|\<forall\>l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> we have
      by (1) that <math|<below|sup|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. So applying (2) it follows that
      <math|><math|<below|inf|l\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>><around*|(|<below|sup|i\<in\><around*|{|l,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|i>|)>>
      is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. Hence using [eq: <reference|eq
      20.432.228>] it follows that <math|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.

      <item>From (3),(4) it follows that <math|><math|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>> and <math|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>> are
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable, combining this fact with [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function comparisation>], proves that
      <math|<around*|{|<below|lim sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. As
      <math|<rigid|D=<around*|{|x\<in\>A\|<around*|(|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>=<around*|{|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|}>>> it follows that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.434.228>D\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\>D> then by definition <math|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>> so that by
      [definition: <reference|extended limit>]
      <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>
      exist and <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>. This allows us
      to define the function

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.435.228><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:D\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        where ><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim
        sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim
        sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> then

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|t\<in\>D<big|cap><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|lim
        sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\>D\<wedge\><around*|(|<below|lim
        sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.435.228>]>>>|<cell|t\<in\>D\<wedge\><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|t|)>\<leqslant\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|t\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that <math|><math|<around*|{|<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>=D<big|cap><around*|{|<around*|(|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D>>
      [as <math|D\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>].
      Finally using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative
      (1)>] it follows that

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:D\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
        is \<cal-A\><rsub|\|D><text|-measurable>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then as <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
      sequence exist>] that

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        where ><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      is a well defined function and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=sup<rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>
      </equation*>

      Finally by (1) we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=sup<rsub|n\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function limit>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    a sequence of <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable functions then for

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\<infty\>>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      definied by >f<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>D>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>X\\D>>>>>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      D=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist>|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    we have that

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      or in other words >f<rsub|\<infty\>><text| is
      >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup
    inf limit>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      D\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and for the function
      ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:D\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by ><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    we have <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    Further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><rsub|D\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>D>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\D>>>>>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>D>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\D>>>>>=f<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|f<rsub|\<infty\>>=<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><rsub|D\<uparrow\>X>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function extension>]

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  A very usefull <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable function is the Charasteristic
  function.

  <\definition>
    <label|charasteristic function>Let <math|X> be a set,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>> then the Charasteristic function
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>> is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      where >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1<text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>
    </equation*>

    If <math|X> is understood from the context then we use the notation
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>> instead of <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function charasteristic properties>Let <math|X> be a
    set and <math|A\<subseteq\>X>, <math|B\<subseteq\>X> then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<big|cap>B>>
      [where <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>> is
      poinwise defined]

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is a
      function then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|B>=f<rsub|\|B>>
      [where <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f> is poinwise defined]

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> is a
      function then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A>=\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A>>
      [where <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f> is poinwise defined]
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>A<big|cap>B>>Then
        <math|x\<in\>A\<wedge\>x\<in\>B> so that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B><around*|(|x|)>=1*\<cdot\>1=1=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<big|cap>B><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\<around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>>>Then
        <math|x\<nin\>A\<vee\>x\<in\>B> so that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B><around*|(|x|)>=0-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<big|cap>B><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      which proves that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<big|cap>B>>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>B> then we have
      <math|><math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|B><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|\|B><around*|(|x|)>>
      which proves that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|B>=f<rsub|\|B>>.

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>A> then we have for <math|x> either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>B>>Then

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A>|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<nin\>B>>Then\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=0=\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A>|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      \ so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A>=\<cal-X\><rsub|A,B>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function charasteristic>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have three possibilities:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<leqslant\>x>>If <math|t\<in\><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>>
      then <math|1\<leqslant\>x\<less\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|t|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1<text|
      if >t\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >t\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<leqslant\>1>
      leading to the contradiction <math|1\<less\>1> hence we have
      <math|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>x\<less\>1>>Let
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>> then
      <math|0\<leqslant\>x\<less\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|t|)>>. If
      <math|t\<nin\>A> then <math|0\<leqslant\>x\<less\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|t|)>=0>
      laeding to the contradiction <math|0\<less\>0> hence we must have that
      <math|t\<in\>A> which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>\<subseteq\>A
      </equation*>

      If <math|t\<in\>A> then <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|t|)>=1\<gtr\>x>
      so that <math|t\<in\><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>> which
      proves that <math|A\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>>.
      Combining this with the above gives
      <math|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>=A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

      <item*|<math|x\<less\>0>>If <math|t\<in\>X> then we have
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|t|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1<text|
      if >t\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >t\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<geqslant\>0\<gtr\>x> so that
      <math|X\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>\<subseteq\>X>,
      hence <math|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>.>
    </description>

    As in all cases <math|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<gtr\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative
    (1)>] that

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>.
    </equation*>

    Further, as <math|\<forall\>t\<in\>X>
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1\<geqslant\>0<text|
    if >t\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0\<geqslant\>0<text| if
    >t\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<geqslant\>0>, it follows that
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    so that we have also\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.349.249>Let <math|X>, <math|A\<subseteq\>X> be a set and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a function then for
    <math|B\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|0\<nin\>B> then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>

      <item>If <math|0\<in\>B> then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
      then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>.
      Assume that <math|x\<in\>X\\A> then we would have
      <math|0=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<cal-A\>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>
      contradicting the hypothese <math|0\<nin\>B> so we must have that
      <math|x\<in\>A>. Then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>
      proving that <math|<rigid|x\<in\>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.440.249><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>> then
      <math|x\<in\>A> and <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B> so that
      <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>.
      So <math|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
      which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.440.249> proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
      then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>,
      consider the following possible cases for <math|x>:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>
        proving that

        <\equation*>
          x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-><around*|(|B|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then clearly
        <math|x\<in\><around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
      </description>

      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.441.249><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation>

      On the other hand if <math|x\<in\><around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
      then we have either\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=0\<in\>B>
        proving <math|x\<in\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
        so that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>
        proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>.
      </description>

      Hence <math|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>
      which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.441.249>] gives

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|measurable function charasteristic product>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a function then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    </enumerate>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then we have for <math|B> either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<nin\>B>>Then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.349.249>]
        <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>,
        further <math|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> [as
        <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
        because <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable], so that
        <math|><math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

        <item*|<math|0\<in\>B>>Then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.349.249>]
        <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>,
        further <math|<rigid|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>
        [as <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<Rightarrow\>X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
        <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> because <math|f> is
        <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable], so that
        <math|><math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      </description>

      So <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have that
      <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      which proves that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable, hence <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<around*|(|1|)>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>. Further
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so that
      <math|><math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      which proves that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<around*|(|1|)>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>.
      Further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> so that
      <math|><math|<rigid|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>>
      which proves that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<around*|(|1|)>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>.
      Further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> so that
      <math|><math|<rigid|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>>
      which proves that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable function in sub space>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|<around*|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|\<rangle\>>>
    the measurable space defined by <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|C\<subseteq\>A|}>>
    [see theorem: <reference|measure sub measure space>] and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a function then we
    have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Leftrightarrow\>f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then as <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.442.249><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      For <math|B> we must consider the following cases:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<in\>B>>Then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.349.249>]\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.443.249><around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><below|\<in\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.442.249>]>>\<cal-A\>
        </equation>

        Further\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|A<big|cap><around*|(|X\\A|)>|)><big|cup><around*|(|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varnothing\><big|cup><around*|(|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|function restricted function
          properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        As <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and by [eq: <reference|eq 20.443.249>]
        <math|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
        it follows that <math|A<big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
        so that by the above <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

        <item*|<math|0\<nin\>B>>Then <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function restricted function
        properties>]>>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.349.249>]>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><below|\<in\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.442.249>]>>\<cal-A\>> proving that
        <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
      </description>

      So <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
      hence as <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|function restricted function
      properties>]>>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\>A> it
      follows that <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>.
      Which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then, as by the hypothesis <math|f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have that <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
      so that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.444.249>A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function restricted function
        properties>]>><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<divides\>A>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      Further\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup><around*|(|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>A|)><big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|X<big|cap><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|A<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      [see eq: <reference|eq 20.444.249>[ and
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<Rightarrow\>X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> it follows
      that <math|<rigid|<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup><around*|(|A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>
      so that by the above\ 

      <\equation>
        <around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      Now for <math|B> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<in\>B>>Then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.349.249>]
        <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|X\\A|)><big|cup>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

        <item*|<math|0\<nin\>B>>Then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.349.249>]
        <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=A<big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><below|\<in\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.444.249>]>>\<cal-A\>>
      </description>

      So <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|measurable function in sub space (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    As <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>] that
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    so that by the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measurable function
    in sub space>] <math|f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
  </proof>

  <\subsection>
    Almost everywhere properties
  </subsection>

  Using the concept of <math|\<mu\>>-negligible sets [see definition:
  <reference|measure null set>] we can introduce the term
  <with|font-series|bold|almost everywhere>. A statement about elements in a
  set is said to be almost everywhere true if it is true for every element in
  the set except for a <math|\<mu\>>-negligible subset. The idea is that we
  can ignore the <math|\<mu\>>-negligible part because because it will not
  influence the final result.

  <\definition>
    <label|almost everywhere><index|<math|\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|P<around*|(|x|)>> a predicat then <math|P<around*|(|x|)>>
    is <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<mu\>>-a.e. true> if
    <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<neg\>P<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>.
    In other words using the definition of <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> [see
    definition: <reference|measure null set>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      P<around*|(|x|)><text| is >\<mu\><text|-a.e.> true<text| or
      >P<around*|(|x|)><text| holds >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>

    if there exists a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<neg\>P<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Using the above we have the following examples that will be used in this
  text.

  <\definition>
    <label|almost everywhere functions>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then we define:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> and
      <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> are functions
      then <with|font-series|bold|<math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.> if there
      exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0>
      such that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> and
      <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> are functions
      then <with|font-series|bold|<math|f\<leqslant\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.>
      if there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
      <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> and
      <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> are functions
      then <with|font-series|bold|<math|f\<less\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.> if
      there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
      <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> then
      <with|font-series|bold|<math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.> if there exist a
      <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
      <math|<rigid|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>>

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><rsup|X>>
      be a sequence of functions and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      <math|> then <with|font-series|bold|<math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=f>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.> if there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that

      <\equation*>
        X\\<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
        exist><infix-and><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        \;
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  One way that almost everywhere equality is used is in masking undesirable
  points from the domain of a function using a charasteristic function. For
  this the following theorem is usefull.

  <\theorem>
    <label|almost everywhere and characteristic>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> [in other words there exist a
    <math|N\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
    <math|A\<subseteq\>N>] and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    then for <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f=f<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
    then <math|x\<in\>X> and <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f*<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>>.
    Assume that <math|x\<nin\>A> then <math|x\<in\>X\\A> so that
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
    which is a contradiction. Hence the assumption is false and we must have
    that <math|x\<in\>A>, which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>

    So by definition\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>f=f<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|almost everywhere transitivity>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space together with functions\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|h:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|k:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and <math|g=h>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then <math|f=h> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.

      <item>If <math|f\<leqslant\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and
      <math|g\<leqslant\>h> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then <math|f\<leqslant\>h>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.

      <item>If <math|f\<leqslant\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and
      <math|g\<leqslant\>f> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then <math|f=g>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.

      <item>If <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\\<around*|{|0|}>> then if
      <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. it follows that
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.

      <item>If <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>,<around*|(|h<around*|(|x|)>,k<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>,<around*|{|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>>
      [in other words <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>> and
      <math|h<around*|(|x|)>+k<around*|(|x|)>> is well defined], <math|f=h>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and <math|g=k> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then
      <math|f+g=h+k> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and <math|g=h>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exist <math|N<rsub|1>,N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>
      such that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1>>
      and <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>>.
      Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      then <math|f*<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<vee\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>>
      [for if <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>=h<around*|(|x|)>>
      then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=h<around*|(|x|)>>] so that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      proving \ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      which, as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0>,
      proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=h<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<leqslant\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and
      <math|g\<leqslant\>h> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exist
      <math|N<rsub|1>,N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>
      such that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1>>
      and <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>>.
      Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      then we have <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<vee\>h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      [for if <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>h<around*|(|x|)>>
      then we have <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>h<around*|(|x|)>>
      contradicting <math|h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>] so that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      proving\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      which, as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0>,
      proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<leqslant\>h<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<leqslant\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and
      <math|g\<leqslant\>f> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exist
      <math|N<rsub|1>,N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>
      such that <math|<rigid|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1>>>
      and <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>>.
      Let <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>
      then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>> hence
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<vee\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
      so that <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      which as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0>,
      proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exists a
      <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
      <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
      Let now <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      then <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|\<alpha\>\<neq\>0>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      proving that <math|><math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
      So

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<neq\>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f=h> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and <math|g=k>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. <math|>there exists
      <math|N<rsub|1>,N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>
      such that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1>>
      and <rigid|<math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>k<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>>>.
      Let <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>+k<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>
      then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>\<vee\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>k<around*|(|x|)>>
      [for if <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=h<around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>=k<around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>=h<around*|(|x|)>+k<around*|(|x|)>>]
      so that <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>k<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>h<around*|(|x|)>+k<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0>
      it follows that <math|f+g=h+k> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|almost everywhere f=g \<less\>=\<gtr\> f-g=0>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> with
    <math|<rigid|f<around*|(|X|)>,g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e. >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >f-g=0<text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>,g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> is needed
      to ensure that <math|g-f> is well defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Note that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X\<wedge\><around*|(|f-g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|f-g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    proving that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|f-g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>,
    from which we have trivially that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>\<Leftrightarrow\>f-g=0<text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The following theorem shows that for a complete measure space a function
  that is <math|a>.e. equal to a measurable function is also measurable.

  <\theorem>
    <label|almost everywhere measurability>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    <with|font-series|bold|complete> measure space [see theorem:
    <reference|measure complete measure>],
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable function and
    <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> satisfies
    <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then <math|g> is
    <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exists a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> and
    <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
    So\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.436.228>x\<in\>X\\N\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    . Let <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>t>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x\<in\>X\\N\<vee\>x\<in\>N|)>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>t>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x\<in\>X\\N\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>t|)>\<vee\><around*|(|x\<in\>N\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>t|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.436.228>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|x\<in\>X\\N\<wedge\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>t|)>\<vee\><around*|(|x\<in\>N\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>t|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\N|)><big|cap><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>t|}>|)>\<vee\>x\<in\><around*|(|N<big|cap><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\N|)><big|cap><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>t|}>|)><big|cup><around*|(|N<big|cap><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.437.228><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>=<around*|(|<around*|(|X\\N|)><big|cap><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>t|}>|)><big|cup><around*|(|N<big|cap><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Given that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable we have by [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] that
    <math|<around*|{|f\<leqslant\>t|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, further as
    <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have also <math|X\\N\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Finally
    as <math|N<big|cap><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>\<subseteq\>N> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> it follows from the fact <math|\<mu\>> is
    complete that <math|N<big|cap><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    So <math|<around*|(|X\\N<big|cap><around*|{|f\<leqslant\>t|}>|)><big|cup><around*|(|N<big|cap><around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.437.228>] proves that
    <math|<around*|{|g\<leqslant\>t|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Applying then
    [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] results in\ 

    <\equation*>
      g<text| is >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|almost everywhere limit is measurable>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    <with|font-series|bold|complete measure space> and
    <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    a sequence of <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable functions and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a function such
    that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=f>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|D=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
    exist>|}>> then we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable function
    limit>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\<infty\>>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      where >f<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>D>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>X\\D>>>>><text| is
      >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>
    </equation*>

    As <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=f>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      X\\<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>

    If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
    exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
    then <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>
    exists and <math|><math|<rigid|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>>
    so that <math|x\<in\>D> and <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>>
    which proves that <math|<rigid|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>|}>>>.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      X\\<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|\<infty\>><around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>X\\<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>

    from which it follows that <math|f=f<rsub|\<infty\>>> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e..
    Using then the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere
    measurability>] we must conclude that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<text| is >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <section|The measure integral>

  We will build up the definition of the measure integral in fases:\ 

  <\enumerate>
    <item>We define a integral over measurable functions whose image is
    finite the so called <with|font-series|bold|simple functions>.

    <item>Extend this integral to non negative measurable functions.

    <item>Extend the integral of non negative measurable function to general
    measurable functions.

    <item>Finally extend the integral to complex valued functions.
  </enumerate>

  <subsection|Integral of simple functions>

  <\definition>
    <label|simple function><index|simple function>Let <math|X> be a non empty
    set then a function <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|simple function> if <math|f<around*|(|X|)>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|finite> <with|font-series|bold|set> and
    <math|<with|font-series|bold|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>>. The
    set of all simple function graphs is noted as
    <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> so\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><rsup|X>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><text|
      and >f<around*|(|X|)><text| is a finite set>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  We have the following alternative definition of a simple function.

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function alternative>Let <math|X> be a non empty set then
    for a function <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> with
    <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> we have the following
    equivalences:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f> is a simple function [in other words
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>]

      <item>There exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection
      <math|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>>
      such that for <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|}>|)>>
      we have:\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
        is pairwise disjoint

        <item><math|X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>

        <item><math|f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>>
      </enumerate>

      <item>There exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a pairwise disjoint
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      such that

      <\equation*>
        f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<Rightarrow\>2>>As <math|f> is a simple function
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>> is finite, further as
      <math|X\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>,
      hence there exists a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>
      </equation*>

      Define <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      by <math|A<rsub|i>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|}>|)>>.
      Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
      then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|j><big|cap>A<rsub|j>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|}>|)><big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function function and intersection and
        union>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|}><big|cap><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|i\<neq\>j\<Rightarrow\>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|}><big|cap><around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>|}>=\<varnothing\>>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varnothing\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.440.229><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>>
      so, using the surjectivity of <math|\<beta\>>, there exist a
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>. Hence
      <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|}>|)>=A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>
      which proves that <math|X\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>X>
      or that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.441.229>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.440.229>,
      <reference|eq 20.441.229>] there exists a
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>> and <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|j>>. Hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|i|}>>f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|i|}>>f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|i|}>>f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)>|)>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.442.229>f=<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)>|)><rsub|>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>>
      </equation>

      <item*|<math|2\<Rightarrow\>3>>By (2) there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection
      <math|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      such that for <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
      defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|}>|)>>
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint, >X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i><text|
        and >f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>

      Define <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      by <math|f<rsub|i>=f<around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      then we have by the above also\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>

      which finish the proof of <math|<around*|(|3|)>>.

      <item*|<math|3\<Rightarrow\>1>>By (3) there exists a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>,
      <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint><text| and >f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|y\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>> then there exists a <math|x\<in\>X>
      such that <math|y=f<around*|(|x|)>>. Using the above there exist a
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>> and <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|i|}>>
      <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|j>> hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|k>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|i|}>>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|i|}>>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|i|}>>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      from which it follows that <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|{|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>>
      a finite set. As the subset of a finite set is finite [see theorem:
      <reference|subset of finite sets>] it follows that
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>> is a finite set, hence <math|f> is a simple
      function by definition.
    </description>
  </proof>

  We have the following important result for functions of the form
  <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>>
  like simple functions.

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function property>Let <math|X> be a non empty set,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|I> finite,
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    pairwise disjoint and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    then for

    <\equation*>
      f=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
    </equation*>

    we have that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I> we have
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A<rsub|i>> that
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|i>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>>
      we have <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=0>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|i\<in\>I> then for <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>> we have, as
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>> is pairwise disjoint,
      that <math|\<forall\>j\<in\>I\\<around*|{|i|}>>
      <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|j>>. So \ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\>I>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|i|}>>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\>I\\<around*|{|i|}>>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|k>><around*|(|x|)><rsub|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|k\<in\>I\\<around*|{|i|}>>f<rsub|k>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|i>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>If <math|x\<in\>X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>>
      then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I> we have <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|i>> so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>0=0
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  The set of simple functions is stable under pointwise sum and scalar
  product.

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function sum scalar product>Let <math|X> be a set then we
    have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> and
      <math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> we have
      <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>\ 
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>We have to consider two cases for <math|\<alpha\>>

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>=0>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>=<around*|{|0|}>>
        which is finite and non empty

        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<neq\>0>>As <math|f> is a simple function
        there exists a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection
        <math|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>>.
        Define now

        <\equation*>
          \<alpha\><rsub|f>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)><text|
          by >\<alpha\><rsub|f><around*|(|i|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>
        </equation*>

        If <math|y\<in\><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>> then
        there exists a <math|x\<in\>X> such that

        <\equation*>
          y=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|\<alpha\>\<neq\>0>f<around*|(|x|)>=<frac|y|\<alpha\>>
        </equation*>

        As <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>> there exists a
        <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that

        <\equation*>
          <math|>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=<frac|y|\<alpha\>>\<Rightarrow\>y=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>=\<alpha\><rsub|f><around*|(|i|)>
        </equation*>

        proving that <math|\<alpha\><rsub|f> is a surjection.> So using
        [theorem: <reference|surjection f:A-\<gtr\>B if A is finite then B is
        finite>] we have that <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>>
        is finite.
      </description>

      so in all cases <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>>
      is finite proving that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f> is a simple function.

      <item>As <math|f> and <math|g> are simple functions we have that
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>> and <math|g<around*|(|X|)>> are finite. As the
      product of finite sets is finite [see theroem: <reference|product of
      finite sets>] we have that

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|X|)><text| is finite>
      </equation*>

      Define now

      <\equation*>
        s:f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|Y|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|x+y\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|Y|)>|}><text|
        by >s<around*|(|x,y|)>=x+y
      </equation*>

      Then if <math|z\<in\><around*|{|x+y\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|Y|)>|}>>
      there exists a <math|x\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>> and a
      <math|y\<in\>f<around*|(|Y|)>> such that
      <math|z=x+y=s<around*|(|x,y|)>> which proves that <math|s> is
      surjective. Applying then [theorem: <reference|surjection f:A-\<gtr\>B
      if A is finite then B is finite>] proves that <math|>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x+y\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|X|)>|}><text|
        is finite>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|y\<in\><around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|X|)>> then there exists a
      <math|x\<in\>X> such that

      <\equation*>
        y=f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|{|x+y\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|X|)>|}>.
      </equation*>

      Hence <math|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x+y\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|X|)>|}>>
      which, as we have proved that <math|<rigid|<around*|{|x+y\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<times\>g<around*|(|X|)>|}>>>
      is finite, proves using [theorem: <reference|subset of finite sets>]
      that <math|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|X|)>> is finite.. This proves
      that <math|f+g> is a simple function.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  A example of a simple function <math|f:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
  of the form

  <\equation*>
    f=f<rsub|1>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|1>>+f<rsub|2>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|2>>+f<rsub|3>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|3>>+f<rsub|4>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|4>><text|
    where >f<around*|(|X|)>=<around*|{|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>,f<rsub|3>,f<rsub|4>|}>
  </equation*>

  is shown in the following figure:

  <small-figure|<with|gr-mode|<tuple|edit|text-at>|gr-frame|<tuple|scale|1cm|<tuple|0.450005gw|0.389983gh>>|gr-geometry|<tuple|geometry|1par|0.6par>|gr-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|1>|gr-edit-grid-aspect|<tuple|<tuple|axes|none>|<tuple|1|none>|<tuple|10|none>>|gr-edit-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-edit-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|1>|gr-color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|gr-arrow-end|\<gtr\>|gr-arrow-begin|\<less\>|gr-auto-crop|true|<graphics||<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|line-width|2ln|<line|<point|-6|0>|<point|7.0|0.0>>>|<with|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|line-width|2ln|<line|<point|0|-3>|<point|0.0|4.0>>>|<text-at|Y|<point|-0.5|4>>|<text-at|X|<point|7|-0.6>>|<with|color|blue|line-width|4|<line|<point|-6|2>|<point|-3.0|2.0>>>|<with|color|blue|line-width|4|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|-3|3>|<point|2.0|3.0>>>|<with|color|blue|line-width|4|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|2|2>|<point|5.0|2.0>>>|<with|color|blue|line-width|4|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|5|4>|<point|7.0|4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-4.5|2>|<point|-4.5|0.0>>>|<with|color|blue|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-0.6|3>|<point|-0.6|0.0>>>|<with|color|blue|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|3.5|2>|<point|3.5|0.0>>>|<with|color|blue|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|6|4>|<point|6.0|0.0>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-6|-0.3>|<point|-3.0|-0.3>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|f<rsub|1>>|<point|-4|1>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|f<rsub|2>>|<point|-0.4|1.5>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|f<rsub|3>>|<point|4|1>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|f<rsub|4>>|<point|6.3|2>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|A<rsub|1>>|<point|-4.5|-0.6>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|-3|-0.3>|<point|2.0|-0.3>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|2|-0.3>|<point|5.0|-0.3>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<gtr\>|arrow-begin|\<less\>|<line|<point|5|-0.3>|<point|7.0|-0.3>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|A<rsub|2>>|<point|-0.7|-0.6>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|A<rsub|3>>|<point|3.5|-0.6>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|A<rsub|4>>|<point|6|-0.6>>>>>|>

  \;

  The area below the above function and the <math|X>-axis is intuitively
  equal to the sum\ 

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|1>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+f<rsub|2>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>+f<rsub|3>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|3>|)>+f<rsub|4>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|4>|)>
  </equation*>

  so the integral of <math|f> which calculates this size should be of the
  form\ 

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>f=f<rsub|1>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+f<rsub|2>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>+f<rsub|3>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|3>|)>+f<rsub|4>\<cdot\>size<around*|(|A<rsub|4>|)>
  </equation*>

  hence we need a way to calculate the sizes of
  <math|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>,A<rsub|3>> and <math|A<rsub|4>>, in other words
  <math|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>,A<rsub|3>> and <math|A<rsub|4>> must be
  measurable so that we can use the measure function to calculate the sizes.
  The integral becomes then\ 

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>f=f<rsub|1>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+f<rsub|2>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>+f<rsub|3>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|3>|)>+f<rsub|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|4>|)>
  </equation*>

  For this to work we must have <math|A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>,A<rsub|3>,A<rsub|4>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
  and as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>> can be <math|\<infty\>>
  <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>,f<rsub|3>,f<rsub|4>> must be non negative. This
  motivates the following defintion.

  <\definition>
    <label|simple function measurable definition (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then the set of <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable real valued simple functions
    is noted as <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>. Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>\|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>|}>\<subseteq\>S<around*|[|X|]>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]><below|\<Rightarrow\>|<text|[definition:
      <reference|simple function>]>>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>=\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]><big|cap>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  \;

  We have a simpler alternative definition for
  <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.371.262>Let <math|X> be a set, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    and <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    pairwise disjoint then for\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)><text|
      defined by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      <text| if >i=n+1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint >
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    If <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
    then we have for <math|i,j> either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i=n+1>>Then <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=<around*|(|X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|k>|)>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|j>\<subseteq\><around*|(|X\\A<rsub|j>|)><big|cap>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item*|<math|j=n+1>>Then <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|k>|)>|)>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|X\\A<rsub|i>|)>=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item*|<math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then as
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      is pairwise disjoint we have that <math|<rigid|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>>.
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint >
    </equation*>

    Further

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>B<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family properties (2)>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|cup>B<rsub|n+1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|X>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>B<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function measurable (0)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then we have the following equivalences:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> [in other words
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]><text| and
      \ >\<forall\>y\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>>
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|y|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item>There exists a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a pairwise disjoint
      family <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      of measurable sets and a family <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      of real numbers so that

      <\equation*>
        f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<Rightarrow\>2>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      <math|f\<in\>S<around*|[|X|]>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      \ Let <math|y\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>> then, as
      <math|f*<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> it follows that
      <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. Applying then [theorem: <reference|measurable
      function comparisation>] it follows from the fact that <math|f> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable that <math|<rigid|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|y|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|measurable function comparisation
      definition>]>><around*|{|f=y|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>.

      <item*|<math|2\<Rightarrow\>3>>As <math|f\<in\>S<around*|[|X|]>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      we can use [theorem: <reference|simple function alternative>] to get a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection
      <math|<rigid|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>>>
      such that for <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      defined by <math|A<rsub|i>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|)>>
      we have

      <\equation*>
        f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><text|
        and ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation*>

      Using (2) we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Finally, define <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
      by <math|f<rsub|i>=\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      then\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        and >f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|3\<Rightarrow\>1>>Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>1,\<ldots\>,n>A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        if >i=n+1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        by >g<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0<text| if
        >i=n+1>>|<row|<cell|f<rsub|i><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      Then using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.371.262>] we have that \ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.443.229><around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><text|,
        ><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint, ><big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>B<rsub|i>=X
      </equation>

      Now <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have, using the hypothesis that
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|n+1>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|n+1>>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=0+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>=f<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.444.229>f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then there exist a
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>> hence <math|<rigid|f<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|simple function property>]>>g<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>> which
      proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>

      So by [theorem: <reference|simple function alternative>] we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>
      </equation*>

      Define <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|)>>
      by

      <\equation*>
        I<rsub|i>=<around*|{|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>\|f<around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>=<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>
      </equation*>

      then we prove that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.445.229>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}><text|
        we have >f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>I<rsub|i>>B<rsub|j><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure countable union is in sigma algebra>]>>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      <\proof>
        Given <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>. Let
        <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>> then
        \ <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>>. As
        <math|x\<in\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>B<rsub|k>>
        there exist a <math|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|k>>. Using [theorem: <reference|simple function
        property>] it follows that <math|<rigid|f<around*|(|B<rsub|k>|)>=<around*|{|g<rsub|k>|}>>>
        which, as <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|k>\<exterior\>f<around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>>,
        proves that <math|g<rsub|i>=g<rsub|k>>, hence
        <math|f<around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>=<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>> or
        <rigid|<math|k\<in\>I<rsub|i>>>. In other words
        <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|k>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>I<rsub|i>>B<rsub|j>>,
        so that

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.446.229>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>I<rsub|i>>B<rsub|j>
        </equation>

        Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>I<rsub|i>>B<rsub|j>> then
        there exist a <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|i>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|k>>, as <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|i>>
        <math|f<around*|(|B<rsub|k>|)>=<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>>, so that
        <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>> or
        <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>>. Hence
        <math|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>I<rsub|i>>B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>>
        which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.446.229>] proves
        <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>I<rsub|i>>B<rsub|j>>
        and thus [eq: <reference|eq 20.445.229>].
      </proof>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>B<rsub|i>>
      then there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> such
      that <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>>. Using [theorem: <reference|simple
      function property>] again it follows that
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>> so that
      <math|<rigid|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>>>.
      Hence we have <math|X\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>>
      and as trivially <math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<subseteq\>X>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.447.229>X=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      We are now ready to prove that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.
      Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then as <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<subseteq\>X> we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cap>X>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.447.229>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)><big|cap>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function function and intersection and
        union>]>>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<big|cap><around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.448.229>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>> then for
      <math|g<rsub|i>> we have either <math|>:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|g<rsub|i>\<in\>B>>Then
        <math|B<big|cap><around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>=<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>> so
        that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
        [see eq: <reference|eq 20.445.229>]

        <item*|<math|g<rsub|i>\<nin\>B>>Then
        <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<big|cap><around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      </description>

      Hence <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<big|cap><around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      so that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<big|cap><around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.448.229>] proves that
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. As
      <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>> was
      choosen arbitrary it follows by definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<text| is >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  We show now that <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> is stable under
  the pointwise sum and product.

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function measurable sum and scalar product>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> and
      <math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> we
      have <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> we have that
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> and <math|f> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. By [theorem: <reference|simple function
      sum scalar product>] we have <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>
      and by [theorem: <reference|measurable function product with scalar>]
      that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. Hence
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>.

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> we have
      that <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> and <math|f,g> are
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. By [theorem: <reference|simple function
      sum scalar product>] we have <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>.
      Further using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>]
      it follows that <math|f+g> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable. Hence
      <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  When defining integral we are only interested in non negative measurable
  simple functions hence the following definition.

  <\definition>
    <label|simple function measurable definition (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then the set of non negative \ <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable real valued
    simple functions is noted as <math|\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    hence

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As by [definition: <reference|simple function measurable definition
      (1)>] <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows that

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>

    <\note>
      If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>,
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subset\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  A trivial example of a non negative measurable simple function is the
  constant function.

  <\example>
    <label|simple function constant>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,A|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|a\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> then
    <math|C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> where\ 

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|a>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| is defined
      by >C<rsub|a><around*|(|x|)>=a
    </equation*>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    As <math|C<rsub|a><around*|(|X|)>=<around*|{|a|}>>, a finite set, we have
    that <math|C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> and
    <math|C<rsub|a><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>. Let
    <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>> then
    for <math|a> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|a\<in\>B>>Then <math|<around*|(|C<rsub|a>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|a=C<rsub|a><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B|}>=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item*|<math|a\<nin\>B>>Then <math|<around*|(|C<rsub|a>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|a=C<rsub|a><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B|}>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    </description>

    so that <math|C<rsub|a><text| is >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>>. Hence it
    follows that <math|C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>.
  </proof>

  We have a simpler alternative definition for
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function measurable (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then we have the following equivalences:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> [in
      other words <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>,
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
      <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable].

      <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]><text|,
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
      >\<forall\>y\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>>
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|y|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item>There exists a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a pairwise disjoint
      family <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      of measurable sets and a family <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      of real non negative numbers so that

      <\equation*>
        f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<Rightarrow\>2>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> and
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>. Using
      [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (0)>] it follows that
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> and
      <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>>
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|y|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.

      <item*|<math|2\<Rightarrow\>3>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>>
      and <math|><math|\<forall\>y\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>>
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|y|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> it
      follows from [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (0)>] that
      there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a pairwise disjoint family
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      and a <math|<around*|{|f<rprime|'><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rprime|'><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>

      Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        by >f<rsub|i>=max<around*|(|0,f<rprime|'><rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Consider now

      <\equation*>
        g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>n|}>>A<rsub|i>>>Then
        there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>. Using [theorem: <reference|simple function
        property>] we have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rprime|'><rsub|i>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\>f<rsub|i>=g<around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        Further as <math|f<rprime|'><rsub|i>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<subseteq\>f<around*|(|X|)><below|\<subseteq\>|hypothesis><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
        it follows that <math|f<rprime|'><rsub|i>=max<around*|(|f<rprime|'><rsub|i>,0|)>=f<rsub|i>>
        so that <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>>.

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>Then
        using [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] we have that
        <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=0=g<around*|(|x|)>=0>
      </description>

      As in all cases we have <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>> it
      follows that <math|f=g> hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><text|
        where ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        and ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|3\<Rightarrow\>1>>By the hypothesis we have that
      <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>>
      where <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      is pairwise disjoint and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (0)>] it follows
      that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>. Let
      <math|x\<in\>X> then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|simple
        function property>]>><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        if >x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|0\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        if >x\<in\>X\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>>>>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\example>
    <label|simple function 2 point measurability>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, <math|a,b\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> then
    for the function

    <\equation*>
      f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| where
      >f<around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a<text| if
      >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|b<text| if >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>
    </equation*>

    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f<around*|(|X|)>=<around*|{|a,b|}>> a finite set it follows that
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X|]>> and
    <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>. Further for
    <math|a,b> we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|a=b>>Then <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|a|}>|)>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|b|}>|)>=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item*|<math|a\<neq\>b>>Then as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A> we have
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=a\<in\><around*|{|a|}>> so that
      <math|A\<subseteq\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|a|}>|)>>. If
      <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|a|}>|)>> then
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=a> and we must have that <math|x\<in\>A> [if
      <math|x\<in\>X\\A> then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=b\<neq\>a>] so that
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|a|}>|)>\<subseteq\>A>. Hence we
      have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|a|}>|)>=A\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Likewise we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\A> that
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=b\<in\><around*|{|b|}>> proving that
      <math|X\\A\<subseteq\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|b|}>|)>>. If
      <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|b|}>|)>> then
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=b> and we must have that <math|x\<in\>X\\A> [if
      <math|x\<in\>A> then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=a\<neq\>b>] so that
      <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|b|}>|)>\<subseteq\>X\\A>. Hence
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|b|}>|)>=X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|a|}>|)>,f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|{|b|}>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    which by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] proves
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Given a non negative measurable simple function
  <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>>
  we want to define the integral of <math|f> as
  <math|<big|int>f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>.
  However the same function <math|f> can have another representation
  <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|B<rsub|i>>>,
  so we must show that the integral is independent of the choosen
  representation of a simple function.

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function representation>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then
    if <math|n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    and <math|<around*|{|b<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    satisfies\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>=f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      The condition that <math|f\<in\>S<rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> is
      needed because this ensures that <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>,<around*|{|b<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>
      are non negative so that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      and <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>
      are well defined [as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>,\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>
      can be <math|\<infty\>>].
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we remove all the terms where <math|a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>
    and <math|b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0>, this will
    not change the sum. Defiine <math|I=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|A<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>\<wedge\>a<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>>
    and <math|J=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\|B<rsub|i>\<neq\>0\<wedge\>b<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>>.
    Then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\I> we have
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|a<rsub|i>=0\<vee\>A<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>0>
    and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\\J\<equallim\><rsub|b<rsub|i>=0\<vee\>B<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>0>
    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    \ and\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\\J>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.449.229><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>=f=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    As <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    and <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>
    are pairwise disjoint we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text| and
      ><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>J>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text| are
      pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>> then there
    exist a <math|i\<in\>I> such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>, so that by
    [theorem: <reference|simple function property>]
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=a<rsub|i>\<neq\>0>. Assume that
    <math|x\<nin\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>J>B<rsub|i>> then
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>J>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>0=0>
    contradicting <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>. Hence the assumption is
    wrong and we must have that <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>J>B<rsub|i>>.
    So\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.451.229><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>J>B<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Likewise if <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>J>B<rsub|i>> then there
    exist a <math|i\<in\>J> such that <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>>, so that by
    [theorem: <reference|simple function property>]
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=b<rsub|i>\<neq\>0>. Assume that
    <math|x\<nin\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>> then
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>0=0>
    contradicting <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>. Hence the assumption is
    wrong and we must have that <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>>.
    So <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>J>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>J>A<rsub|i>>,
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.451.229>] gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.452.229><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>J>B<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then, as
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|j>|}><rsub|j\<in\>J>> are pairwise disjoint,
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|}><rsub|j\<in\>J>> are
    pairwise disjoint and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.453.229>A<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>I>A<rsub|j>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.452.229>]>>A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>J>B<rsub|j>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>J><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> then, \ as
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|j>|}><rsub|j\<in\>I>> are pairwise disjoint
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|j><big|cap>B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|j\<in\>J>> are
    pairwise disjoint and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.454.229>B<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>I>B<rsub|j>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.452.229>]>>B<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>I>A<rsub|j>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|j><big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Define <math|K=<around*|{|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then if <math|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K> we have
    <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> so there exist a
    <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>> and

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
      <reference|simple function property>]>>f<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
      <reference|simple function property>]>>b<rsub|j>
    </equation*>

    In other words\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.455.229>\<forall\><around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K<text| we have
      >a<rsub|i>=b<rsub|j>
    </equation>

    Now

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\I>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\I>0\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.453.229>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\>J><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|a<rsub|i>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|sum index is a product>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>I\<times\>J>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\><around*|(|I\<times\>J|)>\\K>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\><around*|(|I\<times\>J|)>\\K>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.455.229>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\><around*|(|I\<times\>J|)>\\K>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>K>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\><around*|(|I\<times\>J|)>\\K>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>I\<times\>J>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|sum index is a product>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J><around*|(|b<rsub|j>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.454.229>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\>J>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\\J>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|j>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Thanks to the two previous theorems [theorems: <reference|simple function
  measurable (1)>, <reference|simple function representation>] the following
  definition is well defined [existence and uniqueness].

  <\definition>
    <label|simple function integral><index|integral of a simple
    function><index|<math|<big|int><rsup|S>f>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then
    we define <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>> by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    where

    <\equation*>
      f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><text|,
      >n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|, ><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and ><around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    is a representation of <math|f>.

    <\note>
      For the moment we use the special symbol <math|<big|int><rsup|s>f
      d\<mu\>> to differentiate the integral of a simple function from the
      more general integral <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>> that will be based on
      the integral of simple functions.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\example>
    <label|simple function integral constant function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|a\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|C<rsub|a>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> defined by
    <math|C<rsub|a><around*|(|x|)>=a> then
    <math|C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> and
    <math|<big|int><rsup|S>C<rsub|a> d\<mu\>=a\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>>.
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Define <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    by <math|A<rsub|1>=X> and <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    by <math|a<rsub|1>=a> then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
    <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=a\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X><around*|(|x|)>=a=C<rsub|a><around*|(|x|)>>
    so that <math|C<rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>>.
    Hence by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>]

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and using [definition: <reference|simple function integral>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|C>C<rsub|a> d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=a<rsub|1>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=a\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The following lemma will be usefull if we have to compare or add simple
  functions.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.351.229>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    then there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a pairwise disjoint family
    <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    of sets in <math|\<cal-A\>>, a family
    <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    and a family <math|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>><text|
      and >g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    \;
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f,g\<in\>S<rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> we have by
    [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] that there exists
    <math|n<rprime|'>,m<rprime|'>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<rigid|<around*|{|A<rprime|'><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>>
    pairwise disjoint, <math|<around*|{|B<rprime|'><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint, <math|<rigid|<around*|{|a<rprime|'><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>>
    and <math|<around*|{|b<rprime|'><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>|}>>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.456\<point\>229>f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rprime|'><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rprime|'><rsub|i>><text|
      and >g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rprime|'><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rprime|'><rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >A<rsub|i>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>A<rprime|'><rsub|i><text|
      if >i=n<rprime|'>+1>>|<row|<cell|A<rprime|'><rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >B<rsub|i>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>|}>>B<rprime|'><rsub|i><text|
      if >i=m<rprime|'>+1>>|<row|<cell|B<rprime|'><rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>|}>>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>><text|
      by >a<rsub|i>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0<text|
      if >i=n<rprime|'>+1>>|<row|<cell|a<rprime|'><rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|b<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}><text|>><text|
      by >b<rsub|i>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|0
      <text|if >i=m<rprime|'>+1>>|<row|<cell|b<rprime|'><rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>|}>>>>>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.371.262>]
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|\<nobracket\>>>>,
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>>>
    are pairwise disjoint and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.457.229>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>>A<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>>B<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.458.229>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}><text|
      >A<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i><big|cap>X=A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>>B<rsub|j>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.459.229>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}><text|
      >B<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i><big|cap>X=B<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|j><big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.460.229>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n<rprime|'>+1><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsup|m<rprime|'>+1><rsub|j=1><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|m<rprime|'>+1><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j=1><rsup|n<rprime|'>+1><around*|(|A<rsub|j><big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|product of finite sets>]
    <math|<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>>
    is finite and there exists a bijection\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<rightarrow\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>\<times\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}><text|
      where >n=<around*|(|n<rprime|'>+1|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|m<rprime|'>+1|)>
    </equation*>

    Define now

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>A>|<cell|<text|by>>|<cell|C<rsub|i>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<cell|<text|by>>|<cell|f<rsub|i>=a<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<cell|<text|by>>|<cell|g<rsub|i>=b<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.460.229>] there exists
    a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>+1|}>> such that
    <math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>|)>>,
    hence there exist a <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>+1|}>>
    such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>>. As <math|\<beta\>>
    is a bijection there exist a <math|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    such that <math|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)>=<around*|(|i,j|)>> hence
    <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|k|)><rsub|2>>=C<rsub|k>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|k>>.
    Hence <math|X\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|k>>
    and as trivially <math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|k>\<subseteq\>X>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.461.229>X=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    is pairwise disjoint. So let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    with <math|i\<neq\>j> then as <math|\<beta\>> is a bijection we have that
    <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>, for
    <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>,
    <math|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>> we have then either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>=\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then
      we must have that <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=<around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=<around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.462.229><around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and >X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.462.229>] there exist a
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
    <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>
    hence <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>>. For
    <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>> we have the following
    possibilities:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>=n<rprime|'>+1>>Then
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n<rprime|'>+1>=X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>A<rprime|'><rsub|i>|)>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.456\<point\>229>] <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=0>. Further
      using \ [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.462.229>] it follows also that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|i>=a<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>=a<rsub|n<rprime|'>+1>=0=f<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>.

      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>>Then
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>=A<rprime|'><rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.456\<point\>229>] <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=a<rprime|'><rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>>.
      Further using \ [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and
      [eq: <reference|eq 20.462.229>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|i>=a<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>=a<rprime|'><rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>=f<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that in this cases we have also that
      <math|><math|f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>.
    </description>

    To summarize we have proved that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    We use now the same technic for <math|g>. Let <math|x\<in\>X> then by
    [eq: <reference|eq 20.462.229>] there exist a
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
    <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>
    hence <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>. For
    <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>> we have the following
    possibilities:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>=m<rprime|'>+1>>Then
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|n<rprime|'>+1>=X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n<rprime|'>|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.456\<point\>229>] <math|g<around*|(|x|)>=0>. Further
      using [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.462.229>] it follows also that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>=b<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>=b<rsub|m<rprime|'>+1>=0=g<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|g<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>.

      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rprime|'>|}>>>Then
      <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>=B<rprime|'><rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.456\<point\>229>] <math|g<around*|(|x|)>=b<rprime|'><rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>>.
      Further using [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.462.229>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>=b<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>=b<rprime|'><rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>=g<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that in this cases we have also that
      <math|><math|g<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>.
    </description>

    To summarize we have proved that\ 

    <\equation*>
      g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We prove now that the integral of non negativ.e measurable simple functions
  has the usual properties of integration.

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function integral properties>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      and <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>
      d\<mu\>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>>.

      <item>If <math|f,g\<in\>\<b-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> and
        <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f+g|)> d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S>f
        d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>>.

        <item>If <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
        <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>> then
        <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>g
        d\<mu\>>.

        <item><math|f\<wedge\>g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>,
        <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>
        d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>> and
        <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>
        d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>>.

        <item><math|f\<vee\>g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>,
        <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>
        d\<mu\>> and <math|<big|int><rsup|S>g
        d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>
        d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>>.
      </enumerate>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      satisfies <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<mu\>=0>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> there
      exists by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] a
      pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      and a <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      such that <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>>.
      Further we have by definition that

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      \ Then <math|<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      and <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>a<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>a<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i><rsub|>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>a<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i><text|>>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] it follows
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S>f
        d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      there exists by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.351.229>]
      <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      pairwise disjoint, <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>>
      and <math|<around*|{|g<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.463.229>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i><text|,
        >f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>><text|
        and >g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>
      </equation>

      By the definition of <math|<big|int><rsup|S>> we have also\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.464.229><big|int><rsup|S>f
        d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)><text|
        and ><big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Next we have:\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>+g<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|f<rsub|i>+g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|f<rsub|i>+g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        whic proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f+g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|f<rsub|i>+g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>
        </equation*>

        So using [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] it
        follows that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f+g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
        </equation*>

        and using the definition of the integral

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f+g|)>
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|f<rsub|i>+g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.464.229>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>f
          d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item>Assume that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
        <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>. Define
        <math|I=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|C<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>|}>>
        then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I> there exist a
        <math|C<rsub|i>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\>\<exists\>x\<in\>C<rsub|i>>
        so that <math|f<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>>f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>>g<rsub|i>>. Hence we have\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.465\<point\>229>\<forall\>i\<in\>I<text|
          >f<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|i>
        </equation>

        Next\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>f
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\I>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.465\<point\>229>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\I>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>g<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>g
          d\<mu\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then as <math|X\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.463.229>]>><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>>
        there exis a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>. Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.463.229>]
        together with [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] gives
        then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|i>> and
        <math|g<around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>>. So\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|min<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|simple function property>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>min<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        which proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>min<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>>
        </equation*>

        and, as <math|<around*|{|min<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>,
        it follows from [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>]
        that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f\<wedge\>g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
        </equation*>

        Further as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X
        <around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=min*<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>>
        it folllows from (b) that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>
          d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\><text| and
          ><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<wedge\>g|)>
          d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>
        </equation*>

        <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then as <math|X\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.463.229>]>><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>>
        there exis a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>. Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.463.229>]
        together with [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] gives
        then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|i>> and
        <math|g<around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|i>>. So\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|simple function property>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>max<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        which proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>min<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>
        </equation*>

        and, as <math|<around*|{|max<around*|(|f<rsub|i>,g<rsub|i>|)>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
        it follows from [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>]
        that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f\<vee\>g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
        </equation*>

        Further as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X
        f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>max*<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,g<around*|(|x|)>|)>=f\<vee\>g>
        it folllows from (b) that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)>
          d\<mu\><text| and ><big|int><rsup|S>g
          d\<mu\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<vee\>g|)> d\<mu\>
        </equation*>
      </enumerate>

      <item>As <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exist a
      <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.466.229><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
      </equation>

      As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> there
      exists by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a pairwise disjoint family
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      and a <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      such that <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>>.
      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then for <math|f<rsub|i>>
      we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|f<rsub|i>=0>>Then <math|f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>

        <item*|<math|0\<less\>f<rsub|i>>>Then
        <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A<rsub|i>> we have
        <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>>a<rsub|i>\<neq\>0> so that
        <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>>. Hence
        <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.466.229>]>>N>. So
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<equallim\>0> from which it follows that
        <math|f\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>
      </description>
    </enumerate>

    So \ <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have that
    <math|f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0> proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>f d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Using mathematical induction we have the following extension of the above
  theorem.

  <\corollary>
    <label|simple function integral properties (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    then we have that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<big|wedge><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>

      <item><math|<big|vee><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    </enumerate>

    \ 
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let\ 

      <\equation*>
        S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
        ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text|
        then ><big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>|}>
      </equation*>

      then we have:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
        then <math|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>
        so that <math|<rigid|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>>,
        hence <math|1\<in\>S>

        <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Let
        <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
        then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A> we have

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|min<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[corollary:
          <reference|order max min S plus
          element>]>>>|<cell|min<around*|(|min<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|min<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<wedge\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        \ <math|> so that

        <\equation*>
          <big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<wedge\>f<rsub|n+1>
        </equation*>

        As <math|n\<in\>S> we have that <math|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>,
        hence, using [theorem: <reference|simple function integral
        properties>] it follows that <math|<around*|(|<big|wedge><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>,
        proving <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
      </description>

      <item>Let\ 

      <\equation*>
        S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
        ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text|
        then ><big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>|}>
      </equation*>

      then we have:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
        then <math|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>
        so that <math|<rigid|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>>,
        hence <math|1\<in\>S>

        <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Let
        <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
        then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A> we have

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[corollary:
          <reference|order max min S plus
          element>]>>>|<cell|max<around*|(|max<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<vee\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        \ <math|> so that

        <\equation*>
          <big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<vee\>f<rsub|n+1>
        </equation*>

        As <math|n\<in\>S> we have that <math|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>,
        hence, using [theorem: <reference|simple function integral
        properties>] it follows that <math|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>,
        proving <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  The following theorem is the simple function variant of the monotone
  convergence theorem that is one of the central convergence theorems that
  sets the measure integral appart from the Riemann integral.

  <\theorem>
    <label|simple function integral monotone convergence theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)><text|>
    </equation*>

    and for <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    defined by <math|<around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>
    we have <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\><text| exists>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
      sequence exist>] that <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>
      exist, as is needed for the requirements of this theorem.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|> that <math|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|simple function integral
    properties>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.467.229>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k+1>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    So using [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.468.229><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\><text| exists and ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<geqslant\>0
    </equation>

    Further as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
    >\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
    >f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)><text|>>
    we have by \ [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>] again that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.469.229><rigid|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
    </equation>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.470.229>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      <math|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </equation>

    As <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    we have that <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
    d\<mu\>> exist and using [theorem: <reference|simple function integral
    properties>] it follows then that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)>
    d\<mu\>> so that using [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.471.229>0<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.468.229>]>><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Next we prove the opposite inequality. As
    <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    there exists by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] a
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a pairwise disjoint family
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    and a family <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.472.231><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    For <math|><math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    we have two possible cases:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >a<rsub|i>=0>>Then <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.472.231>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>0\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>
      so that we have [using eq: <reference|eq 20.471.229>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        0\<leqslant\><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>f<rsub|i>
        d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.469.229>]>><big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>
        |)>d\<mu\>=0
      </equation*>

      which proves that in this case

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>f<rsub|k>
        d\<mu\>=0=<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>
        |)>d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >a<rsub|i>\<neq\>0>>Then <math|I=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|a<rsub|i>\<neq\>0|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
      so that there exists a <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a bijection
      <math|\<beta\>:<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>\<rightarrow\>I>. Let
      <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.472.231>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|[|theorem:|\<nobracket\>><text|<reference|sum
        bijection on index>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>a<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>a<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving, if we define <math|<around*|{|b<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>
      by <math|b<rsub|i>=a<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>> and
      <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>>
      by <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
      that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.472.229><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        and ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
        >b<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>
      </equation>

      Further, if <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> with
      <math|i\<neq\>j> then <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>
      so that <math|B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>><big|cap>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>=\<varnothing\>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.473.229><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> with
      <math|\<varepsilon\>\<less\>1> then <math|0\<less\>1-\<varepsilon\>>.
      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> and define

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.476.229><around*|{|B<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text|
        by >B<rsub|i,j>=<around*|{|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>\|f<rsub|j><around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      then as <math|>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>\|f<rsub|j><around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>|}>=B<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<rsub|j><around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>|}>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[def:
        <reference|measurable function comparisation
        definition>]>>B<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|{|f<rsub|j>\<geqslant\><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>|}>
      </equation*>

      and <math|B<rsub|i>,<around*|{|f<rsub|j>\<geqslant\><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      [see: theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] it
      follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.477.229>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
        we have ><around*|{|B<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i>> then by [theorem: <reference|simple function
      property>] and [eqs: <reference|eq 20.472.229>, <reference|eq
      20.473.229>] we have <math|<around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>=b<rsub|i>>.
      As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.472.229>] <math|b<rsub|i>\<neq\>0> we have
      <math|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.469.229>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>.
      Hence there exist a <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<less\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>
      which proves that <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i,j>>.
      So that <math|B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i,j>>.
      As <math|\<forall\>j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|B<rsub|i,j>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>\<Rightarrow\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i,j>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.478.229>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
        >B<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i,j>
      </equation>

      Let <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>
      with <math|i\<neq\>j> we have <math|B<rsub|i,k><big|cap>B<rsub|j,k>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.479.229>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have that
        <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i,k>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint><rsub|>>>
      </equation>

      Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> and
      <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then if <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i,k>> we have that
      <math|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>>,
      so \ <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i,k+1>>, hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.480.229>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
        we have >\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
        >B<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i,k+1>
      </equation>

      Using now [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>
      (1)] it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.481.229>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}><text|
        >\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.478.229>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i,j>|)>=<below|lim|j\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,j>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,j>|)>\|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Define now\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.482.229><around*|{|g<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text|
        by >g<rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i,k>>
      </equation>

      then by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] together
      with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.482.229>, <reference|eq 20.477.229>,
      <reference|eq 20.479.229>] we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.483.229><around*|{|g<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X.\<cal-A\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> and <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then for <math|x> we
      have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i,k>>>Then
        there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i,k>> so that
        <math|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>
        which, as <math|g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>,
        proves that

        <\equation*>
          g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        Further as <math|B<rsub|i,k><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.480.229>]>>B<rsub|i,k+1>> we have that
        <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|i,k+1>> so that

        <\equation*>
          g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|simple function property>]>><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|k+1>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|simple function property>]>>g<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        proving, as equality implies inequality, that\ 

        <\equation*>
          g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<nin\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>B<rsub|i,k>>>Then
        <math|g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i,k>><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>0=0>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>=0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        and\ 

        <\equation*>
          g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>=0\<leqslant\>g<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      So we must conclude that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.482.230>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
        >\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
        >g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)><text|
        and >g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation>

      From the above we have using [theorem: <reference|simple function
      integral properties>] that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|<big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|k+1>
      d\<mu\>>. So using [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing /
      decreasing sequence exist>] <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>> exists. Lets calculate this limit. By [theorem:
      <reference|extended limit properties>] and [eq: <reference|eq
      20.481.229>] we have that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>
      <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,k>|)>|)>>
      exists and

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,k>|)>|)>=<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,k>|)>=<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Hence using [theorem: <reference|extended limit and finite sum>] on the
      above it follows that <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,k>|)>|)>>
      exists and

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.484.229><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,k>|)>|)>=<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      <\equation*>
        \;
      </equation*>

      so

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|k>
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.482.229>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i,k>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.484.229>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>b<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.472.229>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.484.230><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|k>
        d\<mu\>=<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>
      </equation>

      By [eq: <reference|eq 20.482.230>] we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|g<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>, so
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|simple function integral
      properties>] that <math|<big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>>. Using
      then [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|k>
        d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      If we combine this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.484.230>] we get

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.485.230><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      Now for <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>>
      we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>=\<infty\>>>Then
        as <math|0\<less\><around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>> we have
        <math|<around*|(|1-\<varepsilon\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>=\<infty\>>
        so that by [eq: <reference|eq 20.485.230>]
        <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>=\<infty\>=<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>>
        so that \ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>>By
        [eq: <reference|eq 20.471.229>] we have
        <math|0\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
        d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>
        so that <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
        d\<mu\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>>.
        Hence by [eq: <reference|eq 20.485.230>] we have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>
        </equation*>

        As <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> with
        <math|\<varepsilon\>\<less\>1> was choosen arbitrary it follows from
        [theorem: <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (2)>]
        that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      which proves that in all cases

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.471.229>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
        d\<mu\>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      \ 
    </description>

    So in all possible cases we have

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Integral of non negative functions>

  <subsubsection|Definition of the integral of non negative functions>

  We extend now the integral <math|<big|int><rsup|S>> of measurable non
  negative simple functions to non negative measurable functions.\ 

  <\definition>
    <label|non negative integral>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then we define <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>> by

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
      >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As by [example: <reference|simple function constant>]
      <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> and
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>=0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
      >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
      so that

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
        d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
        >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
        >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      exists. Hence the definition is well defined.
    </note>

    <\note>
      In the definition of the integral <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>> we
      don't use the <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurability of <math|f>,
      <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
      >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>>
      is also well defined for a non negative function. However the
      requirement that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable will be
      usefull later to construct a alternative definition based on a limiting
      process of simple functions.
    </note>
  </definition>

  We show now that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>> is actually a extension of
  <math|<big|int><rsup|S>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral is a extension>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> we have by
    [definition: <reference|simple function measurable definition (2)>] that
    <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable, hence
    <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> so that

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further, as trivially, \ <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> it follows that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
      >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.487.231><big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
      >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> be such that
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
    then by [theorem: <reference|simple function integral properties>] we
    have that <rigid|<math|<big|int><rsup|S>g
    d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>>>. So it follows that
    <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>> is a upper bound of
    <math|<rigid|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
    d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
    >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>>>
    which proves that

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
      >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    Combining the above with [eq: <reference|eq 20.487.231>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\example>
    <label|non negative integral constant>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|a\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> then
    <math|C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|a> d\<mu\>=a\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation*>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|simple function constant>]
    <math|C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>, hence
    using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral is a extension>]
    <math|C<rsub|a>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|a> d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S>C<rsub|a>
      d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|simple function
      integral constant function>]>>a\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\example>
    <label|non negative integral characteristics><math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> then
    <rigid|<math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<in\>S<rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)>d\<mu\>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    by <math|A<rsub|1>=A> and <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>=\<alpha\>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|I>>
    </equation*>

    proving that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>>.
    From [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] it follows
    then that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Using the definition of <math|<big|int><rsup|S>> [see definition:
    <reference|simple function integral>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral is a extension>]
    <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)>
    d\<mu\>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Integral of non negative measurable functions on a
  measurable subset>

  Next we define the integral on a measurable subset.

  <\definition>
    <label|non negative integral (1)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A,X>\<cdot\>f|)>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>]
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      as is needed in this definition.
    </note>
  </definition>

  If <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> is a measure
  space and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then by [theorem: <reference|measure sub
  measure space>] we can construct the measure subspace
  <math|<around*|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>|\<rangle\>>
  where > <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}>>
  and <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>:\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
  is the restriction of <math|\<mu\>> to <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>. A
  natural question to ask is if there is a corollation between
  <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>> and
  <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>>. As the following theorem shows
  the answer is yes.

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral sub measure space (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a function such that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[see
      definition: <reference|non negative integral
      (1)>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
    </equation*>

    where

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}><text|
      see [definition: <reference|measure sub measure space>]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><text| is the restriction of
      >\<mu\><text| to >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As \ <math|<rigid|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function in sub space>]
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    which proves the first part of the theorem. Now using the definition of
    <math|<big|int><rsup|+>> [see definition: <reference|non negative
    integral>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.598.249><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>A<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.599.249><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    So to prove the theorem we have to prove that the above supremums are the
    same. Let <math|<rigid|I\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
    d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>A<text|
    we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>|}>>> then
    there exist a <math|g\<in\>\<b-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|A>|]>>
    with <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A> <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
    such that <math|<rigid|I=<big|int><rsup|S>g
    d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>. Using the alternate definition of
    simple functions [see theorem: <reference|simple function measurable
    (1)>] there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a
    <rigid|<math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>><space|1em>pairwise
    disjoint and <math|<around*|{|\<alpha\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.498.263>g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A,C<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.499.263>h=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    then by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] it follows
    that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.603.249>h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>>>Then
      <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A> so that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|h<around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\><rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A,C<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|X<rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>C<rsub|i>>>Then
      by [theorem: <reference|simple function property>]
      <math|h<around*|(|x|)>=0\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>
      [as <math|f> is non negative].
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.604.249>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Further we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|[|eq:<text|<reference|eq
      20.498.263>>|]>\<wedge\><text|[definition: <reference|simple function
      integral>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|C<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<alpha\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|[|<text|eq:<reference|eq
      20.499.263>>|]>\<wedge\><text|[definition: <reference|simple function
      integral>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>h d\<mu\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    The above together with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.603.249> and <reference|eq
    20.604.249>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      I\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>.
    </equation*>

    So we have proved that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>A<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq
      20.605.249>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    For the opposite inequality. Let <math|I\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
    d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
    we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>
    then there exist a <math|k\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    with <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|k<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    such that <math|I=<big|int><rsup|S>k d\<mu\>>. Now for <math|x\<in\>X> we
    have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|k<around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>k<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>k|)><around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>As <math|0\<leqslant\>k<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=0>
      so that

      <\equation*>
        k<around*|(|x|)>=0=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>k<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>k|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.606.249>k=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>k
    </equation>

    As <math|k\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> there exist
    by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] a
    <math|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, a <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint and a <math|<around*|{|\<beta\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.504.263>k=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    Then we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.607.249>k<below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.606.249>]>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|B<rsub|i>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      properties>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.506.263>l=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A,A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>
    <math|B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<Rightarrow\>A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    and <math|A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A> it follows that
    <math|<around*|{|A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
    [pairwise disjoint]. \ So using [theorem: <reference|simple function
    measurable (1)>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.608.249>l\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|]>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|x\<in\>A> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>>>Then
      <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>,A> so that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|l<around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A,A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>>>|<cell|\<beta\><rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function property>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.504.263>]>>>|<cell|k<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>>|<cell|f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|x\<nin\>A\\<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>><around*|(|A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>>>Then
      by [theorem: <reference|simple function property>]
      <math|l<around*|(|x|)>=0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>> [as
      <math|f> is non negative]
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.609.249>\<forall\>x\<in\>A<text| we have
      >l<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Further\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>k
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|[|eq:<text|
      <reference|eq 20.504.263>>|]>\<wedge\><text|[definition:
      <reference|simple function integral>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><around*|(|A<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|[|eq:<text|
      <reference|eq 20.506.263>>|]>\<wedge\><text|[definition:
      <reference|simple function integral>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>l
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Combining the above with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.608.249> and
    <reference|eq 20.609.249>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      I\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>A<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    hence\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>A<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.605.249>] gives\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>A<text|
      we have >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Finally substituting the above in [eqs: <reference|eq 20.598.249> and
    <reference|eq 20.599.249>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|non negative integral sub measure space (3)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>]
    and the fact that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
    (1)>] <math|f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|non negative integral (1)>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
    </equation*>

    \;
  </proof>

  The previous theorem shows how we can calulate the integral of the
  restriction of a function to a measurable subset. The next theorem show how
  we can calculate the integral of the extension of a function on a
  measurable subset.

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral sub measure space (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      <rigid|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    where

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}><text|
      see [definition: <reference|measure sub measure space>]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><text| is the restriction of
      >\<mu\><text| to >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by >f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>><text| [see
      definition: <reference|measurable function extension definition>]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function extension>] that

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    which proves the first part of the lemma. Now for the second part note
    that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)><below|=|x\<in\>A>1\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=0=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    which prove that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|measurable function extension
      definition>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|measurable function extension
      definition>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Integral of non negative measurable functions as a limit of
  the integral of simple functions>

  \;

  Every non negative measurable function can be written as a limit of
  increasing simple measurable functions. We will use this fact to write
  <math|<big|int><rsup|+>> in terms of a limit of <math|<big|int><rsup|S>> of
  simple functions.

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative function as limit of simple functions>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then there exists a sequence <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|[>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists and >f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    In other words we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The strategy of the proof is, given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, to divide
    <math|f<around*|(|X|)><big|cap><around*|[|0,n|[>> in
    <math|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>> intervals of the form
    <math|<around*|[|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|[>> and
    aproximate <math|f> by <math|f<rsub|n>> where

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>><text|
      if >x\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)><big|cap><around*|[|0,n|[><text| where
      >k<text| satisfies> x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|n<text|
      if >x\<in\>X\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>|)>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    and prove that <math|f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>>
    \ The following illustrates this for <math|n=2> and <math|X=\<bbb-R\>>
    here

    <\equation*>
      <block*|<tformat|<twith|table-lborder|0ln>|<table|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=0/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|1/4,2/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=1/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|2/4,3/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=2/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|3/4,4/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=3/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|4/4,5/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=4/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|5/4,6/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=5/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|6/4,7/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=6/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|7/4,8/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=7/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|8/4,\<infty\>|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=2>>>>>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|2>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|0|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|1|4>,<frac|2|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|1|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|2|4>,<frac|3|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|2|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|3|4>,<frac|4|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|3|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|4|4>,<frac|5|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|4|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|5|4>,<frac|6|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|5|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|6|4>,<frac|7|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|7|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|7|4>,<frac|8|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|7|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|8|4>,\<infty\>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|8|4><eq-number><label|eq
      20.490.249>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    \;

    <small-figure|<with|gr-mode|<tuple|edit|line>|gr-frame|<tuple|scale|1cm|<tuple|0.410009gw|0.539996gh>>|gr-geometry|<tuple|geometry|1par|0.6par>|gr-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|2>|gr-edit-grid-aspect|<tuple|<tuple|axes|none>|<tuple|1|none>|<tuple|10|none>>|gr-edit-grid|<tuple|empty>|gr-edit-grid-old|<tuple|cartesian|<point|0|0>|2>|gr-auto-crop|true|gr-color|red|gr-snap|<tuple>|gr-arrow-begin|o|gr-line-width|2ln|gr-arrow-end|\<less\>|<graphics||<text-at|<math|>|<point|-4.4|0.4>>|<with|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-4|-3.2>|<point|8.0|-3.2>>>|<with|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-4|-2.4>|<point|8.0|-2.4>>>|<with|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-4|-0.8>|<point|8.0|-0.8>>>|<with|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-4|1.6>|<point|8.0|1.6>>>|<with|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-4|2.4>|<point|8.0|2.4>>>|<text-at|<math|1/4>|<point|-5|-3.2>>|<text-at|<math|2/4>|<point|-5|-2.4>>|<text-at|<math|3/4>|<point|-5|-1.6>>|<text-at|4/4|<point|-5|-0.8>>|<text-at|<math|5/4>|<point|-5|0>>|<text-at|<math|6/4>|<point|-5|0.8>>|<text-at|<math|7/4>|<point|-5|1.6>>|<text-at|<math|8/4>|<point|-5|2.4>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|0/4>|<point|-5|-4>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<less\>|dash-style|10|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|-4|-4>|<point|-3.6|-4.0>>>|<with|color|red|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|arrow-end|\<less\>|<line|<point|-3.6|-3.2>|<point|-3.0|-3.2>>>|<with|color|red|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|arrow-end|\<less\>|<line|<point|-3|-2.4>|<point|-2.0|-2.4>>>|<with|color|red|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|arrow-end|\<less\>|<line|<point|-2|-1.6>|<point|2.0|-1.6>>>|<with|color|red|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|arrow-end|\<less\>|<line|<point|2|-0.8>|<point|3.2|-0.8>>>|<with|color|red|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|arrow-end|\<less\>|<line|<point|3.2|0>|<point|4.0|0.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|7.6|1.6>|<point|7.6|-4.0>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|<text-at|<math|<with|font-series|bold|>>|<point|-2|3.4>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|7.2|2.4>|<point|7.2|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|4.66401|2.4>|<point|4.6|-4.0>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<less\>|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|4.66401|2.4>|<point|7.2|2.4>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-4|0.8>|<point|8.0|0.8>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<less\>|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|7.6|0>|<point|8.0|0.0>>>|<with|line-width|2ln|<smooth|<point|-4|-4>|<point|-3.6|-3.2>|<point|-3.0|-2.4>|<point|-2.0|-1.6>|<point|2.0|-0.8>|<point|3.2|0.0>|<point|4.0|0.8>|<point|4.6|2.4>|<point|6.0|3.2>|<point|7.2|2.4>|<point|7.6|0.8>|<point|8.0|0.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<smooth|<point|3.98431|0.8>|<point|3.92462627331658|-4.08966133086387>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<smooth|<point|3.2|0>|<point|3.17809895488821|-4.08966133086387>>>|<with|color|<pattern|/usr/local/share/TeXmacs/misc/patterns/vintage/wood-xdark.png|1gu|>|dash-style|10|<smooth|<point|8|0>|<point|-4.01213784892181|-0.0230519910041011>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|1.99945|-0.8>|<point|1.96008069850509|-4.07001587511576>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-0.0267421371674196|-1.19818878909354>|<point|0.0|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-2|-1.6>|<point|-1.96901045111787|-4.07001587511576>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-3.03442803818049|-2.4>|<point|-3.0|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-3.6202|-3.2>|<point|-3.6|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|4.26597|1.6>|<point|4.18616880539754|-4.06682431538563>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|7.47761|1.56977>|<point|7.49818097631962|-3.98453829871676>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<less\>|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|4.0216|0.808622>|<point|4.26845482206641|0.829193676412224>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<less\>|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|4.3096|1.56977>|<point|4.65931340124355|1.56976782643207>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<less\>|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|7.21018|1.59034>|<point|7.41589495965075|1.59033933059929>>>|<with|color|red|arrow-end|\<less\>|line-width|2ln|arrow-begin|o|<line|<point|7.45704|0.829194>|<point|7.55989548882127|0.808622172245006>>>>>|<label|figure
    20.6.234><math|f> apprimated by <math|f<rsub|2>>>

    \;

    Now for the details of the proof. Define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.500.231><around*|{|I<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|\<bbb-N\>|)><text|
      by >I<rsub|n>=<around*|{|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|i\<leqslant\>n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|}>=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|}>
    </equation>

    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. Define for <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.501.231>A<rsub|n,k>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|n>|}>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|[>|)>
    </equation>

    Then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\>A<rsub|n,k>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>x\<wedge\>x\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|f\<geqslant\><frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>|}>\<wedge\>x\<in\><around*|{|f\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|f\<geqslant\><frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>|}><big|cap><around*|{|f\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|}>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.502.231>A<rsub|n,k>=<around*|{|f\<geqslant\><frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>|}><big|cap><around*|{|f\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|}>
    </equation>

    As <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] that
    <math|<around*|{|f\<geqslant\><frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>|}>,<around*|{|f\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.503.231>\<forall\>k\<in\>I<rsub|n> we have
      A<rsub|n,k>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|>
    </equation>

    As <math|I<rsub|n>=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|}>> is
    finite hence countable it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure
    countable union is in sigma algebra>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.504.231><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|l,k\<in\>I<rsub|n>> with <math|l\<neq\>k> then we may assume
    that <math|l\<less\>k\<Rightarrow\>l\<leqslant\>k-1> [otherwise
    interchange <math|l> and <math|k>]. Assume that
    <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n,k><big|cap>A<rsub|n,l>> then
    <math|<frac|l-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|l|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>>
    leading to the contradiction <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>,
    hence the assumption is wrong and we have that
    <math|><math|A<rsub|n,k><big|cap>A<rsub|n,l>=\<varnothing\>>. In other
    words we have proved that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.505.231><around*|{|A<rsub|n,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    Given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|n,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >B<rsub|n,k>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|n,k><text| if
      >k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|}>=I<rsub|n>>>|<row|<cell|X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>|)><text|
      if >k=n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1>>>>><below|\<in\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.503.231>, <reference|eq 20.504.231>]>>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|f<rsub|n,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      by >f<rsub|n,k>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>><text|
      if >k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|k>|}>=I<rsub|n>>>|<row|<cell|n<text|
      if >k=n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1>>>>>
    </equation*>

    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.371.262>] together with [eq:
    <reference|eq 20.505.231>] it follows then that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.503.261><around*|{|B<rsub|n,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and ><big|sqcup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1|}>>B<rsub|n,k>=X
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.504.261>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by >f<rsub|n>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1>f<rsub|n,k>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,B<rsub|n,k>>
    </equation>

    Using then [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] together
    with [eq: <reference|eq 20.503.261>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.507.231><around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<exists\>k\<in\>I<rsub|n><text|>> with
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n,k>>>Then as <math|A<rsub|n,k>=B<rsub|n,k>> we
      have by [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.503.261>, <reference|eq 20.504.261>] that
      <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|n,k>=<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>>.

      <item*|<math|\<forall\>k\<in\>I<rsub|n><text| we have
      >x\<nin\>A<rsub|n,k>>>Then <math|x\<in\>X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>|)>=B<rsub|n,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1>>
      so that by \ [theorem: <reference|simple function property>] and [eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.503.261>, <reference|eq 20.504.261>] we have
      <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|n,n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>+1>=n>.
    </description>

    To summarize we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.506.231>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>><text|
      if <math|\<exists\>k\<in\>I<rsub|n>> with
      >x\<in\>A<rsub|n,k>>>|<row|<cell|n<text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>|)>>>>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and take <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,n|[>|)>>
    then <math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n>. Define
    <math|B<rsub|x>=<around*|{|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|}>\<subseteq\>I<rsub|n>>
    a finite set. As <math|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>\<in\>I<rsub|n>> and
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n=<frac|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|n>>> we
    have that <math|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>\<in\>B<rsub|x>> so that
    <math|B<rsub|x>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>. Hence
    <math|l=min<around*|(|B<rsub|x>|)>> exist. There are two possible cases
    for <math|l>

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|l=1>>Then <math|<frac|1-1|2<rsup|n>>=0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|1|2<rsup|n>>>
      so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n,1>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>>

      <item*|<math|1\<less\>l>>Then <math|1\<leqslant\>l-1\<less\>l\<leqslant\>n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>>
      so that <math|l-1\<in\>I<rsub|n>>. As
      <math|l-1\<less\>l=min<around*|(|B<rsub|x>|)>> we must have that
      <math|<frac|l-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|l|2<rsup|n>>>
      so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n,l>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>>
    </description>

    So we have proved that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,n|[>|)>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>>.
    For the opposite inclusion, we have for <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>> that
    <math|<rigid|A<rsub|n,k>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|[>|)><below|\<subseteq\>|0\<leqslant\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>|2<rsup|n>>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>>\<Rightarrow\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>\<subseteq\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>>.
    Summarized we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.508.231>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,n|[>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have the
    following possible cases

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n>>Then
      <math|x\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,n|[>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.508.231>]>><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>>
      hence there exist a <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>> [which is unique by [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.505.231>]] such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n,k>>, hence
      by [eq: <reference|eq 20.506.231>] we have
      <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>>. As
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n,k>> we have also
      <math|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)><text| and
        ><text| >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|n\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>Then
      <math|x\<nin\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,n|[>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.508.231>]>><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>>
      so that <math|x\<in\>X\\<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>>A<rsub|n,k>>
      and from [eq: <reference|eq 20.506.231>] we have that

      <\equation*>
        <text|<math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=n\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    Summarizing the above we have three results

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.509.231>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.510.231>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|,
      <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with >f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n<text|
      there exist a >k\<in\>I<rsub|n><text| with
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.511.231>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|,
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| with
      >n\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)><text| we have
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=n
    </equation>

    Next we show that <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
    is increasing. Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have for
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n>>Then
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n,n+1> so using [eq: <reference|eq
      20.510.231>] there exists <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>,l\<in\>I<rsub|n+1>>
      such that

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>\<wedge\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|l-1|2<rsup|n+1>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|l|2<rsup|n+1>>
      </equation*>

      From this it follows that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<less\><frac|l|2<rsup|n+1>>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|<frac|2\<cdot\>k-2|2<rsup|n+1>>\<less\><frac|l|2<rsup|n+1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|2\<cdot\>k-2\<less\>l>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|2\<cdot\>k-2\<leqslant\>l-1>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|<frac|2\<cdot\>k-2|2<rsup|n+1>>\<leqslant\><frac|l-1|2<rsup|n+1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|l-1|2<rsup|n+1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|n\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n+1>>Then by [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.511.231>] <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=n> and by
      [eq: <reference|eq 20.510.231>] there exist a <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|n+1>>
      such that <math|f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=*<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n+1>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n+1>>>.
      As <math|n\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> it follows that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|n\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n+1>>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n+1>\<less\>k>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|n\<cdot\>2<rsup|n+1>\<leqslant\>k-1>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|n\<leqslant\><frac|k-1|2<rsup|n+1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|n+1\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>Then
      <math|n,n+1\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> so that by \ [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.511.231>] <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=n\<less\>n+1=f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>
      so that we have again\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.500.232>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Using then [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>] on the above it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.501.232>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X>. Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.509.231>] we have that
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.502.232>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inclusion. Assume that
    <math|><math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>|}>|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>|}>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
    </equation*>

    and for <math|f<around*|(|x|)>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>>>By [theorem:
      <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>|}>|)>\<less\>n\<less\>\<infty\>=f<around*|(|x|)>>.
      Using then [eq: <reference|eq 20.511.231>] we have
      <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=n> so that
      <math|n=f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>|}>|)>\<less\>n>
      leading to the contradiction <math|n\<less\>n>.

      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>As
      <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      there exist by [theorem: <reference|complex 0\<less\>x^n\<less\>e>] a
      <math|n<rsub|1>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <frac|1|2<rsup|n<rsub|1>>>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      Further using [theorem: <reference|complex Archimedean property
      consequence (1)>] there exist a <math|n<rsub|2>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such
      that <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n<rsub|2>>. Let
      <rigid|<math|n=max<around*|(|n<rsub|1>,n<rsub|2>|)>>> then
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>n> so that by [eq: <reference|eq
      20.510.231>] there exist a <math|k\<in\>I<rsub|n>> such that

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>.
      </equation*>

      Then

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<less\><frac|k|2<rsup|n>>-<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n>>\<less\><frac|1|2<rsup|n<rsub|1>>>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|<rigid|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<less\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>
      which as <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
      is a contradiction.\ 
    </description>

    As in all cases we reach a contradiction the assumption is invalid and we
    must have that <math|<rigid|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><around*|\||n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|\<nobracket\>>|}>|)>>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.502.232>] proves that
    <math|<rigid|f<around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><around*|\||n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|\<nobracket\>>|}>|)>>>,
    hence using [eq: <reference|eq 20.501.232>] we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist and >f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We set out now to provide a alternative definition of
  <math|<big|int><rsup|+>>. As we can approximate <math|f> by a sequence of
  simple functions <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> we
  will approximate <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>> by
  <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>>. The following illustrates this
  for <math|n=2> and <math|X=\<bbb-R\>> here

  <\equation*>
    <block*|<tformat|<twith|table-lborder|0ln>|<table|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=0/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|1/4,2/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=1/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|2/4,3/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=2/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|3/4,4/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=3/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|4/4,5/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=4/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|5/4,6/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=5/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|6/4,7/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=6/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|7/4,8/4|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=7/4>>|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|\<in\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|8/4,\<infty\>|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=2>>>>>
  </equation*>

  so that\ 

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|2>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|0|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|1|4>,<frac|2|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|1|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|2|4>,<frac|3|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|2|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|3|4>,<frac|4|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|3|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|4|4>,<frac|5|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|4|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|5|4>,<frac|6|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|5|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|6|4>,<frac|7|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|7|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|7|4>,<frac|8|4>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|7|4>+\<cal-X\><rsub|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|8|4>,\<infty\>|[>|)>>\<cdot\><frac|8|4>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  and the simple \ integral becomes\ 

  <\eqnarray*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|0|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|4>|[>|)>|)>+<frac|1|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|1|4>,<frac|2|4>|[>|)>|)>+<frac|2|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|2|4>,<frac|3|4>|[>|)>|)>+<rigid|<frac|3|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|3|4>,<frac|4|4>|[>|)>|)>>+<frac|4|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|4|4>,<frac|5|4>|[>|)>|)>+<frac|5|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|5|4>,<frac|6|4>|[>|)>|)>+<rigid|<frac|6|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|6|4>,<frac|7|4>|[>|)>|)>>+<frac|7|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|7|4>,<frac|8|4>|[>|)>|)>+<frac|8|4>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|8|4>,\<infty\>|[>|)>|)>>>>>
  </eqnarray*>

  which is illustrated in the below figure

  \;

  <\note>
    For the integral <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>> to make sense
    the sets <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|[|<frac|k-1|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|k|2<rsup|n>>|[>>
    must be measurable, which is the reason why we must require that <math|f>
    is measurable. The above figure suggest that the surface beneath <math|f>
    is aproximated from below by a increasing sequence
    <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>> this will provides us with a
    alternative definition for <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>>.
  </note>

  \;

