  grey|line-width|0.5ln|arrow-begin|o|<cline|<point|7.6|-4>|<point|7.6|0.0>|<point|8.0|0.0>|<point|8.0|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-3.6|-4>|<point|-3.6|-3.2>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-3|-4>|<point|-3.0|-2.4>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-2|-1.6>|<point|-2.0|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|2|-0.8>|<point|2.0|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|3.2|0>|<point|3.2|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|4|0.8>|<point|4.0|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|4.20104|1.54835>|<point|4.2|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|4.6|2.4>|<point|4.6|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|7.2|2.4>|<point|7.2|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|7.4|1.6>|<point|7.4|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|7.6|0.8>|<point|7.6|-4.0>>>|<with|color|blue|dash-style|10|<line|<point|8|0>|<point|8.0|-4.0>>>|<with|dash-style|10|<line|<point|-2|-1.6>|<point|-4.0|-1.6>>>>>|<label|figure
  20.7.234><math|f> apprimated by <math|f<rsub|2>>>

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral limit>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    then

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\><text| exist>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|\<cal-S\>>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|non negative
      integral is a extension>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
      sequence exist>] that <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>
      exist, as is needed for the requirements of this theorem.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)><rsub|>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.488.231>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.489.231>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Further using [theorem: <reference|simple function integral properties>]
    we have that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k+1>
    d\<mu\>> which by \ [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing /
    decreasing sequence exist>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.490.231><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\><text| exists and ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    So that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.791.231>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.489.231>]
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    and <math|f<rsub|k>\<in\>S<rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> we have that
    <math|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
    d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
    >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]> and
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    hence we have by [definition: <reference|non negative integral>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.492.231>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      ><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    So <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
    d\<mu\>> is a upper bound for <math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
    d\<mu\>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>>from which it follows that
    \ \ \ <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
    d\<mu\>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
    d\<mu\>> which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.490.231>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.493.231><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inequality. Let <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    be such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
    <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>.
    Then using [theorem: <reference|simple function integral properties>] it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.494.231>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      <math|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then given <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.495.231><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>*<around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|k+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    so that by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.496.231><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.497.231>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    we have for <math|g<around*|(|x|)>> either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|g<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>Then
      <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.488.231>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
      so that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|<rigid|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>>
      hence <math|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>|)>=f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.488.231>]>><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)><below|=|hypothesis>g<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>Then
      by [eq: <reference|eq 20.488.231>] <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
      so that there exist a <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<rsub|k><around*|(|x|)>> hence
      <math|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|k><around*|(|k|)>|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>>.
      Hence <rigid|<math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>>>
      so that <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
      which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.497.231>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        g<around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So in all cases <math|g<around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
    which by [eq: <reference|eq 20.496.231>] proves
    \ <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>>
    hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.498.231><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>=g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|[>
    </equation>

    The above and [eq: <reference|eq 20.495.231>] allows us to apply
    [theorem: <reference|simple function integral monotone convergence
    theorem>] to get that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.499.231><below|lim|k\<rightarrowlim\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\><text| exists and ><below|lim|k\<rightarrowlim\>!><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    By [eq: <reference|eq 20.494.231>] <math|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>
    d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k> d\<mu\>> so that by
    [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>] we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|g\<wedge\>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.499.231>] gives

    <\equation*>
      <text| ><big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    So <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
    d\<mu\>> is a upper bound for <math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
    d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| with
    >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>
    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| with
      >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<circ\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\><rsub|>
    </equation*>

    As <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
    d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g
    d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| with
    >g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<circ\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\><rsub|>
    </equation*>

    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.493.231>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|k>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.490.231>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|k>
      d\<mu\>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we state a alternative way to define the integral
  <math|<big|int><rsup|+>>. The benefit of this alternative definition is
  that it is based on a limiting process making it easier to lift properties
  of <math|<big|int><rsup|S>> to <math|<big|int><rsup|+>>. In this theorem
  the measurability of <math|f> is a requirement.

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral alternative>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then there exist a sequence <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists and >f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\><text| exist and ><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|non negative function as limit of simple
    functions>] there exists a <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>
    and <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<Rightarrow\>f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>>.
    Using this result together with the previous theorem [theorem:
    <reference|non negative integral limit>] proves that
    <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
    d\<mu\>> exists and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Properties of the integral of non negative measurable
  functions>

  \;

  We prove now that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>> has similar properties as
  <math|<big|int><rsup|S>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral zero a.e. function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<mu\>=0>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<equallim\><rsub|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>

    Let <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> be such that
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>.
    Then for

    <\equation*>
      x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<equallim\><rsub|g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    we have <math|0\<less\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> so
    that <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
    Hence <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>
    which proves that <math|g=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Applying then [theorem:
    <reference|simple function integral properties> (5)] results in
    <math|<big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>=0>. So we have proved that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>g d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text|
      with >\<forall\>x\<in\>X g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>=<around*|{|0|}>
    </equation*>

    Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<big|int>g
      d\<mu\>\|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| with
      >\<forall\>x\<in\>X g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=0
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral scalar product, sum inequality>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> we have
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>>.

      <item><math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f+g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>> then
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have that
      \ <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|non negative integral (1)>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>>\ 
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral alternative>]
    there exists <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,<around*|{|g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
    <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>,
    <math|g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>> and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.515.232>f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><text|,
      >g=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><text|,
      ><big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\><text| and ><big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Using [theorems: <reference|measurable function product with
      scalar>, <reference|simple function integral properties>] and the fact
      that <math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>> it follows that

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Given <math|x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|0\<less\>\<alpha\>>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.515.232>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral limit>] we have
      that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>
      d\<mu\>> exists and

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function integral
        properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.515.232>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>Using [theorems: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>,
      <reference|simple function integral properties>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><around*|{|f<rsub|n>+g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Given <math|x\<in\>X> we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        ><around*|(|f<rsub|n>+g<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|f<rsub|n+1>+g<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.515.232>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>+<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|f<rsub|n>+g<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral limit>] we have
      that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f<rsub|n>+g<rsub|n>|)>
      d\<mu\>> exists and\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f+g|)>
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f<rsub|n>+g<rsub|n>|)>
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|simple function integral
        properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>+<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.515.232>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>
        d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n> d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>Assume that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>. Let

      <\equation*>
        y\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>h
        d\<mu\>\|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
        we have >h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>
      </equation*>

      then there exist a <math|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      satisfying <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      \ <math|h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> such that
      <math|y=<big|int><rsup|S>h d\<mu\>>. As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>> we have also
      <math|h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>> it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        y\<in\><around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>h
        d\<mu\>\|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
        we have >h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>
      </equation*>

      Hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>h<space|1em>d\<mu\>\|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
        we have >h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>h<space|1em>d\<mu\>\|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
        we have >h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      So\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>h<space|1em>d\<mu\>\|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
        we have >h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|order sup,inf and inclusion>]>>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|S>h<space|1em>d\<mu\>\|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<wedge\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
        we have >h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>g
        d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)><text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> so that by (3)\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    \;
  </proof>

  Next we use induction to extend the last part of the previous theorem to a
  finite sum of measurable functions.

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral finite sum>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i> d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    \ 
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We use induction to prove this, so let\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      then ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><rigid|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>
      d\<mu\>>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>Let <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      and

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>
        d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>
        d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i> d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      proving that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then as <math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>> we have that
      <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
      hence

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>+f<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|<space|1em>[using
        [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality> (2)]>
      </equation*>

      Further we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|)>
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>+f<rsub|n+1>|)>
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>(2)]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>
        d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n+1>
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>
        d\<mu\>|)>+<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n+1>
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral and a.e. equality>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and let <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <rigid|<math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      H=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>=<around*|{|f\<neq\>g|}>
    </equation*>

    As <math|f,g > are <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable we have by [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function comparisation>] that
    \ <math|H\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Further as <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>=a.e. there
    exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such
    that <math|H\<subseteq\>N> hence <math|\<mu\><around*|(|H|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0>.
    Summarized we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.515.233>H\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and
      >\<mu\>*<around*|(|H|)>=0
    </equation>

    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      h:X\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text| by
      >h<around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text| if
      >x\<in\>H>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>X\\H>>>>>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>x>>Then if
      <math|t\<in\><around*|{|h\<gtr\>x|}>> we have
      <math|h<around*|(|t|)>\<gtr\>x\<geqslant\>0> so that <math|x\<in\>H>
      [otherwise <math|h<around*|(|t|)>=0\<ngtr\>0>] so that
      <math|<around*|{|h\<gtr\>x|}>\<subseteq\>H>. If <math|t\<in\>H> then
      <math|h<around*|(|t|)>=\<infty\>\<gtr\>x> so that
      <math|H\<subseteq\><around*|{|h\<gtr\>x|}>>. Summarized we have

      <\equation*>
        <around|{|h\<gtr\>x|}>=H\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|x\<less\>0>>Then if <math|t\<in\>X> we have either
      <math|t\<in\>H> so that <math|h<around*|(|t|)>=\<infty\>\<gtr\>x> or
      <math|t\<in\>X\\H> giving <math|h<around*|(|t|)>=0\<gtr\>x,> hence
      <math|X\<subseteq\><around*|{|h\<gtr\>x|}>\<subseteq\>X> proving\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|h\<gtr\>x|}>=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\> we have >
    <math|<around*|{|h\<gtr\>x|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> from which it follows,
    using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>] and the
    fact that <math|h<around*|(|X|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>, that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.516.233>h\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|>
    </equation>

    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>> by
      h<rsub|n>=n\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,H>
    </equation*>

    then by [example: <reference|non negative integral characteristics>] we
    have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.517.233>h<rsub|n>=n\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,H>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|S>h<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=n\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|H|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.515.233>]>>0
    </equation>

    Further we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.518.233>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>,\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      that >h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|n<text|
      if >x\<in\>H>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\H>>>>>\<leqslant\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|n+1<text|
      if >x\<in\>H>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\H>>>>>=h<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>H>>Then <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>n\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended lim n to infinite>]>>\<infty\>=h<around*|(|x|)>>.

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\H>>Then <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>0=0=h<around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    proving that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=h<around*|(|x|)>>.
    This fact together with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.517.233> and <reference|eq
    20.518.233>] allows us to use [theorem: <reference|non negative integral
    limit>] giving\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.519.233><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>h<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\><text| exist and ><big|int><rsup|+>h
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>h<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.517.233>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>0=0
    </equation>

    Further if we look again at the possibilities of <math|x\<in\>X> we have\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>H>>Then <math|h<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>> so that
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=g<around*|(|x|)>+\<infty\>=g<around*|(|x|)>+h<around*|(|x|)>>
      and <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=f<around*|(|x|)>+\<infty\>=f<around*|(|x|)>+h<around*|(|x|)>>
      so that <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>+h<around*|(|x|)>>
      and <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>+h<around*|(|x|)>>.

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\H>>Then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>+0=g<around*|(|x|)>+f<around*|(|x|)>>
      and <math|g<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>+0=f<around*|(|x|)>+h<around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>+h<around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>+h<around*|(|x|)>>.
    Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>h d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.519.233>]>><big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>h d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.519.233>]>><big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we prove that every non negative function with a finite integral is
  <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. finite. First we need a lemma.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.377.232>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then for\ 

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|t>=<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>t|}>\<equallim\><rsub|def><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>t|}>
    </equation*>

    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|t>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|t>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A<rsub|t>>f
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><frac|1|t>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]
      <math|A<rsub|t>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> as is requiered for the existence of
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A<rsub|t>>f d\<mu\>> in this lemma.
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Consider the function <math|t\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>>
    where <math|a<rsub|1>=t\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and
    <math|A<rsub|1>=A<rsub|t>> then as <math|A<rsub|t>=<around*|{|f\<geqslant\>t|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    [as <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.516.232>t\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|simple function measurable
      (1)>]>>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| and
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|t\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>a<rsub|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=t\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|t>|)>\<less\>\<infty\><rsup|>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A<rsub|t>>>Then
      <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> and as
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>><around*|(|x|)>=1> we have
      <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,At>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A<rsub|t>>>Then
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>><around*|(|x|)>=0> so that trivially
      <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    hence in all cases we have <math|0\<leqslant\>t\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>]

    <\equation*>
      t\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.516.232>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|t\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>>|)>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|t>>\<cdot\>f|)>
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|t>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A<rsub|t>>f
      d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><frac|1|t>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|non negative integral finite integral>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X:f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<neq\>\<infty\><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using [ theorem: <reference|measurable function comparisation>] we have
      that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>=<around*|{|f=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>|)>>
      is well defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> then
    <math|<frac|1|n>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> so using the above lemma [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.377.232>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>n|}>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|n>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    Now if <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>>
    we have <math|n\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=f<around*|(|x|)>> so that
    <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>n|}>>
    proving that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|f=\<infty\>|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>n|}>
    </equation*>

    As <math|<around*|{|f=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> [see theorem:
    <reference|measurable function comparisation>] we have

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>n|}>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|n>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    As <math|n> is chosen arbitrary and <math|0\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>> we have by [theorem:
    <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (2)>] that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<neq\>\<infty\><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Measure integral>

  <subsubsection|Definition of the measure integral>

  We extend now the integral <math|<big|int><rsup|+>> of non negative
  measurable functions to the measure integral <math|<big|int>> of measurable
  functions. To do this we write a measurable ffunction <math|f> as the
  difference of two non negative measurable functions <math|f<rsup|+>> and
  <math|f<rsup|->>, so <math|f=f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->> and define
  <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>> as <math|<rigid|<big|int>f
  d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+> d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
  d\<mu\>>>. Of course this only works if <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
  d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|-> d\<mu\>> is well defined, so we require
  that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+> d\<mu\>>,
  <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|-> d\<mu\>> are finite. First we define
  <math|f<rsup|+>>, <math|f<rsup|->>.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure integral f+ f->Let <math|X> be a set and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a function then we
    define:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f<rsup|+>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> by
      <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>

      <item><math|f<rsup|->:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> by
      <math|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|-f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|\||f|\|>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      by <math|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      From the definition it follows that
      <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>,
      <math|f<rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> an
      <math|<rigid|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>>.
    </note>
  </definition>

  We have the following alternative defintion for <math|f<rsup|+>>,
  <math|f<rsup|->> and <math|<around*|\||f|\|>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral f+ f- alternative>Let <math|X> be a set and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a function then we
    have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f<rsup|+>=f\<vee\>C<rsub|0>>

      <item><math|f<rsup|->=-<around*|(|f\<wedge\>C<rsub|0>|)>>

      <item><math|<around*|\||f|\|>=f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\>=<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
      so that <math|f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->> is well defined and

      <\equation*>
        f=f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|f\<vee\>C<rsub|0>|)><around*|(|x|)>>.
      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+>=f\<vee\>C<rsub|0>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>|)>=-<around*|(|f\<wedge\>C<rsub|0>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|-<around*|(|f\<wedge\>C<rsub|0>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|->=-<around*|(|f\<wedge\>C<rsub|0>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then for <math|f<around*|(|x|)>> we have
      either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>Then

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>+<around*|(|-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>+<around*|(|-0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>>Then

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>+<around*|(|-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0+<around*|(|-f<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </description>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|>=f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have the following cases for
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>>

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>>>Then

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=max<around*|(|\<infty\>,0|)>=\<infty\>=f<around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        and\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=-min<around*|(|\<infty\>,0|)>=0
        </equation*>

        so that <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|\<infty\>,0|)>\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
        and\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>-0=f<around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|f<rsup|><around*|(|x|)>=-\<infty\>>>Then

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=max<around*|(|-\<infty\>,0|)>=0
        </equation*>

        and\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=-min<around*|(|-\<infty\>,0|)>=\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        so that <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|0,\<circ\>|)>\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
        and\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=0-\<infty\>=-\<infty\>=f<around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then for
        <math|f<around*|(|x|)>> we have either:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>>Then we have\ 

          <\equation*>
            f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0
          </equation*>

          and

          <\equation*>
            f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=-f<around*|(|x|)>
          </equation*>

          so that <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|0,-f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
          and\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>=0-<around*|(|-f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>Then we have\ 

          <\equation*>
            f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>
          </equation*>

          and

          <\equation*>
            f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=-0=0
          </equation*>

          so that <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
          and\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>-0=f<around*|(|x|)>
          </equation*>
        </description>
      </description>
    </enumerate>

    So in all cases we have have \ <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}>>
    and <math|<rigid|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> <math|f<rsup|+>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> then
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><infix-and>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<Rightarrow\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<Rightarrow\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<wedge\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      so that <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>,<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      hence <math|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->,<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        and <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        [see example: <reference|measurable function constant function>] it
        follows from [theorems: <reference|measurable function min and max>,
        <reference|measurable function product with scalar> and
        <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>] that

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+>=f\<vee\>C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and >f<rsup|->=-<around*|(|f\<wedge\>C<rsub|0>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>

        which as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
        <math|0\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>
        proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>As <math|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|-1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        and <math|the fact f<below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|measure
        integral f+ f- alternative>]>>f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->> it follows from
        [theorem: <reference|measurable function properties (1)>] that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows from (1) that <math|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>]
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|><below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|measure
      integral f+ f- alternative>]>>f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|0\<leqslant\><around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral f+ f- properties>Let <math|X> be a set and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>,
    <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> be functions then
    we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> then
      <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>>
      and <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-1>>

      <item>If <math|\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,0|]>> then
      <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>=-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsup|>>
      and <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->=-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>>

      <item>If <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      then we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|f<rsup|+>+g<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x|)>>

        <item><math|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|f<rsup|->+g<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>>
      </enumerate>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
      and <math|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>

      <item>If <math|A\<subseteq\>X> then
      <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>>
      and <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->=<around*|(|f<rsup|-1>|)><rsub|\|A>>

      <item>If <math|A\<subseteq\>X> then
      <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>Y>|)><rsup|+>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>Y>>
      and <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->=<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>>
      [see definition: <reference|measurable function extension definition>]

      <item>If <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      then <math|f=f<rsup|+>> and <math|f<rsup|->=C<rsub|0>>

      <item>If <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      then <math|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|+>=f\<cdot\>g<rsup|+>> and
      <math|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|->=f\<cdot\>g<rsup|->>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>0>>then
        <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>0>

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|-f<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->*<around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item*|<math|0\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>then
        <math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|a\<cdot\><around*|(|-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </description>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then for <math|x> we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>0>>then
        <math|0\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item*|<math|0\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>then
        \ <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|a\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </description>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then

      <\enumerate>
        <item>As <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>
        and <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>
        we have\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        which as <math|0\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>
        proves that <math|><math|max<around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>
        so that\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item>Then <math|-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
        and <math|-g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
        so that\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        which as <math|-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>0>
        proves that <math|-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>
        so that\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </enumerate>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
      and <math|0\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>> so that

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>
      </equation*>

      Further <math|-f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<Rightarrow\>-<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
      and <math|-<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>0> so that
      <math|-<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>,
      hence

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>A\<subseteq\>X> then
      <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)><infix-and><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A>-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>A><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A><around*|(|x|)>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A><text|
        and ><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->=<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>A\<subseteq\>X> then we must consider two cases:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        hence we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><text|
          and ><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->=<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|0,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|0,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        hence we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><text|
          and ><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->=<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have, as
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>,
      that<space|1em><math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> hence

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=f<around*|(|x|)><text|
        and >f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=f<rsup|+><text| and >f<rsup|->=C<rsub|0>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then for <math|g<around*|(|x|)>> we have
      either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>>Then as
        <math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> we have
        <math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>
        so that\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|-min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|+>=f\<cdot\>g<rsup|+><text| and
          ><around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|->=f\<cdot\>g<rsup|->
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>>Then as
        <math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>> we have
        <math|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>
        so that\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-min<around*|(|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|-g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|-min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|+>=f\<cdot\>g<rsup|+><text| and
          ><around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><rsup|->=f\<cdot\>g<rsup|->
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We are ready now to the define the <math|\<mu\>>-measure integral and
  <math|\<mu\>>-integrable functions [the functions for which the
  <math|\<mu\>>-measure integral is defined].

  <\definition>
    <label|measure integral><index|<math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>>><index|measure
    integral>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be
    a measure space then <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    is <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<mu\>>-integrable> if\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+> d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|,
      ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|-> d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    The set of all <math|\<mu\>>-integrable function graphs is noted as
    <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|->f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and the set of all real valued <math|\<mu\>>-integrable function graphs
    is noted as <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    hence\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|->f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<subseteq\>>|<cell|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then the measure integral <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>> is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are
      measurable>] that <math|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Hence <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|->f<rsup|->d\<mu\>> are well defined. Finally
      the condition <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      ensures that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>>
      is well defined.
    </note>

    <\note>
      For the measure space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-L\>,\<lambda\>|\<rangle\>>>
      and <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-L\><rsup|n>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<rangle\>>>
      the measure integral is named the Lebesgue integral.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\example>
    <label|measure integral of zero function (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=0>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have that
    <math|<around*|(|C<rsup|><rsub|0>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>
    and also \ <math|<rigid|<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>,0<rsub|>|)>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)><rsup|+>=C<rsub|0>=<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)><rsup|->
    </equation*>

    Hence we have <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|C<rsup|><rsub|0>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
    <reference|non negative integral zero a.e.
    function>]>>0\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example: <reference|non negative
    integral zero a.e. function>]>><big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>=0>.
    As further <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    [see theorem: <reference|measurable function constant function>] it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      and ><big|int>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=0
    </equation*>

    \;
  </proof>

  The following alternative definition will be a usefull alternative for the
  definition of the measure integral.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral alternative (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then for <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> we
    have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Updownarrow\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<exists\>f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      with ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\><text|
      such that >f=f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Further if <math|\<exists\>f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
    with ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\><text|
    such that >f=f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      The condition <math|f=f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>> is equivalent with
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\><around*|(|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\<times\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>\\<around*|{|<around*|(|-\<infty\>,\<infty\>|)>,<around*|(|\<infty\>,-\<infty\>|)>|}><text|
        and >f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>>
      then by [definition: <reference|measure integral>] and [theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>] we have that for
      <math|f<rsub|1>=f<rsup|+>> and <math|f<rsub|2>=f<rsup|->> that
      <math|f=f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>> and <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>>.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>As <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      with <math|f=f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>> it follows from [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function properties (1)>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.548.282>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>
      </equation>

      and using [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>]
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.549.282>f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>Then

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
          <reference|extended 0\<less\>=x and 0\<less\>=y then
          x-y\<less\>=x>]>>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        and

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0<below|\<leqslant\>|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)><text|
          and >f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>>Then

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0<below|\<leqslant\>|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        and\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=-f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|2><rsup|><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
          <reference|extended 0\<less\>=x and 0\<less\>=y then
          x-y\<less\>=x>]>>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)><text|
          and >f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      Using then the above and [eq: <reference|eq 20.549.282>] allows us to
      apply [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
      inequality>] giving

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.550.282><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
        and ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      Combing the above with [eq: <reference|eq 20.548.282>] it follows by
      definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    Now for the last part of the theorem. As <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>
    d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> and <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>
    d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> it follows from [theorem: <reference|non
    negative integral finite integral>] that

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    Take

    <\equation*>
      N=N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    then we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0+0>
    so that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0
    </equation*>

    Hence if <math|x\<in\>X\\N\<Rightarrow\>x\<nin\>N<rsub|1>\<wedge\>x\<nin\>N<rsub|2>>
    then <math|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    so that <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>,
    by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>
    from which it follows that <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>.
    To summarize we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X\\N<text| \ >f<rsub|><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
    </equation*>

    From the above and <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X\\N<text| we have
      >f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    So that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>|)>*<around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>X\\N>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>N>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>X\\N>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>N>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    whcih proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    As by [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>]
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>>,
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>>,
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->>,
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    we have by [theorem <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,N>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    Combining this with [theorems: <reference|almost everywhere and
    characteristic>,<reference|non negative integral and a.e. equality>]
    gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    As by \ [eq: <reference|eq 20.550.282>] al the integrals are finite we
    finally have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|definitions><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We show now that <math|<big|int>> is a extension of
  <math|<big|int><rsup|S>> and almost a extension of <math|<big|int><rsup|+>>
  [we need a extra condition].

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral extension>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text| and
        ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      satisfies <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> then

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> then
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <rigid|<math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \;

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> we
      have that <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      and <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable, hence
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> further by
      [definition: <reference|simple function integral>]

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|S>f d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation*>

      From [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>] it
      follows that

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+>=f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral is a
        extension>]>>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><infix-and>f<rsup|->=C<rsub|0>
      </equation*>

      then <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\><math|<below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|non negative integral
      is a extension>]>><big|int><rsup|S>f<rsup|+> d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<mu\>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral constant>]>>0\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>=0\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>.
      So we have proved that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text| and
        ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-0\<equallim\><rsub|f<rsup|+>=f><big|int><rsup|S>f
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subset\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Further by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+>=f<text| and >f<rsup|->=C<rsub|0>
      </equation*>

      As <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      \ and <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral constant>]>><rigid|0\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>=0\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]><text| and
        ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f
        d\<mu\>-0=<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> we
      have by definition that <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>,
      further from <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have also that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>. So the conditions for <math|<around*|(|2|)>>
      are satisfied from which it follows that <math|<big|int>f
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Integration on measurable subspaces>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure integral (1)><index|<math|<big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    is <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<mu\>>-integrable>
    <with|font-series|bold|over <math|A>> if if
    <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f> is
    <math|\<mu\>>-integrable> or equivalently

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|,
      ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->
      d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    The set of all <math|\<mu\>>-integrable function graphs is noted as
    <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    hence\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|{|f\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>\|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around*|{|f\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><rsup|X>\|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|-><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->
      d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>|}>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then the measure integral <math|<big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>> is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
      it follows that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f=f>. So that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-L\><rsub|X><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>
        d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|-><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f<rsup|>|)><rsup|->
        d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>|}>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|->f<rsup|->
        d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>|}>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-L\><rsub|X><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>=\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><rsub|X>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int>f
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral to measure over>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>.
    As <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable
    function charasteristic product>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.517.232>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>(8)]>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>(8)]>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>(8)]>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=0\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=0\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>(8)]>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=0\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=0\<leqslant\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    hence in all cases we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    Applying then [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product,
    sum inequality>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.518.232><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
      and >0\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    By [eq: <reference|eq 20.517.232>, <reference|eq 20.518.232>] it follows
    from [definition: <reference|measure integral>] that
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    so that by [definition: <reference|measure integral (1)>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral on sub measure spaces>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|<around*|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>|\<rangle\>>>
    a measure subspace of <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    [see theorem: <reference|measure measure on A\|B>] then we have for
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Then the following are equivalent

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        [meaning <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><infix-or>equivalently
        >f is <math|\<mu\>>-integrable over <math|A>]

        <item><math|f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        [meaning <math|f<rsub|\|A>> is <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>-integrable]
      </enumerate>

      Further in case of <math|<around*|(|a|)>> [and thus also case
      <math|<around*|(|b|)>>] we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
      </equation*>

      <item>The following are equivalent\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

        <item><math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      </enumerate>

      Further in case of <math|<around*|(|a|)>> [and thus also of (b)] we
      have

      <\equation*>
        <big|int>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>=<big|int>f
        d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|a\<Rightarrow\>b>>As
        <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        we have that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f> is
        <math|\<mu\>>-integrable, which by definition means that

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.607.250>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theor:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f- are
          measurable>]>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation>

        and\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.608.250><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        By [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>(8)] we
        have <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>f|)><rsup|+>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><rsub|>>
        and <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>f|)><rsup|->=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|>>
        which after substituting this result in [eq: <reference|eq
        20.607.250>, <reference|eq 20.608.250>] gives\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.550.263>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>,\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<cal-A\>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.551.263><big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<cal-A\>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.552.263><big|int><rsup|->\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        Using then [theorem: <reference|non negative integral sub measure
        space (1)>] on the above three equations gives\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.553.263><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>,<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.554.263><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.555.263><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>(5)] we
        have that <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>=<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>\<wedge\><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>=<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|-1>>
        which after substituting this result in the previous three equations
        gives\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.556.263><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>|]>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.557.263><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        <\equation*>
          \;
        </equation*>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.558.263><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        Hence we have by definition that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>

        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
          <reference|eq 20.557.263>, <reference|eq
          20.558.263>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
          <reference|eq 20.554.263>, <reference|eq
          20.555.263>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<equallim\>|<text|[eqs:
          <reference|eq 20.551.263>, <reference|eq
          20.552.263>]>><rsub|>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>f
          d\<mu\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        gving\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>f<rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|b\<Rightarrow\>a>>Because <math|f<rsub|\|A>> is
        <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>-integrable we have by
        definition that

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.617.250>f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f- are
          measurable>]>><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.618.250><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
          and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>(5)] it
        follows that <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>>
        and <math|> <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>>
        which after substituting this result in [eq: <reference|eq
        20.617.250>] gives\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and ><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>,<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>

        Using then [theorem: <reference|measurable function in sub space>] on
        the above results in\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.619.250>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
          and \<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>,\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation>

        Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
        (1)>] on the above gives

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><below|=|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f-
          properties>(5)]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.618.250>]>>\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|\|A>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><below|=|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f-
          properties>(5)]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|\|A>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.618.250>]>>\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        Finally as by [theorem: \ <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>(8)] <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>\<wedge\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->>
        it follows from the above that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        which together with <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        [see eq: <reference|eq 20.619.250>] proves
        <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
        Hence by [definition: <reference|measure integral (1)>]

        <\equation*>
          f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|a\<Rightarrow\>b>>As <math|f> is
        <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|<around*|\||A|\<nobracket\>>>>>-integrable
        we have that\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.620.250>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f- are
          measurable>]>>f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.621.250><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
          and ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
        (2)>] it follows that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        As by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>(6)]
        <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>>
        and <math|<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>=<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->>
        the above becomes:\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.622.250><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.623.250><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        Using the above and the fact that
        <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><below|\<Rightarrow\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
        (2)>]>>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
        proves that

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X><text| is >\<mu\><text|-integrable >
        </equation*>

        Further we have that\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eqs:
          <reference|eq 20.622.250>.<reference|eq
          20.623.250>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|def>>|<cell|<big|int>f
          d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>> >>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item*|<math|b\<Rightarrow\>a>>As <math|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>> is
        <math|\<mu\>>-integrable we have by definition that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f- are
          measurable>]>><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        Inserting <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|+>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>(5)]>><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>> and
        <math|<around*|(|f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>|)><rsup|->\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>(5)]>><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>> in the
        above gives

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.624.250>f<rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and ><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>,<around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation>

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.625.250><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
        </equation>

        Uisng [theorem: <reference|measurable function extension>] on [eq:
        <reference|eq 20.624.250>] gives\ 

        <\equation*>
          f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
          and >f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>

        Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
        (2)>] on the above we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.625.250>]>>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><rsub|A\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.625.250>]>>\<infty\>
        </equation*>

        From the two equations above it follows by definition that

        <\equation*>
          f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A>>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Properties of the measure integral>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral of almost zero function is zero>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>=0>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<b-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>
    then <math|max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<neq\>0> so that
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0> or <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    hence <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>
    which proves that <math|f<rsup|+>=0> is <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Applying then
    [theorem: <reference|non negative integral zero a.e. function>] proves
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+> d\<mu\>=0\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>
    then <math|-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<neq\>0> so that
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0> or <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    hence <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>
    which proves that <math|f<rsup|->> is <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Applying then
    [theorem: <reference|non negative integral zero a.e. function>] proves
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|-> d\<mu\>=0\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>=0-0=0
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral difference>Let
    \ <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f<rsub|1>,g<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>,g<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    [so that <math|f<rsub|1><around*|(|X|)>,g<rsub|1><around*|(|X|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|X|)>,g<rsub|2><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>]
    and <math|f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>=g<rsub|1>-g<rsub|2>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>=<big|int>f<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>-<big|int>f<rsub|2 >d\<mu\>=<big|int>g<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>-<big|int>g<rsub|2> d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|2> d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then from <math|f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>=g<rsub|1>-g<rsub|2>>
    we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|1>-f<rsub|2>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|g<rsub|1>-g<rsub|2>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>,g<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,g<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>|<cell|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Rightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|1>+g<rsub|2>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|f<rsub|2>+g<rsub|1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.519.232>f<rsub|1>+g<rsub|2>=f<rsub|2>+g<rsub|1>
    </equation>

    As <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>,g<rsub|1>,g<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    so that <math|f<rsub|1><around*|(|X|)>,g<rsub|1><around*|(|X|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|X|)>,g<rsub|2><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|1>,g<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>,g<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and \ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|measure integral
      extension> (3)]<with|font-series|bold|>>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq
      20.520.232>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<rsub|2>
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|measure integral
      extension> (3)]<with|font-series|bold|>>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq
      20.521.232>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int>g<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|measure integral
      extension> (3)]<with|font-series|bold|>>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq
      20.522.232>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int>g<rsub|2>
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|measure integral
      extension> (3)]<with|font-series|bold|>>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|2>
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq 20.523.232>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>] we have that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|2>
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|1>+g<rsub|2>|)>
      d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.519.232>>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|2>+g<rsub|1>|)>
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|1>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    So we have as the integrals are finite that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2> d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|1>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|2>
      d\<mu\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which combined with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.520.232>, <reference|eq
    20.251.220>, <reference|eq 20.522.232> and <reference|eq 20.523.232>]
    proves that

    <\equation*>
      <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|>f<rsub|1>
      d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|>f<rsub|2> d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|>g<rsub|1>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|>g<rsub|2>
      d\<mu\>>>>>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral properties>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>.,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      and <math|<big|int><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int>f
      d\<mu\>>.

      <item><math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      and <math|<big|int><around*|(|f+g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int>f
      d\<mu\>+<big|int>g d\<mu\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      then <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int>g d\<mu\>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f<around*|(|X|)>,g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      which is needed to ensure that <math|f+g> is well defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      and <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function product with scalar>] we
      have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.524.232>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Consider the following cases for <math|\<alpha\>>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>=0>>Then <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f=C<rsub|0>> so
        that by [note: <reference|measure integral of zero function (1)>]
        <math|<big|int><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=0=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int>f
        d\<mu\>>.

        <item*|<math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>>>Then we have that

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.525.232.1><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f-
          properties>]>>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><text| and
          ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f-
          properties>]>>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->
        </equation>

        so

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>|)>
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
          inequality>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq
          20.526.232>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->|)>
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
          inequality>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
          d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq 20.527.232>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        Using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.524.232>, <reference|eq 20.526.232> and
        <reference|eq 20.527.232>] it follows that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<text| is >\<mu\><text|-integrable>
        </equation*>

        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
          <reference|eq 20.526.232>, <reference|eq
          20.527.232>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>-\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f
          <rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
          d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int>f
          d\<mu\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>>Then we have that
        <math|-\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.525.232><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f-
          properties>]>>-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><text| and
          ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|measure integral f+ f-
          properties>]>>-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>
        </equation>

        so

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|->|)>
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
          inequality>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
          d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq
          20.529.232>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+>|)>
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
          inequality>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><eq-number><label|eq 20.530.232>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        Using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.524.232>, <reference|eq 20.529.232> and
        <reference|eq 20.530.232>] it follows that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<text| is >\<mu\><text|-integrable>
        </equation*>

        and\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>
          d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
          <reference|eq 20.529.232>, <reference|eq
          20.530.232>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
          d\<mu\>-<around*|(|-\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
          d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f
          <rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
          d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int>f
          d\<mu\>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>
      </description>

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>.\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>,g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> we have
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g<text| is well defined, >f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        and><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|+>d\<mu\>,<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|-1>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] it
      follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      and by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>] we
      have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+>,g<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->,<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further using \ [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>]
      we have also\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+>+g<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->+g<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further we have by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f-
      properties>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| ><around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)><text|
        and ><around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that we can apply [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar
      product, sum inequality>] giving\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>+g<rsup|+>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>]>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->+g<rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>]>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Next we have that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|+>-<around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|->>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        alternative>]>>>|<cell|f+g>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        alternative>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>-f<rsup|->|)>+<around*|(|g<rsup|+>-g<rsup|->|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsup|+>+g<rsup|+>|)>-<around*|(|f<rsup|->+g<rsup|->|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.531.232>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Finally\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|f+g|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f+g|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral difference>],[eq: <reference|eq
        20.531.232>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>+g<rsup|+>|)>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->+g<rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|->d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>|)>+<around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|->d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int>f
        d\<mu\>+<big|int>g d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      <math|f<around*|(|X|)>,g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|-1\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have by (1) that
      <math|<rigid|<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>,
      hence by (2) it follows that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.532.232>f-g=f+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|g-f|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>-f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.533.232><big|int><around*|(|g-f|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|g-f|)>\<geqslant\>0
      </equation>

      Further we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>g d\<mu\>-<big|int>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int>g
        d\<mu\>+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\><big|int>f
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|1|)>>>|<cell|<big|int>g
        d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|(|<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|2|)>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|g+<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|g-f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<geqslant\>|<text|[theorem
        <reference|eq 20.533.232>]>>>|<cell|0>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int>g d\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We have now the following alternative definition for integrability.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral alternative>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then we have

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      >\<Rightarrow\><text| ><around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|>\<wedge\><around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\><text|
      >f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      then >f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int>f d\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|int><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    \ 
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We prove this in 4 steps:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Assume that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then by definition\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.541.243>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+> d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      Using \ [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>] it
      follows then that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.542.243>f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation>

      Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
      inequality>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.541.243>]>>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      As <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]>>f<rsup|+>+f<rsup|->>
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] it
      follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      To summarize we have proved that

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        >\<Rightarrow\><text| ><around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Assume that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      then using [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>]
      it follows that

      <\equation*>
        <rigid|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f-
      properties>] <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>>
      and <math|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product,
      sum inequality>] we have that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><rsup|>>
      and <math|<rigid|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>>.
      Hence as we assumed that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have by definition that <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>.
      Summarized we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\><text|
        >f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then by definition we have that

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|+>d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      which by (2) proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Finally\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int>f
        d\<mu\>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>|\|>+<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>+g<rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        alternative>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral extension>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral and a.e. equalitiy>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and let <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. If
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    [in other words if <math|f> is <math|\<mu\>>-integrable then <math|g> is
    <math|\<mu\>>-integrable].

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>g d\<mu\>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      H=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>=<around*|{|f\<neq\>g|}>
    </equation*>

    As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
    hence <math|f,g> are <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable, we have by [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function comparisation> that
    <math|H\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Further as <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there
    exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such
    that <math|H\<subseteq\>N>, hence <math|\<mu\><around*|(|H|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0>.
    To summarize\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.540.234>H\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and
      >\<mu\><around*|(|H|)>=0
    </equation>

    As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>]
    that <math|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->,g<rsup|+>,g<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <text|If >f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\>max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,0|)>|)>\<neq\>max<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <text|If >f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\>-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,0|)>|)>\<neq\>-min<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>,0|)>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>A\|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>H<text|
      and ><around*|{|x\<in\>A\|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>H
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|H|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
    20.540.234>]>>0> we have by definition that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsup|+>=g<rsup|+><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e. and
      >f<rsup|->=g<rsup|-><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral and a.e. equality>] we
    have then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|f<text|
      is >\<mu\><text|-integrable>>\<infty\><text| and
      ><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|f<text|
      is >\<mu\><text|-integrable>>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    which, as <math|g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
    proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsup|->d\<mu\>=<big|int>g
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral domination theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    with <math|g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    such that <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int>g d\<mu\>>.

    <\note>
      If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> then
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> so that
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We prove this theorem in two stages:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Assume that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>. As
      <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and
      <math|g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> it follows
      from [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension> (3)] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><infix-and><big|int>g
        d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are
      measurable>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      and <math|f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product,
      sum inequality>]

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>g
        d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\><text| and
        ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>g
        d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      From the above it follows that

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)><below|\<less\>|g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>\<infty\>>
      we have <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> and using
      [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int>f d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int>g d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>Assume that <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\>g>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. Then there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<neg\><around*|(|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>x\<in\>X\\N<text| we have
        ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>N>>Then <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||0\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\N>>Then <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||1\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      </description>

      hence we have that

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| that ><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      Further as <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<Rightarrow\>X\\N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      product>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      So all the conditions for (1) are satisfied and we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int>g
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      Now as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> it follows from [theorem:
      <reference|almost everywhere and characteristic>] that
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f=f<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>,
      using then [theorem: <reference|measure integral and a.e. equalitiy>]
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int>g
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral sigma finite>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>> is
    <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<mu\>>-finite under <math|\<mu\>>>. [see
    definition: <reference|measure sigma finite set>].
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|n>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>=<around*|{|<around*|\||f|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>
    </equation*>

    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>> we have by
    [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>] that
    <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>>
    hence <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|n>=<around*|{|<around*|\||f|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    As <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- properties>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.377.232>] we have then that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||f|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|<frac|1|n>>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>=n\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    To summarize\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.541.234><around*|{|A<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      and >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>> then
    <math|0\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>> so that by [theorem:
    <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|0\<less\><frac|1|n>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>,
    hence <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n>>. This proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.542.234><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>> then
    there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n>>.
    Then <math|0\<less\><frac|1|n>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>
    so that <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>.
    So it follows that <rigid|<math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.542.234>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>> is
    <math|\<mu\>>=finite under <math|\<mu\>>.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral zero is a.e. zero>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    [so that by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>]
    <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>]
    then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>=0> then
      <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      with <math|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|> d\<mu\>=0> then <math|f=0>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>For the first part. Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      with <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|> d\<mu\>=0> then by
      [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>]
      <math|<rigid|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>.
      Hence using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.377.232>] we have for
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.543.234>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>n\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>=0
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>> then
      <math|0\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>> so that by [theorem:
      <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|0\<less\><frac|1|n>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>>.
      Likewise if <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>>
      then there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>\<Rightarrow\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>\<gtr\>0\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>
      proving that <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>.
      So we have proved that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.544.234><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>
      </equation>

      As <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.545.234><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        so that ><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      Hence

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.544.234>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.545.234>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|n=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><frac|1|n>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.543.234>]>>>|<cell|0>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=0<text| <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>]
      that <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      hence <math|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      and we have from [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>(3)]
      that <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>=0>.
      Hence by (1) \ <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>=a.e.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral properties (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|0\<leqslant\><big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>> then <math|f\<geqslant\>0>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral to measure over>] we have
      that <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsub|A><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that <math|<big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>> is well defined].
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|0\<nleqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0|}>=<around*|{|f\<less\>0|}>.>
    Now as <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\><rigid|\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable function alternative (1)>]
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|>
    </equation*>

    If <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0> and
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>=1> so that
      <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>
      hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>=0>
      so that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=0>
      hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=max<around*|(|0,0|)>=0
      </equation*>
    </description>

    so we have proved that <math|><math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>=C<rsub|0>>.
    Hence using [example: <reference|non negative integral constant>] we have
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.546.234><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>=0
    </equation>

    So we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\><big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>=-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>0
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.547.234><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>=0
    </equation>

    Hence\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||<rsup|>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>+<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
      inequality>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.546.234>, <reference|eq 20.547.234>>>>|<cell|0>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral zero is a.e. zero>(1)] on the
    above it follows that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f=0>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Hence there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>

    If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<ngeqslant\>0|}>=<around*|{|f\<less\>0|}>>
    then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0> and
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>=1> proving that
    <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0\<Rightarrow\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>,
    hence <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
    So \ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<ngeqslant\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N<text|
      with >\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0
    </equation*>

    from which it follows that

    <\equation*>
      f\<geqslant\>0<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral properties (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      and ><rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=\<infty\>|}>|)>=0>
    </equation*>

    In other words <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e..
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>] that
    <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>>.
    Hence by [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] we have that
    <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\> >. Using [theorem:
    <reference|non negative integral finite integral>] it follows then that
    <math|<rigid|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>><text|
    and ><rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=\<infty\>|}>|)>=0>>.
  </proof>

  We have now a weaker version of <math|\<mu\>>-integrable functions.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral alternative (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >\<exists\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      with <math|g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> such that >f=g<text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ We have\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      then we must create a function <math|g:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      with <math|g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> such that
      <math|<rigid|f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>. > To do this define the
      set <math|A> of all elements of <math|X> where <math|f<around*|(|x|)>>
      is either <math|+\<infty\>> or <math|-\<infty\>>.

      <\equation*>
        A=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=\<infty\>|}>=<around*|{|<around*|\||f|\|>=\<infty\>|}><text|>
      </equation*>

      By [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>]
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that, using [theorem: <reference|measurable function
      comparisation>(5)], <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and thus also
      <math|X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, further by [theorem: <reference|measure
      integral properties (2)>] <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>. Summarized we
      have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.548.234>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|,
        >X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0
      </equation>

      Define now

      <\equation*>
        g=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
      </equation*>

      then using [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      product>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        g=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further if <math|x\<in\>X> then either <math|x\<in\>A> giving
      <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<nin\>X\\A>0\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      or <math|x\<in\>X\\A> then <rigid|<math|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>\<infty\>>>
      so that <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> it follows from [theorem:
      <reference|almost everywhere and characteristic>] that
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f=f> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. hence

      <\equation*>
        g=f<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral and a.e. equalitiy>]
      together with the above proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      As there exist a function <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      such that <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. we have by [theorem:
      <reference|measure integral and a.e. equalitiy>]
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Limit theorems>

  One benefit that the measure integral has over the Riemann integral are the
  limit theorems which are the prime reason for the invention of the measure
  integral.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)><dueto|Monotone Convergence
    Theorem (1)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    then we have for the function

    <\equation*>
      f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    that <math|f> is well defined and\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=sup<rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\><text| exists and ><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\note>
    As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing /
    decreasing sequence exist>] that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>
    exist and <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
  </note>

  <\note>
    We do not require that <math|f> is <math|\<mu\>>-integrable hence
    <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=\<infty\>> is possible. This also the
    reason why we use <math|<big|int><rsup|+>> instead of <math|<big|int>>.
  </note>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>(6)] it
    follows that

    <\equation*>
      f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    is well defined and\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=sup<rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further as <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrow\>\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
    <math|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>> it follows also that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then as <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>> we
    have by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>] that <math|<rigid|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n+1>d\<mu\>>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.549.240><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>] that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<mu\>>. Hence we have that \ <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>> is a
    upper bound for <math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>>
    so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.550.240><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    So it remains to prove that <math|><math|<big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>>.
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then as
    <math|f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral alternative>] that
    there exist a <math|<around*|{|g<rsub|n,i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.551.240>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >g<rsub|n,i><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n,i+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.552.240>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n,i>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|n,i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.553.240><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|n,i>d\<mu\><text|
      exist and ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>g<rsub|n,i>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.554.240><around*|{|h<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text|
      by >h<rsub|n>=<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>g<rsub|i,n>|)><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|simple function integral properties
      (1)>]>>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|g<rsub|i,n><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|g<rsub|i,n+1><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i,n+1><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>g<rsub|i,n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|h<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.555.240>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence <math|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>g<rsub|i,n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i,n><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.555.240>]>>h<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>. So we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.556.240>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Further as <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|g<rsub|i,n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i,j><around*|(|x|)>\|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.552.240>]>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|i\<leqslant\>n\<Rightarrow\>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.557.240>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>g<rsub|i,n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i,n><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><below||>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.558.240>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)><text|>
    </equation>

    Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.556.240>] together with [theorem:
    <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing sequence exist>] proves
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.559.240><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.558.240>]>>f<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    We prove now by contradiction that <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>.
    So assume that <math|<rigid|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>.
    Then as <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
    there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<less\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.552.240>]
    <math|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup*<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|n,i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
    there exist a <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<less\>g<rsub|n,k><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|k\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|h,k|)>>g<rsub|n,max<around*|(|n,k|)>><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    now

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|g<rsub|n,max<around*|(|n,k|)>><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|n\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,max<around*|(|n,k|)>|}>>>|<cell|max<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|i,max<around*|(|n,k|)>>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,max<around*|(|n,k|)>|}>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|vee><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,max<around*|(|n,k|)>|}>>g<rsub|imax<around*|(|n,k|)>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|h<rsub|msx<around*|(|n,k|)>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which leads to the contradiction <math|h<rsub|max<around*|(|n,k|)>>\<leqslant\>><math|><math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<less\>h<rsub|max<around*|(|n,k|)>>>.
    So the assumption is wrong and we have that
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.559.240>] gives
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>.
    As <math|x\<in\>X> was choosen arbitrary we have

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have >f<around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    or

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.560.240><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n>=f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Next\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.560.240>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral limit>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>h<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral is a
      extension>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>h<rsub|n>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.557.240>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorems:
      <reference|extended limit properties>,<reference|non negative integral
      scalar product, sum inequality>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.550.240>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  If we weaken the requirements <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
  >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)><text| and
  >f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>
  to be hold almost everywhere we get the Monotone Convergence Theorem.\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|Monotone Convergence Theorem (2)><dueto|Monotone Convergence
    Theorem (2)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space, <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    If there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<neg\><around*|(|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N
    </equation*>

    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    In other words if <math|><math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
    >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
    is <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. true then

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    \ 
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let

    <\equation*>
      A=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<neg\><around*|(|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    then by the hypothesis there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      A\<subseteq\>N\<Rightarrow\>X\\N\<subseteq\>X\\A
    </equation*>

    then we have for <math|x> either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>N>>Then <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=0\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=0=0\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|0\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|0\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\N>>Then

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| ><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|x\<in\>X\\N\<Rightarrow\>x\<nin\>A>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=1\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|<rsub|X,X\\N>>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>X\\N\<Rightarrow\>x\<nin\>A>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    So we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)><text|
      and ><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    As <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<Rightarrow\>X\\N\<in\>A> it follows from
    \ [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>] that
    <math|<rigid|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    and <math|<rigid|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>>\<cal-A\>>.
    So the conditions for [theorem: <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem
    (1)>] are satisfied and we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.561.240><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Further using [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere and characteristic>]
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.
      and >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >f<rsub|n>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>

    Hence using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral and a.e.
    equality>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\><below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|non
      negative integral and a.e. equality>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.561.240>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral and a.e.
      equality>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    \ proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  A consequence of the above theorem is the Beppo Levi's theorem.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Beppo Levi's Theorem><dueto|Beppo Levi's Theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    then we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>\<of\>X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by ><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      we have <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      so that by [definition: <reference|extended infinite sum>]
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>> is
      well defined, similar as <math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d\<mu\>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      we have that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d\<mu\>>.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then by [theorem: <reference|non negative
    integral finite sum>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.562.240><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d\<mu\>|)>
    </equation>

    so that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then by [definition: <reference|extended infinite
    sum>] <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>>
    exist and <math|<rigid|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>>.
    Hence using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>] we
    have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Using now the Monotone Convergence Theorem [theorem: <reference|Monotone
    Convergence Theorem (1)>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.563.240><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\><text|
      exists and><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    <\equation*>
      \;
    </equation*>

    As <math|><math|<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|def><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    we have <math|>

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    Substituting this in [eq: <reference|eq 20.563.240>] gives finally

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.563.240>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.562.240>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d\<mu\>|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d\<mu\>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  A application of the Beppo Levi's Theorem is a way of defining a new
  measure on a measure space based on a non negative measurable function.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure integral measure construction>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then if we define

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text| by
      >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    we have that <math|\<nu\>> is a measure on
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>. Further we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\><text| is finite >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| f is
      >\<mu\><text|-integrable>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<varnothing\>>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<varnothing\>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=0\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=C<rsub|0>>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.564.240>\<nu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|\<varnothing\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|x,\<varnothing\>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|non negative integral constant>]>>0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    be pairwise disjoint family. Define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      by f<rsub|i>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>\<cdot\>f<below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      product>]>>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>. Take
    <math|x\<in\>X> then for <math|f<around*|(|x|)>> we have the following
    cases to consider:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>>>then for <math|x> and
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=0>
        and <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>0=0>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=0=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>>
        and <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<infty\>=\<infty\>>
        proving that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>Then for <math|x> and
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=0>
        and <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>0=0>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|X<rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, as
        <math|x\<in\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> there
        exists a unique <math|M\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> such that
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|M>> and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|M|}>>
        <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|i>>. If <math|n\<in\><around*|{|M,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
        then we have

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<around*|(|f\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)><around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|M|}>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|M|}>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|N>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x-f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        proving that

        <\equation*>
          <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </description>

    this proves that in all cases

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    Applying then Beppo Levi [see <reference|Beppo Levi's Theorem>] gives\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|<reference|Beppo
      Levi's Theorem>>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>v<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.564.240>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\><text| is a measure on the measurable space
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>>
    </equation*>

    For the last part of the theorem:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|\<nu\>> is finite then as
      <math|f=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f> we have that
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|X|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      which proves using [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>]
      that <math|f> is <math|\<mu\>>-integrable.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
      <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)><text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>>. Hence using [theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum inequality>] we
      have that <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> which proves that <math|\<nu\>> is a finite
      measure.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|Fatou's lemma><dueto|Fatou's lemma>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
      f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n> d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>] we have
      that <math|><math|<below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>> is well defined.
    </note>

    <\note>
      We can not assume that <math|<below|lim |n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n> d\<mu\>> <math|>exists which is the reason
      <math|<below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n> d\<mu\>> is used instead.
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.565.240><around*|{|g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      by >g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)><below|\<in\>|<text|[theor.:
      <reference|measurable function sup inf
      limit>]>>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>\<infty\>|}>|}>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|order sup,inf and inclusion>] we have

    <\equation*>
      inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    which as also

    <\equation*>
      inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    proves that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.566.240>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text| and
      >g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\ 
    </equation>

    So we have by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>] that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>
    exist and

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing sequence
      exist>]>>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>|}>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    We can now apply the Monotone Convergence Theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)>] giving\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> f<rsub|i>|)>
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n>|)>
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\><eq-number><label|eq 20.567.240>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.566.240>] with [theorem: <reference|non
    negative integral scalar product, sum inequality>] we have
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> that
    <math|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>
    d\<mu\>>. So we have using [theorem: <reference|extended lim inf lim sup
    ineqality (1)>] that <math|<below|lim
    inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>
    d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>
    d\<mu\>>. Substituting this in [eq: <reference|eq 20.567.240>] gives
    finally

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>
      f<rsub|i>|)> d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n> d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|Lebesgue's Dominated Convergence Theorem><dueto|Lebesgue's
    Dominated Convergence Theorem>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space, <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    with <math|g<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>,
    <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <text|(>f=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><text| and
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||f<rsub|n>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<text|) holds
      >\<mu\><text|.a.e.>
    </equation*>

    In other words there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<neg\><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists with ><below|f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      and >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|>|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item><math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item><math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int>f<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties (2)>]
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.568.240>N<rsub|1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      >and \<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>=0
    </equation>

    By the hypothesis there exist a <math|N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<neg\><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists with ><below|f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      and >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      \ ><around*|\||f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|>|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    Take <math|N=N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\N> that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.569.240>g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists with ><below|f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      and >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      \ ><around*|\||f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<around*|(|x|)>|>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.570.240>N\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and
      >\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0
    </equation>

    So using [theorems: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>, <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.571.240>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\|N>\<cdot\>f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.572.240>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    \;

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>N>>Then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=0\<Rightarrow\>0\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| ><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=0=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>\<Rightarrow\><text|
        ><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>0=0=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)><text|
        exist and><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\N>>Then\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)><below|\<neq\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.569.240>]>>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>0\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| ><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.569.240>]>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\><text|
        ><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.569.240>]>>f<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    Hence we have in all cases that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.573.240>\<forall\>x\<in\>X we have
      0\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.574.240>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      ><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.575.240>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Applying \ [corollary: <reference|extended limit absolute value>] on the
    above we have also\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.576.240>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X\|N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|\|><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Using \ [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere and characteristic>]
    together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.570.240>] proves also\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.577.240>g=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g<text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e., >f=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e. and >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >f<rsub|n>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation>

    So that using \ [theorem: <reference|measure integral and a.e.
    equalitiy>] we have, as <math|g> is <math|\<mu\>>-integrable and, that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.578.240>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.573.240>]>>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int>g
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that every <math|f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>>.
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. Using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.571.240>,
    <reference|eq 20.572.240>] and [eq: <reference|eq 20.574.240>] allows us
    to use [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>] to get\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.578.240>][theorem: <reference|measure integral
      extension>]>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.578.240>]>>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    So using [eq: <reference|eq 20.572.240>] and [theorem: <reference|measure
    integral extension>] it follows that <math|<around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    Applying then [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>]
    together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.574.240>] it follows that
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    Using then [theorem: <reference|measure integral and a.e. equalitiy>]
    together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.577.240>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.579.240>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      and ><big|int>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.576.240>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|\|><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit properties>]>\<wedge\><text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.574.240>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.573.240>]>>>|<cell|\<infty\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.580.240><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Using the above and [eq: <reference|eq 20.571.240>] allows us to use
    \ [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>] to get

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      ><reference|eq 20.578.240><text|]>>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    Hence using ]eq: <reference|eq 20.571.240> and [theorem:
    <reference|measure integral alternative>] it follows that
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    Using then [theorem: <reference|measure integral and a.e. equalitiy>]
    together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.577.240>] proves that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.581.240>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Further using [eq: <reference|eq 20.574.240>] and [eq: <reference|eq
    20.580.240>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.582.240><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><text|,
      ><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><text|
      and >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      ><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>
    </equation>

    Given <math|x\<in\>X> and <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then as
    <math|<around*|\||\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.574.240>] we have

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      -<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    so taking in account [eq: <reference|eq 20.582.240>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.628.263>0\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>-<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.629.263>0\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>+<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    Using the above, [eq: <reference|eq 20.571.240>, <reference|eq
    20.572.240>] and [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>,
    <reference|measurable function properties (1)>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.583.240><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.584.240><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Using the above we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>+<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.575.240>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>+<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|extended limit constant sequence>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>+<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit properties>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>-<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.575.240>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>-<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|extended limit constant sequence>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>-<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit properties>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.585.240><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>+<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>=<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.586.240><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>-<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>=<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g-\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>
    </equation>

    Next we have, taking in account that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <rigid|<math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
    [see eqs: <reference|eq 20.578.240>, <reference|eq 20.579.240>], that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|><text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.585.240>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Fatou's lemma>]>\<wedge\><text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.629.263>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended lim inf and lim sup and
      sum>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>+<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence as the integrals are real numbers we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.587.240><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Similar we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>-<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.586.240>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Fatou's lemma>]>\<wedge\><text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.628.263>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>+<around*|(|-<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended lim inf and lim sup and
      sum>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>+<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|-<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended lim inf lim sup and
      negation>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>-<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which as the integrals are real numbers gives\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.635.263><below|lim sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Hence we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.587.240>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim
      inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended lim inf \<less\>= lim sup>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.635.263>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim inf|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>=<below|lim
      sup|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    This proves that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>>
    exist and <math|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>f<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>>.
    Using then [eq: <reference|eq 20.579.240>] and [eq: <reference|eq
    20.581.240>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\><text|
      exist and ><big|int>f d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Complex integration>

  <subsubsection|Measurability of complex valued functions>

  If we want to develop a theory of integration of complex valued functions
  we need first to replace the measure space
  <math|<around*|\<langle\>|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>,\<lambda\>|\<rangle\>>>
  with a measure space based on <math|\<bbb-C\>> instead of
  <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>. To do this we make use of the fact
  that <math|\<bbb-C\>> is actually defined as
  <math|\<bbb-C\>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> [see definition: <reference|complex
  numbers>] where <math|z=x+i\<cdot\>y> is actually equivalent with
  <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>=x+i\<cdot\>y> [see theorem: <reference|complex
  notation (0)>]. Further the topology on <math|\<bbb-C\>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>
  based on the absolute value <math|<around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-C\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
  where <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|x,y|)>|\|>=<sqrt|x<rsup|2>+y<rsup|2>>>
  is by [theorem: <reference|normed all norms in the set of complex numbers
  are equivalent>] is equivalent with the norm
  <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsub|2>:\<bbb-C\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
  defined by <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|x,y|)>|\|><rsub|2>=max<around*|(|<around*|\||x|\|>,<around*|\||y|\|>|)>>
  so that <math|>by [theprem: <reference|normed equivalent norms>] the
  topology <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> [the canonical topology on
  <math|\<bbb-C\>>] is equal to the topology
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>>. Hence the
  following definition makes sence.

  <\definition>
    <label|complex measurable space><index|<math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
    <math|\<bbb-C\>>><index|<math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-C\>,B<around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>|\<rangle\>>>>The
    measurable space for the complex numbers noted as
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-C\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>|\<rangle\>>>
    is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-C\>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|2><text| and
      >\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]><below|=|<text|>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|><rsub|2>>|]>\<equallim\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra on
      R^n>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>|]>
    </equation*>

    In other words\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-C\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>|]>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Once we have defined the measurable space we can define the set of all
  measurable functions to <math|\<bbb-C\>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|complex measurable function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then the set of all
    <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>>-measurable functions is
    noted as <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> so\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|A>\|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><text|
      is >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]><text|-measurable>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Sometimes we want to talk about <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      and <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> at the same
      time. In this case we use the notation
      <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> where
      <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> stands for either
      <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> or
      <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>. Just like
      <math|\<bbb-K\>> stands for either <math|\<bbb-R\>> or
      <math|\<bbb-C\>>.
    </note>
  </definition>

  The following general theorem will be usefull for a alternative definition
  of <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measurable function condition>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> be a measurable space and
    <math|f:A\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> a function then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<text| is >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text|-measurable
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >f<rsub|i>=\<pi\><rsub|i>\<circ\>f:A\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| is
      >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Given <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      we have by [theorems: <reference|normed maximum norm>,
      <reference|continuity projection map is open and continuous>] that
      <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is
      continuous using the topologies <math|\<cal-T\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|><rsub|n>>>.
      So using [theorem: <reference|measurable function and continuity>] it
      follows that <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>> is
      <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable.
      As <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>-measurable
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function composition>]
      that <math|f<rsub|i>=\<pi\><rsub|i>\<circ\>f> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable, hence by
      definition we have that <math|f<rsub|i>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable.

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel
      algebra on R^n (equivalences 2)>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|(|\<cal-H\><rsup|n>|)><text|
        where >\<cal-H\><rsup|n>=<around*|{|<around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<wedge\>a\<in\>\<bbb-R\>|}><text|
        and ><around*|]|-\<infty\>,a|[><rsub|i>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><around*|\||x<rsub|i>\<less\>a|\|>|}>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsup|n>> then there exists a
      <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> and a <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      such that <math|A=<around*|]|-\<infty\>,x|[><rsub|i>> then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|y|)>\<in\><around*|]|-\<infty\>,x|[><rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|f<around*|(|y|)>|)><rsub|i>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<pi\><rsub|i><around*|(|f<around*|(|y|)>|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<pi\><rsub|i>\<circ\>f|)><rsub|i><around*|(|y|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<rsub|i><around*|(|y|)>\<less\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|{|f<rsub|i>\<less\>x|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|{|f<rsub|i>\<less\>x|}><below|\<in\>|f<rsub|i><text|
        is >\<cal-A\><text|-measurable>>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Hence we have by [theorem: <reference|measurable function and generated
      sigma algebra>] that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>-measurable.
    </description>
  </proof>

  Applying the above theorem to <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> yields a simple way
  to check if <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.\ 

  <\definition>
    <index|<math|Re<around*|(|f|)>>><index|<math|Img<around*|(|f|)>>>Let
    <math|X> be a set and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> then we define\ 

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|f|)>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| by
      >Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)><text|>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      Img<around*|(|f|)>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| by
      >Img<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|almost everywhere complex functions>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> and
    <math|g:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e. >\<Leftrightarrow\><text|
      >Re<around*|(|f|)>=Re<around*|(|g|)><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e. and
      >Img<around*|(|f|)>=Img<around*|(|g|)><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \;

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there
      exists a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> and <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
      Assume that <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>Re<around*|(|g|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      then <math|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<neq\>Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      proving that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
      So we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f|)>=Re<around*|(|g|)><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e>
      </equation*>

      If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Img<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>Img<around*|(|g|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      then <math|Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<neq\>Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      proving that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<neq\>Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        Img<around*|(|f|)>=Img<around*|(|g|)><text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>As <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>=Re<around*|(|g|)>>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and <math|Img<around*|(|f|)>=Img<around*|(|g|)>>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. there exists <math|N<rsub|1>,N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      such that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>,
      <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>Re<around*|(|g|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1>>
      and <math|<rigid|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Img<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>Img<around*|(|g|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>>>.
      Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
      then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>> so that either
      <math|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<neq\>Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>
      or <math|Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<neq\>Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>,
      hence

      <\equation*>
        x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>Re<around*|(|g|)><around*|(|x|)>|}><big|cup><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|Img<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>Img<around*|(|g|)><around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      proving that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>g<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>>.
      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1><big|cup>N<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>=0>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measurable function alternative>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f:A\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a function
    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<text| is >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]><text|-measurable
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >Re<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      and >Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    This follows from the previous theorem [see theorem: <reference|complex
    measurable function condition>], <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    and the fact that <math|\<bbb-C\>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>,
    <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>=\<pi\><rsub|1>\<circ\>f> and
    <math|Img<around*|(|f|)>=\<pi\><rsub|2>\<circ\>f>.
  </proof>

  We have now similar properties for <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
  as for <math|\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>. First we define some
  pointwise definitions:

  <\definition>
    Let <math|X> be a set then we define\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> and
      <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a function then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> is defined by
      <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> is a function then
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is defined by
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>.

      <item>If <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> and
      <math|g:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> are functions then we define:\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|f+g:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> by
        <math|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>>

        <item><math|f\<cdot\>g:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> by
        <math|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>>
      </enumerate>

      <item>If <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|X>>
      then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><text|>>
      is defined by <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>>

      <item>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|X>>
      then

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>:<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>
        <text| exist>|}>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><text| is defined by
        ><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measurable function properties>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> be a measurable space then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>we have that
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have <math|f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|A\<in\>A> and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      then <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      and <math|D=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist>|}>> then <math|D\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
      <math|<rigid|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have by the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|complex measurable
      function alternative>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.590.241>Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>,Re<around*|(|g|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>+Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f+g|)>=Re<around*|(|f|)>+Re<around*|(|g|)><text| and
        >Img<around*|(|f+g|)>=Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|g|)>
      </equation*>

      Using the above, [eq: <reference|eq 20.590.241>] and [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] we have that
      <math|Re<around*|(|f+g|)>,Img<around*|(|f+g|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>|]>>
      so that by the previous theorem [see theorem: <reference|complex
      measurable function alternative>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>This is easily proved by induction. So let

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>\|<text|If
        ><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text|
        then ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>|}>
      </equation*>

      then we have:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>If
        <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
        then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
        which proves that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

        <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>then
        if <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
        we have as <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|n+1>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
        [as <math|f<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>,
        <math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>> and (1)]. So <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
      </description>

      induction proves then (2).

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> we
      have by the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|complex measurable
      function alternative>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.591.241>Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f*x|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>=Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>\<wedge\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>=Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>
      </equation*>

      Using the above, [eq: <reference|eq 20.591.241> and [theorems:
      <reference|measurable function product with scalar> and
      <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] we have that

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>,Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      which by [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function alternative>]
      proves\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function
      alternative>] that <math|Ref<around*|(|f|)>,Ref<around*|(|g|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>,<rigid|Img<around*|(|g|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>.
      Further for <math|x\<in\>X> we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>-Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|g|)>-Img<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g|)>+Img<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|Re<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|g|)>-Img<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g|)>>>|<row|<cell|Img<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|Re<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|g|)>+Img<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|g|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Using then [theorems: <reference|measurable function properties
      (1)>,<reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)>,Img<around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      which by [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function alternative>]
      proves that <math|f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> we
      have by [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function alternative>]
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further we have for <math|x\<in\>X>\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      \ proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>\<wedge\>Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>
      </equation*>

      so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      product>]

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>,Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      hence using [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function
      alternative>] we have that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

      <item>As <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function
      alternative>] that

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >Re<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)>,Img<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further using [theorem: <reference|limit complex numbers condition>] we
      have that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>>
      exists if and only if <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|)>>
      and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|)>>
      exists. So if <math|D<rsub|1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>A\|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|)>
      exists|}>> and <rigid|<math|D<rsub|2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>A\|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|)>
      exists|}>>> then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        D=<around*|{|x\<in\>A\|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><text|
        exists>|}>=D<rsub|1><big|cap>D<rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      From [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>] we have
      that <math|D<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\>,D<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> proving
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        D=D<rsub|1><big|cap>D<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Further using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>]
      again and <math|Re<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|X|)>,Img<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      we have that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D<rsub|1>>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D<rsub|2>>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      which as <math|D\<subseteq\>D<rsub|1>,D<rsub|2>> proves by [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function restriction (2)>] that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsub|\|D>,<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsub|\|D>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|D>,\<bbb-C\>|]>.
      </equation*>

      Further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>D> we have by [theorem: <reference|limit
      complex numbers condition>]

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><rsub|\|D>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><rsub|\|D>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><rsub|\|D><around*|(|x|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><rsub|\|D><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|limit complex numbers condition>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>=<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|F<rsub|n>|)>|)><rsub|\|D>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><rsub|\|D>>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function alternative>] we
      finally have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|D,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measurable function absolute value>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|A\<in\>\<Alpha\>> and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|<around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-C\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is continuous
    [see theorem: <reference|continuity norm is uniform continuous>] we have
    by [theorem: <reference|measurable function and continuity>] that the
    function <math|<around*|\|||\|>> is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable.
    As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>A> we have <math|<around*|(|<around*|\|||\|>\<circ\>f|)><around*|(|x=<around*|\|||\|><around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>>
    it follows that <math|<around*|\||f|\|>=<around*|\|||\|>\<circ\>f>. So
    using [theorem: <reference|measurable function composition>] it follows
    that <math|<around*|\||f|\|>> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable
    or as <math|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Complex Measure Integral>

  We can now define the integral of complex valued functions based on the
  integrals of its real and imaginair components of these functions.

  <\definition>
    <label|complex integral><index|complex
    integral><index|<math|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a function then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<text| is >\<mu\><text|-complex integrable if
      >Re<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<wedge\>Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>.\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and the complex integral of <math|f> noted as
    <math|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>> is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    The set of all complex integrable functions is noted as
    <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|X>\|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><text|
      is >\<mu\><text|-complex integrable>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\example>
    <label|complex integral of zero function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    spacen then <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsup|C>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=0> where
    <math|C<rsub|0>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> is defined by
    <math|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>.
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|Re<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)><around*|(|x|)>=Re<around*|(|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)><rsub|>|)>=Re<around*|(|0|)>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>
    and <math|Img<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)><around*|(|x|)>=Img<around*|(|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>|)>=Img<around*|(|0|)>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>.
    This proves that <math|Re<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)>=C<rsub|0><below|\<in\>|<text|[note:
    <reference|measure integral of zero function
    (1)>]>>\<cal-L\><around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>> and
    <math|Img<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)>=C<rsub|0><below|\<in\>|<text|[note:
    <reference|measure integral of zero function
    (1)>]>>\<cal-L\><around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>>. Hence we
    have by definition that <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
    Further we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=<big|int>Re<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|C<rsub|0>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[note:
      <reference|measure integral of zero function (1)>]>>0
    </equation*>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex integral characteristics multiplication>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    spacen, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)><text|
      and >Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> then
    <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>>, <math|Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measure integral to measure over>]
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>,\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that <math|Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>>
    and <math|Img<around*|(|\<b-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The above theorem allows us tor define the integral over a measurable
  function.

  <\definition>
    Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    measure space, <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> then we
    define \ <math|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>> by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int>Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theoren:
        <reference|complex integral characteristics
        multiplication>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int><rsub|A>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </note>

    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsub|A>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int><rsub|A>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  The following shows that the complex integral is a extension of the measure
  integral.

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex integral real integrable functions are complex
    integrable>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      we have that ><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>. Now for the function
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> we have as
    <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> that
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>>
    and <math|Img<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>=Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.592.241>Re<around*|(|f|)>=f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      and >Img<around*|(|f|)>=C<rsub|0><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral of almost zero function is
      zero>]>>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    which proves that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
    So\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.592.241>]>><big|int>f
      d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral of almost zero function is
      zero>]>><big|int>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex integral property>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around*|(|f+g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
        d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>g d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> we
      have that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have by definition that

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f|)>,Re<around*|(|g|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|g|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>=Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|X|)>|)>=Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Re<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>+Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        Img<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>=Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|X|)>|)>=Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Img<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f+g|)>=Re<around*|(|f|)>+Re<around*|(|g|)><text| and
        >Img<around*|(|f+g|)>=Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|g|)>
      </equation*>

      Hence using now [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] it
      follows that

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f+g|)>,Img<around*|(|f+g|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around*|(|f+g|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int>Re<around*|(|f+g|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f+g|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>+Re<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int>Re<around*|(|g|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|g|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
        d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>g d\<mu\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have by definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|)><text|
        and ><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>+Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>=Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>\<wedge\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>=Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|a|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] on the above
      we have that <math|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>>,
      <math|Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      so that by definition\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|f|)>+Img<around*|(|a|)>\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|f|)>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>-Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\>Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+<rigid|i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int>Re<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int>Img<around*|(|f|)>d\<mu\>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
        d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<alpha\>|)>|)>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We have the following equivalent definition of <math|\<mu\>>-complex
  integrable functions:\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex integral alternative>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a complex valued function
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text|
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text|
      and ><around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      so that <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Using then <math|>[theorem: <reference|complex measurable function
      alternative>] it follows that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.594.244>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation>

      and using [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function absolute
      value>]

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.595.244><around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Further as <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|g|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>] that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)>|\|>,<around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)>|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      so that

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)>|\|>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)>|\|><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)>|\|>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral f+ f-
        properties>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)>|\|><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex norm properties>(8)>><around*|\||Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|>=<around*|(|<around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)>|\|>+<around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)>|\|>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      So that by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product,
      sum inequality>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)>|\|>+<around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)>|\|>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>]>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)>|\|>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)>|\|>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      which together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.595.244>] proves by [theorem:
      <reference|measure integral extension>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have using [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function
      alternative>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex norm properties>(3)]>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||Img<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex norm properties>(3)]>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      Using then [theorem: <reference|measure integral domination theorem>]
      it follows that <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      hence we have by definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    For the last part define\ 

    <\equation*>
      w<around*|(|f|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1<text| if
      ><around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>=0>>|<row|<cell|<frac|f <rsup|\<bbb-C\>>d\<mu\>|<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>><text| if ><around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>\<neq\>0|\<nobracket\>>>>>>><text| so that
      ><around*|\||w<around*|(|f|)>|\|>=1
    </equation*>

    then for <math|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>> we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<big|int>f <rsup|\<bbb-C\>>d\<mu\>=0>>Then
      <math|<around*|\||<big|int>f d\<mu\>|\|>=0> and
      <math|w<around*|(|f|)>=1> so that <math|w\<cdot\><around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>>

      <item*|<math|<big|int>f<rsup|\<bbb-C\>> d\<mu\>\<neq\>0>>Then
      <math|<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>|\|>\<neq\>0> and
      <math|w=<frac|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>|\|>>> so that
      <math|w\<cdot\><around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>>
    </description>

    Summarized we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>=w<around*|(|f|)>\<cdot\><around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|><text| and ><around*|\||w<around*|(|f|)>|\|>=1
    </equation*>

    Then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|complex integral property>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int>Re<around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<big|int>Re<around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>,<big|int>Img<around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>,<around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>|<cell|<big|int>Re<around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\><eq-number><label|eq
      20.596.244>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then using [theorem: <reference|complex norm
    properties>] we have that

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|\||w<around*|(|f|)>|\|>=1><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] we have
    that <math|<big|int>Re<around*|(|<frac|1|w<around*|(|f|)>>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.596.244>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|int><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|The spaces <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
  and <math|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>

  <subsubsection|Definition of <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
  and <math|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>

  In this section we introduce infinite Banach spaces that play a very
  important role in physics and functional analyses. Be carefull, in the
  following we use the same symbol <math|<around*|\|||\|>> for the norm on
  <math|\<bbb-R\>> and on <math|\<bbb-C\>> [see examples: <reference|normed
  real numbers>,<reference|normed complex numbers>], context will indicate
  which norm is used.

  <\definition>
    \ Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> then we define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p>\<circ\><around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      so that ><around*|(|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||x|\<nobracket\>><rsup|>\|<rsup|p>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p>\<circ\><around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-C\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      so that ><around*|(|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||x|\|><rsup|><rsup|p>
    </equation*>

    If <math|X> is a set and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> then we define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<circ\>f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      so that ><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|><rsup|p>
    </equation*>

    If <math|X> is a set and <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> then we define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<circ\>f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      so that ><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|><rsup|p>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using \ [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power properties> (2)] we
      have that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      <math|x<rsup|p>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\|||\|><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\><around*|\|||\|><rsup|p><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|LP spaces><index|<math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>Let
    <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measures
    space, <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\><text| or <math|\<bbb-R\>> then we define
    <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>> as
    follows\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>

      <item>If <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>> then
      <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>|}>>
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  We prove now that <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
  is the special case of <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces canonical example>Let
    <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measure space
    then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>=\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    in other words we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>

      <item><math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>=\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
    <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|1><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|1>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|exp generalized power properties>(4)]>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||f|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|1>
    </equation*>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and by
      [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>]
      <math|<rigid|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|1>=<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.597.245>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Further if <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Hence <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      so that by \ [theorem: <reference|measure integral alternative>]
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> which
      proves that <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.597.245>] gives

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      then we have by definition that <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      hence <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function alternative>] it
      follows then that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      and using [theorem: <reference|complex integral alternative>] it
      follows that <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|1>=<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      hence <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.598.245>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation>

      If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      and <math|<around*|\||f|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      which by [theorem: <reference|complex integral alternative>] proves
      that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>,
      hence <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
      Combining this result with [eq: <reference|eq 20.598.245>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>=\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\example>
    <label|LP spaces neutral element>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>
    then for <math|C<rsub|0>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-K\>> defined by
    <math|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>=0> we have that

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    First using [theorem: <reference|measurable function constant function>]
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that
    <math|<around*|\||C<rsub|0>|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\||0|\|><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
    <reference|exp generalized power>]>\<wedge\>p\<neq\>0>0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>
    which proves that <math|<around*|\||C<rsub|0>|\|><rsup|p>=C<rsub|0>>. As
    by [example: <reference|measure integral of zero function (1)>]
    <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we want to prove that \ <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
  is a vector space over <math|\<bbb-R\>> and that
  <math|<rigid|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>>
  is a vector space over <math|\<bbb-C\>>. To do this we need the following
  three lemma's.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.447.245>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> then
    we have that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item><math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item>If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      then <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Using [theorem: <reference|continuity norm is uniform
      continuous>] <math|<around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>>
      is a continuous function [using the canonical topologies on
      <math|\<bbb-R\>>]. By [theorem: <reference|continuity and subspace
      topology (4)>] it follows then that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        is continuous using the topologies
        >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>><text|,
        ><around*|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|)><rsub|\|<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|\<nobracket\>><around*|[||\<nobracket\>>>
      </equation*>

      Further using [theorem: <reference|exp (*)^x is continuous>] we have
      that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p>:<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        is >continuous
      </equation*>

      Hence as <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p>\<circ\><around*|\|||\|>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|continuity composition>] that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| is
        continuous> using the topologies \<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>
      </equation*>

      By [theorem: <reference|measurable function and continuity>]
      <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>> is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable,
      using then [theorem: <reference|measurable function real or extended>]
      it follows that <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable.
      Finally as <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<circ\>f>.
      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measurable function composition>] (1)
      and the fact that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable
      it follows that <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable.
      Finally as <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p><around*|(|f<around*|(|X|)>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Using [theorem: <reference|continuity norm is uniform
      continuous>] <math|<around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-C\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>>
      is a continuous function [using the canonical topologies on
      <math|\<bbb-C\>> and <math|\<bbb-R\>>. By [theorem:
      <reference|continuity and subspace topology (4)>] it follows then that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\|||\|>:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        is continuous using the topologies
        >\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>><text|,
        ><around*|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|)><rsub|\|<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|\<nobracket\>><around*|[||\<nobracket\>>>
      </equation*>

      Further using [theorem: <reference|exp (*)^x is continuous>] we have
      that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p>:<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        is >continuous
      </equation*>

      Hence as <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p>\<circ\><around*|\|||\|>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|continuity composition>] that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>:\<bbb-C\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| is
        continuous> using the topologies \<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>
      </equation*>

      Hence <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>> is
      <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable
      [see theorem: <reference|measurable function and continuity>], using
      then [theorem: <reference|measurable function real or extended>] it
      follows that <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>:\<bbb-C\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable.
      Finally as <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-C\>,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p>\<circ\>f>.
      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measurable function composition>] (3)
      and the fact that <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>-measurable
      it follows that <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable.
      Finally as <math|<around*|\|||\|><rsup|p><around*|(|f<around*|(|X|)>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.448.245>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space, <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>,
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    and <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
    <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
    then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> we have by
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.447.245>] that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then by the hypothesis
    <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
    so that by the fact that the power function is monotone [see theorem:
    <reference|exp generalized power properties>(15)] we have
    <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>>,
    summarized

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have ><around*|\||<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>
    </equation*>

    Further as <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    we have that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      so that ><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    Hence the conditions for [theorem: <reference|measure integral domination
    theorem>] are satisfied so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    which as <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>
    proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.449.245>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|]|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|z<rsub|1>,z<rsub|2>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>> then
    <math|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>|)>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|>>, <math|<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|>> we
    have the following possible cases:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|>>>Then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|>+<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|>\<leqslant\>2\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|>
      </equation*>

      So that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>(15)]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|2\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>(12)]>>>|<cell|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|p>+2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|>\<less\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|>>>Then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>|\|>\<less\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|>+<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|>\<leqslant\>2\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|>
      </equation*>

      So that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>(15)]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|2\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>(12)]>>>|<cell|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|p>+2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||z<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||z<rsub|2>|\|><rsup|p>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces sum scalar product>Let
    <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measure space
    then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      If we use the convention then <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> or
      <math|\<bbb-C\>> then we can rephrase the theorem as follows\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        we have >f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        and >\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\><text| we have
        >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that

      <\equation*>
        f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text| and
        ><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>,<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral extension>]>><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>,<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      From [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] it follows
      that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.599.245>f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      so that using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.447.245>]\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.600.245><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.449.245>]>>2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>|)>=<around*|(|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      applying then [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar
      product, sum inequality>] we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.601.245><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>,<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] that
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>,<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      So from the above [eq: <reference|eq 20.600.245>] we have that
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.600.245>] allows us to use
      [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] to prove that
      <math|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      As <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> [see
      eq: <reference|eq 20.599.245>] we have that

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text| and
        ><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      From [theorem: <reference|measurable function product with scalar>] it
      follows then that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further for <math|x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><around*|\<nobracket\>|<rsup|p>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>
      </equation*>

      As <math|><math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] that
      <math|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      so that <math|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      which combined with <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text| and
        ><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>,<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral extension>]>><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>,<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>
      </equation*>

      From [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function properties>] it
      follows that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.602.245>f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation>

      so that using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.447.245>]\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.603.245><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\>X> then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.449.245>]>>2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>|)>=<around*|(|2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>+<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      applying then [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar
      product, sum inequality>] we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.604.245><big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>,<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] that
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>,<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      So from the above [eq: <reference|eq 20.604.245>] we have that
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.603.245>] allows us to use
      [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] to prove that
      <math|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      As <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X.\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> [see
      eq: <reference|eq 20.602.245>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text| and
        ><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      From [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function properties>] it
      follows then that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Further for <math|x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><around*|\<nobracket\>|<rsup|p>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>
      </equation*>

      As <math|><math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] that
      <math|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      so that <math|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      which combined with <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We extend now <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
  where <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> to the case where
  <math|p=\<infty\>>.

  \;

  <\definition>
    <label|LP spaces (@@)><index|<math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    where <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> or <math|\<bbb-C\>> is defined by\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\|\<exists\>M\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      such that >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M|}>>

      <item>If <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>> then
      <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\|\<exists\>M\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      such that >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
      ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M|}>>
    </enumerate>

    In essence <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    is the set of bounded measurable functions.
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces sum scalar product (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      and <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have that
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      and <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> we have that
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
    </enumerate>

    In other words using the notation <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> or
    <math|\<bbb-C\>> we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      we have >f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      we have >\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\><text| that
      >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \;

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      hence by [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>]
      <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>. Further
      there exists <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such
      that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>>,
      <math|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|2>>. If we
      take <math|M=M<rsub|1>+M<rsub|2>> then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we
      have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>+M<rsub|2>=M
      </equation*>

      which proves that

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      hence by [theorem: <reference|measurable function product with scalar>]
      <math|<rigid|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>.
      Further there exists a <math|M<rprime|'>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such
      that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rprime|'>>.
      Take <math|M=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>+1\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
      then given <math|x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\<less\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>+1=M
      </equation*>

      So we have

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>,
      hence by [theorem: <reference|complex integral property>]
      <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>, and a
      <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>> such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>>,
      <math|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|2>>. If
      <math|M=M<rsub|1>+M<rsub|2>> then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>+M<rsub|2>=M.
      </equation*>

      So we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>,
      hence by [theorem: <reference|complex integral property>]
      <rigid|<math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>>
      and there exists a <math|M<rprime|'>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such that
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rprime|'>>.
      Take <rigid|<math|M=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>+1\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>>
      then given <math|x\<in\>X> we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\<less\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>+1=M.
      </equation*>

      So we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove that <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
  is a vector space.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP vector space>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>,
    <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\><text| or >\<bbb-C\>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measure space
    then if we define\ 

    <\equation*>
      f+g:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-K\><text| by
      ><around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)><text|
      and ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-K\><text| by
      ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)>*<around*|(|x|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a vector space over the field >\<bbb-K\><text| with neutral element
      <math|C<rsub|0>>>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    By [example: <reference|LP spaces neutral element>]
    <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    As further <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-K\><rsup|X>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|vector space function space>] and
    [theorem: <reference|vector space sub-space is a vector space>] together
    with [theorems: <reference|LP spaces sum scalar product>, <reference|LP
    spaces sum scalar product (1)>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a vector space over the field >\<bbb-K\><text| with neutral element
      <math|C<rsub|0>>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces absolute value>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>
    and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> we have two possible cases to
    consider:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|p=\<infty\>>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      we have by definition that

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text| and
        >\<exists\>M\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text| such that
        >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
        ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorems: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>,
      <reference|complex measurable function absolute value>] and
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>> it follows
      that <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>.
      Hence we have by definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>>As
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      we have by definition that

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-K\><text|
        and ><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorems: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>,
      <reference|complex measurable function absolute value>] it follows that
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      further as <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>|)>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  Next we we define what later will become a pseudo norm on
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>. We
  start with the case where <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|LP spaces p-norm (1)>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>
    and <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measure
    space then we define \ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>:\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      by ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces LP norm is zero>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measure space
    and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0> then we have
    that <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and <math|f=0>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then as <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    we have by [definition: <reference|exp generalized power>] that
    <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>,
    hence we have <math|<around*|\||<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>>
    so that <math|<around*|\||<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|\|>=<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>>.
    As <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0> we have that
    <math|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>=0>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power properties>]
    <math|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|\|>d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>=0>.
    Using then [theorem: <reference|measure integral zero is a.e. zero>] it
    follows that <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. So
    there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>
    such that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
    Let <rigid|<math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>>
    then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0\<Rightarrow\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<neq\>0>
    so that <rigid|<math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>>,
    hence <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=0<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  To define <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> we need first
  to first introduce the concept of locally <math|\<mu\>>-null sets.

  <\definition>
    <label|LP spaces locally u-null sets>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|N> <with|font-series|bold|is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null
    >if <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> we have that
    <math|A<big|cap>N\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> [see definition:
    <reference|measure null set>].
  </definition>

  A trivial example is of a locally <math|\<mu\>>-null set is the exmpty set.

  <\example>
    <label|LP spaces empty set is locally m-null>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|\<varnothing\>> is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null.
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then <math|A<big|cap>\<emptyset\>=\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=0> proving that
    <math|\<emptyset\>> is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null.
  </proof>

  We have the following properties for <math|\<mu\>>-null sets.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces locally m-null set properties>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable
    space then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Every negligible set is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null. In otherwords
      <math|\<forall\>N\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> we have that <math|N> is
      locally <math|\<mu\>>-null.

      <item>If <math|B> is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null and
      <math|A\<subseteq\>B> then <math|A> is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null.

      <item>If <math|I> is a countable set [finite or denumerable] and
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      a countable family of locally <math|\<mu\>>-null sets then
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>> is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|N\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>> then there exist a
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> such that
      <math|N\<subseteq\>A>. Let <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> then
      <math|N<big|cap>B\<subseteq\>N\<subseteq\>A> so that
      <math|N<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>. So we have by
      definition that <math|N> is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null.

      <item>Let <math|D\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|D|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> then as <math|B> is
      locally <math|\<mu\>>-null we have that
      <math|B<big|cap>D\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>. Hence there exist a
      <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
      <math|B<big|cap>D\<subseteq\>N>. As <math|A\<subseteq\>B> we have that
      <math|A<big|cap>D\<subseteq\>B<big|cap>D\<subseteq\>N> which proves
      that <math|A<big|cap>D\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>. Hence <math|A> is
      locally <math|\<mu\>>-null.

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
      be a countable family of locally <math|\<mu\>>-null sets. Let
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Given <math|i\<in\>I> we have, as <math|A<rsub|i>> is locally
      <math|\<mu\>>-null, that <math|A<big|cap>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>,
      so by [theorem: <reference|measure null set properties>] we have that
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>.
      So that

      <\equation*>
        A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i><text| is locally
        >\<mu\><text|-null>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to define <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>

  <\definition>
    <label|LP spaces p-norm (2)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space then we define\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>:\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      by ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\proof>
    Of course for this definition to make sense the <math|inf> must exist and
    be finite. Let <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>
    then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> and there
    exist a <math|M\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>.
    From this it follows that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}>=\<varnothing\>>
    a locally <math|\<mu\>>-null set [see example: <reference|LP spaces empty
    set is locally m-null>]. So we have proved that\ 

    <\equation*>
      M\<in\><around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>
    </equation*>

    from which it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)><text| exist>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>M
    </equation*>

    which proves, as <math|M\<less\>\<infty\>>, that

    <\equation*>
      inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We set out now to prove that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>> is
  a norm on the vector space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.463.246>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then as <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    we ave\ 

    <\equation*>
      inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}>|}><text|
      is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null>|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<frac|1|n>
    </equation*>

    there exist a <math|M<rsub|n>> such that
    <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>>
    is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null and <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|n>\<less\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<frac|1|n>>.
    So we have by [theorem: <reference|choice function generating>] a
    <math|<around*|{|M<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.605.246>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      ><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}><text|
      is local >\<mu\><text|-null and ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|n>\<less\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<frac|1|n>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>
    then <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
    so that using [theorem: <reference|complex Archimedean property
    consequence (1)>] there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<frac|1|n>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>,
    so <math|M<rsub|n><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
    20.605.246>]>><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<frac|1|n>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
    from which we deduce that <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.606.246><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>>
    then there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>\<Rightarrow\>M<rsub|n>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>,>
    hence by [eq: <reference|eq 20.605.246>]
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|n>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>
    so that x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>
    which proves that <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.606.246>] proves

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>
    </equation*>

    As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.605.246>] we have
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
    <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>>
    is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null it follows from [theorem: <reference|LP
    spaces locally m-null set properties>] that
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M<rsub|n>|}>>
    is locally <math|\<mu\>>-finite, hence we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-finite>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The following theorem will be crucial for the properties of
  <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Holder's Inequality><dueto|Holder's Inequality>Let
    <math|p,q\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> be such that
    <math|<frac|1|p>+<frac|1|q>=1>, <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    a measure space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|q><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then <math|f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For <math|p,q> we must consider the following cases:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|p=\<infty\>>>Then as <math|1=<frac|1|p>+<frac|1|q>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|extended 1/x>]>>0+<frac|1|q>=<frac|1|q>> we have that
      <math|q=1> [if <math|q=-\<infty\>,\<infty\>> then
      <math|<frac|1|q>=0\<neq\>1> so we must have that
      <math|q=<around*|(|1|)><rsup|-1>=1>], so
      <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
      By [theorem: <reference|measure integral sigma finite>] it follows that
      <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}>>
      is <math|\<mu\>>-finite, so there exist a
      <math|<around*|{|A|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.607.246>A=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Further as <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      we have by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.463.246>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.608.246>B=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
      </equation>

      Using the definitions of <math|A> and <math|B> we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<big|cap>B>|<cell|<below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.607.246>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.609.246>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|B=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>
      is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      we have that <math|<rigid|A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsub|\<mu\>>>>.
      So there exist a <math|N<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|i>|)>=0> such that
      <math|<rigid|A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|i>>>.
      Substituting this in [eq: <reference|eq 20.609.246>] gives

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.610.246>A<big|cap>B=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>N<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      and\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.611.246>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>N<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|lemma 20.298.246>]>>0
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|}>>
      then <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
      and we must have that <math|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0>
      [otherwise we have <math|0=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0>
      leading to the contradiction <math|0\<gtr\>0>], hence by dividing the
      inequality by <math|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>> we have
      <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
      So <rigid|<math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>>
      proving that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}><big|cap><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>
      </equation*>

      \ Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.610.246>] it follows that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>N<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      which as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>N<rsub|i>|)>=0>
      proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.612.246><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g|\|><text|
        >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation>

      As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      and <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      we have that <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
      using then [theorems: <reference|measurable function properties (1)>,
      <reference|complex measurable function properties>] and [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.447.245>] we have that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.660.265><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Further from <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|\||g|\|>=<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      so that by [theorems: <reference|measurable function product with
      scalar>,<reference|complex integral property>]
      <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Using then [eq: <reference|eq 20.612.246>] together with [theorem:
      <reference|measure integral domination theorem>] and the fact that by
      [eq: <reference|eq 20.660.265>] <math|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.613.246><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\||g|\|>d\<mu\>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|\||g|\|>d\<mu\>
      </equation>

      Hence we have by definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation*>

      As <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>\<equallim\><rsub|q=1><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>=<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|1>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|1>>=<big|int><around*|\||g|\|>d\<mu\>>
      it follows from [eq: <reference|eq 20.613.246>] that

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|q=\<infty\>>>This reduces to the case <math|p=\<infty\>>
      if we switch <math|p> with <math|q> and <math|f> with <math|g>.

      <item*|<math|p\<neq\>\<infty\>\<wedge\>q\<neq\>\<infty\>>>Let
      <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>=0\<vee\>g<around*|(|x|)>=0>>Then
        <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0\<leqslant\><frac|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>|p>+<frac|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|q>|q>>

        <item*|<math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0\<wedge\>g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>>Using
        Young's inequality [see <reference|exp Young's inequality>] we have
        that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>|p>+<frac|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|q>|q>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      from which it follows that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.614.246>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
        ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>|p>+<frac|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|q>|q>
      </equation>

      As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
      <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|q><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>,<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] we have
      that <math|<frac|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|p>+<frac|<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>|q>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Further as <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      [see definition: <reference|LP spaces>] we have by [theorems:
      <reference|measurable function properties (1)>,<reference|measure
      integral f+ f- are measurable>,<reference|complex measurable function
      properties>,<reference|complex measurable function absolute value>]
      that <math|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      [so that <math|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,1|[>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>].
      Using then [eq: <reference|eq 20.614.246>] together with [theorem:
      <reference|measure integral domination theorem>] proves that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.615.246><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        so that by definition >f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation>

      and\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.616.246><big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><around*|(|<frac|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|p>+<frac|<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>|q>|)>d\<mu\>=<frac|1|p>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>+<frac|1|q>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>d\<mu\>
      </equation>

      We examine now the case where <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=1=<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>.
      In this case we we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        1\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|exp generalized power
        properties>]>>1<rsup|p>=<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>=<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>><big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        1\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|exp generalized power
        properties>]>>1<rsup|q>=<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>|)>=<around*|(|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|q>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>|)><rsup|q>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>><big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|q>d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      Substituting this in [eq: <reference|eq 20.616.246>] gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><frac|1|p>+<frac|1|q>=1
      </equation*>

      To summarize we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.617.247><text|If >f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|q><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        with ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=1=<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q><text|
        then >f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        and ><big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
      </equation>

      proving the theorem in this special case. Now for the general case we
      have for <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>,
      <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>> the following possible
      cases:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<neq\>0\<wedge\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>\<neq\>0>>Take
        then <math|f<rprime|'>=<frac|f|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>
        and <math|g<rprime|'>=<frac|g|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>>
        so that by [theorem: <reference|LP spaces sum scalar product>]
        <math|f<rprime|'>><math|\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
        <math|g<rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|q><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
        Further we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\||<frac|f|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<frac|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|exp generalized power
          properties>>><around*|(|<frac|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>|)>=<around*|(|<frac|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\||<frac|g|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>|\|><rsup|q><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<frac|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>|)><rsup|q>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|exp generalized power
          properties>>><around*|(|<frac|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|q>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|q>>|)>=<around*|(|<frac|<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|q>>|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        which proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\||<frac|f|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|\|><rsup|p>=<frac|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>\<wedge\><around*|\||<frac|g|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>|\|><rsup|q>=<frac|<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|q>>
        </equation*>

        so that

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||<frac|f|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><frac|<around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<frac|1|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|)><rsup|p>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|exp generalized power
          properties>]>>>|<cell|<frac|1|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|1>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g<rprime|'>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||<frac|g|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>|\|><rsup|q>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><frac|<around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|q>>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|<frac|1|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>|)><rsup|q>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|exp generalized power
          properties>>>>|<cell|<frac|1|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|q>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|1>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        So we can apply [eq: <reference|eq 20.617.247>] giving\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.618.247>f<rprime|'>\<cdot\>g<rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
          and ><big|int><around*|\||f<rprime|'>\<cdot\>g<rprime|'>|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1
        </equation>

        Using [theorem: <reference|LP spaces sum scalar product>] we have
        <math|f\<cdot\>g=<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rprime|'>\<cdot\>g<rprime|'>|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
        and\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|f<rprime|'>\<cdot\>g<rprime|'>|)>d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.618.247>]>><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>\<cdot\>1
        </equation*>

        in other words\ 

        <\equation*>
          f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
          and ><big|int><around*|(|f\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0>>Then by
        [theorem: <reference|LP spaces LP norm is zero>] we have <math|f=0>
        <math|\<mu\>>-a.e so there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
        <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
        If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>
        then <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0> [otherwise
        <math|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||0|\|>=0>]
        so that <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
        Hence <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>
        which proves that <math|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>=0>
        <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral of
        almost zero function is zero> it follows that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>=0\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>=0>>Then by
        [theorem: <reference|LP spaces LP norm is zero>] we have <math|g=0>
        <math|\<mu\>>-a.e so there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
        <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
        If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>
        then <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0> [otherwise
        <math|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||0|\|>=0>]
        so that <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>.
        Hence <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>
        which proves that <math|<around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>=0>
        <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral of
        almost zero function is zero> it follows that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>=0\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      So in all cases we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\>g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q><text|
        and by [eq: <reference|eq 20.615.246>]
        >f\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation*>

      which proves the theorem for the case where
      <math|p,q\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces Minkowski's Inequality><dueto|Minkowski's Inequality>Let
    <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measure space
    then we have <math|\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|LP vector space>]
      <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      so that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>> is well defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    we have by definition that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.619.246>f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      and ><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    For <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> we have the following
    possible cases:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|p=\<infty\>>>Define <math|F=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>
      and <math|G=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<geqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>
      then using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.463.246>] we have that
      <math|F<text| is locally >\<mu\><text|-null and >G<text| is locally
      >\<mu\><text|-null>> so that by ]theorem: <reference|LP spaces locally
      m-null set properties>]

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.620.246>F<big|cup>G<text| is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
      </equation>

      Let <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>
      then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>
      </equation*>

      Assume that <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<wedge\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
      then we would have <math|<rigid|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>
      a contradiction, Hence the assumption is wrong and we must have
      <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<vee\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
      so that <math|x\<in\>F<big|cup>G>. Thus it is proved that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>\<subseteq\>F<big|cup>G
      </equation*>

      Using the above, [eq: <reference|eq 20.620.246>] together with
      ]theorem: <reference|LP spaces locally m-null set properties>] proves
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
      </equation*>

      As by [definition: <reference|LP spaces p-norm (2)>]
      <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      it follows that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\><around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>
      </equation*>

      so that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[definition:
        <reference|LP spaces p-norm (2)>]>>>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}><text|>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence we have proved that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|p=1>>Given <math|x\<in\>X> we have that
      <math|<around*|\||f+g|\|><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] we have
      <math|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|\||g|\|>d\<mu\>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|1>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|>d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|\||g|\|>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|1>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|1>>+<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||g|\|><rsup|1>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|p\<in\><around*|]|1,\<infty\>|[>>>Let
      <math|q=<frac|p|p-1>> then <math|<frac|1|p>+<frac|1|q>=<frac|1|p>+<frac|p-1|p>=<frac|p|p>=1>
      and

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.669.265><around*|(|p-1|)>\<cdot\>q=p
      </equation>

      So if follows that <math|<around*|(|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|)><rsup|q>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|<around*|(|p-1|)>\<cdot\>q>=<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>>.
      So using [eq: <reference|eq 20.619.246>] it follows that
      <math|<around*|(|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|)><rsup|q>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      so that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.621.246><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|q><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation>

      Now as <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
      <math|<frac|1|p>+<frac|1|q>=1> we have using the Holder's inequality
      [see theorem: <reference|Holder's Inequality>] together with the above
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.622.246>f\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<wedge\>g\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|q-1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation>

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.623.246><big|int><around*|\||f\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
      </equation>

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.624.246><big|int><around*|\||g\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|\|>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>
      </equation>

      Further we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p-1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p-1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p-1>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p-1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>+<around*|\||g|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] we have then

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>+<around*|\||g|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|(|<around*|\||g|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eqs:
        <reference|eq 20.623.246>, <reference|eq
        20.624.246>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|q>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|<around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p-1>|)><rsup|q>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|<around*|(|p-1|)>\<cdot\>q>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.669.265>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.625.246><big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>
      </equation>

      For <math|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>=0>>Then we
        have

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>=0<rsup|<frac|1|p>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|exp generalized power
          properties>]>>0\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
        </equation*>

        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<neq\>0>>Then
        by [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power>] we have
        <math|><math|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>\<neq\>0>
        so that using using [eq: <reference|eq 20.625.246>] we have\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|\<geqslant\>>|<cell|<frac|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|q>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|exp generalized power
          properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|1-<frac|1|q>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f+g|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces finite sum>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>
    and <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    This is easily proved by mathematical induction. So let

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|If
      <around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      then ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      and ><around*|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>If <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      and

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1><around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
      </equation*>

      proving that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      then we have, as <math|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, that
      <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>.
      Hence using [theorem: <reference|LP vector space>] it follows that

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>+f<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>+f<rsub|n+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|LP spaces Minkowski's Inequality>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|n+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><rsub|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|n+1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|\<rangle\>>>
  is a pseudo normed space.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces are pseudo normed>Let
    <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|]>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a pseudo normed vectorspace over <math|\<bbb-K\>>.

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|LP vector space>] we have that
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a vector space.

      <\note>
        If <math|A\<in\>\<Alpha\>> such that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>
        and <math|A\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>. As <math|A\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
        there exist a <math|a\<in\>A> so that
        <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A><around*|(|a|)>\<neq\>C<rsub|0><around*|(|a|)>>
        whic proves that in this case <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<neq\>C<rsub|0>>.
        Further using [example: <reference|non negative integral
        characteristics>] we have <math|0=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>d\<mu\>=<around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|)><rsup|1>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|1>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>>.
        Hence it follows that in this case
        <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>> is only a pseudo norm not
        a norm.
      </note>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    By [definitions: <reference|LP spaces p-norm (1)>, <reference|LP spaces
    p-norm (2)>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.626.246><around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>:\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation>

    Further we have using the Minkowski's Inequality [see theorem:
    <reference|LP spaces Minkowski's Inequality>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.627.246>\<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      \ ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
    </equation>

    Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> and
    <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>>. For <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>
    we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>>Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><rsup|p>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|)>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><rsup|p>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|><rsup|p>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|p=\<infty\>>>For <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>> we have
      either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>=0>>As <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0|}>=\<varnothing\>>
        [for if <math|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>M>
        then we have <math|0=<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>M\<geqslant\>0>
        a contradicton] we have by [example: <reference|LP spaces empty set
        is locally m-null>] that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0|}><text|
          is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
        </equation*>

        So that <math|0\<in\><around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>> hence

        <\equation*>
          0\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\|><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0|}><text|
          is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>0
        </equation*>

        which proves that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=0=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<neq\>0>>Let
        <math|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> then we have\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<neq\>0>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>|}>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        proving that\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.628.246><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>|}>
        </equation>

        Define\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><around*|\||<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
          is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|\<nobracket\>>|}>>>|<row|<cell|B>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><around*|\||<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
          is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|\<nobracket\>>|}>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        If <math|M\<in\>A> then <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>> and using [eq: <reference|eq
        20.628.246>] we have that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>|}>>
        is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null. Hence
        <math|M<rprime|'>=<frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>\<in\>B> or
        <math|<rigid|M\<in\><around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B|}>>>
        giving\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.629.246>A\<subseteq\><around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B|}>
        </equation>

        On the other side, if <math|M\<in\><around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B|}>>
        there exists a <math|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B> such that
        <math|<rigid|M=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>>> or
        <math|<frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>=M<rprime|'>\<in\>B> hence
        <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>|}>>
        is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null. Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.628.246>]
        we have then that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}>>
        is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null or <math|M\<in\>A>, proving that
        <math|<around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B|}>\<subseteq\>A>.
        Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.629.246> proves that\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.630.249>A=<around*|{|<around*|\||a|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B|}>
        </equation>

        As <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<equallim\><rsub|def>inf<around*|(|A|)>>
        we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.630.249>] that
        <math|\<forall\>M\<in\>B> <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M>
        so that <math|<frac|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>\<leqslant\>M>
        hence <math|<frac|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|B|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
        or\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.631.246><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
        </equation>

        Further if <math|M\<in\><around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B|}>>
        then <math|M=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>> where
        <math|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B> so that
        <math|<frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>=M<rprime|'>\<in\>B> hence
        <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=inf<around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\><frac|M|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>>
        or <math|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>M>
        so that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rprime|'>\|M<rprime|'>\<in\>B|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.630.249>]>>inf<around*|(|A|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
        </equation*>

        which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.631.246>] proves that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </description>
  </proof>

  It would be nice that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>p> is actually a
  norm. To do this we consider first the sub space of
  <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> consisted of
  functions <math|f> with <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0> and
  use this sub space to construct the factor space [see theorem:
  <reference|vector space factor space>]. This will allows us to define a
  norm on this factor space based on the pseudo norm.

  <\definition>
    <label|LP spaces p-null set><index|<math|\<cal-N\><rsup|p>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space, <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> then we define the
    <with|font-series|bold|p-null set> <math|\<cal-N\><rsup|p>> by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  We have the following equivalences of <math|p>-null sets.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces p-null set (1)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> then

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        and >f=0<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      \ 

      <item>If <math|p=\<infty\>> then\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| >f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        and ><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}><text| is
        locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item> First if <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|><text|
        is a norm>>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<neq\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<neq\>0|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.632.246>\<forall\>p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[><text|
        we have ><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<neq\>0|}>
      </equation>

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
        we have that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
        and <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0>. Hence
        <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> and by
        [theorem: <reference|LP spaces LP norm is zero>] <math|f=0>
        <math|\<mu\>>-a.e..

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If <math|f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. then
        there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> such that
        <math|<rigid|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f*<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>>,
        using [eq: <reference|eq 20.632.246>] we have
        <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>N>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>=0<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
        </equation*>

        As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> we have
        by the above, <math|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-K\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
        and [theorem: <reference|measure integral of almost zero function is
        zero>] that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
          and ><big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>=0\<Rightarrow\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>><below|=|<text|[theorem:
          <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>0
        </equation*>

        so that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
        and <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0 > which proves that

        <\equation*>
          f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      <item> First we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>0|}>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that \ <math|>

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.682.265><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|z|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>0|}>
      </equation>

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
        we have that

        <\equation*>
          f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
        </equation*>

        and <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=0>. Using
        [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.463.246>] it follows that
        \ <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>>. \ As
        <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=0> it follows
        then that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>0|}>>
        is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null or as
        <math|<rigid|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<neq\>0|}><below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.682.265>]>><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>0|}>>>
        it follows that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>0|}><text|
          is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>As <math|><math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>
        is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null we have by [eq: <reference|eq
        20.682.265>] <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>0|}>>
        is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null. Hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          0\<in\><around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
          is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>
        </equation*>

        so that <math|0\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>0> which proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=0
        </equation*>

        As by the hypothesis <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
        it follows from the above that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We prove now that <math|\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
  is a sub-space of <math|\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces null sets is a subspace>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and \ <math|p\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> then
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-N\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a sub-space of <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
    <math|C<rsub|0>> is the neutral element [see theorem: <reference|LP
    vector space>] and by [theorem: <reference|LP spaces are pseudo normed>]
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>> is a pseudo norm it follows
    that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|o>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>\<cal-N\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<neq\>0
    </equation*>

    If <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0=<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>
    so that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0>
    hence <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0>. So we have:

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>f,g\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      we have >f+g\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>>,
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0> so that
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0>
    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\><text|
      we have >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    This concludes the proof that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-N\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a sub-space of <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>.
  </proof>

  We use now [theorem: <reference|vector space factor space>] to define
  <math|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.

  <\definition>
    Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a measure space
    then by [theorems: <reference|LP spaces null sets is a subspace> and
    <reference|vector space equivalence relation>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sim\><rsub|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<times\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      where <math|f\<sim\><rsub|p>g<text| if
      >f-g\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>
    </equation*>

    is a equivalence relation. For every <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    we can then define the equivalence class <math|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>>
    by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>=<around*|{|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\|g\<sim\><rsub|p>f|}>=<around*|{|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\|f-g\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    The set <math|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> is
    then defined as the factor space <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>/\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|vector space factor space>] we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>=<around*|{|<around*|[|x|]><rsub|p>\|x\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|vector space factor space>] the following
    operators are well defined\ 

    <\equation*>
      +:L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<times\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      by ><around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>+<around*|[|g|]><rsub|p>=<around*|[|f+g|]><rsub|p>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cdot\>:\<bbb-K\>\<times\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      by >\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>=<around*|[|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|]><rsub|p>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>><text|
      forms a vector space with neutral element
      ><around*|[|C<rsub|0>|]><rsub|p>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Next we show that we can define a norm on
  <math|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces normed space>Let \ <math|p\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    and <math|<math|><around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> a
    measure space then\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>:L<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      defined by ><around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><text|
      where >x=<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>
    </equation*>

    is a well defined function that defines a norm on
    <math|L<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>> making

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>|\<rangle\>><text|
      a normed space>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we must prove that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsub|p>>>
    is well defined. Let <math|f\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>>
    then there exist a <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that <math|x=<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>>. Assume that there exist
    another <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that <math|x=<around*|[|g|]><rsub|p>> then
    <math|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>=<around*|[|g|]><rsub|p>> so that
    <math|f-g\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
    Hence we have that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0> so
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>-<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|normed absolute value norm
      differences>]>><around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0
    </equation*>

    so that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>.
    Hence it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.633.246><around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>:L<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      defined by ><around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><text|
      where >x=<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p><text| is well defined>
    </equation>

    Next let <math|x,y\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then there exist <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that <math|x=<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>> and
    <math|y=<around*|[|g|]><rsub|p>> then

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x+y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>+<around*|[|g|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|f+g|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsub|p>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|g|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    So we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.634.246>\<forall\>x,y\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      \ ><around*|\<\|\|\>|x+y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>> and
    <math|x\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> then
    there exist a <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that <math|x=<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>> so that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.635.246>\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>,\<forall\>x\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>
    </equation>

    Finally if <math|x\<in\>L<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> is
    such that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=0> then there
    exist a <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that <math|x=<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>>. As
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=0>
    it follows that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
    As further <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    we have that <math|f-C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-N\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    so that <math|x=<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>=<around*|[|C<rsub|0>|]><rsub|p>>
    the neutral element in <math|><math|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
    In other words we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.636.246>\<forall\>x\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      with ><around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=0<text| we have
      \ <math|x=<around*|[|C<rsub|0>|]><rsub|p>>>
    </equation>

    So by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.633.246>, <reference|eq 20.634.246>,
    <reference|eq 20.635.246> and <reference|eq 20.636.246>] it follows that
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>> is a norm.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|The Banach space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>|\<rangle\>>>>

  We will prove now that the norm <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>
  is complete. First we need some lemmas.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.476.246>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    such that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>> exist and
    <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\||<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|<around*|[|\<ast\>|]><rsup|p>:<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>>
    defined by <math|<around*|[|\<ast\>|]><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=x<rsup|p>>
    is continuous on <math|<around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> [see theorem:
    <reference|exp (*)^x is continuous>] we have by [theorem:
    <reference|limit and continuity>[ that
    <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>>
    exist and <math|><math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<ast\>|)><rsup|p><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>>.
    In other words\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.637.247>\<forall\><around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      we have ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p><text|
      exists and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>
    </equation>

    Further as <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>> exist and
    <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> it
    follows from [theorem: <reference|limit of norm is norm of limit>] that
    for <math|<around*|{|<around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>>
    exist and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>=<around*|\||<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|\|>>.
    Using then [eq: <reference|eq 20.637.247>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<around*|\||<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>=<around*|\||<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We extend now the above lemma to limits that can be finite or infinite.
  First we need to extend the power function to the infinite case.

  <\definition>
    <label|LP space power extension>Let <math|p\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then we
    define for <math|x\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    <math|x<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> by\ 

    <\equation*>
      x<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x<rsup|p><text|
      if >x\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>>|<row|<cell|\<infty\><text| if
      >x=\<infty\>>>>>>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  We show now this extension is monotone.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.505.265>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> then
    for <math|x,y\<in\><around*|[|10,\<infty\>|]>> with <math|x\<leqslant\>y>
    we have that <math|x<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x,y\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> with <math|x\<leqslant\>y>
    then for <math|x> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>>Then we have <math|>for
      <math|y> either\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|y\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>>Then by [theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>] we have that
        <math|x<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=x<rsup|p>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|p>=y<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>.

        <item*|<math|y=\<infty\>>>Then <math|x<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=\<infty\><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=y<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>.
      </description>

      <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>>>Then we must have that <math|y=\<infty\>>
      from which it follows that <math|x<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=\<infty\><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=y<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have

    <\equation*>
      x<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\>y<rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.477.246>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    be such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i+1>> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i><text| and
      ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>><text|
      exists where ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>,<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>|}>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>|}>|)>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|0\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i+1>> it follow from
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.505.265>] that
    <math|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|x<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>.
    Using then [theorems: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>,<reference|extended limit properties>] it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.688.265><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i><text|
      and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p><text|
      exist>s
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.689.265>0\<leqslant\><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<wedge\>0\<leqslant\><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then for <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>=\<infty\>>>Then
      <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>=\<infty\><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>
      so that <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>

      \ 

      <item*|<math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>\<less\>\<infty\>>>Then
      <math|x<rsub|i>,<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      and <math|x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power properties>]
      <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>
    </description>

    so that <math|<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>>
    is a upper bound of <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>>.
    Hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.690.265><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.689.265>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<circ\>>
      x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>
    </equation>

    Now for the opposite equation. For <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<in\>>
    we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=\<infty\>>>Then
      <math|\<infty\>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.690.265>]>><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<circ\>>
      x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>> so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<neq\>\<infty\>>>Let
      <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have
      <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p><below|\<in\>|<text|[definition:
      <reference|exp generalized power>]>><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> and

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
      </equation*>

      it follows from [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power properties>]
      that <math|x<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>.
      Hence <math|<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>
      is a upper bound for <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>>.
      So that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.689.265>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Hence using [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power>] again we have
      that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>
      </equation*>

      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.690.265>] gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.689.265>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>=<around*|(|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>|)><rsup|<around*|[|p|]>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.479.246>Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    then we have <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|\||f|\|>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|LP spaces absolute value>]
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      as is required in this lemma.
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|p> we have either\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|p=\<infty\>>>then as <math|<around*|\||f|\|>=<around*|\||<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>|)>|\|>>
      we have that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><around*|\||<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|\<nobracket\>>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><around*|\||<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|\||f|\|><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
        is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|\<nobracket\>>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|p\<in\><around*|]|1,\<infty\>|[>>>then as
      <math|<around*|\||f|\|>=<around*|\||<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>|)>|\|>>
      we have that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>|)>|\|><rsup|p>|)><rsup|<frac|1|p>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|(|<around*|\||f|\|>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.480.246>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space then <math|<around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a Banach space\ 
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|LP spaces normed space>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a normed space>
    </equation*>

    So the only thing left to prove is that this space is complete. Let
    <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    be a Cauchy sequence then there exist a
    <math|<around*|{|F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subset\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|f<rsub|i>=<around*|[|F<rsub|i>|]><rsub|p>>. Let
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then as
    <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> is Cauchy there
    exist a <math|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>n,m\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    we have <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|n>-f<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    So <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|]><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F<rsub|n>|]><rsub|\<infty\>>-<around*|[|F<rsub|m>|]><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|n>-f<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|><rsub|>>
    hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.638.246>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      >\<exists\>N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
      >\<forall\>n,m\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      we have ><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then as
    <math|F<rsub|n>,F<rsub|m>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    a vector space we have that <math|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    so that by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.463.246>] \ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.639.246>A<rsub|n,m>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|m><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|LP spaces locally m-null set properties>] it
    follow that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.640.246>A=<big|cup><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<times\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i,j><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>
    </equation>

    As <math|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    we have by [defnition: <reference|LP spaces (@@)>] that
    <math|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|lemma 20.447.245>]
    <math|<around*|\||F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\|>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    hence <math|<around*|\||F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\|>> is
    <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable
    function alternative (1)>] <math|A<rsub|n,m>=<around*|{|<around*|\||F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|>>.
    As <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows then that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.641.246>\<forall\>n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >A<rsub|n,m>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A=<big|cup><rsub|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>*\<times\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i,j>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Now\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\>X\\A>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>
      x\<nin\>A<rsub|n,m>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|m><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      \|><around*|\||F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|m><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<forall\>n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>
      <text|we have><around*|\||F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|m><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.642.246>X\\A=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|\<forall\>n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      we have ><around*|\||F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|m><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X\\A> and take <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.638.246>] there exist a
    <math|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>n,m\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    we have <math|<around*|\||F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|m><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|n>-F<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X\\A<text| ><around*|{|F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-K\><text|
      is a Cauchy seqeunce in ><around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-K\>,\<\|\|\>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    As <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-K\>,\<\|\|\>|\<rangle\>>> is complete
    [see examples: <reference|complete set of real numbers>,
    <reference|complete the complex numbers are complete>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.643.246>\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\A<text|
      ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><text|
      exist >
    </equation>

    Define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.644.246>F:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-K\><text| by
      >F<around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>X\\A>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>A>>>>>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|F<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    it follows that <math|F<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
    hence using [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>, <reference|complex measurable function properties>] it follows
    that <math|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.645.246><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>0=0=F<around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=F<around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    hence <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have that
    <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    exist and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=F<around*|(|x|)>>.
    Using then [theorems: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>,
    <reference|complex measurable function properties>] together with [eq:
    <reference|eq 20.645.246>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.646.246>F\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and take
    <math|x\<in\>X\\A>. Let <math|n\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>,\<ldots\>\<infty\>|}>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.638.246> for the definition of
    <math|N<rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>] then by [eq: <reference|eq
    20.644.246>] there exist a <math|N<rsub|x>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>m\<in\><around*|[|N<rsub|x>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|]>> we
    have <math|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|m><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
    Take <math|N=max<around*|(|N<rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>,N<rsub|x>|)>>
    then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>+F<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||F<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<around*|\||F<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.638.246>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves, if we take <math|M<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=N<rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>,
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.647.246>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      >\<exists\>M<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
      >\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|M<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      we have >\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\A<text| that
      ><around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    As <math|1\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> we have by the above that
    <math|\<exists\>M<rsub|1>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\A> we have <math|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|M<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>1>.
    As <math|F<rsub|M<rsub|1>>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    it follows from [definition: <reference|LP spaces (@@)>] that there exist
    a <math|M\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
    <math|<around*|\||F<rsub|M<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>. Let
    <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|M<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>+F<rsub|M<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|M<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||F<rsub|M<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>1+M.>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0\<leqslant\>1+M>
    </description>

    Hence <math|F> is bounded so that by [definition: <reference|LP spaces
    (@@)>] and [eq: <reference|eq 20.646.246>] it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.648\<point\>246>F\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      so that >f=<around*|[|F|]><rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\>L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. If
    <math|n\<in\><around*|{|M<rsub|\<varepsilon\>/2>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    then <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|}>\<subseteq\>A>
    [for if <math|x\<in\>X\\A> we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.647.246>]
    that <math|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|\<varepsilon\>>>].
    As <math|A> is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null [see eq: <reference|eq
    20.640.246>] it follows from [theorem: <reference|LP spaces locally
    m-null set properties>] that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|}>>
    is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null. So

    <\equation*>
      <frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<in\><around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>
    </equation*>

    Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|F-F<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|F<around*|(|x|)>-F<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>M|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    Hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.649.246>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
      >\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> we have
      ><around*|\<\|\|\>|F-F<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f-f<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.648\<point\>246>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F|]><rsub|\<infty\>>-<around*|[|F<rsub|n>|]><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F-F<rsub|n>|]><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-F<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which combinded with [eq: <reference|eq 20.649.246>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      >\<exists\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
      >\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text| we have
      ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f-f<rsub|n>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><text| converges in
      ><around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is complete>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.482.247>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space, <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> then
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a Banach space.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|LP spaces normed space>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a normed space>
    </equation*>

    So it remains to prove that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a Banach space. We use [theorem: <reference|series absolute
    convergence equals convergence only in Banach space>] stating that a
    normed space is complete if and only if every absolute convergent series
    is convergent. So let <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    be a absolute convergent series. Then by definition\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.651.247><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp><text|
      converges to a element in ><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>
    </equation>

    As <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    there exist a <math|<around*|{|F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.652.247>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >f<rsub|i>=<around*|[|F<rsub|i>|]><rsub|p>
    </equation>

    As <math|<around*|{|F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    we have using [theorem: <reference|LP spaces absolute value>]
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
    hence using [theorem: <reference|LP spaces finite sum>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.653.247><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    So using the definition of <math|><math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    [see definition: <reference|LP spaces>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.654\<point\>247>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      ><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>=<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|\|><rsup|p>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.655.247>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      ><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\><big|int><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|x\<in\>X> we have that
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||F<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>
    which together with the monotonity of the power function [see theorem:
    <reference|exp generalized power properties>] proves that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.656.247>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<wedge\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><around*|\||F<rsup|><rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>
    </equation>

    Hence using [theorem: <reference|lemma 20.477.246>] it follows that
    <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|def><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>>
    is well defined and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.656.246><label|eq 20.657.247>\<forall\>x\<in\>X
      g<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>=<around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.476.246>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>
    </equation>

    Using the above together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.655.247>] allows us
    to use [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>] giving

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.658.247>g=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    The conditions for the Monotone Convergence Theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)>] are now satisfied by [eq:
    <reference|eq 20.655.247>, <reference|eq 20.656.247>, <reference|eq
    20.658.247>], hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.659.247><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>
      d\<mu\> <text| exist and ><big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    Further, using the monotonicity of the integral [see theorem:
    <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum inequality>] on [eq:
    <reference|eq 20.656.247>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.660.247>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsup|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><around*|\||F<rsup|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    so that by [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing
    sequence exist>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.661.247><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Next\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.655.247>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsup|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|exp generalized power properties>],><around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>
      <text|is a norm>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.479.246>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|extended infinite sum>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.662.247><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>
    </equation>

    Next

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.659.247>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.661.247>]>>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1><around*|\||F<rsub|i>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.662.247>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.651.247>]>>>|<cell|\<infty\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which, as also <math|g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>.<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
    proves by [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.663.247>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties (2)>] it follows
    from the above that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.664.247>N=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><infix-and>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=\<infty\>|}>|)>=0
    </equation>

    As <math|g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.658.247>] it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>,<reference|complex
    measurable function properties>] that
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
    from the above we have by [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere and
    characteristic> that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g=g>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. Finally, as by [eq: <reference|eq 20.663.247>]
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral and a.e.
    equalitiy>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|x\<in\>X> then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|g<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
    if >x\<in\>X\\N>>|<row|<cell|0\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text| if
    >x\<in\>N>>>>>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.665.247>\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|f<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>|}>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X\\N> then <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.657.247>]>>g<around*|(|x|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.664.247>]>>\<infty\>>, hence
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    [otherwise we have <math|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>=\<infty\><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|def>\<infty\>>].
    To summarize we have proved that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.718.265>\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\N<text| >we have
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\><rsup|>
    </equation>

    Using Dominant Convergence [see theorem: <reference|series absolute
    dominant convergence>] on the above for the Banach space
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-C\>,\<\|\|\>|\<rangle\>>> proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.666.247>\<forall\>x\<in\>X\\N<text|
      ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-K\><text|
      exist>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.667.247>F:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-K\><text| by
      >F<around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>X\\N>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if >x\<in\>N>>>>>
    </equation>

    As <math|<around*|{|F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
    <reference|LP spaces>]>><around*|{|F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
    further, using [theorems: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>,<reference|complex measurable function properties>], it follows
    that <math|<around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
    Hence using [theorems: <reference|measurable function sum
    (finite)>,<reference|complex measurable function properties>] it follows
    that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.668.247><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      and ><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\>F<rsub|i>|)>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\>X> then\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.669.247><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>F<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>X\\N>>|<row|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>> 0<text| if
      >x\<in\>N>>>>>=F<around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    So using [theorems: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>,
    <reference|complex measurable function properties>] on the above it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.670.247>F\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      and by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.447.245>] that
      <math|<around*|\||F|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then for <math|x\<in\>X> we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\N>>Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|exp generalized power properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
        <reference|extended infinite sum>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.657.247>]>>>|<cell|g<around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Next

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.669.247>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.476.246>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|limit and lower or upper
        bound>]>>>|<cell|g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>N>>Then <math|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.667.247>]>>0\<leqslant\>0=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    So we have that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.671.247>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >0\<leqslant\><around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X\\N>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.665.247>], <math|<around*|\||F|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.670.247>] it follows from the above and
    [theorem: <reference|measure integral domination theorem>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.672.247><around*|\||F|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    which as <math|F\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> [see
    eq: <reference|eq 20.670.247>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.673.247>F\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X\\N> then given <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    we have, as <math|F<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.667.247>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>>,
    that by [theorem: <reference|sequence convergence equivalences>]
    <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|1>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0>.
    So using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.476.246>]

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|1>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>=<around*|\||<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|1>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|\|><rsup|p>=0<rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|p\<neq\>1>0.
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> it follows\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.674.247><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>=C<rsub|0><text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation>

    Further, as <math|F,<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    [see eqs: <reference|eq 20.668.247>, <reference|eq 20.670.247>] it
    follows from [theorems: <reference|measurable function properties (1)>,
    <reference|complex measurable function properties>] that
    <math|<around*|{|F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>,
    applying then [theorem: <reference|lemma 20.447.245>] \ gives\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.675.247><around*|{|<around*|\||F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\>X\\N> then given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.667.247>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|series partition series>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|\<infty\>>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.718.265>]>\<wedge\><around*|[|<text|theorem:
      <reference|series absolute convergence implies
      convergence>]>|\<nobracket\>>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence using [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power properties>]
    <math|<around*|\||F<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|\||F<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>|\|>|)><rsup|p>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.657.247>]>>g<around*|(|x|)>>. As
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=0> it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.676.247>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>\<leqslant\>g<text|
      >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation>

    So all the conditions for the Lebesgue Dominant Convergence Theorem [see
    theorem: <reference|Lebesgue's Dominated Convergence Theorem>] are
    satisfied [see eqs: <reference|eq 20.663.247>, <reference|eq 20.658.247>,
    <reference|eq 20.675.247>, <reference|eq 20.674.247> and <reference|eq
    20.676.247>], hence we have that

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|\||F-<big|sum><rsub|I=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\|><rsup|p>|)>d\<mu\><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.674.247>]>><big|int>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|measure integral of zero function (1)>]>>0
    </equation*>

    proving

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>=0
    </equation*>

    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.476.246>] it follows then that

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>|)><rsup|1/p><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>|)><rsup|1/p>=0<rsup|1/p>=0
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.677.247><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\<\|\|\>|F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=0
    </equation>

    As <math|F\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    [see <reference|eq 20.673.247>] we have
    <math|<around*|[|F|]><rsub|p>\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F|]><rsub|p>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F|]><rsub|p>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|[|F<rsub|i>|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that by [eq: <reference|eq 20.677.247>] we have that
    \ <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F|]><rsub|p>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=0>.
    Hence using [theorem: <reference|sequence convergence equivalences>] it
    follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><text| converges and
      ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>=<around*|[|F|]><rsub|p>\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    In other words we have proved that every absolute convergent series
    converges which by [theorem: <reference|series absolute convergence
    equals convergence only in Banach space>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a Banach space>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Summarizing the previous lemma's we have\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces are Banach spaces>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> then
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a Banach space.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    This follows from [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.480.246>] and [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.482.247>].
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Density>

  We show now that <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> is dense in
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> [see
  theorem: <reference|normed dense set>]. First we prove this for the case
  where <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>.

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP space density (1)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space, <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+> there exist a
    g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><big|cap>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    such that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> so that
    using [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>] we
    have <math|<rigid|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>.
    Using [theorem: <reference|non negative function as limit of simple
    functions>] there exists <math|<around*|{|h<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,<around*|{|g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.678.247>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>h<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\<wedge\>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.679.247>f<rsup|+>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing sequence
      exist>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|h<rsub|n>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<wedge\>f<rsup|->=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing sequence
      exist>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|n>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Define now\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[definition:
      <reference|simple function measurable definition
      (1)>]>>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      by >f<rsub|n>=h<rsub|n>-g<rsub|n><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|simple function measurable sum and scalar product>]
      >>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Given <math|x\<in\>X> we have <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\||<around*|(|h<rsub|n>-g<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\||h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>,g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<cell|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>+g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.679.247>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    So we can use [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.448.245>] giving\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.680.247.1>f<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><below|\<Rightarrow\>|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>f<rsub|n>-f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    Hence we have by definition

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.680.247>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      ><around*|\||f<rsub|n>-f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrow\><around*|\||f<rsub|n>-f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    Further we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-<around*|(|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\||h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.679.247>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|0\<leqslant\>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>,g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>+f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which, as the power function is monotone [see theorem: <reference|exp
    generalized power properties>], results in

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.681.247>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      ><around*|\||<around*|(|f<rsub|n>-f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then as <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we
    have that <math|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    so that by [eq: <reference|eq 20.679.247>]
    <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    and <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    so that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorems:
      <reference|limit a.x_n>,<reference|limit x_n+y_n>]>
      >>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>h<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.679.247>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which, using [theorem: <reference|sequence convergence equivalences>]
    proves that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>-f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0>.
    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.476.246>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.682.247><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||<around*|(|f<rsub|n>-f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|><rsup|p>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)><text|
      or ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||f<rsub|n>-f|\|><rsup|p>=C<rsub|0>
    </equation>

    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    we have by definition that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.683.247><around*|\||f|\|><rsup|p>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    The conditions for the Lebesgue Dominant Convergence Theorem [theorem:
    <reference|Lebesgue's Dominated Convergence Theorem>] are now satisfied
    by the fact that <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    [see example: <reference|measurable function constant function>] and
    [eqs: <reference|eq 20.680.247>, <reference|eq 20.681.247>, <reference|eq
    20.683.247>]. Hence by applying this theorem we get\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><around*|\||f<rsub|n>-f|\|><rsup|p>d\<mu\>=<big|int><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||f<rsub|n>-f|\|><rsup|p>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.682.247>]>><big|int>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=0
    </equation*>

    proving\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|n>-f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>=0
    </equation*>

    So given <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> there exist a
    <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|N>-f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|p>>.
    Hence using [theorem: <reference|exp generalized power properties>] we
    have that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|N>-f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>=<around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|N>-f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)><rsup|p>|)><rsup|1/p>\<less\><around*|(|\<varepsilon\><rsup|p>|)><rsup|1/p>=\<varepsilon\>>.
    Further we have <math|f<rsub|N>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>>
    and by [eq: <reference|eq 20.680.247.1>]
    <math|f<rsub|N>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    Hence we have proved by taking <math|g=f<rsub|N>> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text| there exist a
      <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><big|cap>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|)><text|
      such that ><around*|\<\|\|\>|g-f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We have a similar theorem for <math|p=\<infty\>>, first we need a little
  lemma.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.491.248>Let <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with
    <math|a\<leqslant\>b> then given <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    there exists a <math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> and
    <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    such that <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i><rsub|>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>>,
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i+1>> and
    <math|a<rsub|i+1>-a<rsub|i>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|l=b-a>. As <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> we have by [theorem:
    <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] that there
    exists a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|<frac|2\<cdot\>l|\<varepsilon\>>\<less\>N> or
    <math|l\<less\>N\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>. Define now
    <math|n=N+1>, so that <math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|a<rsub|i>=a+<around*|(|i-1|)>\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>. Then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>> we have

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|i>=a+<around*|(|i-1|)>\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>a+i\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>=a<rsub|i+1>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|i+1>-a<rsub|i>=a+i\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>-a-<around*|(|i-1|)>\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>=<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    Further <math|a<rsub|1>=a> and <math|a<rsub|n>=a+<around*|(|n-1|)>\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>=a+N\<cdot\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<gtr\>a+l=b>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      b\<less\>a<rsub|n>
    </equation*>

    Given <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|]>> then
    <math|a\<leqslant\>x\<leqslant\>b>, define
    <math|I<rsub|x>=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>\|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>x|}>>.
    Then as <math|a<rsub|1>=a\<leqslant\>x> we have that the finite set
    <math|I<rsub|x>> is not empty so that <math|m=max<around*|(|I<rsub|x>|)>>
    is well defined. We have now two cases to consider:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|m=n-1>>Then <math|a<rsub|n-1>\<leqslant\>x> and as
      <math|x\<leqslant\>b\<less\>a<rsub|n>> it follows that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|n-1>,a<rsub|n>|[>=<around*|[|a<rsub|m>,a<rsub|m+1>|[>>

      <item*|<math|m\<less\>n-1>>Then <math|a<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>x> and as
      <math|m+1\<leqslant\>n-1> we must have <math|x\<less\>a<rsub|m+1>>
      proving that <rigid|<math|x\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|m>,a<rsub|m+1>|[>>>
    </description>

    So we conclude that <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>>
    or that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|a,b|]>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP space density (2)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> there exist a
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> such that
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    and take <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. Using [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.491.248>] there exists a
    <math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> and a family
    <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    with\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.685.248><around*|]|-<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[><text|
      and >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}><text|
      >a<rsub|i>\<less\>a<rsub|i+1>\<wedge\>a<rsub|i+1>-a<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>> then we have that
    <math|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure Borel algebra on R>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure Borel algebra on the extended real numbers
    (1)>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>. As
    <math|<rigid|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> so that
    <math|<rigid|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i-1>|[>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>>.
    Hence we can define

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.686.248><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >A<rsub|i>=f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i-1>|[>|)>
    </equation>

    Next

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|]|-<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.685.248>]>>>|<cell|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.686.248>]>>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>A<rsub|i>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.687.248>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|-<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>|)>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>> with <math|i\<neq\>j>
    then we may assume without loss of generality that <math|i\<less\>j>
    [otherwise switch <math|i> and <math|j>]. Assume that
    <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>> then
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[><big|cap><around*|[|a<rsub|j>,a<rsub|j+1>|[>>
    so that <math|a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>a<rsub|i+1>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>a<rsub|j+1>>
    leading to the contradiction <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>.
    Hence the assumption is wrong and we must have that
    <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>. So its proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.688.248><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    Using then [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (0)>] we have
    that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.689.248>g=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n-1>a<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
    then by [eq: <reference|eq 20.688.248>] there exist a unique
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>> such that
    <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>> from which it follows that
    <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>\<Rightarrow\>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>a<rsub|i+1>>
    so that <math|0\<leqslant\>f<around*|(|x|)>-a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|i+1>-a<rsub|i>=<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
    As <math|g<around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|simple function property>]>>a<rsub|i>> it follows that
    <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.690.248>\<forall\>x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>A<rsub|i><text|
      we have ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
    </equation>

    If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|}>>
    then using the above we must have that
    <math|x\<in\>X\\<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>A<rsub|i>>.
    Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.687.248>] it follows that
    <math|x\<nin\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|<around*|[|-<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>|)>>
    so that <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<nin\><around*|[|-<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>,<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|]>>
    hence <math|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>-<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<vee\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>f<around*|(|x|)>>
    from which we conclude that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>>.
    So that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.691.248><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|}>\<subseteq\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>
    </equation>

    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    we have by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.463.246>] that
    <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|}>>
    is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null so that by [theorem: <reference|LP spaces
    locally m-null set properties>] <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>-g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<gtr\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>|}>>
    is locally <math|\<mu\>>-null. So we have that
    <math|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<in\><around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|f-g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>M|\|>|}><text|
    is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>> so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|\||<around*|(|f-g|)><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>M|\|>|}><text|
      is locally >\<mu\><text|-null>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    In other words\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text| there exist a
      >g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text| such that
      ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The two previous theorem motivates the following definition

  <\definition>
    Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    measure space and <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> then we define

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|\<infty\>>\|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><text|
      if >p=\<infty\>|}>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|<around*|[|f|]><rsub|p>\|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><big|cap>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>|}><text|
      if >p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>>>>>\<subseteq\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      If <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> then <math|f> is
      <math|\<cal-A\>>-measurable and <math|f<around*|(|X|)>> is finite and
      non empty [as <math|X\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>], hence <math|f> is bounded
      above by <math|M=max<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||y|\|>\|y\<in\>f<around*|(|X|)>|}>|)>>
      proving that <math|\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    </note>
  </definition>

  Using this definition we can summarize the two previous theorems.

  <\theorem>
    <label|LP spaces density>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be ameasure space, <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|]>> and
    <math|f\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> then
    <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> there exist a
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    such that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>

    <\note>
      Using the topological space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>|\<rangle\>>>
      we have by [theorem: <reference|normed dense set>] that
      <math|\<cal-S\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> is
      dense in <math|L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>. So
      that by definition of dense subsets [see definition:
      <reference|topology dense set>] we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <wide|S<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>|\<wide-bar\>>=L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|]>> then we have
    either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|p=\<infty\>>>As <math|f\<in\>L<rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      there exists a <math|F\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      such that f=<around*|[|F|]><rsub|\<infty\>>>. Using [theorem:
      <reference|LP space density (2)>] there exists a
      <math|G\<in\>S<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>> such that
      <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-G|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
      Tak<rigid|e <math|g=<around*|[|G|]><rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
      then we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F|]><rsub|\<infty\>>-<around*|[|G|]><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F-G|]><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L\<infty\>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-G|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|p\<in\><around*|[|1,\<infty\>|[>>>As
      <math|f\<in\>L<rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> there
      exists a <math|F\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      such that <math|f=<around*|[|F|]><rsub|p> >. Using [theorem:
      <reference|LP space density (1)>] there exists a
      <math|G\<in\>\<cal-S\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]><big|cap>\<cal-L\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      such that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-G|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
      Take <math|g=<around*|[|G|]><rsub|p>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|p><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f-g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F|]><rsub|p>-<around*|[|G|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|Lp>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|<around*|[|F-G|]><rsub|p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|F-G|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <section|Signed and complex measures>

  Up to now a measure <math|\<mu\>> is defined to be a non negative real
  valued function. In this section we extend this to measures that are real
  or complex.

  <subsection|Signed measures>

  <subsubsection|Definition and properities>

  First we must extend the concept of finite sums in
  <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> and ensure that they are always well
  defined. The problem is that <math|-\<infty\>+\<infty\>> and
  <math|\<infty\>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>> is not defined in
  <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>. To avoid this we restrict ourselve to
  the subsets <math|\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>> or
  <math|\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>. >

  <\definition>
    We define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>=\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|\<infty\>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>=\<bbb-R\><big|cup><around*|{|-\<infty\>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed semi groups>Using the definition of <math|+> in
    <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<bar\>>> restricted to
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>, <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>we
    have the following\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>,+|\<rangle\>>>
      is a Abelian semigroup\ 

      <item><math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>,+|\<rangle\>>>
      is a Abelian semigroup
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>We must prove the axioms of a Abelian semi group:

      <\description>
        <item*|associativity>Let <math|x,y,z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
        then we have for <math|x,y,z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> either:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>then
          using the properties of <math|+> in <math|\<bbb-R\>> we have\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|x+y|)>+z=x+<around*|(|y+z|)>
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z=\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y=\<infty\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|x+\<infty\>|)>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|\<infty\>+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y=\<infty\>\<wedge\>z=\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|x+\<infty\>|)>+\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|\<infty\>+\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<infty\>+y|)>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z=\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<infty\>+y|)>+\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>\<wedge\>y=\<infty\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<infty\>+\<infty\>|)>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>\<wedge\>y=\<infty\>\<wedge\>z=\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<infty\>+\<infty\>|)>+\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>
        </description>

        <item*|neutral element>Then we have for
        <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> either

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>then using the properties of
          <math|+> in the real numbers we have

          <\equation*>
            x+0=x=0+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>>>then

          <\equation*>
            x+0=\<infty\>+0=\<infty\>=0+\<infty\>=0+x
          </equation*>
        </description>

        <item*|commutativity>Then we have for
        <math|x,y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> either

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then using
          the properties of <math|+> in <math|\<bbb-R\>> we have\ 

          <\equation*>
            x+y=y+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y=\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\equation*>
            x+y=x+\<infty\>=\<infty\>=\<infty\>+x=y+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then

          <\equation*>
            x+y=\<infty\>+y=\<infty\>=y+\<infty\>=y+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x=\<infty\>\<wedge\>y=\<infty\>>>Then

          <\equation*>
            x+y=\<infty\>+\<infty\>=\<infty\>=\<infty\>+\<infty\>=y+x
          </equation*>
        </description>
      </description>

      <item>We must prove the axioms of a Abelian semi group:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|associativity>Let <math|x,y,z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>
        then we have vor <math|x,y,z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>> either:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          using the properties of <math|+> in <math|\<bbb-R\>> we have\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|x+y|)>+z=x+<around*|(|y+z|)>
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z=-\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|x+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>|)>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>+y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>z=-\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|x+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>|)>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|-\<infty\>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<infty\>+y|)>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>z=-\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<infty\>+y|)>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>y=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>z\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<infty\>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>|)>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>+z>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          <item*|<math|x=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>y=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>z=-\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x+y|)>+z>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|-\<infty\>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>|)>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<infty\>+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|x+<around*|(|y+z|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>
        </description>

        <item*|netural element>Then we have for
        <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>> either

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then using the properties of
          <math|+> in the real numbers we have

          <\equation*>
            x+0=x=0+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x=-\<infty\>>>Then

          <\equation*>
            x+0=-\<infty\>+0=-\<infty\>=0+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=0+x
          </equation*>
        </description>

        <item*|commutativity>Then we have for
        <math|x,y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> either

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then using
          the properties of <math|+> in <math|\<bbb-R\>> we have\ 

          <\equation*>
            x+y=y+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>y=-\<infty\>>>Then\ 

          <\equation*>
            x+y=x+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=-\<infty\>=-\<infty\>+x=y+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then

          <\equation*>
            x+y=-\<infty\>+y=-\<infty\>=y+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=y+x
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x=-\<infty\>\<wedge\>y=-\<infty\>>>Then

          <\equation*>
            x+y=-\<infty\>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=-\<infty\>=-\<infty\>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=y+x
          </equation*>
        </description>
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  As <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>,+|\<rangle\>>> and
  <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>,+|\<rangle\>>> are semi
  groups we can now use [section: <reference|section sums and products>] to
  work with finite sums in these semi-groups.

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure finite sum distributivity>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then we have
    <math|\<forall\><around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    [or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>]

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
      we have <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>|)>>

      <item><math|><math|\<forall\><around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>
      we have <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>|)>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is not a ring so we can not use [theorem: <reference|sum ring product>]
      in this proof]
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ We use induction to prove this, so define\ 

    <\equation*>
      S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|<text|If
      ><around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>><text|[or
      >\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>><text|] then
      >\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>Let <math|><math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
      [or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>] then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>x<rsub|i>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>|)>>
      which proves that <math|1\<in\>S>.

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>>
      [or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>] then we have that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsup|n+1><rsub|i=1><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|n+1>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|n\<in\>S>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>x<rsub|n+1>|)>+\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended distributitivy>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|x<rsub|n+1>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>x<rsub|i>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that <math|n+1\<in\>S>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  \;

  Next we extend the definition of infinite sums of non negative numbers in
  <math|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> [see definition: <reference|extended
  infinite sum>] to infinite sums of elements in
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|->>. Unfortunately
  this comes at a cost, in contrast to the sum of non negative extended real
  numbers the infinite sum of elements in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> or
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>> is not guaranteed to exist.\ 

  <\definition>
    Let <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    then we say that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>>
    exist if <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>>
    exist and in this case we define <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>>
    by <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>>.
    Likewise if <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>\<subseteq\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>> exist if
    <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>> exist and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>> is defined by
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i>>.

    <\note>
      <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|i>> is defined in
      [definition: <reference|extended limit>]
    </note>
  </definition>

  We are now ready to define a signed measure on a measurable space.

  <\definition>
    <label|signed measure>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space then <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>> if\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item><math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
      or <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      pairwise disjoint we have that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exist and\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      One difference between signed measures and measures is that for
      measures <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      is always defined and equal to <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>|}>|)>>.
      For signed measures it is not guarenteed that
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists. So the existence must either be stated in a definition [as
      above] or proved.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|signed measure finite measure>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then a signed measure <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    is finite if\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Given two measures where one is finite we can create a sgined measure as
  the difference of these measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure as a difference of measures>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    two measures on <math|\<cal-A\>> where <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>> is finite or
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>> is finite so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    is well defined then we have that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>

      <item>If <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>>
      then <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>We have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.692.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| that
        ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>=0-0=0
      </equation>

      For finiteness we have two possible cases:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|1><text| is finite>>>Then for
        <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>Then
          <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>-\<infty\>=-\<infty\>>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          \ <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
        </description>

        Hence we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|2><text| is finite>>>Then for
        <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>Then
          <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          \ <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
        </description>

        Hence we have\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      So it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.693.248><around*|{|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>><text|
        or ><around*|{|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>
      </equation>

      Next we use induction to prove\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.694.248>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><space|1em><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      <\proof>
        Let

        <\equation*>
          S=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
          we have ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|}>
        </equation*>

        then we have:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|1\<in\>S>>Then for
          <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
          we have

          <\equation*>
            <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
          </equation*>

          which proves that <math|1\<in\>S>.

          <item*|<math|n\<in\>S\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>S>>Then for
          <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|>
          > that\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|n\<in\>S>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|n+1>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+1>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          which proves that <math|n+1\<in\>S>.
        </description>

        Mathematical induction proves [eq: <reference|eq 20.694.248>]
      </proof>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then we have, as
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>> are measures, that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        and >\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Then using [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>(3,5)] we
      have that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>
      exist and\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.695.248><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.694.248>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|]eq:
        <reference|eq 20.695.248>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      As <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.696.248><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.692.248>. <reference|eq 20.693.248> and
      <reference|eq 20.696.248>] it follows that
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>-\<mu\><rsub|2>> is a signed measure on
      <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>Assume that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>>.
      For <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|1><text| is finite>>>Then as
        <math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>>
        we have for <math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>> either:

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>>
          so that <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>-\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>Then
          <math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>
          so that \ <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>-\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
        </description>

        <item*|<math|\<mu\><rsub|2><text| is finite>>>Then
        <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
        so that <math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>,\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
        hence <math|<rigid|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A|)>>>
        which proves that \ <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>-\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>.
      </description>

      From the above we conclude that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|2>-\<mu\><rsub|1>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
      </equation*>

      is a well defined function which together with [eqs: <reference|eq
      20.692.248> and <reference|eq 20.696.248>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|2>-\<mu\><rsub|1>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure product with scalar>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    signed mesure on <math|\<cal-A\>> and <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    then <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    defined by <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>
    is a signed measure.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We have\ 

    <\equation>
      <around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)><below|=|\<mu\><text|
      is a signed measure>>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>0=0
    </equation>

    As <math|\<mu\>> is a signed measure we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>For
      <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>>Then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>
        there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
        <math|<rigid|x=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>.
        For <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> we
        have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so
          that <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<infty\>=\<infty\>> so that
          <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
        </description>

        So that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>>Then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>
        there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
        <math|<rigid|x=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>.
        For <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>> we
        have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so
          that <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<infty\>=-\<infty\>> so that
          <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>
        </description>

        So that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>>For
      <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>>Then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>
        there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
        <math|<rigid|x=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>.
        For <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>> we
        have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so
          that <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<infty\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=-\<infty\>> so
          that <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>
        </description>

        So that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>>Then if
        <math|x\<in\><around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>
        there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
        <math|<rigid|x=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>.
        For <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>> we
        have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so
          that <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<infty\>>>Then
          <math|x=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=\<infty\>> so
          that <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
        </description>

        So that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </description>

    Hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.698.248><around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|+><text|
      or ><around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    be a pairwise family of measurable sets. Then as <math|\<mu\>> is a
    signed measure we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.699.248><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
      exist and >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>(3)] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.700.248><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)><text|
      exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|signed measure finite sum distributivity>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.700.248>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.698.248>]>>>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    In other words\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
      exist and ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure finite addivity>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    be a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then
    <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    In other words <math|\<mu\>> is finite additive.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    be pairwise disjoint. Define now

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i> if
      i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|\<emptyset\><text| if
      >i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    If <math|k,l\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|k\<neq\>l> then we have either\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|k,l\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>then
      <math|B<rsub|k><big|cap>B<rsub|l>=A<rsub|k><big|cap>A<rsub|l>=\<emptyset\>>

      <item*|<math|k\<nin\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>then
      <math|B<rsub|k><big|cap>B<rsub|l>=\<emptyset\><big|cap>B<rsub|l>=\<emptyset\>>

      <item*|<math|l\<nin\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>then
      <math|B<rsub|k><big|cap>B<rsub|l>=B<rsub|k><big|cap>\<emptyset\>=\<emptyset\>>
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation*>

    So we have by the definition of a signed measure that
    <math|<math-sl|<math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>>
    exists and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.701.248>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Now if <math|n\<less\>k> we have that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<equallim\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|sum over disjoint subsets
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.702.248>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Further

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit and index
      translation>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+k+1>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.702.248>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|=|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.703.248>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    As

    <\equation*>
      <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    we have using the above and [eqs: <reference|eq 20.701.248> and
    <reference|eq 20.703.248>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Signed measures have many of the same properties as normal measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure properties (1)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> satisfies
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then we have
      <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>

      <item>If <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    If <math|B\<subseteq\>A> then <math|A=<around*|(|A\\B|)><big|cup>B> and
    <math|<around*|(|A\\B|)><big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>> so that by [theorem:
    <reference|signed measure finite addivity>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.704.248>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
    </equation>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Assume that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|B\<subseteq\>A>. For <math|\<mu\>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>If
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<infty\>> then
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.704.248>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<infty\>=\<infty\>>
        contradicting <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> hence we
        must have that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>.

        <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>>If
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=-\<infty\>> then
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.704.248>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+<around*|(|-\<infty\>|)>=-\<infty\>>
        contradicting <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> hence we
        must have that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>.
      </description>

      <item>If <math|B\<subseteq\>A> and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      then for <math|\<mu\>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>Then
        for <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>If
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<infty\>> then from [eq:
          <reference|eq 20.704.248>] we have that
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<infty\>>
          contradicting <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. Hence we
          have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>,\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>,\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
          so that from \ [eq: <reference|eq 20.704.248>] it follows that
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>If
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then by [eq:
          <reference|eq 20.704.248>] <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
          contradicting <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>. Hence we must
          have that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<infty\>> so that
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<infty\>=\<infty\>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.
        </description>

        <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>>Then
        for <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>If
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=-\<infty\>> then from [eq:
          <reference|eq 20.704.248>] we have that
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<infty\>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=-\<infty\>>
          contradicting <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>. Hence we
          have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>,\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>,\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
          so that from \ [eq: <reference|eq 20.704.248>] it follows that
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<infty\>>>If
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then by [eq:
          <reference|eq 20.704.248>] <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
          contradicting <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<infty\>>. Hence we
          must have that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=-\<infty\>> so that
          <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=-\<infty\>=-\<infty\>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.
        </description>
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure properties (2)>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and let <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> be a signed
    measure on <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>. Then we
    have

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> we have that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> such that
      <math|\<exists\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>A<rsub|i>> exists and\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i><text| if
        >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then we may always
      assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange <math|i> and
      <math|j>] then for <math|i> we have either: \ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|i=1>>Then <math|1\<less\>j> so that
        <math|1\<leqslant\>j-1> from which it follows that
        <rigid|<math|A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j-1>\<Rightarrow\>A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|1>>>
        hence <math|<rigid|B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=A<rsub|1><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>|)>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|1>|)>=\<varnothing\>>>
        so that <math|B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>

        <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>Then as
        <math|i\<less\>j> we have <math|i\<leqslant\>j-1> so that
        <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j-1>> from which it follows that
        <math|A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|i>>. So

        <\equation*>
          B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=<around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|j-1>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|j-1>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>\\A<rsub|i>|)>=\<varnothing\>
        </equation*>

        or <math|B<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>.
      </description>

      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.705.248><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      we have that <math|B<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
      if >i=1>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|i>\\A<rsub|i-1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><below|\<subseteq\>|i\<leqslant\>n>A<rsub|n>>
      so that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>>
      and <math|B<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>\<Rightarrow\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>>.
      Summarized we have

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.706.248>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n><text|
        and ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then for <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|n>> we have
      that <math|n\<in\><around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>>
      a finite set so that <math|m=min<around*|(|<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>
      exist. For <math|m> we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|m=1>>Then <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>=B<rsub|1>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>

        <item*|<math|1\<less\>m>>Then <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|m-1>> and
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|m>> so that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|m>\\A<rsub|m-1>=B<rsub|m>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>>
      </description>

      So we have that <math|A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.706.248>] gives\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.707.248>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >A<rsub|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i><text|
        and ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      By the definition of a signed measure we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.708.248><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Finally\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.707.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.708.248>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|signed measure finite addivity>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.707.248>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>First we consider the case where <math|k=1>. So let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be such that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>>. Then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>> so that by [theorem:
      <reference|signed measure properties (1)>] we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.709.248>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
      </equation>

      Define now\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      If <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we have
      <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i><below|\<subseteq\>|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>>A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i+1>=B<rsub|i+1>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i+1>
      </equation*>

      Using <math|<around*|(|1|)>> it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.710.248><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      Now\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family de Morgan>]>>>|<cell|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and
        intersection>}>\<wedge\>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>>>|<cell|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.711.248>A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>> we have
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1>>
      hence as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> it follows
      from [theorem: <reference|signed measure properties (1)>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.712.248>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|,
        ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|1><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
      </equation>

      Next

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.711.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.712.248>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem: <reference|signed
        measure properties (1)>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.710.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|B<rsub|i>=A<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|i><text|,
        [theorem: <reference|signed measure properties (1)>], [eq:
        <reference|eq 20.712.248>] >>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      To summarize we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.713.248>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        with >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text| and
        >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text|
        \ ><rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      </equation>

      We consider now the general cases where <math|1\<leqslant\>k>. So let
      <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      \ be such that <math|\<exists\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|k>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|C<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>C<rsub|i>>.
      Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >A<rsub|i>=C<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Then <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=C<rsub|1+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=C<rsub|k>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>=C<rsub|<around*|(|i+1|)>+<around*|(|k-1|)>>\<subseteq\>C<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=A<rsub|i>>.
      So we can apply [eq: <reference|eq 20.713.248>] giving\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.714.248>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended limit and index
        translation>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>> then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have, as
      <math|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, that
      <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=A<rsub|i>> proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.715.248><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> then
      <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>>. Let
      <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then we hae either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|k,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>Then
        <math|j-<around*|(|k-1|)>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> so that
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j-<around*|(|k-1|)>>=C<rsub|j-<around*|(|k-1|)>+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=C<rsub|j>>

        <item*|<math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k-1|}>>>Then
        <math|j\<less\>k> so that <math|C<rsub|k>\<subseteq\>C<rsub|j>>, as
        <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>=C<rsub|1+<around*|(|k-1|)>>=C<rsub|k>> we have
        <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|j>>.
      </description>

      From the above it follows that <math|\<forall\>j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we hae
      <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|j>> so that <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|j>>
      which proves that <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.715.248>] we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Substituting this in [eq: <reference|eq 20.714.248>] gives finally

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  For the opposite of the previous theorem we have:

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure conditions>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a extended real function such that\ 

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item><math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>
      or <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>

      <item>If <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      is pairwise disjoint we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
    </enumerate-alpha>

    then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> we have that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>
      </equation*>

      then <math|\<mu\>> is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<emptyset\>> we have
      that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>
      then <math|\<mu\>> is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be pairwise disjoint. Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Then using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.102.202>] we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.716.248><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i+1>
      </equation>

      Next\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|assumption>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|c|)>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which together with <math|<around*|(|a|)>> and <math|<around*|(|b|)>>
      proves that <math|\<mu\>> is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be pairwise disjoint. Define\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|B<rsub|i+1>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family properties (2)>]>><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>=B<rsub|i>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.717.248>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Assume that <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>
      then <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|1>=<big|cup><rsub|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|j>>
      so that there exist a <math|j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j>>. Now <math|\<forall\>k\<in\><around*|{|j+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      we have as <math|j\<neq\>k> that <math|A<rsub|j><big|cap>A<rsub|k>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
      so that it follows that <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|k>> so that
      <math|x\<nin\><big|cup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|j+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>=B<rsub|j+1>>
      which contradicts <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>.
      Hence the assumption is wrong and we must have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.718.248><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation>

      Using the hypothesis we have that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>
      exist and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|extended limit and index translation>] it
      follows then that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.719.248><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i+1>|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i+1>|)>=0
      </equation>

      Let <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. If <math|x\<in\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>
      then there exist a <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>> and a
      <math|j\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>\<wedge\>x\<in\>A<rsub|j>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>>.
      However as <math|<rigid|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>\<wedge\>j\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>
      we have <math|i\<leqslant\>k\<less\>k+1\<less\>j\<Rightarrow\>i\<neq\>j>
      so that <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>> and we reach
      a contradiction. Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| \ ><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      and using [theorem: <reference|family properties (2)>]\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<text|
        \ ><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      By <math|<around*|(|c|)>> [finite additivity] we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|c|)>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.720.248>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>
      </equation>

      For <math|\<mu\>> we have now either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>Then
        for <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>
        we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          from [eq: <reference|eq 20.720.248>] it follows that
          <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>,\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>
          so that\ 

          <\equation*>
            \<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
            ><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>|\|>
          </equation*>

          Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then as
          <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.719.248>]>>0> there exist a
          <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
          <math|\<forall\>k\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
          This proves that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
            exist and ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<infty\>>>As
          <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.719.248>]>>0> there exist a
          <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
          <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n+1>|)>|\|>\<less\>1>. So
          we have <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n+1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> which
          combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.720.248>] proves that
          <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<infty\>>.
          Hence if <math|C\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> we have that
          <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|C\<less\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
          which proves by [theorem: <reference|extended limit equivalences>]
          that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
          exist and <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<infty\>>.
          So that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
          </equation*>
        </description>

        <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>>For
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>
        we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>Then
          from [eq: <reference|eq 20.720.248>] it follows that
          <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>,\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>
          so that\ 

          <\equation*>
            \<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
            ><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>|\|>
          </equation*>

          Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then as
          <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.719.248>]>>0> there exist a
          <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
          <math|\<forall\>k\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
          This proves that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
            exist and ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=-\<infty\>>>As
          <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
          <reference|eq 20.719.248>]>>0> there exist a
          <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
          <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n+1>|)>|\|>\<less\>1>. So
          we have <rigid|<math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>
          that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n+1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> which
          combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.720.248>] proves that
          <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=-\<infty\>>>.
          Hence if <math|C\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> we have that
          <rigid|<math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
          <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>-C>>
          which proves by [theorem: <reference|extended limit equivalences>]
          that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
          exist and <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=-\<infty\>>.
          So that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=-\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
          </equation*>
        </description>
      </description>

      So in all cases we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      This together with <math|<around*|(|a|)>> and <math|<around*|(|b|)>>
      proves that <math|\<mu\>> is signed measure.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Hahn and Jordan decomposition>

  In [theorem: <reference|signed measure as a difference of measures>] we
  have showed that for two measures, where one of them is finite, the
  difference of these measures is a signed measure. In this section we want
  to show that every signed measure can be written as the difference of two
  measures.\ 

  <\definition>
    <label|signed measure positive/negative set>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then for <math|A\<subseteq\>X> we say
    that

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|A> is a <with|font-series|bold|positive set for
      <math|\<mu\>>> if <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and
      >\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.

      <item><math|A> is a <with|font-series|bold|negative set for
      <math|\<mu\>>> if <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>0>.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  <\example>
    <label|signed measure empty set is positive/negatie>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then <math|\<varnothing\>> is a
    positive set for <math|\<mu\>> and <math|\<varnothing\>> is a negative
    set for <math|\<mu\>>.
  </example>

  <\proof>
    First <math|\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, second if
    <math|E\<subseteq\>\<emptyset\>> then <math|E=\<emptyset\>> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=0> hence
    <math|\<emptyset\>> is a positive and negative set for <math|\<mu\>>.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.513.248>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then we have for the signed measure
    <math|-\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    defined by <math|<around*|(|-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>
    [see theorem: <reference|signed measure product with scalar>]

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|A> is a positive set for <math|\<mu\>>
      <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> <math|A> is a negative set for <math|-\<mu\>>

      <item>If <math|A> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>>
      <math|\<Leftrightarrow\>> <math|A> is positive set for <math|-\<mu\>>\ 
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|A> is a positive set for
        <math|\<mu\>> then we have for <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|B\<subseteq\>A> that <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
        hence <math|<around*|(|-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>0>
        which proves that <math|A> is a negative set for <math|-\<mu\>>.

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If <math|A> is a negative set for
        <math|-\<mu\>> then we have for <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|B\<subseteq\>A> that <math|<around*|(|-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>0>
        hence <math|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>0> so that
        <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>> which proves that
        <math|\<mu\>> is a positive set for <math|\<mu\>>.
      </description>

      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>If <math|A> is a negative set for
        <math|\<mu\>> then we have for <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|B\<subseteq\>A> that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>0>
        hence <math|<around*|(|-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<geqslant\>0>
        which proves that <math|A> is a positive set for <math|-\<mu\>>.

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>If <math|A> is a positive set for
        <math|-\<mu\>> then we have for <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|B\<subseteq\>A> that <math|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>>
        hence <math|0\<leqslant\>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>> so that
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>0> which proves that
        <math|\<mu\>> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>>.
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.514.248>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|A> is a positive set for <math|\<mu\>> then
      <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>

      <item>If <math|A> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>> then
      <math|\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \;

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|A> is a positive set for <math|\<mu\>> and
      <math|A,B,A\\B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|A\<subseteq\>A,B\<subseteq\>A,A\\B\<subseteq\>A> we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<wedge\>0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<wedge\>0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>
      </equation*>

      \ Now as <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|A=<around*|(|A\\B|)><big|sqcup>B>, hence

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>

      For <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> we have now as
      <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> either\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>then
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.

        <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>then we have by
        [theorem: <reference|signed measure properties (1)>] that
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
        <math|\<mu\>*<around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so that
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B\\A|)>>
        giving <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
      </description>

      So in all cases we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>

      <item>As <math|A> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>> and
      <math|A,B,A\\B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|A\<subseteq\>A,B\<subseteq\>A,A\\B\<subseteq\>A> we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>0\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>0\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<leqslant\>0
      </equation*>

      \ Now as <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|A=<around*|(|A\\B|)><big|sqcup>B>, hence

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>
      </equation*>

      For <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>0 we have now as
      \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>0> either\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<infty\>>>then
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>

        <item*|<math|-\<infty\>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>then by
        \ [theorem: <reference|signed measure properties (1)>]
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> so that, as
        <math|<rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>\<leqslant\>0>>, that
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.
      </description>

      So in all cases we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.515.248>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    be a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>. Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> be
    such that <math|-\<infty\>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>0> then
    there exist a <with|font-series|bold|negative set ><math|B> such that
    <math|B\<subseteq\>A> and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Consider the set <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>>
    then as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>,
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<subseteq\>A> we have that
    <math|0\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<Rightarrow\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.721.248>\<delta\><rsub|1>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      exist and >0\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|1>\<in\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
    </equation>

    For <math|\<delta\><rsub|1>> we have now two possible cases:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<delta\><rsub|1>=\<infty\>>>Then
      <math|0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<delta\><rsub|1>|2>,1|)>=1\<less\>\<infty\>=\<delta\><rsub|1>>

      <item*|<math|\<delta\><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>>>Then
      <math|0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<delta\><rsub|1>|2>,1|)>\<less\><frac|\<delta\><rsub|1>|2>\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|1>>
    </description>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<delta\><rsub|1>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    so that there exist <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\><around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}>>
    with <math|min<around*|(|<frac|\<delta\><rsub|1>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|1>>.
    Define now

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|n>,B<rsub|n>|)>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    </equation*>

    recursively by:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|n=1>>Then we define <math|<around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|1>,B<rsub|1>|)>>
      by <math|\<rho\><rsub|1>=\<delta\><rsub|1>> and
      <math|B<rsub|1>=A<rsub|1>> so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        B<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A<text| and
        >0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|1>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|1>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>Then
      <math|\<emptyset\>\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n-1>B<rsub|i><rsub|>|)>,\<emptyset\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      and <math|0=\<mu\><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>>. So that

      <\equation*>
        0\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n-1>B<rsub|i>|)>|}>
      </equation*>

      hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<rho\><rsub|n>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n-1>B<rsub|i>|)>|}>|)><text|
        exist and >0\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|n>
      </equation*>

      For <math|\<rho\><rsub|n>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<rho\><rsub|n>=\<infty\>>>Then
        <math|0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>=1\<less\>\<infty\>=\<rho\><rsub|n>>

        <item*|<math|\<rho\><rsub|n>\<less\>\<infty\>>>Then
        <math|0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>\<less\>\<rho\><rsub|n>>
      </description>

      so that

      <\equation*>
        0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<rho\><rsub|n>
      </equation*>

      Hence there exist a <math|B<rsub|n>\<in\><around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|}>>
      such that <math|<rigid|min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|n>>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        B<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)><text|
        and >0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|n>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So we have constructed a sequence <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|\<rho\><rsub|n>,B<rsub|n>|)>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.775.265>\<rho\><rsub|n>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
      if n=1>>|<row|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|}>|)><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.776.265>B<rsub|n>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}><text|
      if >n=1>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|}><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>>>>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.777.265>0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then we may always
    assume that <math|i\<less\>j> [otherwise exchange <math|i> and <math|j>].
    Then as <math|i\<leqslant\>j-1> we have that
    <math|B<rsub|j>\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,j-1|}>>B<rsub|k>|)>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>A\\B<rsub|i>>
    so that <math|B<rsub|j><big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><around*|(|A\\B<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>B<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.723.248><around*|{|B<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    Define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.724.148>B<rsub|\<infty\>>=<big|sqcup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      and >B=A\\B<rsub|\<infty\>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| then >B\<subseteq\>A
    </equation>

    We will prove now that <math|B> is the required negative set. As
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have from [eq: <reference|eq
    20.777.265>] that

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<delta\><rsub|1>|2>,1|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    so that <math|0\<less\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>.
    As further <math|\<mu\>> is a signed measure we have

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.723.248>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|n>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.724.148>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>
    </equation*>

    so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.725.248>0\<less\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>
    </equation>

    Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.776.265>] we have
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that <math|B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A> so
    that <math|B<rsub|\<infty\>>\<subseteq\>A> hence
    <math|A=<around*|(|A\\B<rsub|\<infty\>>|)><big|sqcup>B<rsub|\<infty\>>=B<big|sqcup>B<rsub|\<infty\>>>
    so that by [theorem: <reference|signed measure finite addivity>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.726.248>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|0\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>> we have by
    [theorem: <reference|extended x\<less\>=y then x+z\<less\>=y+z>] that
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>+0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.727.248>B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and
      >\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    This proves the last part of the lemma, the only thing that still must be
    proved is that <math|B> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>>.\ 

    As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.776.265>] <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|B<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>A> it follows that
    <math|B<rsub|\<infty\>>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>A>,
    further by the hypothesis <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, so
    using [theorem: <reference|signed measure properties (1)>] we have
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>,
    applying then [theorem: <reference|series terms converges to zero>]
    proves that

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then, using the above,
    there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[<reference|eq
      20.777.265>]>><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>-0|\|>\<less\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>,<frac|1|2>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.777.265>]
    <math|min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>>
    so that <math|min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<leqslant\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>,<frac|1|2>|)>>.
    Assume that <math|1\<less\><frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>> then
    <math|min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>=1\<less\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>,<frac|1|2>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>>
    giving the contradiction <math|1\<leqslant\><frac|1|2>>. Hence must have
    <math|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>\<leqslant\>1> so that
    <math|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>=min<around*|(|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|n>|2>,1|)>\<less\>min<around*|(|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>,<frac|1|2>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>
    from which it follows that <math|<around*|\||\<rho\><rsub|n>-0|\|>=\<rho\><rsub|n>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    This proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.728.248><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<rho\><rsub|n>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|E\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|E\<subseteq\>B>. For
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|n=1>>Then as <math|B<rsub|1>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=B<rsub|\<infty\>>>
      we have that <math|B=A\\B<rsub|\<infty\>>\<subseteq\>A\\B<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>A>
      so that <math|E\<subseteq\>A> which proves that
      <math|E\<in\><around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A|}>>. From this
      it follows that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.775.265>]>>\<rho\><rsub|1>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>Then as
      <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=B<rsub|\<infty\>>>
      we have that

      <\equation*>
        E\<subseteq\>B=A\\B<rsub|\<infty\>>\<subseteq\>A\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|E\<in\><around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|}>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>|}>|)>=\<rho\><rsub|n>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.729.248>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    Assume that <math|0\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>> then as
    <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<rho\><rsub|n><below|=|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.728.248>]>>0> there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such
    that <math|\<rho\><rsub|n>=<around*|\||\<rho\><rsub|n>-0|\|>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>>
    contradicting [eq: <reference|eq 20.729.248>]. Hence we must have that
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>0>. So the assumption is false and
    we must have that <math|\<mu\>*<around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>0>. In other
    words we have proved that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>E\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| with >E\<subseteq\>B<text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>0
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      B<text| is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>>>
    </equation*>

    The above combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.727.248>] proves the lemma.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.516.248>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>. If <math|A> and <math|B> are
    negative sets for <math|\<mu\>> such that
    <math|A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>> then <math|A<big|cup>B> is a negative
    set for <math|\<mu\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>> be such that <math|C\<subseteq\>A<big|cup>B>.
    Then <math|C=C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cup>B|)>=<around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)><big|cup><around*|(|C<big|cap>B|)>>
    and <math|<around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)><big|cap><around*|(|C<big|cap>B|)>=C<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)>=\<emptyset\>>.
    So

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>*<around*|(|C|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|C<big|cap>B|)>
    </equation*>

    As <math|A,B> are negative sets for <math|\<mu\>>,
    <math|C<big|cap>A,C<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|C<big|cap>A\<subseteq\>A>, <math|C<big|cap>B\<subseteq\>B>, we have
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>0> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|C<big|cap>B|)>\<leqslant\>0>. So we have that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|C|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|C<big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|C<big|cap>B|)>\<leqslant\>0
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|A<big|cup>B> is a negative set.
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|signed measure Hahn Decomposition>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>. Then <math|<around*|(|P,N|)>> is a
    <with|font-series|bold|Hahn Decomposition of <math|\<mu\>>> if\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|X=P<big|cup>N<text| and
      >P<big|cap>N=\<varnothing\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|P<text| is a
      positive set for >\<mu\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|N<text| is a
      negative set for >\<mu\>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </definition>

  It is possible that there are two Hahn Decompositions of a measure but then
  there is a relation between the positive and negative sets.

  <\theorem>
    <label|signed measure Hahn Decomposition condition>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|*<around*|(|P<rsub|1>,N<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|<around*|(|P<rsub|2>,N<rsub|2>|)>> be two Hahn decompositions of
    <math|\<mu\>> then <math|\<mu\><around*|(|P<rsub|1><big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|P<rsub|2><big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|P<rsub|1><big|cap>N<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|P<rsub|1><big|cap>N<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>P<rsub|1>>,<math|P<rsub|1><big|cap>N<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>,>
    <math|P<rsub|1>> a positive set for <math|\<mu\>> and <math|N<rsub|2>> a
    negative set for <math|\<mu\>>, we have
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|P<rsub|1><big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>0>
    proving that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|P<rsub|1><big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    Likewise, as <math|P<rsub|2><big|cap>N<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|P<rsub|2><big|cap>N<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>P<rsub|2>>,<math|P<rsub|2><big|cap>N<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1>,>
    <math|P<rsub|2>> a positive set for <math|\<mu\>> and <math|N<rsub|1>> a
    negative set for <math|\<mu\>>, we have
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|P<rsub|2><big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>0>
    proving that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|P<rsub|2><big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>=0
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|Hahn Decomposition Theorem><index|Hahn Decomposition
    Theorem><dueto|Hahn Decomposition Theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|<rigid|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>>
    a signed measure on <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Then there exists a Hahn decomposition of <math|\<mu\>>. In other words
    there exists subsets <math|P,N\<subseteq\>X> such that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|X=P<big|cup>N\<wedge\>P<big|cap>N=\<emptyset\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|P
      <text| is a positive set for >\<mu\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|N<text|
      is a negative set for >\<mu\>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For <math|\<mu\>> we have two cases to consider:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>As
      <math|\<varnothing\>> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>> [see example:
      <reference|signed measure empty set is positive/negatie>] and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0> it follows that
      <math|0\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A<text| is a negative
      set for >\<mu\>|}>> so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.730.248>L=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A<text|
        is a negative set for >\<mu\>|}>|)><text| exist and >L\<leqslant\>0
      </equation>

      Using [theorem: <reference|extended limit based on supremum and
      infinnum>] there exist a sequence <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A<text|
      is a negative set for >\<mu\>|}>> such that
      <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|n>> exist and
      <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>x<rsub|n>=L>. Hence there
      exist a sequence <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
      of negative sets for <math|\<mu\>> such that
      <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>=x<rsub|n>> so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.731.248><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>=L
      </equation>

      By the definition of a negative set we have
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.732.248>N=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation>

      We prove now that <math|N> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>>. Using
      [theorem: <reference|measure countable union pairwise>] there exist a
      sequence <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.733.248><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        is pairwise disjoint, >N=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Let <math|E\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|E\<subseteq\>N> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|E<big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|E<big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|E<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|E<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)><eq-number><label|eq
        20.734.248>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Given <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have by [eq: <reference|eq
      20.733.248>] <math|E<big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and
      <math|E<big|cap>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>>.
      As <math|A<rsub|i>> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>> it follows that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|E<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>0>. Hence

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.734.248>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|E<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|E<big|cap>B<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|extended limit properties>]>>0
      </equation*>

      So <math|\<forall\>E\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|E\<subseteq\>N> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>0> which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.735.248>N<text| is a negative set for >\<mu\>
      </equation>

      From the above it follows that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A<text|
      is a negative set for >\<mu\>|}>> so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.736.248>L=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A<text|
        is a negative set for >\<mu\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>
      </equation>

      Further given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|A<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
      <math|A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=N>
      and <math|N> is a negative set. So it follows from [theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.514.248>] <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>>.
      Using [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>] it follows then
      that <rigid|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<leqslant\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.731.248>]>>L>> which combined with [eq: <reference|eq
      20.736.248>]\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.737.248>L=\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|L<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq 20.730.248>]>>0>
      we have from the above that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<leqslant\>0\<less\>\<infty\>>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<neq\>\<infty\>>, further as
      <math|<rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)><below|\<in\>|N\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>>
      ><math|>we have that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<neq\>-\<infty\>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.738.248>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|P=X\\N> then we prove that <math|P> is a positive set for
      <math|\<mu\>>. As <math|\<Nu\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have that
      <math|P\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Assume that there exist a
      <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|C\<subseteq\>P> such that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|C|)>\<less\>0>. As
      <math|<rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|C|)>\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>
      we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|C|)>\<neq\>-\<infty\>> so that
      <math|-\<infty\>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|C|)>\<less\>0>. Using then
      [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.515.248>] it follows that there exist a
      negative set <math|B> for <math|\<mu\>> such that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.739.248>B\<subseteq\>C<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|C|)>\<less\>0
      </equation>

      Also we have <math|N<big|cap>B\<subseteq\>N<big|cap>C\<subseteq\>N<big|cap>P=N<big|cap><around*|(|X\\N|)>=\<varnothing\>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.740.248>N<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>
      </equation>

      As <math|N,B> are negative sets for <math|\<mu\>> we have by the above
      and ]lemma: <reference|lemma 20.516.248>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.741.248>N<big|cup>B<text| is a negative set for
        <math|\<mu\>>>
      </equation>

      Now as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<less\>0> [see eq: <reference|eq
      20.738.248>, <reference|eq 20.739.248>] we have that
      <math|<rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>=L>>.
      So

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|N<big|cup>B|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|N<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|L>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A<text|
        is a negative set for >\<mu\>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.741.248>>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|N<big|cup>B|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      leading to the contradiction <math|\<mu\><around*|(|N<big|cup>B|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|N<big|cup>B|)>>.
      So the assumption is wrong and we must have that
      <math|\<forall\>C\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|C\<subseteq\>P> we have
      <math|0\<leqslant\>C> which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.742.248>P<text| is a positive set for <math|\<mu\>>>
      </equation>

      To summarize

      <\equation*>
        N<text| is a negative set for <math|\<mu\><text|, >P<text| is a
        positive set for >\<mu\><text|, <math|\<varnothing\>=P<big|cap>N> and
        >X=P<big|cup>N>>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>>Using
      [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.513.248>] we have that
      <math|\<nu\>=<around*|(|-\<mu\>|)>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>. Further if
      <math|x\<in\><around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>
      then there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
      <math|x=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>. As we have
      either <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<Rightarrow\>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      or <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=-\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>>.
      \ Using then the previous case [see eq: <reference|eq 20.742.248>] for
      <math|\<nu\>> there exist a positive set <math|N> for <math|\<nu\>> and
      a negative set <math|P> for <math|\<nu\>> such that
      <math|P<big|cap>N=\<varnothing\>> and <math|X=P<big|cup>N>. Using
      [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.513.248>] it follows that <math|P> is a
      positive set for <math|-\<nu\>=-<around*|(|-\<mu\>|)>=\<mu\>> and
      <math|N> is a negative set for <math|-\<nu\>=-<around*|(|-\<mu\>|)>=\<mu\>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  The Hahn Decompositions allows us to write every signed measure as the
  difference of two measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Jordon Decomposition theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>> a
    signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> and <math|<around*|(|P,N|)>> a Hahn
    decomposition <math|<around*|(|P,N|)>> of <math|\<mu\>> then we have:

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| \ >\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>\<subset\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>\<wedge\>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+><text| or >\<mu\><rsup|-><text| is finite>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    Further we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      and >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. As <math|P> is a positive set for
    <math|\<mu\>> and <math|N> is a negative set for <math|\<mu\>> we have
    that <math|N,P\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that
    <math|A<big|cap>P,A<big|cap>N\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. As also
    <math|A<big|cap>P\<subseteq\>P>, <math|A<big|cap>N\<subseteq\>N> it
    follows that <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>\<leqslant\>0> so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.743.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>\<subset\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>\<wedge\>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
    </equation>

    Further we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\><big|cap>P|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0<text|
      and >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\><big|cap>N|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    and for every <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>P|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i-1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|-1|)>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.744.248>\<mu\><rsup|+>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      and >\<mu\><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      are measures >
    </equation>

    For <math|\<mu\>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>Suppose
      that there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>> then
      <math|<rigid|\<infty\>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>=-\<infty\>>>
      contradicting <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
      Hence we have that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>\<subset\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>,
      in other words <math|\<mu\><rsup|->> is finite.

      <item*|<math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>>Suppose
      that there exist a <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>> then
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>=\<infty\>> contradicting
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|-\<infty\>>>.
      Hence we have that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>\<subset\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>,
      in other words <math|\<mu\><rsup|+>> is finite.
    </description>

    Hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.745.248>\<mu\><rsup|+><text| is finite or
      >\<mu\><rsup|-><text| is finite>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then as <math|X=P<big|sqcup>N> and
    <math|P<big|cap>N=\<varnothing\>> we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<around*|(|<big|cap><around*|(|P<big|sqcup>N|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)><big|cup><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|P<big|cap>N=\<varnothing\>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.746.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    So the only thing left to prove is that we can define
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|+>> and <math|\<mu\><rsup|->> in terms of the
    supremums. Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then if <math|B\<in\>\<Alpha\>>
    with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.746.248>]>>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|B|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|B|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.745.248>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem: <reference|extended
      0\<less\>=x and 0\<less\>=y then x-y\<less\>=x>]>>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|B|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.747.248>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    Further, as <math|A<big|cap>P\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|A<big|cap>P\<subseteq\>A> and <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>>,
    we have <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>>>
    so that <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.747.248>] proves that
    <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.748.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|<around*|(|A|)>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Similar, let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then if <math|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
    <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      -\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      20.746.248>]>>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|B|)>-\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|B|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.745.248>]>\<wedge\><text|[theorem: <reference|extended
      0\<less\>=x and 0\<less\>=y then x-y\<less\>=x>]>>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|B|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.749.248>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    Further as <math|A<big|cap>N\<in\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|A<big|cap>N\<subseteq\>A> and <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|N<big|cap>A|)>>,
    we have that <rigid|<math|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
    and >B\<subseteq\>A|}>>> so that <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
    and >B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>>. Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq
    20.749.248>] proves that <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>>
    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|Jordon Decomposition><index|Jordon Decomposition><dueto|Jordon
    Decomposition>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    measurable space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    a signed measure then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      and >sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+><text| <with|font-series|bold|or> >\<mu\><rsup|-><text|
      are finite>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <text|If >\<mu\><text| is finite then >\<mu\><rsup|+><text|
      <with|font-series|bold|and> <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><text| are finite>>>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<mu\><rsup|+>-\<mu\><rsup|->
    </equation*>

    Further there exist a Hahn Decomposition <math|<around*|(|P,N|)>> of
    <math|\<mu\>> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)><text|
      and >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>
    </equation*>

    The function\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>=\<mu\><rsup|+>+\<mu\><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure and >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    For every Hahn Decomposition <math|<around*|(|P<rprime|'>,N<rprime|'>|)>>
    of <math|\<mu\>> we have that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P<rprime|'>|)>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N<rprime|'>|)>
    </equation*>

    Further if there exists a measure <math|\<nu\>> on
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>
    then <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have that
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
    In other words <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is the smallest positive
    measure such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
    The pair <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<mu\><rsup|->|)>> is called a
    <with|font-series|bold|Jordon Decomposition> of <math|\<mu\>>.
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|+>> is called the <with|font-series|bold|positive part
    of <math|\<mu\>>> and <math|\<mu\><rsup|->> is called the
    <with|font-series|bold|negative part of <math|\<mu\>>.> The function
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is called the
    <with|font-series|bold|variation of <math|\<mu\>>>. the
    <with|font-series|bold|total variation> of the signed measure
    <math|\<mu\>> noted as <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>> is
    defined to be\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using the Hahn Decomposition Theorem [see theorem: <reference|Hahn
    Decomposition Theorem>] there exist a Hahn Decomposition
    <math|<around*|(|P,N|)>> of <math|\<mu\>>. Using the previous theorem
    [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition theorem>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.751.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>\<subset\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>\<wedge\>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.752.248>\<mu\><rsup|+>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.753.248>\<mu\><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.754.248>\<mu\><rsup|+><text| or >\<mu\><rsup|-><text| is
      finite>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.755.243>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.756.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      and >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Applying [eq: <reference|eq 20.756.248>] on [eqs: <reference|eq
    20.751.248>, <reference|eq 20.752.248>, <reference|eq 20.753.248>,
    <reference|eq 20.754.248> and <reference|eq 20.755.243>] we have that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| \ >sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      and >sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>=-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\><text|>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+><text| or >\<mu\><rsup|-><text| is finite>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<mu\><rsup|+>>, <math|\<mu\><rsup|->> are measures on
    <math|\<cal-A\>> we have by [theorem: <reference|measure sum and scalar
    product of measures>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>=\<mu\><rsup|+>+\<mu\><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    If <math|\<mu\>> ia finite then <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    so that we have

    <\equation*>
      <text|If >\<mu\><text| is finite then >\<mu\><rsup|+><text| and
      >\<mu\><rsup|-><text| are finite>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>,\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|<around*|(|P<rprime|'>,N<rprime|'>|)>> is any Hahn
    Decomposition of <math|\<mu\>> we have by [theorem: <reference|Jordon
    Decomposition theorem>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P<rprime|'>|)><rsup|>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N<rprime|'>|)>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|\<nu\>> be a measure on <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
    If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.752.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.751.248>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.753.248>]>>>|<cell|-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.751.248>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    So

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.752.248>, <reference|eq
      20.753.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>=\<varnothing\>>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|<around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|A<big|cap>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cap><around*|(|P<big|sqcup>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Complex Measure>

  <subsubsection|Definition and properties>

  Just as we defined signed measures it is possible to define complex
  measures.

  <\definition>
    <label|complex measure><index|complex measure>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then a function <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> is a
    <with|font-series|bold|complex measure> if

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<emptyset\>|)>=0>

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      paiwise disjoint we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exists >
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i=\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      \ 
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> it follows that
      <math|Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>,Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>,
      or the real and imaginary parts are finite.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measure real/imaginare part>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a complex measure then\ 

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)><text|
      is a finite signed measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)><text|
      is a finite signed measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>+Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.757.248>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>|)>=Re<around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0<text|
      and >Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>|)>=Img<around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.758.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      >Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
      and >Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    be pairwise disjoint then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
    exist and <math|><math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>.
    Using [theorem: <reference|series of complex numbers>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.759.248><big|sum><rsub|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><rsup|\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)><text|
      exist with ><big|sum><rsub|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><rsup|\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=Re<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.760.248><big|sum><rsub|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><rsup|\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)><text|
      exist with ><big|sum><rsub|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><rsup|\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=Img<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>
    </equation>

    Hence we have, as <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|Re*<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>
    and <math|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>,
    that

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|i=1>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
      exist and ><big|sum><rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|i=1>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
      exist>
    </equation*>

    \ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|i=1>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Re<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.759.248>]>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|i=1>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Img<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.760.248>]>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    The above together with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.757.248>,<reference|eq
    20.758.248>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><text| and >Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><text| are
      finite signed measures>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Complex measures are evidently finite additive

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measure finite addivity>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>>
    a complex measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      pairwise disjoint we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we
      have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
    </enumerate>

    \ 

    In other words <math|\<mu\>> is finite additive.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Using the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|complex measure
      real/imaginare part>] we have that <math|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>> and
      <math|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>> are signed measures, hence by [theorem:
      <reference|signed measure finite addivity>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>Re<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        and >Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>Img<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      So that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|Re<around*|(|u|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>As <math|B\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|A=<around*|(|A\\B|)><big|cup>B> with
      <math|<around*|(|A\\B|)><big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>> so that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|1|)>>\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A\\B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We have now similar theorems as [theorems: <reference|signed measure
  properties (2)>, <reference|signed measure conditions>] for complex
  measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measure properties>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a complex measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\>> then we have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> we have that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>
      <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> <math|>we have that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>>, <math|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>>
      are finite signed measures [see theorem: <reference|complex measure
      real/imaginare part>] it follows from [theorem: <reference|signed
      measure properties (2)>] that

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
        exist
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|limit complex numbers condition>] on the
      above we have then that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex measure real/imaginare
        part>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)><text|
        exist>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex measure real/imaginare
        part>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|limit complex numbers condition>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Summarized\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>>, <math|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>>
      are <with|font-series|bold|finite >signed measures [see theorem:
      <reference|complex measure real/imaginare part>] <math|>it follows from
      [theorem: <reference|signed measure properties (2)>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
        exist
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        and ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|limit complex numbers condition>] on the
      above we have then that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex measure real/imaginare
        part>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)><text|
        exist>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex measure real/imaginare
        part>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|limit complex numbers condition>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Summarized\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measure condition>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space and let <math|\<mu\>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> be a
    complex function such that\ 

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item><math|\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>

      <item>For all finite pairwise disjoint measurable sets
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
    </enumerate-alpha>

    then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>> we have that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and \ <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>>
      then <math|\<mu\>> is a complex measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> and
      <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<emptyset\>> we have
      that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>
      then <math|\<mu\>> is a complex measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be a pairwise disjoint sequence of measurable sets. Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|n>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Then using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.102.202>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i><text|
        and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i+1>
      </equation*>

      Hence we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|hypothesis>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)><text|
        [where the limit exist]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|a|)>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|i>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which together with <math|<around*|(|a|)>> proves that <math|\<mu\>> is
      a complex measure.

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be a pairwise disjoint sequence of measureable sets. Define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|i>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j>
      </equation*>

      Then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|B<rsub|i+1>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|j><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family properties (2)>]>><big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|i,\<ldots\>,i|}>>A<rsub|j>=B<rsub|i>>
      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.768.248>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >B<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|i>
      </equation>

      Assume that <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>
      then <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|1>> so there exist a
      <math|k\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|k>>. From <math|x\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>
      we have <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|k+1>> so there exist a
      <math|j\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|j>> hence <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|k><big|cap>A<rsub|j>\<equallim\><rsub|k\<neq\>j>\<varnothing\>>
      giving the contradiction <math|x\<in\>\<varnothing\>>. So the
      assumption is false and we must have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      Using the hypothesis it follows that
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>
      exist and <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=0>.
      Using then [theorem: <reference|sequence limit start value>] it follows
      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.769.248><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i+1>|)>=0
      </equation>

      Let <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. If <math|x\<in\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>
      then <math|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>> and a
      <math|j\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>>. As
      <math|i\<leqslant\>k\<less\>k+1\<leqslant\>j> we have that
      <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<emptyset\>> contradicting
      <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>> so we have

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<emptyset\>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        <big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cup><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      So that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|b|)>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|k+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|(|b|)>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.770.248>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        ><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>|\|>
      </equation>

      Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. Then by [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.769.248>] there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such
      that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k+1>|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\><below|\<Rightarrow\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.770.248>]>><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><text|
        exist and ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      This together with <math|<around*|(|a|)>> proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><text| is a complex measure>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Jordan decomposition of a complex measure>

  As every complex measure <math|\<mu\>> can be written as
  <math|\<mu\>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>+i\<cdot\>Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>>, we
  can do a Jordan Decomposition of the real and imaginair part of
  <math|\<mu\>>. This lead to the following definition.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Jordon Decomposition complex measures>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and <math|\<mu\>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a complex measure then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a finite measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a finite measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a finite measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a finite measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>-\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>-\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>
    </equation*>

    The quadruple is called the Jordan Decomposition of <math|\<mu\>>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|complex measure real/imaginare part>] it
    follows that <math|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>> and
    <math|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>> are finite signed measures such that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>+Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>
    </equation*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by ><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by ><around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by ><around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|->:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by ><around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|-Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)><around*|(|B|)>\|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B\<subseteq\>A|}>|)><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>=<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>-<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|-><text|
      and >Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>=<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>-<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>
    </equation*>

    As <math|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>,Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>> are finite we
    have by [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition>] that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|->,<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|-><text|
      are finite measures>
    </equation*>

    Further

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>+<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|->+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>-<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|->|)>
    </equation*>

    The theorem is then proved by taking <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>=<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>=<around*|(|Re<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|->>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>=<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|+>>
    and <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>=<around*|(|Img<around*|(|\<mu\>|)>|)><rsup|->>.>
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|complex measure variation><dueto|Variation of a Complex
    Measure>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    measurable space, <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a
    complex measure then the <with|font-series|bold|variation> of
    <math|\<mu\>> noted by <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is defined by

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      where ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    In the above definition we use <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>
    > to indicate that <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    is pairwise disjoint and <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>.
    The <with|font-series|bold|total variation >of <math|\<mu\>> noted by
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>> is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>> where
      <math|A<rsub|1>=A> we have that

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>
    </note>

    so that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>
    is well defined.
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|complex measure variation is a measure>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\>> a complex measure then the
    variation\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>
    </equation*>

    is a finite measure. Further we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have that
      ><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we prove that <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is finite. Use
    [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition complex measures>] to get a
    Jordon decomposition of <with|font-series|bold|finite measures>
    <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>>
    of <math|\<mu\>>. Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and take
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint with <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>>
    then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|-><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|c><rsup|-><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|><rsup|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|c><rsup|-><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|measures
      are nonnegative>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|c><rsup|-><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>>
    is a real upper bound of\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    So that by [theorems: <reference|complex RC is conditional complete>,
    <reference|extended sup and inf exist>] we have that
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<Alpha\>>

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.771.248>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text| and
      ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
    </equation>

    Next we use [theorem: <reference|measure measure conditions>] to prove
    that <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is a measure. Let
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    be a pairwise disjoint sequence of measurable sets such that
    <math|\<varnothing\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> then we
    have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
    <math|A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<varnothing\>|\|>=0>.
    Hence <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>=0>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>\<varnothing\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|0|}>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    proving that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.772.248><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|A<big|cap>B=\<varnothing\>>. Let
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    be pairwise disjoint such that <math|<rigid|A<big|cup>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>>,
    Then given <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|A<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i><big|cap><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=<around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>>
    so that <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|><below|=|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|complex measure finite addivity>]>><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>|\|>>.
    Hence we have that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>|\|>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>|\|><eq-number><label|eq
      20.773.248>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Further\ 

    <\equation*>
      A=A<big|cap><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      B=B<big|cap>*<around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=B<big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>B|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which combined by [eq: <reference|eq 20.773.248>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>+<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>+<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>>
    is a upper bound for

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A<big|sqcup>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    so that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A<big|sqcup>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>+<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.774.248><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>+<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>
    </equation>

    For the opposite inequality. Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    be pairwise disjoint such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i>>,
    <math|B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>B<rsub|i>>. Define

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>><text|
      by ><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|C<rsub|i>=A<rsub|i><text| if
      >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|i>=B<rsub|i-n><text|
      if >i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    then

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\>A<big|cup>B>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>A\<vee\>x\<in\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
      x\<in\>A<rsub|i>|)>\<vee\><around*|(|\<exists\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>
      x\<in\>B<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
      x\<in\>C<rsub|i>|)>\<vee\><around*|(|\<exists\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>
      x\<in\>C<rsub|j+n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
      x\<in\>C<rsub|i>|)>\<vee\><around*|(|\<exists\>j\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>
      x\<in\>C<rsub|j>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>
      x\<in\>C<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>C<rsub|i>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.775.248>A<big|cup>B=<big|cup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+m>C<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Further If <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>> with
    <math|i\<neq\>j> then we have either\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<wedge\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then
      <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<emptyset\>>

      <item*|<math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\<wedge\>j\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>Then
      <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=A<rsub|i><big|cap>B<rsub|j-n>\<subseteq\>A<big|cap>B=\<emptyset\>>

      <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>n+m|}>\<wedge\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>Then
      <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=B<rsub|i-n><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<subseteq\>B<big|cap>A=\<emptyset\>>

      <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>\<wedge\>j\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,n+m|}>>>Then
      <math|C<rsub|i><big|cap>C<rsub|j>=B<rsub|i-n><big|cap>B<rsub|j-n>=\<emptyset\>>
    </description>

    hence we have that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.776.248><around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint >
    </equation>

    So

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i+n>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=n+1><rsup|n+m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n+m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\><big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>C<rsub|i>=A<big|cup>B|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.777.248>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Then as <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    we have that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>
    so that <math|<rigid|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>
    is a upper bound for

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><rigid|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>
    </equation*>

    As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.771.248>]
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>,<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>,\<in\>\<bbb-R\>,<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>-<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>-<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>
    is a upper bound for

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>B=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>-<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>+<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.774.248>] proves that
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<big|sqcup>B|)>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>+<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|B|)>>.
    Using then [theorem: <reference|measure union intersection stable>] we
    have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.778.248>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      pairwise disjoint we have ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> such that
    <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>. Then as
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>>
    are finite measures we have by [theorem: <reference|measure measure space
    properties (3)>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then there exists
    <math|N<rsup|+><rsub|r>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>,N<rsup|-><rsub|r>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>,N<rsup|+><rsub|c>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>,N<rsup|-><rsub|c>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N<rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      >\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-0|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N<rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      >\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-0|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N<rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      >\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-0|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N<rsup|-><rsub|c>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      >\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|\||\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-0|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
    </equation*>

    Take <math|N=max<around*|(|N<rsup|+><rsub|r>,N<rsup|-><rsub|r>,N<rsup|+><rsub|c>,N<rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>>
    then we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-0|\|>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.771.248>]>>\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0>.
    Hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.779.248>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text| and
      ><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\><text| we have
      ><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=0
    </equation>

    So the conditions for [theorem: <reference|measure measure conditions>]
    are satisfied by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.769.248>, <reference|eq
    20.778.248> and <reference|eq 20.779.248>] proves that
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>. This
    together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.771.248>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a finite measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    Finally let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Take

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >A<rsub|1>=A
    </equation*>

    then we have that <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    is pairwise disjoint and <math|A=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1|}>>A<rsub|i>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>|\|>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|1><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<in\><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>\<in\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    so that <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>\<in\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Banach space of signed (complex) measures>

  We show now that the collection of finite signed measures [or complex
  measures] is a Banach space.

  <\definition>
    <label|space of measures><index|<math|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>><index|<math|M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then we define <math|M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>> where
    <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> or <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>> by

    <\equation*>
      M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>=<around*|{|\<mu\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|\<cal-A\>>\|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is a <with|font-series|bold|finite >signed measure on
      <math|\<cal-A\>>>|}>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>=<around*|{|\<mu\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\><rsup|\<cal-A\>>\|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><text|
      is a complex measure on >\<cal-A\>|}>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|space of measures total variation is finite>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|)><text| we
      have >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| that
      ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    More specific we have for the total variation
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>> that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definitions:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>, <reference|complex measure
      variation>]>>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We must consider the following cases for
    <math|M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>>:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>>>If
      <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>=M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      then <math|\<mu\>> is a finite signed measure. Using [theorem:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>] we have that
      <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><rsup|+>-\<mu\><rsup|->> where
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<mu\><rsup|->> are finite measures on
      <math|\<cal-A\>> and <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>=\<mu\><rsup|+>+\<mu\><rsup|->>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
        ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>.
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>>>If
      <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>=M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      then <math|\<mu\>> is a complex measure. Hence using [theorem:
      <reference|complex measure variation is a measure>]
      <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is a finite measure so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
        ><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  We construct now on <math|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> a addition
  <math|+> and scalar product <math|\<cdot\>> operation making
  <math|<rigid|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>|\<rangle\>>>>
  a vector space.\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|space of measures is a vector space>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then <math|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a vector space over <math|\<bbb-K\>> where\ 

    <\equation*>
      +:M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<times\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      is defined by ><around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<cdot\>:\<bbb-K\>\<times\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
      is defined by ><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|0>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text| defined by
      >C<rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>=0<text| is the neutral element>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    We have for <math|\<bbb-K\>> either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|0><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>,
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|C<rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>=<around*|{|0|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
      </equation*>

      so that <math|C<rsub|o>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      is a finite signed measure. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.780.248>C<rsub|0>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>\<Rightarrow\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Then

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0+0=0
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|<around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>=<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      pairwise disjoint then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>,<math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>,
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>.
      \ By [theorem: <reference|extended infinite sum properties>] we have
      that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>
      exist and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|\<mu\>+\<nu\>> is a finite signed measure.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.781.248>\<forall\>\<mu\>,\<nu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        we have that >\<mu\>+\<nu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>,
      <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> then by
      [theorem: <reference|signed measure product with scalar>]
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is a signed measure, as also
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>=<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.782.248>\<forall\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)><text|
        we have >\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text| that
        >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>
      </equation>

      Using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.780.248>, <reference|eq 20.781.248> and
      <reference|eq 20.782.248>] together with [theorems: <reference|vector
      space function space>, <reference|vector space sub-space is a vector
      space>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>><text|
        is a vector space> \ over \<bbb-R\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>>>Let
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then\ 

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|0><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>0=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>,
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|0><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
      </equation*>

      so that <math|C<rsub|0>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      is a complex measure. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.783.248>C<rsub|0>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>\<Rightarrow\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>> then
      we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.784.248><around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0+0=0
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>,
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exists and <math|<rigid|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>,
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|I>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>.
      By [theorem: <reference|serie sum and scalar product>] we have that
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>
      exist and

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\>+\<nu\>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|\<mu\>+\<nu\>> is a complex measure. Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.785.248>\<forall\>\<mu\>,\<nu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        we have that >\<mu\>+\<nu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>> and
      <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<nu\>|)><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<nu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>0=0
      </equation*>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exist and <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|serie sum and scalar product>]
      <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
      exist and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.786.248>\<forall\>u\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)><text|
        we have >\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\><text| we have
        >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation>

      Using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.784.248>, <reference|eq 20.785.248> and
      <reference|eq 20.786.248>] together with [theorems: <reference|vector
      space function space>, <reference|vector space sub-space is a vector
      space>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>><text|
        is a vector space over >\<bbb-C\>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  Next we prove that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
  is a normed space.

  <\theorem>
    <label|space of measures normed space real case>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>> be a measurable space
    then <around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a normed space
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and let
    <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<mu\><rsup|->|)>> be the Jordon
    Decompsition of <math|\<mu\>> and <math|<rigid|<around*|(|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><rsup|+>,<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><rsup|->|)>>>
    be the Jordon Decomposition of <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> [see
    theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition> for the existence]. Let further
    <math|<rigid|<around*|(|P,N|)>>> be a Hahn Decomposition of <math|\<mu\>>
    [see theorem: <reference|Hahn Decomposition Theorem>]. For
    <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> we have either:.\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|0\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|A\<subseteq\>P> [a positive set for <math|\<mu\>>] then we have
      <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> so that
      <math|<rigid|0<below|\<leqslant\>|0\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>>>
      which proves by [definition: <reference|signed measure
      positive/negative set>] that <math|P> is a positive set for
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>>. Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|A\<subseteq\>N> [a negative set for <math|\<mu\>>] then we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>0> so that
      <rigid|<math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)><below|\<leqslant\>|0\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>>0>
      which proves that <math|N> is a negative set for
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>>. Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|P,N|)><text| is a Hahn Decomposition of
        >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      Then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|X<big|cap>P|)>-<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|X<big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>P|)>-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>P|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|0\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      \ <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>>Then if <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|A\<subseteq\>P> [a positive set for <math|\<mu\>>] then
      <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> so that
      <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)><below|\<leqslant\>|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>0>
      which proves that <math|P> is a negative set for
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>>. Likewise if <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
      with <math|A\<subseteq\>N> [a negative set for <math|\<mu\>>] then
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>0> so that
      <math|0<below|\<leqslant\>|\<alpha\>\<less\>0>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>>
      which proves that <math|N> is a positive set for
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|N,P|)><text| is a Hahn Decomposition of
        <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>>>
      </equation*>

      Then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|X<big|cap>N|)>-<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|X<big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>N|)>-\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|-\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>P|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|\<alpha\>\<leqslant\>0>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>P|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|X<big|cap>N|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    So we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.787.248>\<forall\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      we have >\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text| that
      ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>> are finite and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    By the Hahn Decomposition Theorem [see theorem: <reference|Hahn
    Decomposition Theorem>] there exists Hahn docompositions
    <math|<around*|(|P<rsub|1>,N<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|<around*|(|P<rsub|2>,N<rsub|2>|)>,<around*|(|P<rsub|12>,N<rsub|12>|)>>
    of <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>>
    respectively. Then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|P<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|1>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>0\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|1><text|
      is finite>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.514.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|1>|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.788.248>>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<geqslant\>|N<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>P<rsub|1>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|1>|)>\<geqslant\>0\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|1><text|
      is finite>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<geqslant\>|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.514.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.789.248>>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|2>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|P<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|2>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>\<leqslant\>0\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|2><text|
      is finite>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.514.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|2>|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.790.248>>>|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|2>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<geqslant\>|N<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>P<rsub|2>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>P<rsub|2>|)>\<geqslant\>0\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|2><text|
      is finite>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|12><big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<geqslant\>|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.514.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)><eq-number><label|eq
      20.791.248>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Now

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|X<big|cap>P<rsub|12>|)>-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|X<big|cap>N<rsub|12>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|12>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.789.248>, <reference|eq
      20.791.248>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|12>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.788.248>>,<reference|eq
      20.790.248>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|P<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|P<rsub|2>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<big|cap>P<rsub|1>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<big|cap>N<rsub|1>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<big|cap>P<rsub|2>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<big|cap>N<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>+<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>+<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|1>|\|><around*|(|X|)>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|2>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence we have proved the triangular inequality

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.792.248>\<forall\>\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      we have ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> be such that
    <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=0> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>\<wedge\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><text| is a
      measure>><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    So that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.793.248><text|If ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=0<text|
      then >\<mu\>=C<rsub|0><text| the neutral element in
      >M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> <math|\<mu\>>
    is a <with|font-series|bold|finite >signed measure so that by [theorem:
    <reference|Jordon Decomposition>] <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is a
    finite measure. Hence <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>,
    this together with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.787.248>, <reference|eq
    20.792.248> and <reference|eq 20.793.248>] proves that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><text| is a norm on
      >M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we prove the same for the vector space
  <math|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>

  <\theorem>
    <label|space of measures normed space complex case>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>> be a measurable space
    then <around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a normed space.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> then by
    [theorem: <reference|complex measure variation is a measure>] it follows
    that <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is a finite measure, hence we have
    that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>.
    In other words

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.794.248>\<forall\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text|
      we have ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> and
    <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>> then by [theorem: <reference|complex
    measure variation is a measure>] <math|<around*|\||\<mu\>|\|>> is a
    finite measure so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.795.248><around*|\|||\<nobracket\>>sup<around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
      exist>
    </equation>

    Further we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|complex measure variation>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|complex sup(a.x) inf(a.x)>]>\<wedge\><text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.795.248>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.796.248>\<forall\>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text|
      we have >\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\><text| that
      ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
    and

    <\equation*>
      x\<in\><around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    then <math|\<exists\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint with <math|X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>
    such that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>+sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|m\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>>
    is a upper bound for the set

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i=<around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<leqslant\><rigid|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.797.248>\<forall\>\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text|
      we have ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>+\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> is
    such that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=0> then we have for
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|complex measure variation is a
      measure>]>><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>\<wedge\><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><text| is a
      measure>><around*|\||\<mu\>|\|><around*|(|X|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.798.248><text|If ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=0<text|
      then >\<mu\>=C<rsub|0><text| the neutral element in
      >M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.794.248>, <reference|eq 20.796.248>,
    <reference|eq 20.797.248> and <reference|eq 20.798.248>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><text| is a norm on
      >M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we prove that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
  is not only a normed space but also a Banach space.

  <\theorem>
    <label|space of measures is a Banach space>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then <math|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a Banach space
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorems: <reference|space of measures normed space real case>,
    <reference|space of measures normed space complex case>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a normed space>
    </equation*>

    So to prove that <math|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    is a Banach space we must prove that every Cauchy sequence in
    <math|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> is a convergent series in
    <math|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>. So let
    <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    be a Cauchy sequence then we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.799.248>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      there exist a <math|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| with
      >\<forall\>n,m\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>.\<infty\>|}>>
      <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|\|><around*|(|X|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then we have, as
    <math|\<forall\>n,m\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|\|>> is a finite measure
    [see theorems: <reference|space of measures is a vector
    space>,<reference|Jordon Decomposition>, <reference|complex measure
    variation is a measure>], that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|m><around*|(|A|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|)><around*|(|A|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorems:
      <reference|Jordon Decomposition>, <reference|complex measure variation
      is a measure>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|\|><around*|(|X|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.799.248>]>>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    In other words\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.800.248>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0<text|
      \ \<exists\>>N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
      >\<forall\>n,m\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      and >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|m><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    Hence we have that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-K\>>
    is a Cauchy sequence in <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-K\>,\<\|\|\>|\<rangle\>>>.
    As by [examples: <reference|complete set of real numbers>,
    <reference|complete the complex numbers are complete>]
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-K\>,\<\|\|\>|\<rangle\>>> is a Banach
    space it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| ><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>
    </equation*>

    Hence we can define the following function\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.801.248>\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-K\><text|
      where >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>
    </equation>

    From which it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.802.248><text|If >\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\><text| then
      ><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|\<infty\>>
    </equation>

    Take <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and let
    <math|n\<in\><around*|{|\<bbb-N\><rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    then given a arbitrary <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have as
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>>
    that there exist a <math|M<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|M<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    we have <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
    Let <math|M=max<around*|(|N<rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>,M<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>>
    then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves, if we take <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=N<rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>,
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.803.248>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      >\<exists\>K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
      >\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}><text|
      we have >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      ><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    Now we prove that <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
    In this proof we will use [theorems: <reference|signed measure
    conditions>, <reference|complex measure condition>], so our first task is
    to prove that the conditions for these theorems are satified. \ First we
    have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.804.248>\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>0=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> be a finite pairwise disjoint family of
    measurable sets. Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have, as
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.801.248>]>><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>,
    that there exist a <math|N<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>k\<in\><around*|{|N<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> we
    have <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|n>>.
    Take <math|N=max<around*|(|<around*|{|N<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>>
    then we have <math|\<forall\>k\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|N<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>N>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><frac|\<varepsilon\>|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.805.248><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Further we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.801.248>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|k>\<in\>\<Mu\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><rsub|k><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.805.248>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsup|n><rsub|i=1>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    proving finite additivity. To summarize\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.806.248>\<forall\><around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      pairwise disjoint we have that >\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>B<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Let now <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|A<rsub|i+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> such that
    <math|<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>>. For
    <math|\<bbb-K\>> we have then two slightly different cases to consider:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>>>Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<in\>M<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><rsub|i>>
      is a finite signed measure, hence using \ [theorem: <reference|signed
      measure properties (2)>] it follows that
      <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|k>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>.

      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>>>Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><rsub|i>>
      is a complex measure so that using [theorem: <reference|complex measure
      properties>] we have that <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|k>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>.
    </description>

    So in all cases we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.807.248>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then by [eq:
    <reference|eq 20.803.248>] there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such
    that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|N><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
    From [eq: <reference|eq 20.807.248>] it follows that there exist a
    <math|K\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|K,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    we have <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|N><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
    Let <math|n\<in\><around*|{|K,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|N><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|N><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|N><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|N><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>=\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>>=0.
    To summarize we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.808.248>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      with >A<rsub|k+1>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|k><text| such that
      ><big|cap><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|k>=0<text| we have
      ><below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Again we look at the the two cases for <math|\<bbb-K\>>\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>>>Then [eqs: <reference|eq 20.804.248>,
      <reference|eq 20.802.248>, <reference|eq 20.806.248> and <reference|eq
      20.808.248>] allows us to use [them: \ <reference|signed measure
      conditions>] to prove that <math|\<mu\>> is a signed measure. As by
      [eq: <reference|eq 20.802.248>] <math|\<mu\>> is finite it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>>>Then [eqs: \ <reference|eq
      20.804.248>, <reference|eq 20.806.248> and <reference|eq 20.808.248>]
      allows us to use [theorem: <reference|complex measure condition>] to
      prove that <math|\<mu\>> is a complex measure. Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So we have proved that for <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> or
    <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>>

    <\equation>
      <label|20.809.248>\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation>

    Next we must prove that <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|n>>
    converges using the norm <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>. Consider
    for <math|\<bbb-K\>> either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>>>Let
      <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then for
      <math|n\<in\><around*|{|K<rsub|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>>,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.803.248>] that
      <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        -<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>=-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>> is a upper bound for

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A\<subseteq\>X|}><text|
        and ><around*|{|-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A\<subseteq\>X|}>
      </equation*>

      Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|X|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>>sup<around*|{|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A\<subseteq\>X|}>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|X|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]>>sup<around*|{|-<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A\<subseteq\>X|}>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
      </equation*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><rsup|+>+<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><rsup|->=<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>=<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|n>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>>>Let
      <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. As
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> is Cauchy
      there exist a <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|\<forall\>n,m\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> we have
      <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>>.
      Hence using [definition: <reference|complex measure variation>] we have
      that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.810.248>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|m>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
      </equation>

      Take <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
      pairwise disjoint such that <math|X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>>.
      Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.803.248>] there exist a
      <math|K\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|\<forall\>l\<in\><around*|{|K,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
      we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>> that
      <rigid|<math|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|l>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4\<cdot\>k>>>.
      So that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.811.248><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|l>-\<mu\>|\|>\<less\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><frac|\<varepsilon\>|4\<cdot\>k>=<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>
      </equation>

      Take now <math|n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> and
      <math|M=max<around*|(|N,K|)>> then we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.811.248>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||\<mu\><rsub|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|M><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\><rsub|M>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.810.248>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|4>>|<cell|=>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      So <math|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>> is a upper bound for
      <math|<around*|{|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|}>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|def>>sup<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k><around*|\||<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|n>-\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|n>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    In conclusion we have proved that for every Cauchy sequence
    <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
    <math|\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|k>> exist in
    \ <math|<around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    or in other words that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      is a Banach space>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Integrals based on signed or complex measures.>

  As a signed measure <math|\<mu\>> can be written as
  <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><rsup|+>-\<mu\><rsup|->> where
  <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<mu\><rsup|->|)>> is the Jordan
  decomposition of <math|\<mu\>> it make sense to define <math|<big|int>f
  d\<mu\>> as <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsup|+>-<big|int>f
  d\<mu\><rsup|+>>. Likewise as a complex measure can be written as as
  <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|+>-\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>-\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>>
  where <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>>
  is the Jordan decomposition of <math|\<mu\>> it make sense to define
  <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>> as <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>f
  d\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|+>-<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|->+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int>f
  d\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>-<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>> but this
  works only if the integrals on the left side are finite. Hence as with the
  definition of <math|\<mu\>>-integrable functions we need a extra condition
  on the measurable functions. This is provided in the following definition.

  \;

  <\definition>
    <label|space of measures bounded measurable functions><index|bounded
    measurable function><index|<math|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then the set of <with|font-series|bold|bounded measurable functions
    ><math|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\|\<exists\>M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      such that >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    more specific we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\|\<exists\>M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      such that >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\|\<exists\>M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      such that >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have
      ><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  We show now that that <math|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> is
  actually a normed vector space.

  <\theorem>
    <label|space of measures bounded normed space>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    then we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|\<langle\>|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a vector space [where <math|+> and <math|\<cdot\>> are pointwise
      defined] and <math|C<rsub|0>> defined by
      <math|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>=0> is the neutral element.

      <item><math|<around*|\<langle\>|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a vector space [where <math|+> and <math|\<cdot\>> are pointwise
      defined] and <math|C<rsub|0>> defined by
      <math|C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>=0> is the neutral element.

      <item>If we define

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>:B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        by ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      then <math|<around*|\<langle\>|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a normed space.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      [see example: <reference|measurable function constant function>] and
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|\||C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0\<leqslant\>0>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|0>\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>\<varnothing\>=B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|f,g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> then
      <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and there
      exist a <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> such
      that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>> and
      <math|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|2>>. Using
      [theorem: <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>] it follows that
      <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>, further
      we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that
      <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>+M<rsub|2>>
      which proves that <math|f+g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>f,g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        >f+g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      If <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then by [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function product with scalar>] we have
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>,
      further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rsub|1>+1>
      which proves that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
        >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\ 
      </equation*>

      Using \ [theorem: <reference|vector space function space>] and
      [theorem: <reference|vector space sub-space is a vector space>] it
      follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<langle\>|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>><text|
        is a vector space>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      [see example: <reference|measurable function constant function>] and
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> <math|<around*|\||C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0\<leqslant\>0>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|0>\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>\<varnothing\>=B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|f,g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> then
      <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> and there
      exist a <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> such
      that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>> and
      <math|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|2>>. Using
      [theorem: <reference|complex measurable function properties>] it
      follows that <math|f+g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>,
      further we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that
      <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|1>+M<rsub|2>>
      which proves that <math|f+g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>f,g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text|
        >f+g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      If <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then by [theorem: <reference|complex
      measurable function properties>] we have
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>,
      further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>M<rsub|1>+1>
      which proves that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text| we have
        >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\ 
      </equation*>

      Using \ [theorem: <reference|vector space function space>] and
      [theorem: <reference|vector space sub-space is a vector space>] it
      follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<langle\>|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>,+,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>><text|
        is a vector space>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> then
      <math|\<exists\>M\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> such that
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>
      so that <math|M> is a upper bound of
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>> which
      is not empty as <math|X\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> by the definition of a
      measurable space [see definition: <reference|measure measurable
      space>]. As <math|\<bbb-R\>> is conditional complete [see theorem:
      <reference|complex RC is conditional complete>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        exist >
      </equation*>

      So the fnction\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.813.248><around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>:B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
        where ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>
      </equation>

      is well defined. Let <math|f,g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>
      then we have then we have <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||*<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>+<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|)>+sup<around*|(|<around*|\||g<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
      </equation*>

      so that <math|><math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
      is a upper bound of <math|<around*|{|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>>.
      Hence

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
      </equation*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.814.248>\<forall\>f,g\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        we have ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f+g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>+<around*|\<\|\|\>|g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
      </equation>

      Let <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
      <math|f\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex sup(a.x) inf(a.x)>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.815.248>\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>,\<forall\>f\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        we have ><around*|\<\|\|\>|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=<around*|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>
      </equation>

      If <math|f\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> is such that
      <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=0> then we have
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>

      <\equation*>
        0\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\|x\<in\>X|}>|)>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=0
      </equation*>

      proving that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>=0=C<rsub|0><around*|(|x|)>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.816.248>\<forall\>f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]><text|
        with ><around*|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=0<text| we have
        that >f=C<rsub|0>
      </equation>

      From [eqs: <reference|eq 20.813.248>, <reference|eq 20.814.248>,
      <reference|eq 20.815.248> and <reference|eq 20.816.248>] it follows by
      definition that <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> is
      a norm on <math|B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Next we show that all bounded measurable functions are integrable.

  <\theorem>
    <label|space of measures bounded functions are integrable>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>> a finite measure and <math|f\<in\>B<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|)>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>
    </equation*>

    In other words if <math|f\<in\>B<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-K\>|]>> then
    <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>> exist.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    For <math|\<bbb-K\>> we have either\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>>>Then
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and there
      exist a <math|M\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we
      have <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>. By [theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- are measurable>]
      <math|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      further we have by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f-
      properties>] that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      <math|f<rsub|+><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M=C<rsub|M><around*|(|x|)>>.
      So

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>]>><big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|M>d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[example:
        <reference|non negative integral constant>]>>M\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)><below|\<less\>|\<mu\><text|
        is finite>>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
        inequality>]>><big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|M>d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[example:
        <reference|non negative integral constant>]>>M\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)><below|\<less\>|\<mu\><text|
        is finite>>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Hence by [definition: <reference|measure integral>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-C\>>>Then
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|complex measurable function
      alternative>]>>Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      and there exist a <math|M\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have <math|<around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M>.
      Then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have ><around*|\||Re<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex norm properties>]>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| we have ><around*|\||Img<around*|(|f|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>=<around*|\||Re<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)>|\|><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex norm properties>]>><around*|\||f<around*|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M
      </equation*>

      Using then the case <math|\<bbb-K\>=\<bbb-R\>> on the above proves that
      <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Hence it follows from <math|>[definition: <reference|complex integral>]
      that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
    </description>
  </proof>

  Now we are ready to define integration using signed and complex measures:

  <\definition>
    <label|signed measure intgral real function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and
    <math|f\<in\>B<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>> then we define
    <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>> as\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsup|+>-<big|int>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|-><text| where ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<mu\><rsup|->|)><text|
      is the Jordon Decomposition of >\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> we have by
      [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition>] that <math|\<mu\><rsup|+>>,
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|->> are finite so that by [theorem: <reference|space
      of measures bounded functions are integrable>]
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<wedge\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|->,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
      Hence <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsup|+>-<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsup|->> is
      well defined.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|signed measure integral complex function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> and
    <math|f\<in\>B<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>> then we define
    <math|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>> as\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|+>-<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\><rsup|-><text| where
      ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<mu\><rsup|->|)><text| is the Jordon
      Decomposition of >\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> we have by
      [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition>] that <math|\<mu\><rsup|+>>,
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|->> are finite measures so that by [theorem:
      <reference|space of measures bounded functions are integrable>]
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<wedge\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|->,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
      Hence <math|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|+>-<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\><rsup|->> is well
      defined.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|complex measure integral real function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> and
    <math|f\<in\>B<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>> then we define
    <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\>> as\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>f d\<mu\>=<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>-<big|int>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>-<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>
    </equation*>

    <math|<text| where ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)><text|>>
    is the Jordon Decomposition of <math|\<mu\>>.

    <\note>
      As <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> we have by
      [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition complex measures>] that
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|->>,
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>>, <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>> are finite
      measures so that by [theorem: <reference|space of measures bounded
      functions are integrable>] <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<wedge\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|->,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
      Hence <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|+>>, <math|<big|int>f
      d\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|->>,<math|<big|int>f
      d\<mu\><rsub|c><rsup|+>>,<math|<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsub|c><rsup|->> are
      well defined.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|complex measure integral complex function>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space,
    <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> and
    <math|f\<in\>B<around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|)>> then we define
    <math|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>> as\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>-<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>-<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>
    </equation*>

    <math|<text| where ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)><text|>>
    is the Jordon Decomposition of <math|\<mu\>>.

    <\note>
      As <math|\<mu\>\<in\>M<around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> we have by
      [theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition complex measures>] that
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|->>,
      <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>>, <math|\<mu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>> are finite
      measures so that by [theorem: <reference|space of measures bounded
      functions are integrable>] <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|+>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<wedge\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\><rsup|->,\<bbb-C\>|]>>.
      Hence <math|<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|+>>, <math|<big|int>f
      d\<mu\><rsub|r><rsup|->>,<math|<big|int>f
      d\<mu\><rsub|c><rsup|+>>,<math|<big|int>f d\<mu\><rsub|c><rsup|->> are
      well defined.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <subsubsection|Radon-Nikodym Theorem>

  <\definition>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym absolute continuity><index|absolute continuity of
    measures>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    measurable space and <math|\<mu\>>, <math|\<nu\>> measures on
    <math|\<cal-A\>> then<with|font-series|bold| <math|\<nu\>>>
    <with|font-series|bold|is absolute continuous with respect to
    <math|\<mu\>>> noted as <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> if\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have if
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0<text| then >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=0
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\example>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym absolute continuity trivial example>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then <math|\<mu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> it
    follows trivially that <math|\<mu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>>
  </proof>

  <\example>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym integral example>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text| defined
      by >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </example>

  <\proof>
    Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral measure construction>] it
    follows that <math|\<nu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>. Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> be such
    that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> then if
    <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>>
    we must have that <math|x\<in\>A> [for if <math|x\<in\>X\\A> then
    <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|A><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<around*|(|x|)>=0>]
    which proves that <math|<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|<around*|(|f\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>|)><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>0|}>\<subseteq\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>. Hence
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Using [theorem:
    <reference|non negative integral zero a.e. function>] it follows that
    <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>=0>.
  </proof>

  The following lemma explains why we use the name absolute continuity for
  <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> and shows the similarity with absolute continuity
  on functions based on the <math|\<varepsilon\>>-<math|\<delta\>>
  definition.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.555.149>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|k=n><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n-1>>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|n=1>>Then <math|<big|sum><rsub|k=n><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>=<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|series power series in R>]>><frac|<frac|1|2>|1-<frac|1|2>>=1=<frac|1|2<rsup|1-1>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n-1>>>

      <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|1|}>>>Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
        <reference|series power series in
        R>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|series partition series>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|n-1><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>+<big|sum><rsub|k=n><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|sum of powers lemma>]>>>|<cell|<frac|1|2>\<cdot\><around*|(|<frac|1-<frac|1|2<rsup|n-1>>|1-<frac|1|2>>|)>+<big|sum><rsub|k=n><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|1-<frac|1|2<rsup|n-1>>+<big|sum><rsub|k=n><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that <math|<big|sum><rsub|k=n><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n-1>>>.
    </description>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym absolute continuity alternative>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space and let <math|\<nu\>> be a <with|font-series|bold|finite measure
    >on <math|\<cal-A\>> then we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Updownarrow\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      >\<exists\>\<delta\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text| such that
      >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| with
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<delta\><text| we have
      >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>We prove this by contradiction. So asssume
      that there exist a <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such
      that <math|\<forall\>\<delta\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> there exist
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<delta\>>
      such that <math|\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>. More
      specific <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> there exist a
      <math|A<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>\<less\><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>> such
      that <math|\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)>>.
      Define now\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|B<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
        by >B<rsub|n>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|k><text|
        and >B=<big|cap><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>

      Then we have <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that

      <\equation*>
        B<rsub|n+1>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|n+1,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|k><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|family properties (2)>]>><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|k>=B<rsub|n>
      </equation*>

      Further we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|1>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|k>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (2)>]>><big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|extended infinite sum properties>]>><big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|k>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
        <reference|series power series in R>]>>1\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Hence we can use [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties
      (3)>] to get\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.817.249>\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|k>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Further, as <math|\<nu\>> is finite, we have by \ [theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.818.249>\<nu\><around*|(|B|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|cap><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|k>|)>=<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Also for <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|k>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (2)>]>><big|sum><rsub|k=n><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|k>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.555.149>]>><frac|1|2<rsup|n-1>>
      </equation*>

      so that by [example: <reference|limit b^n>[ and [theorem:
      <reference|limit sequence dominated by a sequence converting to 0>]
      <math|<below|lim|k\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|B<rsub|k>|)>=0>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.817.249>] gives
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|B|)>=0>. As <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> we must
      have also\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.819.249>\<nu\><around*|(|B|)>=0
      </equation>

      Now <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\><around*|{|n,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>A<rsub|n>=B<rsub|n>>
      so that <math|\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|n>|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>>.
      Hence <math|\<varepsilon\>> is a lower bound of
      <math|<around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>>
      from which it follows that <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|B<rsub|n>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.818.249>]>>\<nu\><around*|(|B|)>> which contradicts
      [eq: <reference|eq 20.819.249>]. So the assumption is wrong and we must
      have that

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
        >\<exists\>\<delta\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text| such that
        >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| with
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<delta\><text| we have
        >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> and assume that
      <math|0\<less\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. Then by
      the hypothesis there exists a <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> such if
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<delta\>> then
      <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>> a
      contradiction. Hence the assumption is wrong and we must have
      <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> which proves that

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove the Radon-Nikodym theorem. This theorem states
  that given a measurable space <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
  and two <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measures <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>> on
  <math|\<cal-A\>> such that <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> then there exist a
  <math|\<mu\>>-a-e. unique non negative measurable function <math|h> such
  that <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h
  d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>>.
  We do this in different steps.\ 

  <\enumerate>
    <item>First we prove that if <math|\<nu\>> is finite and
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h
    d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>> then
    <math|f=h<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>

    <item>We extended (1) to the cases where <math|\<nu\>> is
    <math|\<sigma\>>-finite

    <item>Next we prove that there exist a <math|h> such that
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f
    d\<mu\>>
  </enumerate>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.557.249>Let <math|X> be a set and
    <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>>, <math|h:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> be
    two functions then we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>>
      where <math|F=<around*|{|f\<gtr\>h|}>\<equallim\><rsub|def><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>>

      <item><math|<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G>\<cdot\><around*|(|h-f|)>>
      where <math|G=<around*|{|f\<less\>h|}>\<equallim\><rsub|def><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|f<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>h<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>F>>then <math|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>-h<around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>0>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>=<rigid|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\F>>then <math|><math|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>-h<around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>0>
        so that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>G>>then <math|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>-h<around*|(|x|)>\<less\>0>
        so that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->=-min<around*|(|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=h<around*|(|x|)>-f<around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|h-f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<rigid|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G>\<cdot\><around*|(|h-f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\G>>then <math|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x|)>-h<around*|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>0>
        so that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->=-min<around*|(|<around*|(|f-h|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=0=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|h-f|)><around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G>\<cdot\><around*|(|h-f|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G>\<cdot\><around*|(|h-f|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.558.249>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|\<mu\>> a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|\<nu\>> a <with|font-series|bold|finite >measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\>> and <math|h,f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| ><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f
      d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=h<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f
    d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[defintion: <reference|non negative
    integral (1)>]>>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[defintion:
    <reference|non negative integral (1)>]>>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then as <math|\<nu\>> is finite we have
    <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)>
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    hence, using [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] it follows
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<wedge\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h
      d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f
      d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    Using the linearity of <math|<big|int>> [see theorem: <reference|measure
    integral properties>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f
      d\<mu\>-<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h d\<mu\>=0
    </equation*>

    or\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.820.249>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>d\<mu\>=0
    </equation>

    Define now <math|F=<around*|{|f\<gtr\>h|}>> and
    <math|G=<around*|{|f\<less\>h|}>> then <math|F,G\<in\>\<cal-A\>> [see
    theorem: <reference|measurable function comparisation>] and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.821.249><around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.557.249>]>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)><text|
      and ><around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.557.249>]>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G>\<cdot\><around*|(|h-f|)>=<rigid|-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>>
    </equation>

    Then

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.821.249>]>><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.820.249>]>>0
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.821.249>]>>-<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,G>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-h|)>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.820.249>]>>0
    </equation*>

    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||f-h|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>+<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure integral properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>+<big|int><around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0+0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Using now [theorem: <reference|measure integral zero is a.e. zero>] it
    follows that <math|f-h=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. or using [theorem:
    <reference|almost everywhere f=g \<less\>=\<gtr\> f-g=0>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=h<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we extend the previous lemma to the cases where <math|\<nu\>> is a
  <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measure [see definition: <reference|measure sigma
  finite measure>].\ 

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.559.249>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|\<mu\>> a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|\<nu\>> a <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<sigma\>>-finite >measure
    on <math|\<cal-A\>> and <math|f,h\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have ><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h
      d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    then we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      h=f<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<nu\>> is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite we have by [definition:
    <reference|measure sigma finite measure>] and [theorem:
    <reference|measure countable union pairwise>] that there exist a
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.822.249>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i><text|
      and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then and take the measure spaces [see
    theorem: <reference|measure measure on A\|B>]

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|A<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>,\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>|\<rangle\>><text|
      ><around*|\<langle\>|A<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    where\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>=<around*|{|B\<in\>\<cal-A\>\|B\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>|}>
      <text| and >\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>,\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><text|>><text|
      are the restrictions of >\<nu\>,\<mu\><text| to
      >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    Then for <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>> we have
    <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|C\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> so that
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>><around*|(|C|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|C|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    which proves that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.870.265>\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>><text|
      is a finite measure>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function in sub space (1)>] and the
    fact that <math|f,h\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.871.266>f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>,h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>> then
    <math|C\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|C\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>> then using
    \ [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>] that

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>,C>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>,\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>,C>\<cdot\>h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|A<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><rsub|><text|
      [see definition: <math|<reference|charasteristic function>>]>
    </equation*>

    so that the following is well defined

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|C>h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>,C>\<cdot\>h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic properties>]
      >>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C>\<cdot\>h|)><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C>\<cdot\>h|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i><big|cap>C>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|C\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|C>h
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|C|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>|<cell|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|C|)>>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|C>f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>,C>\<cdot\>f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic properties>]
      >>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C>\<cdot\>f|)><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral sub measure space
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i>>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C>\<cdot\>f|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|i><big|cap>C>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|C\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|C>f
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|C|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>>|<cell|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|C|)>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.872.266>\<forall\>C\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><text|
      we have ><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|C>h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>=\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>><around*|(|C|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|C>f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>
    </equation>

    The above together with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.870.265>,<reference|eq
    20.871.266>] allows us to apply the previous lemma [see lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.558.249>] giving <math|h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>=f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><text|
    >\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>><text|-a.e.>> hence as
    <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> was choosen arbitray we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.871.265>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>=f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><text|
      >\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>><text|-a.e.>
    </equation>

    Using the definition of <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>><text|-a.e.>>
    the above becomes

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.874.265>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >\<exists\>N<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      such that ><around*|{|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>\|h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\>N<rsub|i><text|
      and >\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>><around*|(|N|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let now <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X\|h<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>>
    then as <math|X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>> there exist
    a <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<in\>A<rsub|i>> and
    <math|h<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=h<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|\|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>
    so that by [eq: <reference|eq 20.874.265>]
    <math|x\<in\>N<rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>N<rsub|i>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|x\<in\>X\|h<around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>f<around*|(|x|)>|}>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>N<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    which as also\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>N<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|N<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>><around*|(|N<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>0=0
    </equation*>

    proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      h=f<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Next we prove the existence part of the Radon-Nikodym theorem for finite
  measures.

  <\lemma>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym (finite case)><dueto|Radon-Nikodym Theorem (finite
    case>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    measurable space and let <math|\<mu\>> and <math|\<nu\>> be
    <with|font-series|bold|finite> measures on <math|\<cal-A\>> such that
    <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> [in other words <math|\<nu\>> is absolute
    convergent in relation to <math|\<mu\>>] then there exist a
    <math|h\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,A,\<bbb-R\>|]>> such that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h
      d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-F\>=<around*|{|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      we have ><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f
      d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    Take <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-F\>> then using [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function min and max>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.875.265>f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and define

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>A\|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|}>=A<big|cap><around*|{|f<rsub|1>\<gtr\>f<rsub|2>|}><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function comparisation>]>>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>A\|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|}>=A<big|cap><around*|{|f<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|2>|}><below|\<in\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function comparisation>]>>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    Then clearly we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      A=A<rsub|1><big|cup>A<rsub|2><text| and
      >A<rsub|1><big|cap>A<rsub|2>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X> then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|x\<in\>A\<Rightarrow\>x\<nin\>A<rsub|1>,A<rsub|2>>
      so that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<cal-A\>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0\<cdot\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>+0\<cdot\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>>>Then
      <math|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>\<gtr\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>> and
      <math|x\<nin\>A<rsub|2>> so that <math|max<around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>=f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>
      hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<cal-A\>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>+0\<cdot\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A<rsub|2>>>Then
      <math|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>> so
      that <math|max<around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>=f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>
      hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>|)>|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,\<cal-A\>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>max<around*|(|f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0\<cdot\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X\\A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    As <math|f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [theorems: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>, <reference|measurable function sum (finite)>,
    <reference|measurable function min and max>] that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A><around*|(|f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>+\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1>|)>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A<rsub|1>>f<rsub|1>d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A<rsub|2>>f<rsub|2>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>+\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|1><big|cup>A<rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that <math|f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-F\>>. To
    summarize\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.876.265>\<forall\>f<rsub|1>,f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-F\><text|
      we have >f<rsub|1>\<vee\>f<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-F\>
    </equation>

    By [example: <reference|non negative integral constant>]
    <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>C<rsub|0>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\>=0\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|C<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-F\>> so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.877.265>\<cal-F\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>
    </equation>

    From the above it follows that <math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> so that by [theorem:
    <reference|extended sup and inf exist>]

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.878.265>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)><text| exists [it could be
      <math|\<infty\>>]>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|extended limit based on supremum and
    infinnum>] there exist a <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-F\>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.879.265><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Define <math|<around*|{|g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> recursively
    by \ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| by
      >g<rsub|n>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|1><text| if
      >n=1>>|<row|<cell|g<rsub|n>=g<rsub|n-1>\<vee\>f<rsub|n><text| if
      >n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    then we prove by induction that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.880.265>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      \ >g<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-F\><text| and <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>
      >0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation>

    <\proof>
      Take <math|\<b-S\>=<around*|{|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\|g<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-F\>\<wedge\>f<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n>\<wedge\>g<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n+1>|}>>
      then we have\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|1\<in\>\<b-S\>>>if <math|n=1> Then
        <math|g<rsub|1>=f<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-F\>> and

        <\equation*>
          \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| >0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|g<rsub|1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|g<rsub|1>\<vee\>f|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|2><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        proving that <math|1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.

        <item*|<math|n\<in\>\<b-S\>\<Rightarrow\>n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>>Then
        <math|g<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-F\>> and using [eq: <reference|eq
        20.876.265>] we have that

        <\equation*>
          g<rsub|n+1>=g<rsub|n>\<vee\>f<rsub|n+1>\<in\>\<cal-F\>
        </equation*>

        Further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
        <math|0\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><below|\<leqslant\>|n\<in\>\<cal-S\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>>,

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|g<rsub|n>\<vee\>f<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        and

        <\equation*>
          g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>max<around*|(|g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>,f<rsub|<around*|(|n+1|)>+1><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|g<rsub|n+1>\<vee\>f<rsub|<around*|(|n+1|)>+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<rsub|<around*|(|n+1|)>+1><around*|(|x|)>.
        </equation*>

        which proves that <math|n+1\<in\>\<cal-S\>>.
      </description>

      mathematical induction finishes the proof of [eq: <reference|eq
      20.880.265>]
    </proof>

    Now, as <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>g<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-F\>>, we have that
    <math|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)>>, further from [eq: <reference|eq
    20.880.265>] it follows using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral
    scalar product, sum inequality>] that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>
    d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n+1>d\<mu\>>, hence
    <math|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>
    is increasing. So using [theorem: <reference|extended limit increasing /
    decreasing sequence exist>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.881.265><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>
      d\<mu\>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Applying [theorem: <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
    inequality>] on [eq: <reference|eq 20.880.265>] gives
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
    <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>>.
    Using then [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>] and [eq:
    <reference|eq 20.879.265>] it follows that
    <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>>.
    Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.881.265>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.882.265><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X>, <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.880.265>] we have by [theorem:
    <reference|extended limit increasing / decreasing sequence exist>] that
    <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>> exist
    and <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>.
    Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf limit>] it follows
    that the function defined by\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.884.265>g=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n>:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
      where g<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<geqslant\>0
    </equation>

    exist and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.885.266>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    So we can use the Monotone Convergence Theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)>] to get\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.886.266><big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\><below|\<equallim\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.882.265>]>><rsub|<text|>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\>\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then as <math|<around*|{|g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-F\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [corollary: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>] that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      ><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)><text|
      if >x\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>X\\A>>>>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n+1>|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    Also for <math|x\<in\>X> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>0=0=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    proving that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n>|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence all conditions for the Monotone Convergence Theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)>] are satisfied so that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n>|)>|)>d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g<rsub|n>|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|def><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|g<rsub|n><below|\<in\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 20.880.265>]>>\<cal-F\>> we have by the definition of
    <math|\<cal-F\>> that <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>,
    hence using [theorem: <reference|extended limit properties>] we have that
    <math|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
    To summarize we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.886.265>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g d\<mu\>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g<rsub|n>d\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)><below|\<less\>|\<nu\><text|
      is finite>>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    In the special case of <math|A=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have using the above
    that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.888.268><text| <math|<big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|X>g
      d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>>>
    </equation>

    Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.885.266>, <reference|eq 20.886.265>] it
    follows from the definition of <math|\<cal-F\>> that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.888.266>g=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>g<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-F\>
    </equation>

    As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.885.266>]
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measure integral measure
    construction>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.880.266>\<rho\>:A\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<rho\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+<rsub|>><rsub|A>g<rsub|>d\<mu\><text|
      is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> <math|\<rho\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
    d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
    20.886.265>]>>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> it follows that
    <math|\<rho\>> is a finite measure, as also <math|\<nu\>> is finite
    [theorem: <reference|signed measure as a difference of measures>] applies
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.890.266>\<nu\><rsub|0>=\<nu\>-\<rho\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      where >\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<rho\><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>-<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\><text| is a finite measure>
    </equation>

    We prove now by contradiction that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>0>. Assume that
    <math|\<exists\>A<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
    <math|0\<less\>\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A<rsub|0>|)>>. As
    <math|A<rsub|0>\<subseteq\>X\<Rightarrow\>0\<less\>\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|X|)>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|\<nu\><rsub|0><text|
    is finite>>\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|X|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>, further
    as <math|\<mu\>> is finite <math|\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>+1\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>,
    hence <rigid|<math|\<varepsilon\>=<frac|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|X|)>|2\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>+1|)>>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>>.
    For <math|\<varepsilon\>> we have then
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>=<frac|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|X|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>|2\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>+1|)>>\<less\><frac|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|X|)>|2>\<less\>\<nu\><around*|(|X|)>>.
    To summarize we found a <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.891.269>\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>\<less\>\<nu\><rsub|o><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<nu\><rsub|0>> is a measure and
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is a measure [see theorem:
    <reference|measure sum and scalar product of measures>] it follows from
    [theorem: <reference|signed measure as a difference of measures>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.892.266>\<nu\><rsub|0>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><text|
      is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|(|P,N|)>> be a Hahn decomposition of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|o>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> [see theorem:
    <reference|Hahn Decomposition Theorem>] then for <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    we have <math|A<big|cap>P\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| and
    >A<big|cap>P\<subseteq\>P> so that <math|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|\<nu\><rsub|o>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>=\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>>.
    As <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\><rsub|0>> are finite measures it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.893.266>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|0>*<around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>
    </equation>

    Now for <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have [taking in account that
    \ <math|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g d\<mu\>>,
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>> are finite} that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.890.266>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\>+\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<geqslant\>|\<nu\><rsub|0><text|
      is a measure>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\>+\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<geqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.893.266>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\>+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>P|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|non negative integral characteristics>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<big|cap>P>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      properties>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>
      d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,P>|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
      inequality>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|g+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,P>|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A><around*|(|g+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,P>|)>d\<mu\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which using the definition of <math|\<cal-F\>> proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.894.266>g+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,P>\<in\>\<cal-F\><text|>
    </equation>

    If <math|\<mu\><around*|(|P|)>=0> then as <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> we
    must have <math|\<nu\><around*|(|P|)>=0> so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|X|)>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|X|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|X=P<big|sqcup>N>>|<cell|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|P|)>+\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|N|)>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|P|)>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|N|)>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<nu\><rsub|0>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|N|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<around*|(|P,N<text|
      is a Hahn decomposition of >\<nu\><rsub|0>-\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)>>>|<cell|0>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which contradicts [eq: <reference|eq 20.891.269>]. Hence we must have
    that <math|0\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|P|)>> then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|g+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,P>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral scalar product, sum
      inequality>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>+<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,p>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|non negative integral characteristics>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>g
      d\<mu\>+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|P|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    As <math|><math|<big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> [see eq:
    <reference|eq 20.888.268>] and <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|P|)>>
    it follows that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>\<less\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|g+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,P>|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    However\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|g+\<varepsilon\>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,P>|)>d\<mu\><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.894.266>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<big|int><rsup|+>f\|f\<in\>\<cal-F\>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.886.266>]>><big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    which leads to the contradiction <math|<big|int><rsup|+>g
    d\<mu\>\<less\><big|int><rsup|+>g d\<mu\>>. Hence the assumption
    <math|\<exists\>A<rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
    <math|0\<less\>\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A<rsub|0>|)>> is false and we
    must have that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>0<below|\<Rightarrow\>|0\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>>\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>=0>.
    Hence <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>-<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
    d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
    20.890.266>]>><rsub|>\<nu\><rsub|0><around*|(|A|)>=0> so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.896.267>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text|
      ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    By [eq: <reference|eq 20.888.268>] <math|<big|int><rsup|+>g
    d\<mu\>\<less\>\<infty\>> so that by [theorem: <reference|non negative
    integral finite integral>] we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      M=<around*|{|x\<in\>X\|g<around*|(|x|)>=\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><infix-and>\<mu\><around*|(|M|)>=0
    </equation*>

    Define now <math|h=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\>g> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>M>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=0\<cdot\>g<around*|(|x|)>=0\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>

      <item*|<math|X\<in\>X\\M>>Then <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|x|)>=g<around*|(|x|)><below|\<in\>|x\<in\>M>\<bbb-R\>>
    </description>

    So <math|><math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\>g|)><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    and as <math|g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>]>>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.897.268>h=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\>g\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>>As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|M|)>=0> we have by [theorem:
    <reference|almost everywhere and characteristic>] that
    <math|><math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. so that by [theorem: <reference|non negative integral
    and a.e. equality>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.898.268><big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.896.267>]>>\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    Further we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\>g|)>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X\\M>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.898.268>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    the above together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.897.268>] finishes the
    theorem.
  </proof>

  Next step is to extend the above theorem to <math|\<sigma\>>-finite
  measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym Theorem><dueto|Radon-Nikodym Theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measurable space
    and let <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>> be <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<sigma\>>-finite
    measures on <math|\<cal-A\>>>. If <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> [in other
    words <math|\<nu\>> is absolute convergent in relation to <math|\<mu\>>]
    then there exist a <math|h\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h
      d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|def><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    Further if there is another <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>> then

    <\equation*>
      f=h <text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>> are <math|\<sigma\>>-fnite measures there exist
    by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma finite disjoint>] two pairwise
    disjoint families <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|B<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>,\<nu\><around*|(|B<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    and <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>=X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>B<rsub|i>>.
    Using [theorem: <reference|denumerable union properties>] there exist a
    bijection <math|\<beta\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-N\>\<times\>\<bbb-N\>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      X=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)><text|
      where ><around*|{|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then as
    <math|\<beta\>> is a bijection <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)>>.
    For <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>,\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>
    we have either

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then
      we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>=\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>>>Then
      we must have <math|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>\<neq\>\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>
      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>|)>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|j|)><rsub|2>>=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    Further <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    and <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    So if we define\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      by >C<rsub|i>=A<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|1>><big|cap>B<rsub|\<beta\><around*|(|i|)><rsub|2>>
    </equation*>

    then we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.900.269><around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint, >X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i><text|
      and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. Consider the measurable space
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|C<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>|\<rangle\>>>
    with measures <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>>,
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>> [see theorem:
    <reference|measure measure on A\|B>]. Then if
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>> we have
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A\<subseteq\>C<rsub|i>> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    and<rigid| <math|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><around*|(|C<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>
    hence we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>><text| and
      >\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>><text| are finite measures
      on >\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>> such that
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>><around*|(|A|)>=0> then
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>, as <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> it follows
    that <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>, so that
    <rigid|<math|\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>>.
    Hence we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>\<ll\>\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>
    </equation*>

    So we can apply the finite version of the Radon-Nicodym Theorem [theorem:
    <reference|Radon-Nikodym (finite case)>] to get\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.901.269>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >\<exists\>h<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|C<rsub|i,>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      such that >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>><text|
      >\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h<rsub|i>\<cdot\>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. As <math|h<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|C<rsub|i,>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    we have, using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral sub measure
    space (2)>], that <math|<around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    As further <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<text|
    ><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|h<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
    if >x\<in\>C<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|0\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
    if >x\<in\>X\\C<rsub|i>>>>>>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> it follows
    that <math|>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.902.269><around*|{|<around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>> then
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A\<subseteq\>C<rsub|i>> \ and by [theorem:
    <reference|measurable function charasteristic product>]
    \ <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|C<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>.
    Applying then [theorem: <reference|non negative integral sub measure
    space (2)>] results in

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>
    </equation*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measurable function extension (3)>] we have
    that <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>>
    so that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>
    </equation*>

    or\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|X<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\><rsub|>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h<rsub|i>
      s\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>
    </equation*>

    Further for <math|x\<in\>X> we have <math|<around*|(|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
    if >x\<in\>C<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|0\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>>>>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
    so that <math|<rigid|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>.
    To summarize we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.903.270><around*|{|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|C<rsub|i>,A>\<cdot\>h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      and >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|,>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>><text|
      ><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|X<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\><rsub|>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h<rsub|i>
      d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|A<rsub|i>>>
    </equation>

    Given <math|x\<in\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>> there
    exist a <with|font-series|bold|unique> <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i>> which allows us to define the following function:

    <\equation*>
      h:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| by
      >h<around*|(|x|)>=h<rsub|i><around*|(|x|)><text| where <math|i> is such
      that <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|i>>>
    </equation*>

    Then as <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|h<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|C<rsub|i,>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrow\>h<rsub|i><around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.904.271>h<around*|(|X|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|x\<in\>X>. Let
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then as
    <math|x\<in\>X=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>> there exist a
    <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|N>> such that
    <math|h<rsub|><around*|(|x|)>=h<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>> so that
    <math|<around*|(|h<rsub|N>|)><rsub|C<rsub|N>\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|x\<in\>C<rsub|n>>h<rsub|\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|x|)>>
    and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>\\<around*|{|N|}>> we have
    <math|x\<nin\>C<rsub|i>> so that <math|<around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>=0>.
    Let <math|n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>> then we have for
    <math|x\<in\>C<rsub|N>> either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A>>Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)><around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||h<around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|x\<in\>C<rsub|N>>>|<cell|<around*|\||h<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||h<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>-h<rsub|N><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X\\A>>Then\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)><around*|(|x|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|0-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    which proves that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    exist and <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)><around*|(|x|)>>
    Hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.903.269>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h
    </equation>

    Further using [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>] on [eq: <reference|eq 20.902.269>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.904.269><around*|{|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    So the conditions for the Beppo Levi's Theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Beppo Levi's Theorem>] from which it follows that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.905.269>\<cal-X\><rsub|A,X>\<cdot\>h=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|R|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.907.270><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)>d\<mu\>
    </equation>

    As <math|X\<in\>\<cal-A\>> and <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,X>\<cdot\>h=h> it
    follows from ]eq: <reference|eq 20.905.269>] and [eq: <reference|eq
    20.904.271>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.909.271>h\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation>

    \;

    Further we have <math|A=A<big|cap>X=A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>>
    where <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<wedge\>A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>C<rsub|i>><math|>
    so that <math|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<nu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.901.269>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>>h<rsub|i>d\<mu\><rsub|\|\<cal-A\><rsub|\|C<rsub|i>>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.903.270>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)>d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic
      properties>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,C<rsub|i>>\<cdot\><around*|(|h<rsub|i><rsub|>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)>|)>d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|measurable function extension
      (4)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|(|h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|C<rsub|i>\<uparrow\>X>|)>|\<nobracket\>>d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.907.270>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>h|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h
      d\<mu\>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence we have found a <math|h\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.909.271>] such that
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>h d\<mu\>> which
    proves existence. Now for uniqueness, assume that there exist a
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|(|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|)>> such
    that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>> then by
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.559.249>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=h<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We extend now the Radon-Nikodym theorem to signed and complex measures.
  First we extend the concept of absolute continuity.

  <\definition>
    <index|absolute continuity of signed measures><index|absolute continuity
    of complex measures>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measure space then we define:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then
      <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<nu\>> is absolute continuous with
      respext to <math|\<mu\>> noted as <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>>> if
      <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|>\<ll\>\<mu\>> where
      <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|>> is the variation of the signed measure
      <math|\<nu\>> [see theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition>].

      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a comple measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then
      <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<nu\>> is absolute continuous with
      respext to <math|\<mu\>> noted as <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>>> if
      <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|>\<ll\>\<mu\>> where
      <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|>> is the variation of the signed measure
      <math|\<nu\>> [see theorem: <reference|complex measure variation>].
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  We have the following equivalent definition for absolute contiuity on
  signed (complex) measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym absolute continuity equivalence>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>> be a measure
    space then we have the following equivalences:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> with
      Jordon decomposition <math|<around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+>,\<nu\><rsup|->|)>>
      [see theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition>] then we have

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\><text| >\<Leftrightarrow\><text|
        >\<nu\><rsup|+>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|->\<ll\>\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a complex measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> with
      Jordon decomposition <math|<around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>>
      [see theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition complex measures>] then
      we have

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\><text| >\<Leftrightarrow\><text|
        >\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>\<ll\>\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> be such that
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> then as <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> we
        have <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>=0>. As
        <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>>
        and <math|0\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>,\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>>
        we must have <math|\<mu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=0=\<mu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>>
        which proves that <math|\<nu\><rsup|+>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|->\<ll\>\<mu\>>

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> with
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0> then as
        <math|\<nu\><rsup|+>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|->\<ll\>\<mu\>>
        we have <math|\<nu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>=0=\<nu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>>
        so that <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|>=\<nu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>=0>
        which proves that <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|>\<ll\>\<mu\>>
      </description>

      <item>\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>. As <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> it
        follows that <math|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>=0>, so, as
        <math|<around*|\||\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around*|\||\<nu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>
        [see theorem: <reference|complex measure variation is a measure>], it
        follows that <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>. So as

        <\equation*>
          \<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>|)>
        </equation*>

        we must have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=0=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>+\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>
        </equation*>

        which as <math|0\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>,\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>>
        gives <math|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=0>.
        Hence we have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>\<ll\>\<mu\>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> such that
        <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=0>. As
        <math|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>\<ll\>\<mu\>\<wedge\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>\<ll\>\<mu\>>
        we have that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=0
        </equation*>

        Take <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
        pairwise disjoint such that <math|A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>>
        then we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
        <math|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=0>,
        <math|\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>=0>,
        <math|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=0>
        and <math|<rigid|\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>=0>>
        so that <math|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>=0>.
        Hence

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<nu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>+i\<cdot\>\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-i\<cdot\>\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|\||\<nu\><rsub|r><rsup|+><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>+<around*|\||\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>0>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        so that

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||\<nu\>|\|><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>\<wedge\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsup|>\<wedge\>A=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|0|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        proving that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  To prove the Radon-Nikodym theorem for signed or complex measures, we first
  prove uniqueness.\ 

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.594.271>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|\<mu\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measure on
    <math|\<mu\>>, <math|\<nu\>> a <with|font-series|bold|finite signed
    measure >on <math|\<cal-A\>> and <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|LP spaces canonical example>]>>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| ><big|int><rsub|A>f
      d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsub|A>g d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then we have by [theorems: <reference|measure
    integral to measure over>,<reference|measure integral properties>]\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>|)>d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>-<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>g|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.910.271>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>|)>d\<mu\>=0
    </equation>

    Define now <math|F=<around*|{|f\<gtr\>g|}>> and
    <math|G=<around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>> then
    <math|<around*|{|f\<less\>g|}>,<around*|{|g\<less\>f|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
    [see theorem: <reference|measurable function comparisation>] \ we have by
    [theorem: <reference|lemma 20.557.249>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 19.759.043><around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|+>=\<cal-X\><rsub|F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)><text|
      and ><around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|->=\<cal-X\><rsub|G>\<cdot\><around*|(|g-f|)>=-\<cal-X\><rsub|G>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>
    </equation>

    Next we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|+>|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|+>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|F>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.910.271>]>>0<rsub|>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <tabular|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|->|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|->>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|f-g|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>=-<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|G>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>d\<mu\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.910.271>]>>0<rsub|>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral zero is a.e. zero>] we have
    then that <math|<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and
    <math|*<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. As by [theorem:
    <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]
    <math|<around*|(|f-h|)>=<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|+>-<around*|(|f-h|)><rsup|->>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere transitivity>]
    that <math|<around*|(|f-h|)>=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.. Hence by [theorem:
    <reference|almost everywhere f=g \<less\>=\<gtr\> f-g=0>]

    <\equation*>
      f=h<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>

    which concludes the lemma.
  </proof>

  We have a similar lemma for complex measures.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.595.271>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|\<mu\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\>>, <math|\<nu\>> a <with|font-series|bold|complex measure>
    on <math|\<cal-A\>> and <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|LP spaces canonical example>]>>\<cal-L\><rsup|1><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>f
      d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\>
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>|)>
      d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<around*|[|theorem:|]><text|<reference|complex
      integral property>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f
      d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>g
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|def>>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f
      d\<mu\>-<big|int><rsub|A><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>g
      d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|0>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence

    <\equation>
      <big|int>Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>|)>d\<mu\>=0\<wedge\><big|int>Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\><around*|(|f-g|)>|)>d\<mu\>=0
    </equation>

    As <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>> is real it follows from the above and
    [theorem: <reference|complex integral characteristics multiplication>]
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.913.271>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      ><big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>=0\<wedge\><big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>=0
    </equation>

    Define \ 

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|r>=<around*|{|Re<around*|(|f|)>\<gtr\>Re<around*|(|g|)>|}>,G<rsub|r>=<around*|{|Ref<around*|(|g|)>\<less\>Re<around*|(|f|)>|}>,F<rsub|c>=<around*|{|Img<around*|(|f|)>\<gtr\>Img<around*|(|g|)>|}>,G<rsub|c>=<around*|{|Img<around*|(|f|)>\<less\>Img<around*|(|g|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    Using [definition: <reference|complex integral>] and [definition:
    <reference|measure integral>] we have

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|f|)>,Re<around*|(|g|)>,Img<around*|(|f|)>,Img<around*|(|g|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
    </equation*>

    and it follows by [theorem: <reference|measurable function
    comparisation>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.914.271>F<rsub|r>,G<rsub|r>.F<rsub|c>,G<rsub|c>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Next we have S

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<cwith|5|5|2|2|cell-block|no>|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.557.249>]>>>|<cell|<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|F<rsub|r>>\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.913.271>, <reference|eq
      20.914.271>]>>>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.557.249>]>>>|<cell|<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|G<rsub|r>>\<cdot\><around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.913.271>, <reference|eq
      20.914.271>]>>>>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.557.249>]>>>|<cell|<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|F<rsub|c>>\<cdot\><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<math|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.913.271>, <reference|eq
      20.914.271>]>>>>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><around*|\||<around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->|\|>d\<mu\>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->>>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.557.249>]>>>|<cell|<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|G<rsub|c>>\<cdot\><around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.913.271>, <reference|eq 20.914.271>]>>>|<cell|0>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral zero is a.e. zero>] on the
    above gives\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>>|<cell|0>|<cell|\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->>|<cell|0>|<cell|\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>>|<cell|0>|<cell|\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>>|<cell|0>|<cell|\<mu\><text|-a.e.>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    As by [theorem: <reference|measure integral f+ f- alternative>]

    <\equation*>
      Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>=<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>-*<around*|(|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>=<around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|+>-*<around*|(|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>|)><rsup|->
    </equation*>

    so it follows from [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere transitivity>]
    that <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>-Re<around*|(|g|)>=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e and
    <math|Img<around*|(|f|)>-Img<around*|(|g|)>=0> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e..
    Applying [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere f=g \<less\>=\<gtr\>
    f-g=0>] proves that gives <math|Re<around*|(|f|)>=Re<around*|(|g|)>>
    <math|\<mu\>>-a.e. and <math|Img<around*|(|f|)>=Img<around*|(|g |)>>
    <math|\<mu\>>=a.e. Finally using [theorem: <reference|almost everywhere
    complex functions>] again proves that

    <\equation*>
      f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We are now finally ready to prove the Radon-Nikodym Theorem for signed and
  complex measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym signed/complex><dueto|Radon-Nikodym Theorem
    (Signed/Complex>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> be
    a measurable space, <math|\<mu\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measure on
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> then we have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a <with|font-series|bold|finite signed>
      measure on <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> so that
      <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> then there exists a
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X.\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>> such
      that

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
        <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>>

        <item>If <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
        such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
        <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A>g d\<mu\>> then
        <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.
      </enumerate>

      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a <with|font-series|bold|complex> measure on
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>> so that
      \ <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> then there exists a
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X.\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>> such
      that

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
        <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>>

        <item>If <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
        such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>
        <math|\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A>g d\<mu\>> then
        <math|f=g> <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.
      </enumerate>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|\<nu\>> is a signed measure there exist a Jordan
      Decomposition [see theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition>]
      <math|<around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+>,\<nu\><rsup|->|)>> of <math|\<nu\>>, so
      that <math|\<nu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+>-\<nu\><rsup|->>. As <math|\<nu\>> is
      finite we have also that <math|\<nu\><rsup|->,\<nu\><rsup|+>> are
      finite measures on <math|\<cal-A\>>. Using the Radon-Nikodym theorem
      [see <reference|Radon-Nikodym Theorem>] there exists
      <math|f<rsub|+>,f<rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| \ ><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|+>|)>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f<rsub|+>d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
        >\<wedge\><text| ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|->|)>d\<mu\><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f<rsub|->d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] on the above
      gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
        >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|+>,\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|take
        A=X>f<rsub|+>,f<rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        <text|>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
        ><big|int><rsub|A>f<rsub|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|->d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>\<wedge\><big|int><rsub|A>f<rsub|->d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|-><rsup|>d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      Take <math|f=f<rsub|+>-f<rsub|->> so that
      <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f=\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|+>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|->>
      then by [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>] we have

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|>
        <text| and >f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation*>

      and

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| ><big|int><rsub|A>f
        d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f
        d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsub|->d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+><around*|(|A|)>-\<nu\><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      Summarizing\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<exists\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        such that >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| ><big|int>f
        d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      Finally if there exists another <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> <math|<big|int><rsub|A>g
      d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>> then using [lemma: <reference|lemma
      20.594.271>] it folows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|\<nu\>> is a complex measure there exists a Jordan
      Decomposition [see theorem: <reference|Jordon Decomposition complex
      measures>] <math|<rigid|<around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>>>
      of <math|\<nu\>> so that <math|\<nu\>=<around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>-\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>-\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>>
      where <math|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r>,\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>,\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>>
      are finite measures on <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>.
      Using the Radon-Nikodym theorem [see <reference|Radon-Nikodym Theorem>]
      there exists <math|f<rsup|r><rsub|+>,f<rsup|r><rsub|->,f<rsup|c><rsub|+>,f<rsup|c><rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      such that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>|<cell|<text|we
        have >>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f<rsup|r><rsub|->d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|->d\<mu\>=<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f<rsup|c><rsub|->d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Using [theorem: <reference|measure integral extension>] on the above
      gives\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.915.271>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
        >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>,<text|
        >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|->,<text|
        >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>,<text|
        >\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      and taking <math|A=X> in the above

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.916.271>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>,<text|
        >f<rsup|r><rsub|->,<text| >f<rsup|c><rsub|+>,<text|
        >f<rsup|c><rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      further

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>|<cell|<text|we
        have >>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>f<rsup|r><rsub|->d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|->d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>f<rsup|c><rsub|->d\<mu\>=<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|X,A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|->d\<mu\>=\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c><around*|(|A|)><eq-number>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Take <math|f=f<rsup|r><rsub|+>-f<rsup|r><rsub|->+i\<cdot\><around*|(|f<rsup|c><rsub|+>-f<rsup|c><rsub|->|)>>
      then by combining [theorem: <reference|measure integral properties>]
      and [eqs: <reference|eq 20.915.271>, <reference|eq 20.916.271>] we have
      for <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|f|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|r><rsub|+>-f<rsup|r><rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>|<row|<cell|Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>-\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>|<row|<cell|Img<around*|(|f|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsup|c><rsub|+>-f<rsup|c><rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>|<row|<cell|Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>-\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|->\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      \ which by definition of the integral of complex functions proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>\<wedge\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>f
        d\<mu\>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f
        d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int>Re<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>
        d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int>Img<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>-\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|->|)>d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>-\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|->|)>d\<mu\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|r><rsub|->d\<mu\>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|+>d\<mu\>-<big|int>\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<cdot\>f<rsup|c><rsub|->d\<mu\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|r><around*|(|A|)>-\<nu\><rsub|r><rsup|-><around*|(|A|)>+i\<cdot\><around*|(|\<nu\><rsup|+><rsub|c>-\<nu\><rsup|-><rsub|c>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<nu\>*<around*|(|A|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Summarizing we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]><text| and
        >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have <math|<big|int>f
        d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>>>
      </equation*>

      Finally if there exists another <math|g\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
      <math|<big|int><rsub|A>g d\<mu\>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>> then using
      [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.595.271> we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f=g<text| >\<mu\><text|-a.e.>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  For the Riemann Integral on a Banach space [see definition:
  <reference|riemann integral>] we have the Fundamental Theorem of Analysis
  [see theorem: <reference|diff fundamental theorem of calculus (2)>]. \ This
  states that if a function <math|F:<around*|[|a,b|]>\<rightarrow\>X> [where
  <math|X> is a Banach space] has a derivate <math|F<rprime|'>> on
  <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>> then <math|<big|int><rsub|a><rsup|b>F<rprime|'>=F<around*|(|b|)>-F<around*|(|a|)>>.
  If we take the usual notation of <math|F<rprime|'>=<frac|d F|d x>> for the
  derivative and <math|<big|int><rsub|a><rsup|b>f d x> the Riemann integral
  this becomes <math|>

  <\equation*>
    l<around*|(|<around*|[|F<around*|(|a|)>-F<around*|(|b|)>|]>|)>=F<around*|(|b|)>-F<around*|(|a|)>=<big|int><rsub|a><rsup|b><frac|d
    F|d x> d x
  </equation*>

  where <math|l<around*|(|<around*|[|F<around*|(|a|)>-F<around*|(|b|)>|]>|)>>
  is the measure of the interval <math|<around*|[|F<around*|(|a|)>-F<around*|(|b|)>|]>>.
  Compare this with the Radon-Nikodym theorem which says that for measures
  <math|v,\<mu\>> with <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> there exist a integrable
  function <math|h> such that\ 

  <\equation*>
    \<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A>h d\<mu\>
  </equation*>

  Hence it seems logical to consider <math|f> as a kind of derivate and write
  <math|f> as follows <math|f=<frac|d \<nu\>|d \<mu\>>> so that we have\ 

  <\equation*>
    \<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A><frac|d \<nu\>|d \<mu\>>d\<mu\>
  </equation*>

  This motivates the definition of the Radon-Nikodym derivative.

  <\definition>
    <label|Radon-Nikodym derivative>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space, <math|\<mu\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\>> then we have the following definitions:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measure on
      <math|\<cal-A\>> then a Radon-Nikodym derivative of <math|\<nu\>> with
      repsect to <math|\<mu\>> is a <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A><rsup|+>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a finite signed measure on <math|\<cal-A\>>
      then a Radon-Nikodym derivative of <math|\<nu\>> with repect to
      <math|\<mu\>> is a <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|\<nu\>> is a complex measure on <math|\<cal-A\>> then a
      Radon-Nikodym derivative of <math|\<nu\>> with repect to <math|\<mu\>>
      is a <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have

      <\equation*>
        \<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      Using [theorems: <reference|Radon-Nikodym Theorem>,
      <reference|Radon-Nikodym signed/complex>] it follows that if
      <math|\<nu\>\<ll\>\<mu\>> then there exist a Radon-Nikodym derivative
      of <math|\<nu\>> with respect to <math|\<mu\>>.
    </note>

    <\note>
      A Radon-Nikodym derivative of <math|\<nu\>> with respect to
      <math|\<mu\>> is noted by <math|<frac|d \<nu\>|d\<mu\>>>. Be aware that
      this notation is misleading as it suggest that there exist only on
      Radon-Nikodym while [theorems: <reference|Radon-Nikodym Theorem>,
      <reference|Radon-Nikodym signed/complex>] ensure existance but not
      uniqueness. The best way to think of this notation is that
      <math|f=<frac|d \<nu\>|d \<mu\>>> is the same as saying that <math|f>
      is a Radon-Nikodym derivative so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
        >\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><rsub|A>f
        d\<mu\><text| if >\<nu\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-finite
        measure>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsub|A>f d\<mu\><text| if
        >\<nu\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-finite
        measure>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>><rsub|A>f
        d\<mu\><text| if >\<nu\><text| is a complex measure>>>>>>
      </equation*>

      Using this interpretation we have that if <math|f=<frac|d \<nu\>|d
      \<mu\>>> and <math|g=<frac|d \<nu\>|d\<mu\>>> then using [lemmas:
      <reference|lemma 20.559.249>, <reference|lemma 20.594.271> and
      <reference|lemma 20.595.271>] we have that <math|f=g>
      <math|\<mu\>>-a.e.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <section|Product measures>

  In this section we examine how given a finite family of measurable spaces
  we can define a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra and measure on the product of
  these spaces. The prime objective is to show how a integral on the product
  of these spaces can be calculated based on the integrals on the measurable
  spaces.

  <subsection|Product of <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras>

  Given a finite family <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
  of measurable spaces we show how a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra can be defined
  on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>.
  First we show how given a family <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>>
  of measurable spaces, s set <math|X> and a family of mappings
  <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>> \ we
  can define on <math|X> a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra such that <math|>these
  mappings are measurable.

  <\definition>
    <label|product >Let <math|X,Y> be sets, <math|f:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a
    function and <math|\<cal-A\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|Y|)>> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsup|-1><around*|[|\<cal-A\>|]>=<around*|{|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>
    </equation*>

    In other words <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|[|\<cal-A\>|]>> is the set of
    pre-images by <math|f> of sets in <math|\<cal-A\>>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|product generated sigma algebra>Let <math|I> be a set,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>>
    a family of measurable sets, <math|X> a set and
    <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>>>
    a family of functions then we define the <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>> on <math|X> by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>|]>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  The benefit of the above <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra is that it is the
  smallest <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> such that every
  <math|f<rsub|i>> is measurable.

  <\theorem>
    <label|product generated sigma algebra (1)>Let <math|I> be a set,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>>
    a family of measurable sets, <math|X> a set and
    <math|<rigid|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>>>
    a family of functions then we have for the <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>> on <math|X> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\>I<text| >f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i><text| is
      >\<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i><text|-measurable>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|\<cal-A\>> is another <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
    <math|X> such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I>
    <math|f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i>> is
    <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>-measurable then

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|i\<in\>I> then <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> we
    have that

    <\equation*>
      f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|A|)>\<in\><around*|{|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>=f<rsup|-1><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>\<subset\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsub|i><rsup|-1><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>\<subseteq\>\<sigma\><around*|[|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|f<rsub|i>=\<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i><text|-measurable>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\>I<text| >f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i><text| is
      >\<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i><text|-measurable>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|\<cal-A\>> be another <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> such
    that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>I> <math|f<rsub|i>:X\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i>>
    is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>-measurable. Let
    <math|A\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>>
    then there exist a <math|i\<in\>I> such that
    <math|A\<in\>f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>=<around*|{|f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>>
    then <math|\<exists\>A<rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> such that
    <math|A=f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|A<rprime|'>|)>>. As <math|f<rsub|i>>
    is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>-measurable it follows that
    <math|f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|A<rprime|'>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Hence we have that
    <math|><math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|f<rsub|i>\|i\<in\>I|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>f<rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We are now ready to define the product of <math|\<sigma\>>-algebras.

  <\definition>
    <label|product algebra>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    be a finite family of measurable spaces then the product
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>
    noted as <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>>
    is defined by\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<pi\><rsub|i>:i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|]>
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>:<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i>>
    is the <math|i>-the projection map defined by
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|i><around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=x<rsub|i>>
    [see definition: <reference|product projection function>]. The measurable
    space\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    is called the <with|font-series|bold|product> of the measurable spaces
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>.

    <\convention>
      If <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<cal-A\>> then\ 

      <\equation*>
        <wide*|\<cal-A\>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|n>\<equallim\><rsub|notation>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>
      </equation*>
    </convention>
  </definition>

  A imediate consequence of [theorem: <reference|product generated sigma
  algebra (1)>] it the following

  <\theorem>
    <label|product algebra properties (1)>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    be a finite family of measurable spaces then

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >\<pi\><rsub|i>:<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>\<rightarrow\>X<rsub|i><text|
      is >\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>-measurable.
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|\<cal-A\>> is another <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>> such
    that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>-measurable
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    This follows directly from [definition: <reference|product algebra>] and
    \ [theorem: <reference|product generated sigma algebra (1)>].
  </proof>

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|product algebra equivalences>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    be a finite family of measurable spaces such that
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|]>> and
    there exist a <math|<around*|{|E<rsub|i,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>>
    such that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,k+1>> and
    <math|X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i,k>> then we have
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>|]>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we prove that for any <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>> we
    have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.918.249>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >\<pi\><rsub|i><text| is >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i><text|-measurable
      >\<Leftrightarrow\><text| ><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    <\proof>
      We have

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|E\<in\><text|
        ><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>> then
        <math|E=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>>
        where <rigid|<math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
        <math|E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>>>. Now\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>E=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
          x<rsub|i>\<in\>E<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|x<rsub|i>=\<pi\><rsub|i><around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
          \<pi\><rsub|i><around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>E<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
          ><around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        which proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          E=<big|cap><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>
        </equation*>

        As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
        <math|E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>
        and <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>-measurable
        we have that <math|\<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
        So it follows from the above and [theorems: <reference|measure sigma
        algebra equivalences>, <reference|measure algebra properties>] that
        <math|E\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Hence we have proved that\ 

        <\equation*>
          <text| ><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
          >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
        </equation*>

        <\equation*>
          \;
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Fix <math|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
        > and take <math|E\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>. Define\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|{|F<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| by
          >F<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|k,i><text|
          where >F<rsub|k,i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|j><text|
          if >i=j>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|i,k>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i><text| if
          >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|j|}><rsub|>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>
        </equation*>

        Then we have as <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
        <math|F<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|k,i>>
        and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
        <math|F<rsub|k,i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> that
        <math|<rigid|F<rsub|k>\<in\><text|
        ><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>, hence\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.919.279><around*|{|F<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
        </equation>

        Further we have <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
        <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
        <math|F<rsub|k,i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E=F<rsub|k+1,i><text|
        if >i=j>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,k+1><text| if
        >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|j|}><rsub|>>>>>>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|k+1,i>>
        so that by [theorem: <reference|product inclusion>]
        <math|F<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|k,i>\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|k+1,i>>.
        To summarize\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.290.279>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
          >F<rsub|k>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|k+1>
        </equation>

        Define\ 

        <\equation*>
          G=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>G<rsub|i><text|
          where >G<rsub|i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<text| if
          >i=j>>|<row|<cell|X<rsub|i><text| if
          >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|j|}>>>>>>
        </equation*>

        Let <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
        <math|F<rsub|k,i>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E=G<rsub|i><text|
        if >i=j>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|i>=G<rsub|i><text|
        if >i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|j|}>>>>>>\<subseteq\>G<rsub|i>>
        so that by [theorem: <reference|product inclusion>] we have
        <math|F<rsub|k>\<subseteq\>G> from which it follows that\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 20.921.279><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|k>\<subseteq\>G
        </equation>

        Let <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>G>. Take
        <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|i=j>>Then <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>G<rsub|i>=E=F<rsub|1,i>>
          so if we take <math|k<rsub|i>=1> we have
          <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>F<rsub|k<rsub|1>,i>>

          <item*|<math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|j|}>>>Then
          as <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>G<rsub|i>=X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i,k>>
          there exist a <math|k<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
          <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>E<rsub|i,k<rsub|i>>=F<rsub|k<rsub|i>,i>>
        </description>

        Hence if we take <math|k=max<around*|(|<around*|{|k<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>>
        then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
        <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>F<rsub|k<rsub|i>,i><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.921.279>]>>F<rsub|k,i><rsub|>> so that
        <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|k,i>=F<rsub|k>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|k>>.
        Hence <math|G\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|k>>
        which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.921.279>] proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          G=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|k>
        </equation*>

        As by [eq: <reference|eq 20.921.279>] it follows from the above that\ 

        <\equation*>
          G\<in\>\<cal-A\>
        </equation*>

        Now if <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>G> then
        <math|\<pi\><rsub|j><around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=x<rsub|j>\<in\>G<rsub|j>=E>
        so that <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|j><around*|(|E|)>>.
        Likewise if <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|j><around*|(|E|)>\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>
        then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>\\<around*|{|j|}>>
        we have <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>=G<rsub|i>> and for <math|i=j>
        <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>E=G<rsub|i>> so that <math|x\<in\>G>. Hence we
        must conclude that <math|\<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|j><around*|(|E|)>=G\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
        So we have have proved that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text| we have
          >\<forall\>E\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|j> that
          \<pi\><rsup|-1><rsub|j><around*|(|E|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
        </equation*>

        \ In other words\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
          <math|\<pi\><rsub|j><text| is >\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i><text|-measurable>>>
        </equation*>
      </description>
    </proof>

    As <math|><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>>
    is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>
    so that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|i>> is <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>-measurable
    [see theorem: <reference|product algebra properties (1)>] it follow from
    [eq: <reference|eq 20.918.249>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>
    </equation*>

    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.922.279>\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    Take <math|\<cal-B\>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>|]>> then as

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\>
    </equation*>

    a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra it follows from [eq: <reference|eq
    20.918.249>] that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|i><text| is >\<cal-B\>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>=measurable.
    Hence using [theorem: <reference|product generated sigma algebra (1)>]
    and [definition: <reference|product algebra>] it follows that
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>\<subset\>-\<cal-B\>>
    or\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>
    </equation*>

    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.922.279>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|product algebra equivalences (1)>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    be a finite family of measurable spaces then we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>|]>
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then by [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra (1)>] we have that
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>>.
    Further if we define <math|<around*|{|E<rsub|i,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>
    by <math|E<rsub|i,k>=X<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,k1+1>><space|1em>and
    <math|X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i,k>>. So all the
    conditions of the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|product algebra
    equivalences>] are satisified which proves\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  A application of [theorem: <reference|product algebra equivalences>] is
  that we can write the Borel algebra <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
  on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> as a product of the Borel algebra's
  <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|product algebra on Borel algebras of R>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=<wide*|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|n>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Define <math|<around*|{|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    by <math|X<rsub|i>=\<bbb-R\>> and <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<equallim\><rsub|definition><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    Further using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on R>] we have
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Further definine <math|<around*|{|<around*|[|-k,k|[>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>
    then we have trivially

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| ><around*|[|-n,n|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-<around*|(|n+1|)>,n+1|[>
    </equation*>

    Further if <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then by [theorem: <reference|complex
    Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
    <math|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|<around*|\||x|\|>\<less\>k> so
    that <math|-x\<less\>k\<wedge\>x\<less\>k> hence
    <math|-k\<less\>x\<less\>k\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\><around*|]|-k,k|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-k,k|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-n,n|[>>.
    Which as <math|<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-k,k|[>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
    proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-R\>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-k,k|[>
    </equation*>

    So all the conditions for [theorem: <reference|product algebra
    equivalences>] are satisfied and we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.923.279><wide*|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|n>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>|]>
    </equation>

    Now if <math|E\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>> then
    <math|E=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
    where <math|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> [see definition: <reference|measure
    bounded intervals>] so that by [definitions: <reference|measure [a,b[ in
    R^n>, <reference|measure half open intervals in R^n>]
    <math|E\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>. Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.294.279><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>
    </equation>

    Also as <math|E\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>> then by
    [definitions: <reference|measure [a,b[ in R^n>, <reference|measure half
    open intervals in R^n>] <math|E=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|[>>
    where <math|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
    <math|a<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>> so that by [see definition:
    <reference|measure bounded intervals>] we have that
    <math|E\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>>. Hence
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>\<subseteq\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>> which combined with
    [eq: <reference|eq 20.294.279>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>
    </equation*>

    Now\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><rsup|><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel algebra on R^n (equivalences
      1)>]>>\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|}>|]>
    </equation*>

    and combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 20.923.279>] proves finally
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=<wide*|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|n>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Product measures>

  <\theorem>
    <label|product algebra measure>Let <math|n\<in\><around*|{|2,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>,\<mu\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    a finite family of measure spaces such that
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have that
    <math|\<exists\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    such that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|]>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> we have
      <math|A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> [in other words
      <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> is <math|\<cap\>>-stable]

      <item><math|\<exists\><around*|{|E<rsub|i,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,k+1>>,
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|A<rsub|i,k>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <math|X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i,k>>
    </enumerate>

    then if <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>> are two measures on
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.925.279>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<nu\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|\<cal-E\>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
    >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>> then by [theorem:
    <reference|product algebra equivalences>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-E\>|]>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|E,F\<in\>\<cal-E\>> then <math|E=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>>
    and <math|F=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|i>>
    where <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|E<rsub|i>,F<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> then we have that

    <\equation*>
      E<big|cap>F=<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>|)><big|cap><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|product and intersection>]>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|E<rsub|i><big|cap>F<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> is <math|\<cap\>>-stable
    <math|E<rsub|i><big|cap>F\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> it follows that
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|(|E<rsub|i><big|cap>F<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-E\>>
    hence <math|E<big|cap>F\<in\>\<cal-E\>>. In other word\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.926.279>\<cal-E\><text| is >\<cap\><text|-stable >
    </equation>

    Further if we define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.927.279><around*|{|F<rsub|k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-E\><text|
      by >F<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i,k>\<in\>\<cal-E\>
    </equation>

    then we have by [theorem: <reference|product inclusion>] and the fact
    that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,k+1>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.928.279>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >F<rsub|k>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|k+1>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>
    then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have that
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i,k>\<Rightarrow\>\<exists\>k<rsub|i>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    such that <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>E<rsub|i,k<rsub|i>>>. Take
    <math|m=max<around*|(|<around*|{|k<rsub|i>\|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>|}>|)>>
    then we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\>E<rsub|i,k<rsub|i>>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,m>> so that
    <math|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>F<rsub|m>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|k>>.
    Hence <math|><math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|k>>.
    Further <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have that
    <math|F<rsub|k>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.929.279><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|k>
    </equation>

    Further we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.930.279>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|k>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i,k>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i,k>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i,k>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|k>|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have
    <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|A<rsub|i,k>|)>> is finite we have from the
    above that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.931.279>\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|k>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>
    </equation>

    The conditions for [theorem: <reference|measure uniqueness of extension
    of a measure>] are fulfilled by \ [eqs: <reference|eq 20.926.279>,
    <reference|eq 20.927.279>, <reference|eq 20.928.279>, <reference|eq
    20.929.279> ,<reference|eq 20.930.279> and <reference|eq 20.931.279>], so
    using this theorem it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<nu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|product algebra measure (1)>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|i>,\<cal-A\><rsub|i>,\<mu\><rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>>
    a finite family of measure spaces such that
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>>
    is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite and <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>> are two measures on
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    For <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> take
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> then

    <\equation*>
      \<sigma\><around*|[|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>=\<cal-A\><rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    and trivially we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i>=\<cal-A\><rsub|i><text| we have
      >A<big|cap>B\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<cal-E\><rsub|i><text| [in other
      words <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|i>> is <math|\<cap\>>-stable]>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|i>> is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite [theorem:
    <reference|measure sigma finite increasing>] we have that
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> there exist a\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|E<rsub|i,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<cal-E\><rsub|i><text|
      with >\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,k+1>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i,k>|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
      and >X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i,k>
    </equation*>

    By the hypothesis we have \ <math|>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>=\<cal-E\><rsub|i>|}>
    </equation*>

    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><rsub|i><around*|(|E<rsub|i>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>E<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence all the conditions for [theorem: <reference|product algebra
    measure>] are satisfied so that we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<nu\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  This theorem does not prove existence of a measure on
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cdots\>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|n>>
  satisfying [eq: <reference|eq 20.925.279>] only that if such measure exist
  then it is unique. The remaining part of this section is to prove that such
  a measure exist. We first prove existence for <math|n=2>. First we
  introduce the idea of a section of a set.

  <\definition>
    Let <math|<around*|{|X<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>> be a
    family of two sets let <math|A\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>
    then we define

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| >A<rsup|><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A|}>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| >A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A|}>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    We call <math|A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>> the <math|1,x>-section of
    <math|A> and <math|A<rsub|2,y>> the <math|2,y>-section of <math|A>

    <\note>
      <math|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> is a short notation for
      <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>X<rsub|i>>
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.613.279>Let <math|<around*|{|X<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>>
    be a family of two sets then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>
      <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=X<rsub|2>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>>
      <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=X<rsub|1>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| that
      <math|\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=\<varnothing\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> we have
      <math|\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=\<varnothing\>>>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>,>
      <math|\<forall\>A,B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>
      <math|<around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>,>
      <math|\<forall\>A,B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>
      <math|<around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>

      <item>If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>>,
      <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>

      <item>If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>>,
      <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>

      <item>Let <math|A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>>,
      <math|A<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|2>> then we have

      <\enumerate>
        <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| we have
        ><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<times\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|2><text|
        if >x\<in\>A<rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\><text| if
        >x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|1>>>>>>>

        <item><math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| we have
        ><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<times\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1><text|
        if >y\<in\>A<rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\><text| if
        >y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\A<rsub|2>>>>>>>
      </enumerate>

      <item>Let <math|A,B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> and
      <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then <math|<around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>><big|cap>A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>

      <item>Let <math|A,B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> and
      <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then <math|<around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>><big|cap>A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>. If <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then
      <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> so that
      <math|y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|}>=<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>.
      Hence <math|X<rsub|2>\<subseteq\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>
      and as by definition <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|2>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=X<rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>>. If <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then
      <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> so that
      <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|}>=<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>.
      Hence <math|X<rsub|1>\<subseteq\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>
      and as by definition <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,y|]>>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>>
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,y|]>>=X<rsub|1>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then
      <math|\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>\<varnothing\>|}>=\<varnothing\>>.
      Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then <rigid|<math|\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>\<varnothing\>|}>=\<varnothing\>>.>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> and
      <math|A,B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>. Then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\\B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\\B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<nin\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A|}>\<wedge\>y\<nin\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<wedge\>y\<nin\>B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>/B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> and
      <math|B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>. Then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\\B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\\B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<nin\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A|}>\<wedge\>x\<nin\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<wedge\>x\<nin\>B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>/B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>>
      and <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\<wedge\>\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that ><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that >y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that >y\<in\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>>
      and <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<wedge\>\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that ><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that >x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|i>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that >c\<in\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>>,
      <math|A<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|2>> then we have:\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>\<wedge\>A<rsub|2>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|2>>>|<cell|y\<in\>A<rsub|2>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          which proves that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=A<rsub|2>
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|1>>>Then if
          <math|y\<in\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>
          we have that <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>\<Rightarrow\>x\<in\>A<rsub|1>>
          which contradicts with <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|1>>. Hence we
          must have that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=\<varnothing\>
          </equation*>
        </description>

        <item>Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|y\<in\>A<rsub|2>>>Then\ 

          <\eqnarray*>
            <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|y\<in\>A<rsub|2>\<wedge\>A<rsub|1>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>>>|<cell|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>>>>>
          </eqnarray*>

          which proves that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=A<rsub|1>
          </equation*>

          <item*|<math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\A<rsub|2>>>Then if
          <math|x\<in\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>
          we have that <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>\<Rightarrow\>y\<in\>A<rsub|2>>
          which contradicts with <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\A<rsub|2>>. Hence we
          must have that\ 

          <\equation*>
            <around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=\<varnothing\>
          </equation*>
        </description>
      </enumerate>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>, <math|A,B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>
      then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<big|cap>B|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>>|<cell|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<big|cap>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A|}>\<wedge\>y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<wedge\>y\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>><big|cap>B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>><big|cap>B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>>, <math|A,B\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>
      then we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<big|cap>B|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>>>|<cell|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A<big|cap>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A\<wedge\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>A|}>\<wedge\>x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<wedge\>x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>><big|cap>B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|A<big|cap>B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>><big|cap>B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>

    \;
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.614.279>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>> be two
    measurable spaces and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    then we have

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
      >A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
      >A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> is a
      <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>X<rsub|i>>
      so that <math|A\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> as is required
      for this lemma to make sense. \ 
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> and define

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.932.279>\<cal-B\>=<around*|{|A\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\|A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|}>
      </equation>

      Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] we have that
      <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=X<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.933.279>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>\<in\>B
      </equation>

      Further if <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then
      <math|B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> and by [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] <math|<around*|(|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=X<rsub|2>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      so \ that <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-B\>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.934.279>\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-B\><text| we have that
        ><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-B\>
      </equation>

      \ Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\>>
      then <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      so that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>.
      As by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]
      <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      hence <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\>>.
      So\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.935.279>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><text|
        we have ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\>
      </equation>

      Combining [eqs: <reference|eq 20.933.279>, <reference|eq 20.934.279>,
      <reference|eq 20.935.279>] proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.936.279>\<cal-B\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra on
        >X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>
      </equation>

      Let <math|><math|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>> then
      <math|E<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<wedge\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>.
      Using \ [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] we have then that
      <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      if >x\<in\>E<rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      if >x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\E<rsub|1>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> so that
      <math|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-B\>>. So we have proved
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\>
      </equation*>

      As by [corollary: <reference|product algebra equivalences (1)>]
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>|]>> we have using the above, [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.936.279>] and [theorem: <reference|measure generated
      sigma algebra>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<subseteq\>B
      </equation*>

      Hence <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      we have <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\>> so that

      <\equation*>
        A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> and define

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.937.279>\<cal-B\>=<around*|{|A\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\|A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>|}>
      </equation>

      Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] we have that
      <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=X<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|<lprime|`>>>
      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.938.279>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>\<in\>B
      </equation>

      Further if <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then
      <math|B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>> and by [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] <math|<around*|(|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=X<rsub|1>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>
      so \ that <math|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-B\>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.939.279>\<forall\>B\<in\>\<cal-B\><text| we have that
        ><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\B\<in\>\<cal-B\>
      </equation>

      \ Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\>>
      then <math|<around*|{|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>
      so that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>.
      As by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]
      <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>
      it follows that <math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>
      hence <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\>>.
      So\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.940.279>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><text|
        we have ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\>
      </equation>

      Combining [eqs: <reference|eq 20.938.279>, <reference|eq 20.939.279>,
      <reference|eq 20.940.279>] proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.941.279>\<cal-B\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra on
        >X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>
      </equation>

      Let <math|><math|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\><around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>> then
      <math|E<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<wedge\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>.
      Using \ [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] we have then that
      <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1><text|
      if >x\<in\>E<rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1><text|
      if >x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\E<rsub|2>>>>>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>> so that
      <math|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-B\>>. So we have proved
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\>
      </equation*>

      As by [corollary: <reference|product algebra equivalences (1)>]
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
      >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}>|]>> we have using the above, [eq:
      <reference|eq 20.941.279>] and [theorem: <reference|measure generated
      sigma algebra>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<subseteq\>B
      </equation*>

      Hence <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      we have <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\>> so that

      <\equation*>
        A<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.619.279>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>>
    be two measure spaces where <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>>
    are finite measures then we have <math|\<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f<rsub|1,Q>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by <math|f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>
      we have that <math|f<rsub|1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item><math|f<rsub|2,Q>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by <math|f<rsub|2,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>
      we have that \ <math|f<rsub|2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>> are finite measures it
      follows that <math|><math|f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|X<rsub|1>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>\<wedge\>f<rsub|2,Q><around*|(|X<rsub|2>|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ We use the concept of Dynkin systems to prove this theorem.

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then we have
      <math|f<rsub|1,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      so that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.942.279>f<rsub|1,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>=C<rsub|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>|)>>
      </equation>

      which by [example: <reference|measurable function constant function>]
      proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.943.279>f<rsub|1,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> then
      for <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|1,A\\B><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<equallim\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>]>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|2><text| is
        finite>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|1,A><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|1,B><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|1,A>-f<rsub|1,B>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.944.279>\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        we have >f<rsub|1,A\\B>=f<rsub|1,A>-f<rsub|1,B>
      </equation>

      In the special cases where <math|A=X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>><space|1em>the
      above becomes \ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.945.279>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        then >f<rsub|1,<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\A>=f<rsub|1,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>-f<rsub|1,A>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      be pairwise disjoint. Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> and
      <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then we have
      \ <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>>\<varnothing\>>. Hence

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.946.279>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| we have
        ><around*|{|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      So for <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|1,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.946.279>]>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|2><text| is a
        measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.947.279>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        pairwise disjoint we have >f<rsub|1,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation>

      Define now

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-D\>=<around*|{|D\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\|f<rsub|1,D>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>
      </equation*>

      Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.943.279>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.948.279>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-D\>> then <math|f<rsub|1,A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      further by [eq: <reference|eq 20.943.279>]
      <math|f<rsub|1,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function properties (1)>]
      <math|f<rsub|1,<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\Q>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.945.279>]>>f<rsub|1,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>-f<rsub|1,A>\<in\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.949.279>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-D\><text| we have that
        ><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\A\<in\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      that <math|f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>
      <math|f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      it follows that <math|f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Using Beppo's \ [theorem: <reference|Beppo Levi's Theorem>] it follows
      that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      hence, as <math|f<rsub|1,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.947.279>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>>,
      it follows that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.
      To summarize

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.950.279>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><text|
        pairwise disjoing we have ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      By [eqs: <reference|eq 20.948.279>, <reference|eq 20.949.279>,
      <reference|eq 20.950.279>] it follows by [definition:
      <reference|measure Dynkin system>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.951.279>\<cal-D\><text| is a >Dynkin system
      </equation>

      Take\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-E\>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|product algebra equivalences>]>>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|E=E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-E\>> then
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>E<rsub|1>>>Then by [lemma: <reference|lemma
        20.613.279>] we have that <math|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=E<rsub|2>>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|1,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|E<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|E<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1,>E<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\E<rsub|1>>>Then [lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] we have that
        <math|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=\<varnothing\>>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|1,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|0|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|E<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,E<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|1,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|E<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,E<rsub|1>>
      </equation*>

      As <math|E<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>> it follows from [theorems:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic>,<reference|measurable
      function product with scalar>] \ that

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|1,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|E<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,E<rsub|1>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|E=E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.
      Hence we have proved that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.952.279>\<cal-E\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      If <math|E=E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>,F=F<rsub|1>\<cdot\>F<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-E\>>
      then we have that <math|E<rsub|1>,F<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>> and
      <math|E<rsub|2>,F<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        E<big|cap>F\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|product and
        intersection>]>><around*|(|E<rsub|1><big|cap>F<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|E<rsub|2><big|cap>F<rsub|2>|)>
      </equation*>

      which, as <math|E<rsub|1><big|cap>F<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>,
      <math|E<rsub|2><big|cap>F<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>, proves that
      <math|E<big|cap>F\<in\>\<cal-E\>>. Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.953.279>\<forall\>E,F\<in\>\<cal-E\><text| we have
        >E<big|cap>F\<in\>\<cal-E\>
      </equation>

      Using then [theorem: <reference|measure Dynkin system condition for
      sigma algebra (1)>] on [eq: <reference|eq 20.952.279>, <reference|eq
      20.953.279>] gives

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-E\>|]>=\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-E\>|]>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<cal-E\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>> and <math|\<cal-D\>> is a
      Dynkin system [see eq: <reference|eq 20.951.279>] we have by [theorem:
      <reference|measure Dynkin system generated>[ that
      <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-E\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>> so that
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>.
      Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        we have >Q\<in\>\<cal-D\><text| so that
        >f<rsub|1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then we have
      <math|f<rsub|2,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>
      so that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.954.280>f<rsub|2,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>=C<rsub|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>|)>>
      </equation>

      which by [example: <reference|measurable function constant function>]
      proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.955.280>f<rsub|2,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<bbb-R\>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> then
      for <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|2,A\\B><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|A\\B|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>\\B<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<equallim\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>]>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|1><text| is
        finite>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>-\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<rsub|2,A><around*|(|x|)>-f<rsub|2,B><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f<rsub|2,A>-f<rsub|2,B>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.956.280>\<forall\>A,B\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        we have >f<rsub|2,A\\B>=f<rsub|2,A>-f<rsub|2,B>
      </equation>

      In the special cases where <math|A=X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>><space|1em>the
      above becomes \ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.957.280>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        then >f<rsub|2,<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\A>=f<rsub|2,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>-f<rsub|2,A>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      be pairwise disjoint. Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> and
      <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then we have
      \ <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>=\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>>\<varnothing\>>. Hence

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.958.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| we have
        ><around*|{|<around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text|
        is pairwise disjoint>
      </equation>

      So for <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|2,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><rsub|<text|>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.958.280>]>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|1><text| is a
        measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.959.280>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        pairwise disjoint we have >f<rsub|2,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>
      </equation>

      Define now

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-D\>=<around*|{|D\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\|f<rsub|2,D>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>
      </equation*>

      Using [eq: <reference|eq 20.955.280>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.960.280>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-D\>> then <math|f<rsub|2,A>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      further by [eq: <reference|eq 20.955.280>]
      <math|f<rsub|2,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function properties (1)>]
      <math|f<rsub|2,<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\Q>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.957.280>]>>f<rsub|2,X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>-f<rsub|2,A>\<in\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.961.280>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-D\><text| we have that
        ><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>|)>\\A\<in\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>
      be pairwise disjoint then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      that <math|f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2>>
      <math|f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
      it follows that <math|f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
      Using Beppo's \ [theorem: <reference|Beppo Levi's Theorem>] it follows
      that <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>,
      hence, as <math|f<rsub|2,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.961.280>]>><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>>,
      it follows that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.
      To summarize

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.962.280>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><text|
        pairwise disjoing we have ><big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      By [eqs: <reference|eq 20.960.280>, <reference|eq 20.961.280>,
      <reference|eq 20.962.280>] it follows by [definition:
      <reference|measure Dynkin system>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.963.280>\<cal-D\><text| is a >Dynkin system
      </equation>

      Take\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-E\>=<around*|{|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|}><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|product algebra equivalences>]>>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|E=E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-E\>> then
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>E<rsub|2>>>Then by [lemma: <reference|lemma
        20.613.279>] we have that <math|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>=E<rsub|1>>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|2,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|E<rsub|1>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|E<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,E<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\E<rsub|2>>>Then [lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.613.279>] we have that
        <math|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>=\<varnothing\>>
        so that\ 

        <\equation*>
          f<rsub|2,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|E<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,E<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|2,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|E<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,E<rsub|2>>
      </equation*>

      As <math|E<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>> it follows from [theorems:
      <reference|measurable function charasteristic>,<reference|measurable
      function product with scalar>] \ that

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|2,E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|E<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,E<rsub|2>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|E=E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.
      Hence we have proved that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.964.280>\<cal-E\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>
      </equation>

      If <math|E=E<rsub|1>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2>,F=F<rsub|1>\<cdot\>F<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-E\>>
      then we have that <math|E<rsub|1>,F<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>> and
      <math|E<rsub|2>,F<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        E<big|cap>F\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem: <reference|product and
        intersection>]>><around*|(|E<rsub|1><big|cap>F<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|E<rsub|2><big|cap>F<rsub|2>|)>
      </equation*>

      which, as <math|E<rsub|1><big|cap>F<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>,
      <math|E<rsub|2><big|cap>F<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>, proves that
      <math|E<big|cap>F\<in\>\<cal-E\>>. Hence we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.965.280>\<forall\>E,F\<in\>\<cal-E\><text| we have
        >E<big|cap>F\<in\>\<cal-E\>
      </equation>

      Using then [theorem: <reference|measure Dynkin system condition for
      sigma algebra (1)>] on [eq: <reference|eq 20.964.280>, <reference|eq
      20.965.280>] gives

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>=\<sigma\><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>>E<rsub|i>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}><text|
        >E<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-E\>|]>=\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-E\>|]>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<cal-E\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>> and <math|\<cal-D\>> is a
      Dynkin system [see eq: <reference|eq 20.963.280>] we have by [theorem:
      <reference|measure Dynkin system generated>[ that
      <math|\<delta\><around*|[|\<cal-E\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>> so that
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>.
      Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        we have >Q\<in\>\<cal-D\><text| so that
        >f<rsub|2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We extend now the previous lemma to the case of <math|\<sigma\>>-finite
  measures.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.621.280>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>>
    be two measure spaces where <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>>
    are <math|\<sigma\>>-finite measures [see definition: \ measures then we
    have <math|\<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    that\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|f<rsub|1,Q>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by <math|f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>
      we have that <math|f<rsub|1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>

      <item><math|f<rsub|2,Q>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by <math|f<rsub|2,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>
      we have that \ <math|f<rsub|2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>> is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite it follows
      from [definition: <reference|measure sigma finite measure>] and
      [theorem: <reference|measure sigma finite increasing>] that there exist
      a sequence <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>>,
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <math|X<rsub|2>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>. Let
      <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and define the measure [see theorem:
      <reference|measure sum and scalar product of measures>(2)]

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.954.279>\<mu\><rsub|i,2>:\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        by >\<mu\><rsub|i,2><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      then <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>> is a finite measure and we can apply the
      previous lemma [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.619.279>] which proves that
      <math|\<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> we
      have for the function

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.955.279>f<rsub|i,1,Q>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        defined by >f<rsub|i,1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2,i><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.954.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>
      </equation>

      that

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.956.279>f<rsub|i,1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>.\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> and take
      <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then we have for
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>
      and <math|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=X<rsub|2><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>.
      Using then [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>]
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.957.279>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.955.279>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i,1,Q><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Define now\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|1,Q>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        by <math|f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>
      </equation*>

      then we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.957.279>] that
      <math|f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i,1,Q><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>,
      hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|1,Q>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i,1,Q>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|f<rsub|i,1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>.\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf
      limit>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>As <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>> is <math|\<sigma\>>-finite it follows
      from [definition: <reference|measure sigma finite measure>] and
      [theorem: <reference|measure sigma finite increasing>] that there exist
      a sequence <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1>>,
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
      <math|X<rsub|1>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>. Let
      <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and define the measure [see theorem:
      <reference|measure sum and scalar product of measures>(2)]

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.970.280>\<mu\><rsub|i,1>:\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        by >\<mu\><rsub|i,1><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation>

      then <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>> is a finite measure and we can apply the
      previous lemma [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.619.279>] which proves that
      <math|\<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> we
      have for the function

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.971.280>f<rsub|i,2,Q>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        defined by >f<rsub|i,2,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2,i><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.970.280>]>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>
      </equation>

      that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.972.280>f<rsub|i,2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>.\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation>

      Let <math|Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> and take
      <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then we have for
      <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>
      that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>>
      and <math|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>=X<rsub|1><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>=<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>.
      Using then [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>]
      it follows that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 20.973.280>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.971.280>]>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i,2,Q><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Define now\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|2,Q>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
        by <math|f<rsub|2,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>
      </equation*>

      then we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.973.280>] that
      <math|f<rsub|2,Q><around*|(|x|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i,2,Q><around*|(|x|)>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>>,
      hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|2,Q>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|f<rsub|i,2,Q>\|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|f<rsub|i,2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>.\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      it follows using [theorem: <reference|measurable function sup inf
      limit>] that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  In the following we use the following convention to simplify notation

  <\convention>
    \ <label|integral convention>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a measurable space then we use the following simplifying conventions

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The notation

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+>E<around*|(|x|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d x|)>
      </equation*>

      is equivalent with saying that for the function defined by\ 

      <\equation*>
        f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| where
        >f<around*|(|x|)>=E<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,A,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+>E<around*|(|x|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d x|)>=<big|int>f
        d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>The notation

      <\equation*>
        <big|int>E<around*|(|x|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d x|)>
      </equation*>

      is equivalent with saying that for the function defined by\ 

      <\equation*>
        f:X\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text| where
        >f<around*|(|x|)>=E<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,A,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int>E<around*|(|x|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d x|)>=<big|int>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>

      <item>The notation

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>E<around*|(|x|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d x|)>
      </equation*>

      is equivalent with saying that for the function defined by\ 

      <\equation*>
        f:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-C\><text| where
        >f<around*|(|x|)>=E<around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,\<mu\>,\<bbb-C\>|]>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>E<around*|(|x|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d
        x|)>=<big|int><rsup|\<bbb-C\>>f d\<mu\>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </convention>

  <\theorem>
    <label|product measure (1)>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>>
    be two measure spaces where <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>>
    are <with|font-series|bold|<math|\<sigma\>>-finite> then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>:\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|d
      x|)>
    </equation*>

    is a

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2><text| is
      >\<sigma\><text|-finite> measure
    </equation*>

    such that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1><text|,
      >\<forall\>A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text| we have
      ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text| we
      have ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|d
      x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|d
      x|)>
    </equation*>

    Further if there exist another measure

    <\equation*>
      \<pi\>:\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      >
    </equation*>

    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1><text|,
      >\<forall\>A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text| we have
      >\<pi\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<pi\>=\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    The measure <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>> on
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> is called the product
    measure of <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>> and <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>>.

    <\note>
      Let <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>,
      <math|A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> then by [theorem:
      <reference|product algebra equivalences>]
      <math|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      so that <math|<rigid|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>>>
      is well defined.
    </note>

    <\note>
      We use the previous convention [see convention: <reference|integral
      convention>] here, so\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
        we have ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|d
        x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|d
        x|)>
      </equation*>

      in the above is equivalent with the following. For the functions\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|1,Q>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><op|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
        defined by f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|2,Q>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><op|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
        defined by f<rsub|2,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>
      </equation*>

      we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<rsub|1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>f<rsub|2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,Q>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,Q>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|1,Q>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><op|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|2,Q>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><op|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
      defined by f<rsub|2,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>
    </equation*>

    \ then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.621.280>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.974.280>f<rsub|1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>f<rsub|2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> <math|f<rsub|1,Q><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    and <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2>>
    <math|f<rsub|2,Q>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    it follows from the above that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.975.280>f<rsub|1,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>f<rsub|2,Q>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    So we can define the function\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.976.280><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>:\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by ><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,Q>d\<mu\><rsub|1>\<equallim\><rsub|notation><big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|d
      x|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then <math|f<rsub|1,\<varnothing\>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>
    which proves that <math|f<rsub|1,\<varnothing\>>=C<rsub|0>>. Applying
    then [theorem: <reference|non negative integral constant>] we have that
    <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,\<varnothing\>>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=0\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>|)>=0>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.977.280><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    be pairwise disjoint and take <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>. Let
    <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>=\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.978.280><around*|{|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|1,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.978.280>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|1,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    Using the Beppo's Levi theorem [see theorem: <reference|Beppo Levi's
    Theorem>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|(|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,\<Alpha\><rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      pairwise disjoint then ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    The above together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.977.280>] proves that
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>> is a measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>. To summarize\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.979.280><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>:\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by ><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,Q>d\<mu\><rsub|1><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>,
    <math|A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> then we have for
    <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> that either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A<rsub|1>>>Then
      <math|f<rsub|1,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|[|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|]><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,A<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\A<rsub|1>>>Then
      <math|f<rsub|1,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|[|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|]><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,A<rsub|1>><around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    which proves that

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|1,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,A<rsub|1>>
    </equation*>

    Then

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,A<rsub|1>>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|non negative integral characteristics>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    To summarize we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.980.280>\<forall\>A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<forall\>A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      we have ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|12><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>
    </equation>

    Now if <math|\<pi\>> is another measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<forall\>A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|>>
    we have <math|<rigid|\<pi\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>=\<pi\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<pi\><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>>>
    then by [corollary: <reference|product algebra measure (1)>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.981.280>\<pi\>=\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>> are <math|\<sigma\>>-finite there
    exist by [theorem: <reference|measure sigma finite increasing>] there
    exist <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,2|}>> a

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.982.280><around*|{|E<rsub|i,k>|}><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|i><text|
      with >\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      >E<rsub|i,k>\<subseteq\>E<rsub|i,k+1>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|E<rsub|i,k>|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
      and >X<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|i,k>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x=<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,x<rsub|2>|)>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>
    then <math|x<rsub|1>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<wedge\>x<rsub|2>\<in\>X<rsub|2>>
    then by the above there exist <math|k,l\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|x<rsub|1>\<in\>E<rsub|1,k>\<wedge\>x<rsub|2>\<in\>E<rsub|2,k>> so
    that <math|x=<around*|(|x<rsub|1>,x<rsub|2>|)>\<in\>E<rsub|1,max<around*|(|k,l|)>>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2,max<around*|(|k,l|)>>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|E<rsub|1,k>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2,k>|)>>.
    As also <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
    <math|E<rsub|1,k>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2,k>\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.983.280><big|cup><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>E<rsub|1,k>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2,k>=X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Further <math|\<forall\>k\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have by [theorem:
    <reference|product algebra equivalences>] <math|>that
    <math|E<rsub|1,k>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2,k>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    and by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.980.280>, <reference|eq 20.982.280>] that
    <math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|E<rsub|1,k>\<cdot\>E<rsub|2,k>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|E<rsub|1,k>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|E<rsub|2,k>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    hence, taking in account [eq: <reference|eq 20.983.280>], we have

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2><text| is
      >\<sigma\><text|-finite>
    </equation*>

    Finally the only thing left to prove is that
    <math|><math|<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|d
    x|)>>. Define now\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<pi\>:\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >\<pi\><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,Q>d\<mu\><rsub|2><below|\<equallim\>|notation><big|int><rsup|+><rsub|>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|d
      x|)>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then <math|f<rsub|2,\<varnothing\>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0>
    which proves that <math|f<rsub|2,\<varnothing\>>=C<rsub|0>>. Applying
    then [theorem: <reference|non negative integral constant>] we have that
    <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,\<varnothing\>>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int><rsup|+>C<rsub|0>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=0\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>|)>=0>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.984.280>\<pi\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    be pairwise disjoint and take <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>>. Let
    <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with <math|i\<neq\>j> then

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>=\<varnothing\><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.985.280><around*|{|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<rsub|2,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.985.280>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|2,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    Using the Beppo's Levi theorem [see theorem: <reference|Beppo Levi's
    Theorem>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|(|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<pi\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,\<Alpha\><rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<pi\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      pairwise disjoint then >\<pi\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<pi\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    The above together with [eq: <reference|eq 20.985.280>] proves that
    <math|\<pi\>> is a measure on <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>.
    To summarize\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.986.280>\<pi\>:\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by ><around*|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|Q|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,Q>d\<mu\><rsub|2><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>,
    <math|A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> then we have for
    <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> that either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>A<rsub|2>>>Then
      <math|f<rsub|2,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|[|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|]><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\A<rsub|2>>>Then
      <math|f<rsub|2,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|[|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|]><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|lemma 20.613.279>]>>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|2>><around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    which proves that

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|2,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|2>>
    </equation*>

    Then

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<pi\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>>d\<mu\><rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,A<rsub|1>>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|non negative integral characteristics>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    To summarize we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.987.280>\<forall\>A<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<forall\>A<rsub|2>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      we have >\<pi\><around*|(|A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|A<rsub|1>|)>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|A<rsub|2>|)>
    </equation>

    Hence using [eqs: <reference|eq 20.986.280>, <reference|eq 20.987.280>
    and <reference|eq 20.981.280>] we have that
    <math|\<pi\>=\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>> or using the
    definition of <math|\<pi\>> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>Q\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text| we
      have ><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|1>\<cdot\>A<rsub|2>>d\<mu\><rsub|2>\<equallim\><rsub|notation><big|int><rsup|+>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|d
      x|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Having defined product measures it is time to define the integral of
  product measures. First we define sections of functions.

  <\definition>
    Let <math|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|2>,Y> be sets and
    <math|f:X<rsub|1>\<circ\>X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\>Y> a function then we
    define:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>
      <math|f<around*|(|x,.|)>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\>Y<text| where
      >f<around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<around*|(|x,y|)>>\ 

      <item><math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>>
      <math|f<around*|(|.,y|)>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\>Y> where
      <math|f<around*|(|.,y|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|x,y|)>>
    </enumerate>

    We call <math|f<around*|(|.,x|)>> and <math|f<around*|(|x,.|)>> the
    sections of <math|f>.
  </definition>

  Applying the above definition on charasteristic function we have.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.628.280>Let <math|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|2>,Y> be sets then we
    have:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>Q\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>*\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> we have
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> that
      <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>Q\<subseteq\>X<rsub|1>*\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>> we have
      <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> that
      <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|.,y|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|y\<in\>Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>Then
        <math|y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>Q|}>>
        so that <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>Q> hence we have that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q><around*|(|x,y|)>=1=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>><around*|(|y|)><rsub|>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>Then
        <math|y\<nin\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>Q|}>>
        so that <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<nin\>Q> hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q><around*|(|x,y|)>=0=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>><around*|(|y|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      which proves that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>.

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then we have either:\ 

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|x\<in\>Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>Then
        <math|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>Q|}>>
        so that <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>Q> hence we have that

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|.,y|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q><around*|(|x,y|)>=1=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>><around*|(|x|)><rsub|>
        </equation*>

        <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>Then
        <math|x\<nin\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>Q|}>>
        so that <math|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<nin\>Q> hence\ 

        <\equation*>
          <around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|.,y|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q><around*|(|x,y|)>=0=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>><around*|(|x|)>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      which proves that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,Q>|)><around*|(|.,y|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,Q<rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  If we want to integrate the sections of a function we have to show that
  sections are measurable which is indeed the case as the following lemma
  shows.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.629.280>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>> be measurable spaces
    and

    <\equation*>
      f:X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\>Y
    </equation*>

    a <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable
    function then:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>>
      <math|f<around*|(|x,.|)>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\>Y> is a
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable function.

      <item><math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>>
      <math|f<around*|(|.,y|)>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\>Y> is a
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<cal-B\>>-mesurable function.
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      In particular if <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-B\>|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\<langle\>|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|\<rangle\>>>
      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        If f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        we have >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
        >f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
        >f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        If f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        we have >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
        >f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
        and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
        >f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> and <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then we
      have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>\<in\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|{|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>
      </equation*>

      As <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable
      it follows that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      so that by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.614.279>] it follows that
      <math|><math|<around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>.
      Hence we have by definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|x,.|)><text| is >\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> and <math|B\<in\>\<cal-B\>> then we
      have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|f<around*|(|.,y|)>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|.,y|)><around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|f<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>B>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|<around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|}>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|f<around*|(|.,y|)>|)><rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>=<around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>
      </equation*>

      As <math|f> is <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<cal-B\>>-measurable
      it follows that <math|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
      so that by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.614.279>] it follows that
      <math|><math|<around*|(|f<rsup|-1><around*|(|B|)>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,y|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>.
      Hence we have by definition that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|.,y|)><text| is >\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<cal-B\><text|-measurable>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.630.280>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>>
    be measurable spaces such that <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>> are <math|\<sigma\>>-finite and\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|]><text|
      [see definition: <reference|simple function measurable definition (2)>]
      >
    </equation*>

    then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| we have
      >f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|]><text|
      and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| we have
      >f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>|]>
    </equation*>

    and for the functions

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|S>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral is a
      extension>]>><big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|S>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral is a
      extension>]>><big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>I<rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|]>>
    there exist by [definition: <reference|simple function integral>] a
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    and <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.988.281><big|int><rsup|S>f
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x,y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x,y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.628.280>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>><around*|(|y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>>|)><around*|(|y|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Likewise if <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|1>> that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f<around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|y,x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|y,x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.628.280>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>><around*|(|y|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>>|)><around*|(|y|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.988.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| we have that
      >f<around*|(|x,.|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>><text|>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.989.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| we have that
      >f<around*|(|.,x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>>
    </equation>

    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.614.279>] we have
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> that
    <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,z|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    and <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2>>
    <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>
    which proves by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>]
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.990.280>f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>|]><text|
      and >f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>|]>
    </equation>

    Using the definition of the integral of simple functions we have then
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.991.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| we have that
      >I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|S>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.992.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| we have that
      >I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|S>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)>
    </equation>

    Define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.993.280>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      where <math|f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|1,x|]>>|)><text|>>>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.994.280>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      where <math|f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>><around*|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><rsub|<around*|[|2,x|]>>|)><text|>>>
    </equation>

    then we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.995.280>I<rsub|f,1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>><text|
      and >I<rsub|f,2>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    \ Using [theorem: <reference|product measure (1)>] we have that
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.996.280>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.997.280><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Using then [theorems: <reference|non negative integral scalar product,
    sum inequality>, <reference|non negative integral finite sum>] on the
    above equations [eqs: <reference|eq 20.995.280>, <reference|eq
    20.996.280>, <reference|eq 20.997.280>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>I<rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|1,A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \;
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|2,A<rsub|i>>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    Combining the above with [eq: <reference|eq 20.988.281>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|S>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    Finally using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral is a extension>]
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|Tonelli theorem (1)><dueto|Tonelli><index|Tonelli's theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>>
    be measurable spaces such that <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|2>> are <math|\<sigma\>>-finite and

    <\equation*>
      f:X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
    </equation*>

    a function such that <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>.
    Then we have for the functions\ 

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>I<rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using the convention [convention: <reference|integral convention>] the
      above is equivalent with saying that

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|d
        x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,y|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d
        y|)>|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d x|)>
      </equation*>

      \ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|int><rsup|+>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|.,y|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>|)>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|d
        y|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,y|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d
        x|)>|)>\<mu\><around*|(|d y|)>
      </equation*>
    </note>

    <\note>
      The condition that <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>> are
      <math|\<sigma\>>-finite is neccesary to ensure that
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>> is a measure on
      <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>> [see theorem:
      <reference|product measure (1)>]
    </note>

    <\note>
      By [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.629.280>] we have that
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> that
      <math|f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| that
      >f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      so that <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>> and
      <math|<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>> are well
      defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    there exist by [theorems: <reference|non negative integral alternative>,
    <reference|non negative integral is a extension>] a sequence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.999.280><around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1000.280>\<forall\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2><text|
      we have <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,y|)><text|
      exist and >f<around*|(|x,y|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,y|)>>>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1001.280>\<forall\><around*|(|x,y|)>\<in\>X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2><text|
      >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|i+1><around*|(|x,y|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1002.280><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><text|
      exist>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then by the previous lemma [see lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.630.280>] we have that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1003.280>I<rsub|f,i,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      where >I<rsub|f,i,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by >I<rsub|f,i,1><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2><text|>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1004.280>I<rsub|f,i,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      where >I<rsub|f,i,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by >I<rsub|f,i,2><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1><text|>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1005.280><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,i,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,i,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Further we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1006.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
      >\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| we have
      >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|i+1><around*|(|x,y|)>=f<rsub|i+1><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1007.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
      >\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| we have
      >\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >f<rsub|i><around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<rsub|i><around*|(|y,x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|i+1><around*|(|y,x|)>=f<rsub|i+1><around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> we
    have <math|f<around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<around*|(|x,y|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|]eq:
    <reference|eq 20.1000.280>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,y|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>>.
    Also for <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> we have
    <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|1>> we have
    <math|f<around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<around*|(|y,x|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|]eq:
    <reference|eq 20.1000.280>]>><below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|y,x|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>>
    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1008.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| we have
      >f<around*|(|x,.|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,.|)><text|
      and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| we have
      >f<around*|(|.,x|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|i><around*|(|.,x|)>
    </equation>

    Further as <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.629.280>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1009.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
      >f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
      >f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    The above [see eqs: <reference|eq 20.106.200>, <reference|eq
    20.1007.280>, <reference|eq 20.1008.280> and <reference|eq 20.1009.280>]
    allows us to use the Monotone Convergence Theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)>] to prove that \ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1010.2080>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
      ><big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>\<exterior\><text|>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
      ><big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>
    </equation>

    Substituting [eqs: <reference|eq 20.1003.280>, <reference|eq
    20.1004.280>] in the above gives\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1011.280>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
      ><big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>I<rsub|f,i,1><around*|(|x|)><text|
      and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
      ><big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>I<rsub|f,i,2>
    </equation>

    Define the functions\ 

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      \ by >I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1><text|
      and >I<rsub|f,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      defined by >I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    then we have by [eq: <reference|eq 20.1011.280>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>I<rsub|f,i,1><text| and
      >I<rsub|f,2>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>I<rsub|f,i,2>
    </equation*>

    As by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.1003.280>, <reference|eq 20.1004.280>]
    <math|I<rsub|f,i,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>I<rsub|f,i,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from the Monotone Convergence theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)>] and the above that\ 

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>I<rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,i,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1005.280>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1002.280>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,i,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1005.280>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsub|i>
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1002.280>]>>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<mu\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+>f d\<mu\><around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 20.635.281>Let <math|X<rsub|1>,Y<rsub|1>> be sets and
    <math|f:X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>>
    be a function then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| ><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|+><text|
      and ><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x,.|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|->
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| ><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|.,x|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|+><text|
      and ><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|.,x|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|->
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|2>> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|x,y|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,y|)>,0|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>,0|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<rsup|-><around*|(|x,y|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,y|)>,0|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)><around*|(|y|)>,0|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation*>

    hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| ><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|+><text|
      and ><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x,.|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|->
    </equation*>

    Likewise, let <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>> then we have
    <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X<rsub|1>> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|y,x|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|y,x|)>,0|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>,0|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|+><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>=f<rsup|-><around*|(|y,x|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|y,x|)>,0|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)><around*|(|y|)>,0|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|-><around*|(|y|)>
    </equation*>

    hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text| ><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|.,x|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|+><text|
      and ><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|.,x|)>=<around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|->
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The extension of the Tonelli's theorem to integrable functions instead of
  non negative measurable functions is Fubini's theorem.\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|Fubini's theorem><dueto|Fubini's Theorem>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|\<rangle\>>>
    be measurable spaces whee <math|\<mu\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|2>> is
    <math|\<sigma\>>-finite and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|]>>
    then we have \ 

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|]>|}>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\D<rsub|1>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|]>|}>\<wedge\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\D<rsub|2>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>|]><text|
      where >I<rsub|f,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
      <text|is defined by >I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2><text|
      if <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|1>>>>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\D<rsub|1>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>|]><text|
      where >I<rsub|f,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>
      <text|is defined by >I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1><text|
      if <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|2>>>>>|<row|<cell|0<text| if
      >x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\D<rsub|2>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int>f
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      As <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\D<rsub|1>> we have
      <math|x\<nin\>D<rsub|1>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<nin\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\D<rsub|1>|)>> we have
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|x,.|)><text| is >\<mu\><rsub|1><text|-a.e
        >\<mu\><rsub|2><text|-integrable>
      </equation*>

      Likewise, as <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\D<rsub|2>> we have
      <math|x\<nin\>D<rsub|2>\<Rightarrow\>f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<nin\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
      and <math|\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\D<rsub|2>|)>> we have
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        f<around*|(|.,x|)><text| is >\<mu\><rsub|2><text|-a.e
        >\<mu\><rsub|1><text|-integrable>
      </equation*>
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|]>>
    we have by definition [theorem: <reference|measure integral>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1010.281>f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<wedge\>f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1011.281><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1015.285><big|int>f d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>=<big|int>f
      <rsup|+>d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>-<big|int>f<rsup|->
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>
    </equation>

    Using Tonelli's Theorem [theorem: <reference|Tonelli theorem (1)>] on
    [eq: <reference|eq 20.1010.281>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text| ><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
      ><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|(|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1014.281>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      where >I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by >I<rsub|f,1><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1015.281>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      where >I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by >I<rsub|f,2><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1016.281>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      where >I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by >I<rsub|f,1><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1017.281>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      where >I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      is defined by >I<rsub|f,2><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1018.281><big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1011.281>]>>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    <\equation*>
      \;
    </equation*>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1019.281><big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|->I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int><rsup|->f<rsup|->d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1011.281>]>>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|non negative integral finite integral>] on
    [eqs: <reference|eq 20.1014.281>, <reference|eq 20.1015.281>,
    <reference|eq 20.1016.281>, <reference|eq 20.1017.281>] and [eqs:
    <reference|eq 20.1018.281>, <reference|eq 20.1019.281>] results in\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1020.281>N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>|)>=0
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1021.281>N<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|f,1><rsup|+>|)>=0
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1022.281>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|f,1><rsup|+>|)>=0
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1023.281>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>\<neq\>\<infty\>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|f,2><rsup|->|)>=0
    </equation>

    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>\<cdot\>X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    [see eq: <reference|eq 20.1010.281>] it follows from [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 20.629.280>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1024.281>\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|1><text|
      >f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and >\<forall\>x\<in\>X<rsub|2><text|
      >f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Now we have the following equivalences

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|x\<in\>D<rsub|1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|<math|f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      by [eq: <reference|eq 20.1024.281>]>>>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|2>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|2>\<less\>\<infty\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.635.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>\<less\>\<infty\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.1014.281>, <reference|eq
      20.1016.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.1020.281>, <reference|eq
      20.1022.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|x\<in\>N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    and

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|x\<in\>D<rsub|2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|<math|f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      by [eq: <reference|eq 20.1024.281>]>>>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.635.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>\<less\>\<infty\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.1015.281>, <reference|eq
      20.1017.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>\<wedge\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Updownarrow\>|<text|[eqs:
      <reference|eq 20.1021.281>, <reference|eq
      20.1023.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|x\<in\>N<rsup|+><rsub|f,2><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Using the above if follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1025.281>D<rsub|1>=N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,1><text|
      and >D<rsub|2>=N<rsup|+><rsub|f,2><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>
    </equation>

    Now <math|><math|X<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>>,
    <math|X<rsub|2>\\<around*|(|N<rsup|+><rsub|f,2><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>
    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\<around*|(|N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|<around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>|)><big|cup><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\N<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\N<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\N<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)>=0<text|
      and>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\<around*|(|N<rsup|+><rsub|f,2><big|cap>N<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|<around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\N<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>|)><big|cup><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\N<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\N<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>|)>+\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\N<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 20.1025.281>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1026.281>D<rsub|1>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\|f<around*|(|x,.|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1><text|
      and >\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|X<rsub|1>\\D<rsub|1>|)>=0
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1027.281>D<rsub|2>=<around*|{|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\|f<around*|(|.,x|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>|}>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|2><text|
      and >\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|X<rsub|2>\\D<rsub|2>|)>=0
    </equation>

    The above allows us to define\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1028.281>I<rsub|f,1>:X<rsub|1>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by >I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2><text|
      if >x\<in\>D<rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|0>>>>>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1029.281>I<rsub|f,2>:X<rsub|2>\<rightarrow\><wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>><text|
      by >I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1><text|
      if >x\<in\>D<rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|0>>>>>
    </equation>

    Take\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1033.283>N<rsub|1>=X<rsub|1>\\D<rsub|1>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|1>\<wedge\>N<rsub|2>=X<rsub|2>\\D<rsub|2>\<in\>\<b-A\><rsub|2><text|
      so that <math|X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>=D<rsub|1>\<wedge\>X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>=D<rsub|2>>>
    </equation>

    As by [eqs: <reference|eq 20.1014.281>, <reference|eq 20.1016.281>] and
    [eqs: <reference|eq 20.1015.281>, <reference|eq 20.1017.281>]
    <math|I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>>, <math|I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <rigid|<math|I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>>,
    <math|I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|measurable function charasteristic
    product>] that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1034.284>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>,\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1,>X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<exterior\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1035.284>\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>,\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    Further we have as <math|\<mu\><rsub|1><around*|(|N<rsub|1>|)>,=0=\<mu\><rsub|2><around*|(|N<rsub|2>|)>>
    that by [theorems: <reference|almost everywhere and
    characteristic>,<reference|non negative integral and a.e. equality>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1036.284><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1018.281>]>>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1037.284><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1019.281>]>>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1038.284><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1018.281>]>>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1039.284><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1019.281>]>>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Further we have for <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>N<rsub|1>>>Then <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\D<rsub|1>>
      so that

      <\equation*>
        I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)><below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.1028.281>]>>0=0-0=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>>Then <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|1>>
      so that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I<rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.1028.281>]>>>|<cell|<big|int>f<around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|2>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|x,.|)>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.635.281>]>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|x,.|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
        <reference|eq 20.1014.281>,<reference|eq
        20.1016.281>]>>>|<cell|I<rsub|f,1><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    Likewise if <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|1>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|x\<in\>N<rsub|2>>>Then <math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\D<rsub|2>>
      so that

      <\equation*>
        I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)><below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        20.1029.281>]>>0=0-0=<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)><around*|(|x|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|x\<in\>X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>>Then <math|x\<in\>D<rsub|2>>
      so that\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I<rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 20.1029.281>]>>>|<cell|<big|int>f<around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|+>d\<mu\><rsub|1>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<around*|(|.,x|)>|)><rsup|->d\<mu\><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<text|[lemma:
        <reference|lemma 20.635.281>]>>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|+>|)><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsup|->|)><around*|(|.,x|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eqs:
        <reference|eq 20.1015.281>,<reference|eq
        20.1017.281>]>>>|<cell|I<rsub|f,2><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>-I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    From the above it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1040.284>I<rsub|f,1>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1,>X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,1><text|
      and >I<rsub|f,2>=\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>-\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2,>X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>\<cdot\>f<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>
    </equation>

    Taking in account [eqs: <reference|eq 20.1034.284>, <reference|eq
    20.1035.284>, <reference|eq 20.1036.284>, <reference|eq 20.1037.284>,
    <reference|eq 20.1038.284>, <reference|eq 20.1039.284> and <reference|eq
    20.1040.284>] we can use [theorem: <reference|measure integral
    alternative (2)>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1041.285>I<rsub|f,1>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|1>,\<cal-A\><rsub|1>,\<mu\><rsub|1>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and><big|int>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 20.1042.285>I<rsub|f,2>\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|X<rsub|2>,\<cal-A\><rsub|2>,\<mu\><rsub|2>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and><big|int>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|12>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>
    </equation>

    Now\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|1>,X<rsub|1>\\N<rsub|1>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1036.284>, <reference|eq
      20.1037.284>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>-<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1018.281>, <reference|eq
      20.1019.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1015.285>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|>f
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,2>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|1>-<big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|X<rsub|2>,X<rsub|2>\\N<rsub|2>>*\<cdot\>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,2>|)>d\<mu\><rsub|2>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1038.284>, <reference|eq
      20.1039.284>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|+><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>-<big|int><rsup|+>I<rsup|-><rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1018.281>, <reference|eq
      20.1019.281>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 20.1015.285>]>>>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|>f
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which combined with [eqs: <reference|eq 20.1041.285>, <reference|eq
    20.1042.285>] gives finally:

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>I<rsub|f,1>d\<mu\><rsub|1>=<big|int>I<rsub|f,2>d\<mu\><rsub|2>=<big|int>f
      d<around*|(|\<mu\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<mu\><rsub|2>|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <section|Change of variables in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>>

  <subsection|Regularity and Radon measures>

  <subsubsection|Definition of regular and Radon measures>

  We examine now the properties of Borel measures.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.530.285>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a topological space and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a <with|font-series|bold|finite> measure on the <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\>|]>>
    and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
      is closed>|}>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<Updownarrow\>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      >\<exists\>U<text| open>,\<exists\>F<text| closed with
      >F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<text| and
      >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and closure>] it
      follows that <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>> contains all
      open en closed sets so that the above equivalences make sense.
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<Rightarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>>
      and take <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. As
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is finite we have

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      so that there exist a <math|F> closed with <math|F\<subseteq\>A> such
      that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\<Rightarrow\>-\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\<less\>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation*>

      Likewise we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation*>

      so that there exist a <math|U> open with <math|A\<subseteq\>U> such
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation*>

      Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U
      </equation*>

      Further as <math|\<mu\>> is finite we have

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)><below|=|<text|[theorem: <reference|measure
        measure space properties (1)>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>=\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<Leftarrow\>>>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>>.
      Let <math|F> be a closed set such that <math|F\<subseteq\>A> then using
      [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>] we have
      that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
      Hence <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is a upper bound of
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
      is closed>|}>> so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.991.285>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation>

      Assume that <math|><math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
      is closed>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> then

      <\equation*>
        \<varepsilon\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>
      </equation*>

      By the hypothesis there exist a <math|U> open and a <math|F> closed
      such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      As <math|A\\F<below|\<subseteq\>|A\<subseteq\>U>U\\F> we have by
      [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>] that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>
      so that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>+\<varepsilon\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>+\<varepsilon\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which is a contradiction, hence the assumption is wrong and we must
      have that <math|<rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
      is closed>|}>|)>>>. Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 21.991.285>]
      gives\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|U> be a open set such that <math|A\<subseteq\>U> then using
      [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>] we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>. Hence
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is a lower bound for
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>> so that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.992.285.1>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Assume that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>> then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<varepsilon\>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>
      </equation*>

      So by the hypothesis there exist a <math|U> open and a <math|F> closed
      such that

      <\equation*>
        F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      As <math|F\<subseteq\>A> we have <math|U\\A\<subseteq\>U\\F> so that by
      [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>]
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>
      so that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<gtr\>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>-\<varepsilon\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<geqslant\>>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-\<varepsilon\>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-<around*|(|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which is a contradiction, hence the assumption is wrong and we must
      have that <math|><math|<rigid|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>. Combining this with
      [eq: <reference|eq 21.992.285.1>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <label|eq 21.992.285>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.532.285>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a normed space with the norm topology
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>> and
    <math|<rigid|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>>
    a finite measure on the <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra and
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
    then <math|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> there exist a
    open set <math|U> and a closed set <math|F> with
    <math|F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U> such that
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First note that by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and
    closure>] every open or closed set used in this proof is a element of
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>.
    Consider the set\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.993.285>\<cal-A\>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>\|\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
      >\<exists\>U<text| open, >\<exists\>F<text| closed with
      >F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<text| such that
      >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>|}>
    </equation>

    We prove now that <math|\<cal-A\>> is a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
    <math|X>. As <math|X> is open and closed,
    <math|X\<subseteq\>X\<subseteq\>X> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|X\\X|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>
    <math|\<forall\>e\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.994.285>X\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    If <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then given
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> there exists a open set
    <math|U>, a closed set <math|F> such that
    <math|F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U>. Take <math|F<rprime|'>=X\\U> and
    <math|U<rprime|'>=X\\F> then <math|U<rprime|'>> is a open set and
    <math|F<rprime|'>> is a closed set, further as
    <math|F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U> we have
    <math|F<rprime|'>=X\\U\<subseteq\>X\\A\<subseteq\>X\\F=U<rprime|'>>, also
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U<rprime|'>\\F<rprime|'>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|X\\F|)>\\<around*|(|X\\U|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|class inclusion and union and
    intersection>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>. Hence we
    conclude that <math|X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.995.285>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    and take <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>. Given
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have as <math|A<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> there
    exists a open set <math|F<rsub|n>> and a open set <math|U<rsub|n>> such
    that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.996.285>F<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>U<rsub|n><text|
      and >\<mu\><around*|(|U<rsub|n>\\F<rsub|n>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n+1>>
    </equation>

    Define <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|n>> a open set and
    <math|S=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|n>> [which is in general
    not closed] then we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.997.285>S\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>U
    </equation>

    Further we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\S|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>U<rsub|n>|)>\\S|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|n>\\S|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|n=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|U<rsub|n>\\S|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|F<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>S\<Rightarrow\>U<rsub|n>\\S\<subseteq\>U\\F<rsub|n>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|n=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|U<rsub|n>\\F<rsub|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|n=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|n+1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|n=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|n>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
      <reference|series power series in R>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.998.285>\<mu\><around*|(|U\\S|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
    </equation>

    If <math|S> would be closed then we are done but unfortunately this is
    not always true. To solve this we find a closed set that approximate
    <math|S> from below. Given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> consider the closed
    set <math|<rigid|S<rsub|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>F<rsub|n>>>
    then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.102.202>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      S=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>S<rsub|n><text| and
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >S<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|n+1><text|
      and as ><around*|{|F<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      we have also >S<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    Using then [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>] on
    the above we have

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|S|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|n>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|n>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<mu\>> is finite we have by [theorem: <reference|extended limit
    equivalences>] that <math|\<exists\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
    <math|\<forall\>n\<in\><around*|{|N,\<ldots\>,\<infty\>|}>>
    <math|<around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|S|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|n>|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>,
    as <math|\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|N>|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|n>|)>\|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>|}>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|S|)>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|N>\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| , >S<rsub|N>\<subseteq\>S<text| and
      >\<mu\><around*|(|S\\S<rsub|N>|)><below|=|\<mu\><text| is
      finite>\<wedge\><text|[theorem: <reference|measure measure space
      properties (1)>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|S|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|S<rsub|N>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
    </equation*>

    Now\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|U\\S<rsub|N>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|S\<subseteq\>U>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|U\\S|)><big|sqcup>S|)>\\S<rsub|N>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|U\\S|)>\\S<rsub|N>|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|S\\S<rsub|N>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|class set difference and union ,
      intersection>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|U\\<around*|(|S<big|cup>S<rsub|N>|)>|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|S\\S<rsub|N>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|S<rsub|N>\<subseteq\>S>>|<cell|<around*|(|U\\S<rsub|N>|)><big|sqcup><around*|(|S\\S<rsub|N>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|U\\S<rsub|N>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|U\\S<rsub|N>|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|S\\S<rsub|N>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.998.285>]>><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    Further using [eq: <reference|eq 21.998.285>] we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|N>\<subseteq\>S\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>U
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.999.285>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      we have ><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    From [eqs: <reference|eq 21.994.285>, <reference|eq 21.995.285> and
    <reference|eq 21.999.285>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1000.285>\<cal-A\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra>
    </equation>

    The next logical step is to prove that
    <math|\<cal-T\>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>. Let
    <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>> then we have
    two possible cases:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|X\\U=\<varnothing\>>>Then <math|U=X\<in\>\<cal-A\>>

      <item*|<math|X\\U\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Consider the disance function
      [see definition: <reference|normed distinct function>]

      <\equation*>
        \<delta\><rsub|X\\U>:X\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><text| where
        >\<delta\><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|x|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>\|y\<in\>X\\U|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      Define now

      <\equation*>
        <around*|{|F<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text| by
        >\<delta\><rsup|-1><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>|)>
      </equation*>

      Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>. Using the fact that
      <math|<around*|[|<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>> is closed in
      <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> [see theorem: <reference|normed
      generalized intervals that are open>], <math|\<delta\><rsub|X\\U>> is
      continuous [see theorem: <reference|continuity distance function is
      Lipschitz>] it follows from [theorem: <reference|continuity
      equivalences>] that <math|F<rsub|n>> is a closed set. Further if
      <math|x\<in\>F<rsub|n>> then <math|\<delta\><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>
      so that <math|<frac|1|n+1>\<less\><frac|1|n>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\>\<delta\><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|x|)>\<in\><around*|[|<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>>
      or <math|x\<in\>F<rsub|n+1>>. To summarise\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1001.285>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >F<rsub|n><text| is closed and >F<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|n+1>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\>F<rsub|n>> then <math|0\<less\><frac|1|n>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|x|)>>.
      Assume that <math|x\<in\>X\\U> then
      <math|0\<in\><around*|{|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>\|y\<in\>X\\Y|}>>
      so that <math|\<delta\><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|x|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>\|y\<in\>X\\U|}>|)>\<leqslant\>0>
      contradicting <math|0\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|X\\U><around*|(|x|)>>,
      hence the assumption is false and we must have that <math|x\<nin\>X\\U>
      which proves that <math|x\<in\>U>. So we conclude that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1002.285>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        >F<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>U<text| from which it follows that
        ><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>U
      </equation>

      If now <math|x\<in\>U> then there exist a
      <math|\<delta\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such that
      <math|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>><around*|(|x,\<delta\>|)>\<subseteq\>U>,
      hence <math|\<forall\>y\<in\>X\\U> we have that
      <math|y\<nin\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>><around*|(|x,\<delta\>|)>\<Rightarrow\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\><around*|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>>
      so that <math|\<delta\>> is a lower bound for
      <math|<around*|{|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>\|y\<in\>X\\U|}>>. In
      other words\ 

      <\equation*>
        0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|X\\Y><around*|(|x|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x-y|\<\|\|\>>\|y\<in\>X\\U|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      Using the Archimedean property of the real numbers [see theorem:
      <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>] there exist a
      <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|0\<less\><frac|1|n>\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|X\\Y><around*|(|x|)>>,
      hence <math|x\<in\>\<delta\><rsup|-1><rsub|X\\Y><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|1|n>,\<infty\>|[>|)>=F<rsub|n>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|n>>,
      this proves that <math|U\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|n>>
      which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1002.285>] results in\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1003.285>U=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|n>
      </equation>

      Consider now <math|<around*|{|U\\F<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >U\\F<rsub|n+1><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
        21.1001.285>]>>U\\F<rsub|n>
      </equation*>

      and\ 

      <\equation*>
        <big|cap><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U\\F<rsub|n>|)>=U\\<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|n>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1003.285>]>>U\\U=\<varnothing\>
      </equation*>

      So the conditions for [theorem: <reference|measure measure space
      properties (3)>] are satisfies [note that <math|\<mu\>> is finite] from
      which it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F<rsub|n>|)><text|
        exist and >0=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F<rsub|n>|)>
      </equation*>

      Hence we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
        >\<exists\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| such that
        >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F<rsub|N>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation*>

      As also <math|F<rsub|N>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>F<rsub|n>=U\<subseteq\>U>
      and <math|F<rsub|N>> is closed it follows that

      <\equation*>
        U\<in\>\<cal-A\>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have <math|U\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. In other words we have
    proved that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    As we have already proved that <math|\<cal-A\>> is a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] that
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>.
    Hence if <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
    then <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>\<varepsilon\><text| there exists a closed set <math|F> and a
      open set <math|U> such that >F\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<text| and
      >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\F|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The above two lemmas can be summarized in the following corollary.

  <\corollary>
    <label|corollary 21.533.285>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a normed space with topology <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>>
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a <with|font-series|bold|finite >measure then
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
      is closed>|}>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    This follows directly from [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.530.285>] and
    [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.532.285>].
  </proof>

  Unfortunately we can not use the above corollary on the Borel measures
  <math|\<lambda\>> and <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> because these measures are
  not finite. For this we introduce the concept of locally finite mesures,
  compact finite measures, regular measures and Radon measures.\ 

  <\definition>
    <label|measure regularity>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>>
    be a topological space, <math|\<cal-A\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on
    <math|X> with <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    measure then we definie:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|locally finite >if
      <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> there exist a open set <math|U> such that
      <math|x\<in\>U> and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|external regular >if
      <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have that
      <math|\<mu\>*<around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|internal regular> if
      <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
      is closed>|}>|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <label|compact finite measure><label|Radon measure definition>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    <with|font-series|bold|Hausdorff> topological space, <math|\<cal-A\>> a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> with
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a measure
    then we define:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|compact finite >if
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> for every compact set.

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|compact internal regular
      >if <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact >|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|regular >if <math|\<mu\>>
      is <with|font-series|bold|external and compact internal regular>.

      <item><math|\<mu\>> is a <with|font-series|bold|Radon measure >if
      <math|\<mu\>> is <with|font-series|bold|locally finite> and
      <with|font-series|bold|regular>.
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      The Hausdorff condition is essential as it ensures by [theorem:
      <reference|compact set is closed in a Hausdorff space>] that a compact
      set <math|K> is closed, hence a element of <math|\<cal-A\>> so that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>> makes sense.
    </note>
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure regularity and scalar product>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>> be a topological
    space, <math|\<cal-A\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> with
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a measure,
    <math|\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> then we have for the
    measure <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> [see theorem: <reference|measure
    sum and scalar product of measures>] that:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<mu\>> is locally finite then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is locally finite.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>> is external regular then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is external regular.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>> is internal regular then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>,\<mu\>> is internal regular.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>> is a Radan measure then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is a Radon measure.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|x\<in\>X> then as <math|\<mu\>> is locally finite there
      exist a open set <math|U> such that <math|x\<in\>U> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> so that
      <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|U|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      which proves that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is locally finite.

      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then as <math|\<mu\>> is external
      regular we have that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>>. Hence we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex sup(a.x) inf(a.x)>]>>>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then as <math|\<mu\>> is internal
      regular we have that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|C\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
      is closed>|}>|)>>. Hence we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex sup(a.x) inf(a.x)>]>>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      <item>This follows from (2) and (3).
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure compact finite and scalar product>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>> be a topological
    space, <math|\<cal-A\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> with
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a measure,
    <math|\<alpha\>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>> then we have for the
    measure <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> [see theorem: <reference|measure
    sum and scalar product of measures>] that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<mu\>> is compact finite then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is compact finite.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>> is compact internal regular then
      <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is compact internal regular
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|\<mu\>> is compact finite we have for every compact set
      <math|K> that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> so that
      <math|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|K|)>=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      from which it follows that <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>> is compact
      finite

      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then as <math|\<mu\>> is compact
      internal regular we have that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|C\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>.
      </equation*>

      Hence we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|complex sup(a.x) inf(a.x)>]>>>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|<around*|(|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>\<mu\>|)><around*|(|K|)>\|C\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  The regularity of a measure tells us how a measure can be aproximated by
  open, closed or compact sets which shows a relation between the measure and
  the topology. This can be used to prove uniqueness of measures.\ 

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure regularity and uniqueness>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>> be a topological
    space, <math|\<cal-A\>> a <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> with
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>> then we
    have that:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      and <math|\<nu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> are
      external regular measures such that for every open set <math|U> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|U|)>> then
      <math|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>.

      <item>If <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      and <math|\<nu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> are
      internal regular measures such that for every closed set <math|F> we
      have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|F|)>> then
      <math|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>.

      <item>If additional <math|\<cal-T\>> is Hausdorff then we have: If
      <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> and
      <math|\<nu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> are
      compact internal regular measures such that for every compact set
      <math|K> we have <math|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|K|)>>
      then <math|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ The proof is trivial

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then we have as <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>>
      are external regular we have

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>.

      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then we have as <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>>
      are internal regular we have

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|F|)>\|F\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>F<text|
        is closed>|}>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>.

      <item>Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then we have as <math|\<mu\>,\<nu\>>
      are compact internal regular we have

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<nu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>=\<nu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurable locally finiteness>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\>|\<rangle\>>> be a
    <with|font-series|bold|Hausdorff> topological space, <math|\<cal-A\>> a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|X> with
    <math|<rigid|\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>>
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    local finite measure then <math|\<mu\>> is compact finite.

    <\note>
      If <math|\<cal-T\>> is a metric space or normed space then by [theorem:
      <reference|separation metric spaces are Hausdorff>] <math|\<cal-T\>> is
      Hausdorff so that the this theorem is also valid for metric and normed
      spaces.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|K> be a compact set then <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>K> there exist
    by local finitness a open set <math|U<rsub|x>> with
    <math|x\<in\>U<rsub|x>> and <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U<rsub|x>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    hence <math|K\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|x\<in\>K>U<rsub|x>>. As <math|K>
    is compact there exists a finite set <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|}>\<subseteq\>K>
    such that <math|K\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>U<rsub|x<rsub|i>>>.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|K|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>U<rsub|x<rsub|i>>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (4)>]>><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\<mu\><around*|(|U<rsub|x<rsub|i>>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>> is finite.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure compact finite and intervals>Let <math|\<cal-A\>> be a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\>> with
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    compact finite measure then we have <math|\<forall\>I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    [see definition: <reference|measure bounded intervals>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|I|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|I|]>>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|I|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>I|)><text|
      is a finite measure>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>>> then
    <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|]>> where <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>\<wedge\>a\<leqslant\>b>,
    using the Heine-Borel theorem [theorem: <reference|compact Heine Borel
    (1)>] <math|<around*|[|a,b|]>> is a compact set. As <math|\<mu\>> is
    compact finite it follows then that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    or in other words\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then either <math|I=\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>=0\<less\>\<infty\>>
    or there exist a <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with <math|a\<less\>b> such
    that <math|I=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<vee\><around*|]|a,b|]>\<vee\><around*|]|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|]>>
    so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>\<subseteq\>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|]>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}><text|
      that >\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|I|]>><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    pairwise disjoint that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|I|]>><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>I|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>I|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>I|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|I|]>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|I|]>>> is a measure on
    <math|\<cal-A\>>. Finally as <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|I|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>I|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|\|I><text| is a finite measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  \;

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure compact finite and rectangles>Let <math|\<cal-A\>> be a
    <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> such that
    <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>> a
    compact finite measure then we have <math|\<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|R|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|R|]>>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|R|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>R|)><text|
      is a finite measure>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra on R^n rectangles>] we
      have that <math|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|R|)>> is well defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>>> then by
    [theorem: <reference|measure open closed rectangles>] <math|R> is a
    compact set. As <math|\<mu\>> is compact finite it follows then that
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|R|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> or in other words\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|R|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then either <math|R=\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|R|)>=0\<less\>\<infty\>>
    or there exist a <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<less\>b>
    such that <math|R=<around*|[|a,b|[>\<vee\><around*|]|a,b|]>\<vee\><around*|]|a,b|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|a,b|]>>
    so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|R|)>\<subseteq\>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|]>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>R\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}><text|
      that >\<mu\><around*|(|R|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|I\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||]>><big|cup>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|]||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
    then by [theorem: <reference|measure sum and scalar product of
    measures>(3)]\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|R|]>>:\<cal-A\>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|R|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>R|)>
    </equation*>

    is a mesure on <math|\<bbb-R\>>. Finally as
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> we have
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|R|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>R|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|R|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|R|]>><text| is a finite measure on >\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|<math|\<lambda\>> and <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> are Radon
  measures>

  We set out now to prove that the Borel measures <math|\<lambda\>> and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> on <math|\<bbb-R\>> and <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
  are Radon measures, for this we need the following two theorems that state
  that compact finite measures on <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> and
  <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> are regular.

  <\lemma>
    <label|measure Radon condition>Let <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a compact finite measure then <math|\<mu\>> is regular.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First we prove external regularity. Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> then we have the following
    possible cases to consider:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>If <math|U> is a open set
      with <math|A\<subseteq\>U> then by the monotonicity of <math|\<mu\>>
      [see theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>] it
      follows that <math|\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=\<infty\>>.
      Hence <math|\<infty\>\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>> so that <math|\<infty\>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>> proving that
      <math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>=\<infty\>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>If
      <math|x\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>> then <math|x=\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>> where <math|U> is a
      open set with <math|A\<subseteq\>U>. By the monotonicity of
      <math|\<mu\>> [see theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>] it follows that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=x>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is a lower bound of
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>>. Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1004.286>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Assume that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>>. Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and
      define <math|<around*|{|Q<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>
      by <math|Q<rsub|i>=<around*|]|-i,i|[>> then by [theorem:
      <reference|measure compact finite and intervals>] we have for
      <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)><text|
        is a finite measure><rsub|>
      </equation*>

      Hence using [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.532.285>] we have that there
      exists a open <math|U<rsub|i>> and a closed set <math|F<rsub|i>> such
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i><text| and
        >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i>>
      </equation*>

      As <math|F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|U<rsub|i>\\A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>> it follows from the
      monotonicity of <math|\<mu\>> [see theorem: <reference|measure measure
      space properties (1)>] that <rigid|<math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i>>>>.
      To summarize we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1005.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| there exist a
        open >U<rsub|i><text| such that >A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i><text| and
        >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i+1>>
      </equation>

      Defne now <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>>
      which is open as <math|U<rsub|i>,Q<rsub|i>=<around*|]|-i,i|[>> is open.
      Let <math|x\<in\>A> then as <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>Q<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[corollary:
      <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>]>>\<bbb-R\>>
      there exist a <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>Q<rsub|i>>, as further <math|A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>> by
      [eq: <reference|eq 21.1005.286>], it follows that
      <math|x\<in\>U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>U>, hence it
      follows that <math|A\<subseteq\>U>. Further

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>|)>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>\\A|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and
        intersection>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|series absolute dominant convergence>]>\<wedge\><text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1005.286>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i+1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|i>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
        <reference|series power series in
        R>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Summarized we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1006.286>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
        there exist a open set >U<text| with >A\<subseteq\>U<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation>

      From the above it follows, by using <math|\<varepsilon\>=1>, that there
      exist a <math|V> open such that <math|A\<subseteq\>V> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>-\<mu\>*<around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|V\\A|)>\<less\>1>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>. Hence

      <\equation*>
        inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>> is finite. As we have assumed that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>> we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<varepsilon\>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>
      </equation*>

      So using [eq: <reference|eq 21.1006.286>] there exist a open set
      <math|U> with <math|A\<subseteq\>U> such that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>. As
      <math|U<below|=|A\<subseteq\>U><around*|(|U\\A|)><big|sqcup>A>, we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|A\<subseteq\>U<text|
        open>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      leading to the contradiction <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>
      from which it follows that the assumption
      <math|<rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>>> is false. Hence <rigid|<math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>> which combined with
      [eq: <reference|eq 21.1004.286>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    Hence we have proved that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
    is open>|}>|)>> which by definition means that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1007.286>\<mu\><text| is external regular>
    </equation>

    Next we have to prove external regularity. Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> then we have two possible
    cases:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>Define
      <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      by <math|C<rsub|i>=<around*|[|-i,i|]>> and consider the measures
      <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>>
      where <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>>
      then from [theorem: <reference|measure compact finite and intervals>]
      it follows that <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>>> are finite.
      Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then by [lemma:
      <reference|lemma 21.532.285>] there exist a open <math|U<rsub|i>> and a
      closed set <math|F<rsub|i>> such that
      <math|F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>> and
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|>>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|A\\F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|A\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>.
      To summarize we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1008.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| there exist a
        closed set >F<rsub|i><text| such that >F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<text|
        and >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|A\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation>

      As for <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> we have
      that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|C<rsub|i>=<around*|[|-i,i|]>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-<around*|(|i+1|)>,i+1|]>=C<rsub|i+1>>
      and <math|<rigid|\<bbb-R\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|]>\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>>
      so that <math|\<bbb-R\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>>.
      Hence for <math|<around*|{|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      we have that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<big|cap>C<rsub|i+1>> and
      <math|<rigid|A=A<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>=A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>>>.
      Hence by [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>] we
      have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> it follows from
      [theorem: <reference|extended limit equivalences>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1009.286>\<exists\>N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        such that >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|A<big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<subseteq\>A><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|n<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation>

      Now\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1009.286>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>+<tfrac|\<varepsilon\>|2>-\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]>><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]>><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]><text|
        is finite>>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]>><around*|(|A\\F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1008.286>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1010.286>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation>

      As <math|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>=F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap><around*|[|-N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|]><rsub|>>
      is a closed set and <math|><math|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|]>>,
      which is compact by Heine Borell [see theorem: <reference|compact Heine
      Borel (1)>], it follows from [theorem: <reference|compact every closed
      subset of a compact set is compact>] that
      <math|><math|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
      is compact. So if we take <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1008.286>]>>A> then we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1011.286>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
        there exist a compact set >K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|><text| such
        that >K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation>

      Assume now that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> then

      <\equation*>
        \<varepsilon\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>
      </equation*>

      so that by [eq: <reference|eq 21.1011.286>] there exist a compact
      <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      from which it follows that

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      which is a a contradiction. Hence the assumption is wrong and we must
      have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1012.286>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>> then there exist a compact set <math|K\<subseteq\>A>
      such that <math|x=\<mu\><around*|(|K|)><below|\<leqslant\>|K\<subseteq\>A>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
      Hence <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is a upper bound of
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>> which proves that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>. Combing this with
      [eq: <reference|eq 21.1012.286>] gives us
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>>. Hence we have proved that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1013.286>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        with >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>Define
      \ <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> by
      <math|A<rsub|i>=A<big|cap><around*|]|-i,i|[>> then we have that

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1><text|
        and >A=A<big|cap>\<bbb-R\>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[coroll:
        <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence
        (1)>]>>A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>]
      we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1014.286>\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|A<rsub|i>=A<big|cap><around*|[|-i,i|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-i,i|]>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|]|-i,i|[>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure compact finite and intervals>]>>\<infty\>> we can
      use [eq: <reference|eq 21.1013.286>] to get\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1015.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-1<below|\<less\>|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>> we have by the definition of the supremum that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1016.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| there exist a
        compact set <math|K<rsub|i><text| such that
        >K<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|i>|)>>>
      </equation>

      Assume now that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> then as
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1014.286>]>>\<infty\>> <math|> we have by [theorem:
      <reference|extended limit equivalences>] that there exist a
      <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|<rigid|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>+1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>>>, as also
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> we have that

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>-1
      </equation*>

      Using [eq: <reference|eq 21.1016.286>] there exist a <math|K<rsub|N>>
      such that <math|K<rsub|N>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|N>> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>-1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>>
      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|K<rsub|N>\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<rsub|n><text|
        is compact>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>-1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>
      </equation*>

      leading to the contradiction <math|\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>>.
      Hence the assumption is false and we must have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>=\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves tha

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1017.286>\<mu\><text| is compact internal regular>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<mu\>> is external regular and compact internal regular [see
    eqs: <reference|eq 21.1007.286>, <reference|eq 21.1017.286>] it follows
    by [definition: <reference|Radon measure definition>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><text| is regular>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We have a similar theorem for <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>.

  <\lemma>
    <label|measure Radon condition (1)>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a compact finite measure then <math|\<mu\>> is regular.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First we prove external reagularity. Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> then we have the
    following possible cases to consider:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>If <math|U> is a open set
      with <math|A\<subseteq\>U> then by the monotonicity of <math|\<mu\>>
      [see theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (1)>] it
      follows that <math|\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>\<Rightarrow\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=\<infty\>>.
      Hence <math|\<infty\>\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>> so that <math|\<infty\>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<infty\>> proving that
      <math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>=\<infty\>>. Hence\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>If
      <math|x\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subset\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>> then <math|x=\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>> where <math|U> is a
      open set with <math|A\<subseteq\>U>. By the monotonicity of
      <math|\<mu\>> [see theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>] it follows that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=x>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is a lower bound of
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>>. Hence we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1018.286>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      Assume that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>>. Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> and
      define <math|<around*|{|Q<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|]||[>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>>
      by <math|Q<rsub|i>=<around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||]>>>
      then by [theorem: <reference|measure compact finite and rectangles>] we
      have for <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> that

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)><text|
        is a finite measure><rsub|>
      </equation*>

      Hence using [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.532.285>] we have that there
      exists a open <math|U<rsub|i>> and a closed set <math|F<rsub|i>> such
      that\ 

      <\equation*>
        F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i><text| and
        >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i>>
      </equation*>

      As <math|F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A> we have
      <math|U<rsub|i>\\A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>> it follows from the
      monotonicity of <math|\<mu\>> [see theorem: <reference|measure measure
      space properties (1)>] that <rigid|<math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i>>>>.
      To summarize we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1019.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| there exist a
        open >U<rsub|i><text| such that >A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i><text| and
        >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i+1>>
      </equation>

      Defne now <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>>
      which is open as <math|U<rsub|i>,Q<rsub|i>=<around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>>
      is open [see theorem: <reference|measure open closed rectangles>] Let
      <math|x\<in\>A> then as <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>Q<rsub|i>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[proposition:
      <reference|R^n is the union of ]-i,i[>]>>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> there exist
      a <math|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|x\<in\>Q<rsub|i>>, as further
      <math|A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>> by [eq: <reference|eq 21.1019.286>], it
      follows that <math|x\<in\>U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>U>,
      hence it follows that <math|A\<subseteq\>U>. Further

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>|)>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>\\A|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (2)>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsub|i><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|class inclusion and union and
        intersection>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)><big|cap>Q<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|Q<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|series absolute dominant convergence>]>\<wedge\><text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1019.286>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|i+1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|2<rsup|i>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example:
        <reference|series power series in
        R>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Summarized we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1020.286>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
        there exist a open set >U<text| with >A\<subseteq\>U<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation>

      From the above it follows, by using <math|\<varepsilon\>=1>, that there
      exist a <math|V> open such that <math|A\<subseteq\>V> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>-\<mu\>*<around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure measure space properties
      (1)>]>\<wedge\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|V\\A|)>\<less\>1>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>. Hence

      <\equation*>
        inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
      </equation*>

      which proves that <math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>> is finite. As we have assumed that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>> it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<varepsilon\>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>
      </equation*>

      So using [eq: <reference|eq 21.1020.286>] there exist a open set
      <math|U> with <math|A\<subseteq\>U> such that
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>>. As
      <math|U<below|=|A\<subseteq\>U><around*|(|U\\A|)><big|cup>A>, we have

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U\\A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>+\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|A\<subseteq\>U<text|
        open>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      leading to the contradiction <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>
      from which it follows that the assumption
      <math|<rigid|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>>> is false. Hence <rigid|<math|inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
      is open>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>> which combined with
      [eq: <reference|eq 21.1018.286>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
        is open>|}>|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    Hence we have proved that <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=inf<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>\|A\<subseteq\>U\<wedge\>U<text|
    is open>|}>|)>> which by definition means that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1021.286>\<mu\><text| is external regular>
    </equation>

    Next we have to prove external regularity. Let
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> then we have two
    possible cases:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>>Define
      <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
      by <math|C<rsub|i>=<around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>> and consider the
      measures <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>>
      where <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|B|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|B<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>>
      then from [theorem: <reference|measure compact finite and intervals>]
      it follows that the measures <math|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>>>
      are finite. Let <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> then by
      [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.532.285>] there exist a open
      <math|U<rsub|i>> and a closed set <math|F<rsub|i>> such that
      <math|F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>> and
      <math|<rigid|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2<rsup|>>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|A\\F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>U<rsub|i>\\F<rsub|i>>\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|A\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>.
      To summarize we have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1022.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| there exist a
        closed set >F<rsub|i><text| such that >F<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<text|
        and >\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|i>|]>><around*|(|A\\F<rsub|i>|)>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation>

      As for <math|<around*|{|C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> we have
      that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|C<rsub|i>=<around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-<around*|(|i+1|)>,i+1|]><rsup|n>=C<rsub|i+1>>
      and <math|<rigid|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><below|=|<text|[proposition:
      <reference|R^n is the union of ]-i,i[>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|[|-i,i|]><rsup|n>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>>>.
      Hence for <math|<around*|{|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
      we have that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<big|cap>C<rsub|i+1>> and
      <math|<rigid|A=A<big|cap>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>=A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>C<rsub|i>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>>>.
      So using [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>] we
      have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation*>

      As <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> it follows from
      [theorem: <reference|extended limit equivalences>] that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1023.286>\<exists\>N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
        such that >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|A<big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<subseteq\>A><around*|\||\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|n<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>|\|>\<less\><frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>
      </equation>

      Now\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1023.286>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|A<big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>+<tfrac|\<varepsilon\>|2>-\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]>><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]>><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]><text|
        is finite>>\<wedge\><around*|[|theorem:<reference|measure measure
        space properties (1)>|]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|<around*|[|C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|]>><around*|(|A\\F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1022.286>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>+<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<varepsilon\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      hence\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1024.286>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation>

      As <math|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>=F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap><around*|[|-N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|]><rsup|n><rsub|>>
      is a closed set and <math|><math|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|]><rsup|n>>,
      which is compact by Heine Borell [see theorem: <reference|compact Heine
      Borel (1)>], it follows from [theorem: <reference|compact every closed
      subset of a compact set is compact>] that
      <math|><math|F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
      is compact. So if we take <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><big|cap>C<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>\<subseteq\>F<rsub|N<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1022.286>]>>A> then we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1025.286>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+><text|
        there exist a compact set >K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|><text| such
        that >K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
      </equation>

      Assume now that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> then

      <\equation*>
        \<varepsilon\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>
      </equation*>

      so that by [eq: <reference|eq 21.1025.286>] there exist a compact
      <math|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> such that\ 

      <\equation*>
        K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A<text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>-sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      from which it follows that

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|K<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<subseteq\>A>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation*>

      which is a a contradiction. Hence the assumption is wrong and we must
      have\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1026.286>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<leqslant\>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      If <math|x\<in\><around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>> then there exist a compact set <math|K\<subseteq\>A>
      such that <math|x=\<mu\><around*|(|K|)><below|\<leqslant\>|K\<subseteq\>A>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>.
      Hence <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>> is a upper bound of
      <math|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>> which proves that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>>. Combing this with
      [eq: <reference|eq 21.1026.286>] gives us
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>>. Hence we have proved that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1027.286>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
        with >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>\<less\>\<infty\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      <item*|<math|\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<infty\>>>Define
      \ <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>> by
      <math|A<rsub|i>=A<big|cap><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>> then we have that

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| >A<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i+1><text|
        and >A=A<big|cap>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[proposition:
        <reference|R^n is the union of ]-i,i[>]>>A<big|cap><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>|)>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Hence using [theorem: <reference|measure measure space properties (3)>]
      we have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1028.286>\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|A<rsub|i>=A<big|cap><around*|[|-i,i|[><rsup|n>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|]|-i,i|[><rsup|n>|)><below|\<less\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure compact finite and intervals>]>>\<infty\>> we can
      use [eq: <reference|eq 21.1027.286>] to get\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1029.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>
      </equation>

      As <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-1<below|\<less\>|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i>\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>> we have by the definition of the supremum that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1030.286>\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| there exist a
        compact set <math|K<rsub|i><text| such that
        >K<rsub|i>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|i><text| and
        >\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>-1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|i>|)>>>
      </equation>

      Assume now that <math|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> then we have from
      <math|<below|lim|i\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1028.286>]>>\<infty\>> <math|>by [theorem:
      <reference|extended limit equivalences>] that there exist a
      <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that <math|<rigid|sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>+1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>>>, as also
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> we have that

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>-1
      </equation*>

      Using [eq: <reference|eq 21.1030.286>] there exist a <math|K<rsub|N>>
      such that <math|K<rsub|N>\<subseteq\>A<rsub|N>> and
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>-1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>>
      then we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)><below|\<leqslant\>|K<rsub|N>\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<rsub|n><text|
        is compact>>sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|A<rsub|N>|)>-1\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>
      </equation*>

      leading to the contradiction <math|\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>\<less\>\<mu\><around*|(|K<rsub|N>|)>>.
      Hence the assumption is false and we must have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
        is compact>|}>|)>=\<infty\>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=sup<around*|(|<around*|{|\<mu\><around*|(|K|)>\|K\<subseteq\>A\<wedge\>K<text|
      is compact>|}>|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1031.286>\<mu\><text| is compact internal regular>
    </equation>

    As <math|\<mu\>> is external regular and compact internal regular [see
    eqs: <reference|eq 21.1021.286>, <reference|eq 21.1031.286>] it follows
    by [definition: <reference|Radon measure definition>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><text| is regular>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|Borel measure on R and R^n are locally finite>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The Lebesgue measure <math|\<lambda\>> in
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<lambda\>|\<rangle\>>>
      [see theorem: <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on R>] is
      locally finite.

      <item>For <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> the Lebesgue measure
      <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> in <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<rangle\>>>
      [see theorem: <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on R^n>] is
      locally finite.
    </enumerate>

    <\note>
      As by [theorem: <reference|measurable locally finiteness>] locally
      finite Borel measures are compact finite it follows that\ 

      <\enumerate>
        <item>The Borel measure <math|\<lambda\>> in
        <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<lambda\>|\<rangle\>>>
        is compact finite.

        <item>For <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> the Borel measure
        <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> in <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<rangle\>>>
        is compact finite.
      </enumerate>
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then
      <math|x\<in\><around*|]|x-1,x+1|[>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
      so that

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<cwith|1|1|3|3|cell-block|no>|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|]|x-1,x+1|[>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>]>>>|<cell|<with|font-series|bold|>\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|[|x-1,x+1|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
        R>]>>>|<cell|len<around*|(|<around*|[|x-1,x+1|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|2>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<infty\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      Hence <math|<wide|\<lambda\>|\<wide-bar\>><around*|(|<around*|]|x-1,x+1|[>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      which as <math|x\<in\><around*|]|x-1,x+1|[>> is open proves that
      <math|\<lambda\>> is locally finite.

      <item>If <math|x=<around*|(|x<rsub|1,>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
      then <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|x<rsub|i>-1,x<rsub|i>+1|[>>
      a open set and

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|]|x<rsub|i>-1,x<rsub|i>+1|[>|)>>|<cell|<below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure measure space properties
        (1)>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|x<rsub|i>-1,x<rsub|i>+1|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
        R^n>]>>>|<cell|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|x<rsub|i>-1,x<rsub|i>+1|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>2>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|2<rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<less\>>|<cell|\<infty\>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> is locally finite.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Now we are ready to prove that <math|\<lambda\>> and
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> are Radon measures.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Lebesgue borel measures are Radon measures>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>The Borel measure <math|\<lambda\>> in
      <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<lambda\>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a Radon measure.

      <item>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then the Borel measure
      <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> in <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<rangle\>>>
      is a Radon measure.
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ First to that <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> and
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>> are Hausdorf
    [see theorem: <reference|separation conditions>]

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Using [lemma: <reference|Borel measure on R and R^n are locally
      finite>] \ <math|\<lambda\>> is locally finite and locally compact,
      hence by [lemma: <reference|measure Radon condition>] <math|\<lambda\>>
      is regular. As <math|\<lambda\>> is locally finite we have by
      [definition: <reference|Radon measure definition>] that
      <math|\<lambda\>> is a Radon measure.

      <item>Using [lemma: <reference|Borel measure on R and R^n are locally
      finite>] <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> is locally finite and locally
      compact, hence by [lemma: <reference|measure Radon condition (1)>]
      <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> is regular. As <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> is
      locally finite we have by [definition: <reference|Radon measure
      definition>] that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> is a Radon measure.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Translation invariance and Radon Measures>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure translation of a measurable set>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>> be
    a normed space with the norm topology
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>> then\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item><math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> and
      <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
      we have

      <\equation*>
        x+A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>
      </equation*>

      where <math|x+A=<around*|{|x+a\|a\<in\>A|}>=<around*|{|a+x\|a\<in\>A|}>=x+A>.

      <item><math|\<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>> and
      <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
      we have

      <\equation*>
        \<alpha\>\<cdot\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>
      </equation*>

      where <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>A=<around*|{|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>a\|a\<in\>A|}>>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Take <math|x\<in\>X> then by [theorem: <reference|continuity of
      sum (1)>] <math|\<delta\><rsub|-x>:X\<rightarrow\>X> defined by
      <math|\<delta\><rsub|-x><around*|(|y|)>=-x+y> is a continuous function.
      Hence by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and continuity>] we
      have that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1032.286>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]><text|
        that >\<delta\><rsub|-x><rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>
      </equation>

      Further

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>\<delta\><rsup|-1><rsub|-x><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<delta\><rsub|-x><around*|(|y|)>\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|-x+y\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=x+<around*|(|-x+y|)><text|
        where ><around*|(|-x+y|)>\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=x+a<text|
        where >a\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>x+A>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that <math|\<delta\><rsup|-1><rsub|-x><around*|(|A|)>=x+A>.
      Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1032.286>] proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>x\<in\>X<text| and >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]><text|
        we have >x+A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <item>Take <math|\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\>> and
      <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
      then we have two cases for <math|\<alpha\>> to consider:

      <\description>
        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>=0>>For <math|A> we have either:\ 

        <\description>
          <item*|<math|A=\<varnothing\>>>Then
          <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>A=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>

          <item*|<math|A\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then
          <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>A=<around*|{|0|}>> which is closed [see
          theorems: <reference|separation metric spaces are Hausdorff>,
          <reference|separation Hausdorff every finite set is closed>] so
          that by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel algebra and closure>]
          <math|\<alpha\>\<cdot\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>.
        </description>

        <item*|<math|\<alpha\>\<neq\>0>>Using [theorem: <reference|continuity
        scalar product (1)>] the function
        <math|\<mu\><rsub|1\\\<alpha\>>:X\<rightarrow\>X> by
        <math|\<mu\><rsub|1\\\<alpha\>><around*|(|x|)>=<frac|1|\<alpha\>>\<cdot\>x>
        is a continuous function. Hence by [theorem: <reference|measure Borel
        algebra and continuity>] we have that\ 

        <\equation>
          <label|eq 21.1033.286>\<mu\><rsup|-1><rsub|1\\\<alpha\>><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]><rsup|>
        </equation>

        Further\ 

        <\eqnarray*>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>\<mu\><rsup|-1><rsub|1\\\<alpha\>><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|1\\\<alpha\>><around*|(|y|)>\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<frac|1|\<alpha\>>\<cdot\>y\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=\<alpha\>\<cdot\><around*|(|<frac|1|\<alpha\>>\<cdot\>y|)><text|
          where ><frac|1|\<alpha\>>\<cdot\>y\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=\<alpha\>\<cdot\>a<text|
          where >a\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>\<alpha\>\<cdot\>A>>>>
        </eqnarray*>

        which proves that <math|\<mu\><rsup|-1><rsub|1\\\<alpha\>><around*|(|A|)>=<frac|1|\<alpha\>>\<cdot\>A>.
        Combining this with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1033.286>] proves that\ 

        <\equation*>
          \<alpha\>\<cdot\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>
        </equation*>
      </description>

      In other words we have\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<forall\>\<alpha\>\<in\>\<bbb-K\><text| and
        >\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]><text|
        that >\<alpha\>\<cdot\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\definition>
    <label|measure translation invariant>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>|\<rangle\>>> be
    a normed space with the normed topology
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>> then a measure
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    is translation invariant if <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> and
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|(|A+x|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)><text| where
      >A+x=<around*|{|a+x\|a\<in\>A|}>=<around*|{|x+a\|a\<in\>A|}>=x+A
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      Using [corollary: <reference|measure translation of a measurable set
      (1)>] we have that <math|\<forall\>x\<in\>X> we have
      <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>
      that <math|<rigid|x+A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>>|]>>>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|x+A|)>> makes sense.
    </note>
  </definition>

  We prove now that <math|\<lambda\>> and <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> are
  translation invariant, first we need a little lemma:

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.558.288>Let <math|X> be a vector space, <math|x\<in\>X>,
    <math|I> a set and <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>\<subseteq\>\<cal-P\><around*|(|X|)>>
    then we have:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>> is pairwise
      disjoint then <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>+x|}><rsub|i\<in\>I>> is
      pairwise disjoint.

      <item><math|<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>+x=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Let <math|<around*|(|i,j|)>\<in\>I> with <math|i\<neq\>j>. Assume
      that <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>+x|)>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>
      then there exist a <math|<rigid|y\<in\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>+x|)>>>
      from which it follows that there exists <math|a<rsub|i>\<in\>A<rsub|i>>
      and <math|a<rsub|j>\<in\>A<rsub|j>> such that
      <math|a<rsub|i>+x=y=a<rsub|j>+x>, hence <math|a<rsub|i>=a<rsub|j>>
      which leads to the contradiction <math|a<rsub|i>\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>>.
      Hence the assumption is false and we must conclude that
      <math|<around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)><big|cap><around*|(|A<rsub|j>+x|)>=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item>We have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>+x>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>a\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i><text|
        such that >y=a+x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that >a\<in\>A<rsub|i><text| and
        >y=a+x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>i\<in\>I<text|
        such that >y\<in\>A<rsub|i>+x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation*>
        <around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I>A<rsub|i>|)>+x=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>I><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure the Borel measure is translation invariant (1)>The Borel
    measure <math|\<lambda\>> in the measure space
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>,\<lambda\>|\<rangle\>>>
    is translation invariant.\ 
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|x>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|A+x|)>
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<lambda\>> is the Borel measure on
    <math|\<sigma\><around*|[|S<rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>> [see theorem:
    <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on R>]. Then we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1036.287>\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>+x|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    be pairwise disjoint then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.558.288>] we
    have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1037.287><around*|{|A<rsub|i>+x|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1038.287><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+x=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>
    </equation>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1038.287>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1037.287>]>\<wedge\>\<lambda\><text| is a
      measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<lambda\><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1036.287>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|x>:\<sigma\><around*|[|S<rsub|<around*|[||[>>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      is a measure>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>=\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then we have for <math|A> either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|A=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=len<around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=len<around*|(|A|)>>.

      <item*|<math|A\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>>Then there exists
      <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> with <math|a\<less\>b> such that
      <math|A=<around*|[|a,b|[>>. Hence\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>A+x>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>+x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>y<rprime|'>\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[><text|
        with >y=y<rprime|'>+x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y-x\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|a\<leqslant\>y-x\<leqslant\>b>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|a+x\<leqslant\>y\<leqslant\>b+x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1039.287>A+x=<around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>
      </equation>

      Nect

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|A+x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1039.287>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
        R>]>>>|<cell|len<around*|(|<around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|b+x-<around*|(|a+x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|b-a>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|len<around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that <math|\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=len<around*|(|A|)>>.
    </description>

    So we have proves that <math|\<lambda\><rsub|x\|\<cal-S\><rsub|<around*|[||[>>>=len>
    which by [theorem: <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on R>]
    proves that <math|\<lambda\><rsub|x>=\<lambda\>> or in other words we
    have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><around*|(|A+x|)>=\<lambda\><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure the Borel measure is translation invariant (2)>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, then the Borel measure <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>>
    in <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>|\<rangle\>>>
    is translation inveriant.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> and define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A+x|)>
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> is the Borel measure defined in [theorem:
    <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on R^n>]. Then we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1040.290>\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>+x|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
    pairwise disjoint then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.558.288>]\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1041.290><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1042.290><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+x=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>
    </equation>

    Hence we have that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>+x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1042.290>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1041.290>]>\<wedge\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><text| is a
      measure>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>+x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1040.290>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x>:\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text|
      is a measure on >\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
    then we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|A=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>+x|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0=vol<rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>>

      <item*|<math|A=\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|x>>>Then there exist
      <math|a,b\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> with <math|a\<less\>b> such that
      <math|A=<around*|[|a,b|[>>. Hence we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>A+x>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[>+x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>t\<in\><around*|[|a,b|[><text|
        such that <math|y=t+x>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>t\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text|
        such that >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        >a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i><text| and
        >y<rsub|i>=t<rsub|i>+x<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        we have >a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
        we have >a<rsub|i>+x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>\<less\>b<rsub|i>+x<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      proving that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1043.290>A+x=<around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>
      </equation>

      Next\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A+x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1043.290>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
        R^n>]>>>|<cell|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a+x,b+x|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>+x<rsub|i>-<around*|(|a<rsub|i>+x<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|b<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|a,b|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=vol<rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>>.
    </description>

    So we have proved that <math|<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x>|)><rsub|\|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>=vol<rsup|n>>
    from which it follows by [theorem: <reference|measure Lebesgue measure
    space on R^n>] that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x>=\<lambda\><rsup|n>>.
    Hence if <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-R\><rsup|n><rsub|<around*|[||[>><big|cup><around*|{|\<varnothing\>|}>|]>>
    then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A+x|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><rsub|x><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We prove now a surprising relation between all translation invariant Radon
  measures on <math|\<bbb-R\>> and the Borel measure <math|\<lambda\>>. First
  we introduce Dyadic intervals [similar to Dyadic cubes in [definition
  <reference|measure Dyadic cubes>] and prove that every open set in
  <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>><space|0.6spc>can be written as the
  union of disjoint Dyadic intervals.

  <\definition>
    <label|measure Dyadic intervals><index|Dyadic
    interval><index|<math|\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>><index|<math|\<cal-D\>>><dueto|Dyadic
    interval>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>, <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>
    then <math|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>> is
    called a Dyadic interval of order <math|n>. The set of Dyadic intervals
    of order <math|n> is noted as <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-D\><rsub|n>=<around*|{|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>|}>
    </equation*>

    and the set of all Dyadic intervals is noted as <math|\<cal-D\>> hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-D\>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.562.291>Let <math|n,k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> with
    <math|k\<leqslant\>n> and <math|v,z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> then we have

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[><text|>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>=\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<nsubseteq\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>
    </equation*>

    in other words we have either <math|<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>=\<varnothing\>>
    or <math|<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>
    but not both.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>>
    and <math|J=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>. As
    <math|k\<leqslant\>n> we have <math|0\<leqslant\>l=n-k> and\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|J>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>|2<rsup|k>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>>,<frac|<around*|(|z+1|)>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>|2<rsup|k>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>|2<rsup|k>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>>,<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>+2<rsup|n-k>|2<rsup|k>\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|n-k>+2<rsup|n-k>|2<rsup|n>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|[|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>|[>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|21.1044.291>J=<around*|[|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>>. Then if
    <math|><math|<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>>
    then <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>> and if
    <math|><math|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|v|2<rsup|n>>>
    we have also <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>>, hence we must have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>\<less\><frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>\<wedge\><frac|v|2<rsup|n>>\<less\><frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>
    </equation*>

    after multiplying both sides in the above by <math|2<rsup|n>> we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1045.291>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>\<less\>v+1\<wedge\>v\<less\>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>
    </equation>

    Assume that <math|<frac|v|2<rsup|n>>\<less\><frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>>
    then <math|v\<less\>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\>l\<Rightarrow\>2<rsup|l>,v,z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>v+1\<leqslant\>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 21.1045.291>]>>v+1> leading to the contradiction
    <math|v+1\<less\>v+1>, so we must have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1046.291><frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|v|2<rsup|n>>
    </equation>

    Assume that <math|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>\<less\><frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>>
    then <math|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>\<less\>v+1\<Rightarrowlim\><rsub|0\<leqslant\>l\<Rightarrow\>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>,2<rsup|l>,v\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>\<leqslant\>v<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 21.1045.291>]>>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>> which leads to
    the contradiction <math|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>\<less\>z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>>,
    so we must have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1047.291><frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\><frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>
    </equation>

    Hence <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eqs:
    <reference|eq 21.1046.291>, <reference|eq
    21.1047.291>]>><around*|[|<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z\<cdot\>2<rsup|l>+2<rsup|l>|2<rsup|n>>|[><below|=|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 21.1044.290>]>>J>. So we have proved that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>
    </equation*>

    Let <math|<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>=\<varnothing\>>.
    Assume that <math|<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<subseteq\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>
    then

    <\equation*>
      <frac|v|2<rsup|n>>\<in\><around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>=<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>=\<varnothing\>
    </equation*>

    which lead to the contradiction <math|<frac|v|2<rsup|n>>\<in\>\<varnothing\>>,
    hence we must have that <math|<around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<nsubseteq\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[><big|cap><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>=\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\><around*|[|<frac|v|2<rsup|n><rsup|>>,<frac|v+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<nsubseteq\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|k>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|k>>|[>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.563.291>Dyadic intervals have the following properties:\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|n,k\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> with <math|k\<leqslant\>n>
      then we have <math|\<forall\>I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>,
      <math|\<forall\>J\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|k>> that\ 

      <\equation*>
        I<big|cap>J\<neq\>\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\>I\<subseteq\>J
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>\<Rightarrow\>I\<nsubseteq\>J
      </equation*>

      <item><math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> and
      <math|\<forall\>I,J\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> with <math|I\<neq\>J> we
      have <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>>

      <item><math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> we have
      <math|\<bbb-R\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>I> [by (2)
      pairwise disjoint union]

      <item><math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> and
      <math|\<forall\>I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> we have
      <math|len<around*|(|I|)>=2<rsup|-n>>

      <item><math|\<cal-D\>> is denumerable
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>This follows directly form [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.562.291>].

      <item>Take <math|I\<neq\>J> and assume that <math|I<big|cap>J\<neq\>0>
      as <math|n\<leqslant\>n> it follows from (1) that that
      <math|I\<subseteq\>J\<wedge\>J\<subseteq\>I> leading to the
      contradiction <math|I=J\<wedge\>I\<neq\>J> hence the assumption is
      wrong and we must have that <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item>Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> then for <math|x\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>> we
      have by [theorem: <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence
      (1)>] there exist a <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> such that
      <math|<rigid|z\<leqslant\>x\<cdot\>2<rsup|n>\<less\>z+1>> from which it
      follows that <math|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>x\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>>.
      Hence <math|x\<in\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>
      which proves that <math|\<bbb-R\>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>I>.
      As trivially <math|<big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>I\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\>>
      we must conclude that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-R\>=<big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>I
      </equation*>

      <item>If <math|I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> then there exist a
      <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> such that <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>>
      so that

      <\equation*>
        len<around*|(|I|)>=len<around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>|)>=<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>-<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n>>=2<rsup|-n>
      </equation*>

      <item>Given <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> define
      <math|\<beta\>:\<bbb-Z\>\<rightarrow\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> by
      <math|\<beta\><around*|(|z|)>=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>>
      then <math|\<beta\>> is a bijection.

      <\description>
        <item*|injectivity>If <math|\<beta\><around*|(|x|)>=\<beta\><around*|(|y|)>>
        then <around*|[|<frac|x|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|x+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>=<around*|[|<frac|y|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|y+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>
        which by [theorem: <reference|generalized intervals and boundaries>]
        proves that <math|<frac|x|2<rsup|n>>=<frac|y|2<rsup|n>>> from whih it
        follows that <math|x=y>.

        <item*|surjectivity>If <math|I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> then there
        exist a <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> such that
        <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>=\<beta\><around*|(|z|)>>.
      </description>

      As <math|\<bbb-Z\>> is denumerable [see theorem: <reference|complex
      embedding>] and <math|\<beta\>> is a bijection it follows that
      <math|\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|n>> is
      denumerable. Finally by [theorem: <reference|denumerable union of a
      denumerable family of denumerable sets is denumerable>] it follows that
      <math|\<cal-D\>=<big|cup><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>
      is denumerable.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  Next we prove that every non empty open set can be written as a disjoint
  countable union of Dyadic intervals.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Dyadic intervals and open sets>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|\<rangle\>>>
    be the topological spaces based on the canonical norm
    <math|<around*|\|||\|>> on <math|\<bbb-R\>> then
    <math|\<forall\>U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> with
    <math|U\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> we have that
    <math|\<exists\><around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>
    pairwise disjoint such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      U=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<Nu\>>I<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|U> be a non empty open set in <math|\<bbb-R\>>. Define now\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1048.291>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0><rsub|><text|
      >S<rsub|n>=<around*|{|I\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>\|I\<subseteq\>U|}>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1049.291>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0><text|
      >\<cal-I\><rsub|n>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|0><text|
      if >n=0>>|<row|<cell|<around*|{|I\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n>\|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n-1|}><text|
      we have >\<forall\>J\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|i><text|
      >I\<nsubseteq\>J|}><text| if >n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>>>>>\<subseteq\>S<rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>
    </equation>

    \ and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1050.291>\<cal-I\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-I\><rsub|i>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>=\<cal-D\>
    </equation>

    Next we prove that <math|U=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I>. Let
    <math|x\<in\>U> then as <math|U> is open there exist a
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1051.291>x\<in\><around*|]|x-\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>,x+\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>|[>\<subseteq\>U.
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|complex Archimedean property consequence (1)>]
    there exist a <math|n<rsub|x>\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1052.291><frac|1|n<rsub|x>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>
    </equation>

    using [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.563.291>(3)] there exist a
    <math|I<rsub|x>\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n<rsub|x>>> so that
    <math|x\<in\>I<rsub|x>>. Hence there exist a <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> such
    that

    <\equation>
      x\<in\>I<rsub|x>=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>|[>\<Rightarrow\><frac|z|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>\<leqslant\>x\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>
    </equation>

    If <math|t\<in\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>|[>>
    then <math|<frac|z|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>\<leqslant\>t\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>>
    so that <math|x-t\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>-<frac|z|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 21.1052.291>]>>\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>> so that
    <math|x-\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>\<less\>t>. Further
    <math|t-x\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>-<frac|z|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>>=<frac|1|2<rsup|n<rsub|x>>><below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 21.1052.291>]>>\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>> so that
    <math|t\<less\>x+\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>>. Hence
    <math|t\<in\><around*|]|x-\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>,x+\<varepsilon\><rsub|x>|[><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 21.1051.291>]>>U> which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1054.291>x\<in\>I<rsub|x>\<subseteq\>U
    </equation>

    Hence by definition [eq: <reference|eq 21.1048.291>] we have that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1055.291>I<rsub|x>\<in\>S<rsub|n<rsub|x>>
    </equation>

    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|x>=<around*|{|i\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,n<rsub|x>|}>\|\<exists\>I\<in\>S<rsub|i><text|
      such that >x\<in\>I\<subseteq\>U|}>
    </equation*>

    then by [eqs: <reference|eq 21.1051.291>, <reference|eq 21.1055.291>] it
    follows <math|n<rsub|x>\<in\>\<cal-K\><rsub|x>> which proves that
    <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|x>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>. Hence\ 

    <\equation*>
      m<rsub|x>=min<around*|(|\<cal-K\><rsub|x>|)><text| exist>
    </equation*>

    For <math|m<rsub|x>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|m<rsub|x>=0>>Then <math|\<exists\>I\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|0>>
      such that <math|x\<in\>I> which as \ <math|S<rsub|0><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1049.291>]>>\<cal-I\><rsub|0>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-I\><rsub|i>=\<cal-I\>>
      proves that <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I>.

      <item*|<math|0\<less\>m<rsub|x>>>Then
      <math|\<exists\>I\<in\>S<rsub|m<rsub|x>>> such that
      <math|x\<in\>I\<subseteq\>U> and <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m<rsub|x>-1|}>>
      we have <math|\<forall\>J\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> that
      <math|\<neg\><around*|(|x\<in\>J\<wedge\>J\<subseteq\>U|)>\<Rightarrow\>x\<nin\>J\<vee\>J\<nsubseteq\>U>.
      As <math|J\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|i>> we have by [eq: <reference|eq
      21.1048.291>] that <math|J\<subseteq\>U>, hence we must have that
      <math|x\<nin\>J> which as <math|x\<in\>I> proves that
      <math|I\<nsubseteq\>J>. Hence by [eq: <reference|eq 21.1049.291>] we
      have that <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|m<rsub|x>>\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-I\><rsub|i>=\<cal-I\>>
      which proves that <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I>.
    </description>

    so in all cases we have <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I>.
    Ss we have choosen <math|x\<in\>U> arbitrary it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1056.291>U\<subseteq\><big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\><big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I> then there exist a
    <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>> such that <math|x\<in\>I>. As
    <math|\<cal-I\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-I\><rsub|n>>
    there exist a <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> such that
    <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|n>> which as
    <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n>> means that
    <math|I\<subseteq\>U> so that <math|x\<in\>U>. Hence we have
    <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I\<subseteq\>U> which combined with
    [eq: <reference|eq 21.1056.291>] proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1057.291>U=<big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I
    </equation>

    Next we prove that the above union is pairwise disjoint. Let
    <math|I,J\<in\>\<cal-I\>> with <math|I\<neq\>j> then as
    <math|\<cal-I\>=<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>>\<cal-I\><rsub|i>>
    there exists a <math|n,m\<in\>\<bbb-N\><rsub|0>> such that
    <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|n>> and <math|J\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|m>>. For
    <math|n,m> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|n=m>>Then <math|I,J\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>
      then by [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.563.291>]
      <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>>

      <item*|<math|n\<less\>m>>Then <math|n\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,m-1|}>>
      and by [eq: <reference|eq 21.1049.291>] we have that
      <math|I\<nsubseteq\>J> hence as <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>,
      <math|J\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|m>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsub|m>> and
      <math|n\<less\>m> it follows by [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.563.291>]
      that <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>>.

      <item*|<math|m\<less\>n>>Then <math|m\<in\><around*|{|0,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>>
      and by [eq: <reference|eq 21.1049.291>] we have that
      <math|J\<nsubseteq\>I> hence as <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|n>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsub|n>>,
      <math|J\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|m>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><rsub|m>> and
      <math|m\<less\>n> it follows by [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.563.291>]
      that <math|I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\>>.
    </description>

    This proves\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1058.291>\<forall\>I,J\<in\>\<cal-I\><text| with
      >I\<neq\>J<text| we have >I<big|cap>J=\<varnothing\><text| and
      >U=<big|sqcup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I
    </equation>

    Next we prove that <math|\<cal-I\>> is denumerable. By [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 21.563.291>] <math|\<cal-D\>> is denumerable which, as
    <math|\<cal-I\><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
    21.1050.291>]>>\<cal-D\>>, proves by [theorem: <reference|denumerable
    every subset is finite denumerable.>] that <math|\<cal-I\>> is either
    finite or denumerable. Assume that <math|\<cal-I\>> is finite. As
    <math|U\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> we have that
    <math|<big|sqcup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I\<neq\>\<varnothing\>> it
    follows that <math|\<cal-I\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>. Use [theorem:
    <reference|generalized intervals and boundaries>] to define the function\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\>:\<cal-D\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\> <text| by
      >\<alpha\><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>|)>=<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>
    </equation*>

    then <math|\<alpha\><rsub|\|\<cal-I\>>:\<cal-I\>\<rightarrow\>\<alpha\><around*|(|\<cal-I\>|)>>
    is a surjection. As <math|\<cal-I\>> is finite it follows from [theorem:
    <reference|surjection f:A-\<gtr\>B if A is finite then B is finite>] that
    <math|\<alpha\><around*|(|\<cal-I\>|)>> is finite, further, as
    <math|\<cal-I\>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>,
    <math|\<alpha\><around*|(|\<cal-I\>|)>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>. Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1059.291>m=min<around*|(|\<alpha\><around*|(|\<cal-I\>|)>|)><text|
      exist>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>> then
    <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>> so that
    <math|\<alpha\><around*|(|I|)>=\<alpha\><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>|)>=<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>\<in\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>=I\<subseteq\>U>
    which proves that <math|\<alpha\><around*|(|\<cal-I\>|)>\<subseteq\>U>.
    As <math|m\<in\>\<alpha\><around*|(|\<cal-I\>|)>> it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1060.291>m\<in\>U
    </equation>

    As <math|U> is open there exist a <math|\<varepsilon\>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|+>>
    such that <math|m\<in\><around*|]|m-\<varepsilon\>,m+\<varepsilon\>|[>\<subseteq\>U>.
    Using [theorem: <reference|complex densitiy theorem>] there exist a
    <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1061.291>m-\<varepsilon\>\<less\>x\<less\>m\<less\>m+\<varepsilon\>
    </equation>

    From the above it follows that <math|x\<in\><around*|]|m-\<varepsilon\>,m+\<varepsilon\>|[>\<subseteq\>U>,
    as <math|U<below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
    21.1058.291>]>><big|cup><rsub|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>>I> there exist
    <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>\<in\>\<cal-I\>>
    such that <math|x\<in\>I=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|n>>|[>>
    from which it follows that <math|\<alpha\><around*|(|I|)>=<frac|z|2<rsup|n>>\<leqslant\>x<below|\<less\>|<text|[eq:
    <reference|eq 21.1061.291>]>>m>. As <math|I\<in\>\<cal-I\>> we have that
    <math|m<below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
    21.1059.291>]>>min<around*|(|\<alpha\><around*|(|\<cal-I\>|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><around*|(|I|)>>
    so that we reach the contradiction <math|m\<less\>m>. Hence the
    assumption is wrong and <math|\<cal-I\>> is not finite. This proves that
    <math|\<cal-I\>> is denumerable from which it follows that that there
    exist a bijection <math|\<sigma\>:\<bbb-N\>\<rightarrow\>\<cal-I\>>.
    Define <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>
    by <math|I<rsub|i>=\<sigma\><around*|(|I|)>> then we have using
    \ [theorem: <reference|measure pairwise disjoint sets and bijections>]
    together with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1058.291>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\><text|
      is pairwise disjoint and >U=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|i>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We are ready now to prove that every translation invariant Radon measure
  <math|\<mu\>> is essential equal to the Borel measure <math|\<lambda\>>
  mulitplied by a constant.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Radon and Borel measure on R>Let
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    be a translation invariant Radon measure then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    or in other words we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we calculate <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>> in terms
    of Dyadic intervals. Given <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> consider the family
    <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>+1|}>>>
    by <math|a<rsub|i>=<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>> then we have
    <math|a<rsub|1>=0\<wedge\>a<rsub|2<rsup|N>+1>=1> and
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>> we have
    <math|a<rsub|i>=<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|i|2<rsup|N>>=a<rsub|i+1>>.
    Then from [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.287.224>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1044.290>\<forall\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|{|<around*|[|<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|i|2<rsup|N>>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>>=<around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1045.290>\<forall\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      ><around*|[|0,1|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|i+1>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><around*|[|<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|i|2<rsup|N>>|[>
    </equation>

    Let <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>> then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|i|2<rsup|N>>|[>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>\<leqslant\>y\<less\><frac|i|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|0\<leqslant\>y-<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|1|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y-<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsub|N>>|[>+<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1046.290><around*|[|<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|i|2<rsup|N>>|[>=<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsub|N>>|[>+<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>
    </equation>

    Next\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1045.290>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><around*|[|<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|i|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1044.290>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|2<rsup|N>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|i|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1046.290>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|2<rsup|N>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>+<frac|i-1|2<rsup|N>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
      is translation invariant>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|2<rsup|N>>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|2<rsup|N>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    \;

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1065.291>\<forall\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>=2<rsup|N>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>
    </equation>

    Consider now Dyadic intervals [see definition: <reference|measure Dyadic
    intervals>]. Let <math|I\<in\>\<cal-D\>> then there exist a
    <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>> such that
    <math|I=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>. Now\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>\<leqslant\>y\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|0\<leqslant\>y-<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>-<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|0\<leqslant\>y-<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|1|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y-<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>+<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>+<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
      is translation invariant>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1065.291>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|I|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>+<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure the Borel measure is translation invariant
      (1)>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
      R>]>>>|<cell|len<around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1066.291>\<forall\>I\<in\>\<cal-D\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|I|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|I|)>
    </equation>

    We extend now the above relation to open sets. Let
    <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>> be a open set then we have
    either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|U=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>0=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|U|)>>.

      <item*|<math|U\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Using [theorem: <reference|measure
      Dyadic intervals and open sets>] there exist a pairwise disjoint
      <math|<around*|{|I<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>
      such that

      <\equation*>
        U=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      Hence

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1066.291>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<lambda\><around*|(|I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>I<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|I|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>><text| we have
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|U|)>>>
    </equation*>

    The above, the fact that <math|\<mu\>> is a Radon measure and by
    [theorems: <reference|measure regularity and scalar product>,
    <reference|Lebesgue borel measures are Radon measures>]
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\>> is a Radon
    measure we have using [theorem: <reference|measure regularity and
    uniqueness>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We have a similar result and proof for Radon measures on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> and the Borel measure <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Radon and Borel measure on R^n>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a translation invariant Radon measure then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    or in other words we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we calculate <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>>
    using Dyadic cubes. Let <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and consider
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> the family
    <math|<around*|{|a<rsub|i,j>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>+1|}>>>
    defined by <math|a<rsub|i,j>=<frac|j-1|2<rsup|N>>> then we have
    <math|a<rsub|i,1>=0>, <math|a<rsub|i,2<rsup|N>+1>=1> and
    <math|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>> we have
    <math|a<rsub|i,j>=<frac|j-1|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|j|2<rsup|N>>=a<rsub|i,j+1>>.
    Then from [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.287.224>] it follows that
    <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> \ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1067.291>\<forall\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><around*|{|<around*|[|<frac|j-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|j|2<rsup|N>>|[>|}><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>>=<around*|{|<around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,a<rsub|i,j+1>|[>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    and\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1068.291>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>,\<forall\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text|
      we have ><around*|[|0,1|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|i,j>,a<rsub|i,j+1>|[>=<big|sqcup><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><around*|[|<frac|j-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|j|2<rsup|N>>|[>
    </equation>

    So we have by [lemma: <reference|lemma 20.269.224>] that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|0,1|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[lemma:
      <reference|lemma 20.269.224>]>>>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|a<rsub|j,\<rho\><rsub|j>>,a<rsub|j,\<rho\><rsub|j>+1>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sqcup><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|<label|eq
      21.1069.291>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><rsub|>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)><eq-number>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Now given <math|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>>
    we have that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>|2<rsup|N>>|[>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>
      x<rsub|j>\<in\><around*|[|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>|2<rsup|N>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      ><frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|j>\<less\><frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >0\<leqslant\>x<rsub|j>-<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>|2<rsup|N>>-<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >0\<leqslant\>x<rsub|j>-<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|1|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >x<rsub|j>-<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|x-<frac|\<rho\>-1|2<rsup|N>>|)>\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>+<frac|\<rho\>-1|2<rsup|N>><eq-number><label|eq
      21.1070.291>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1069.291>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><rsub|>\<mu\><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>-1|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|\<rho\><rsub|j>|2<rsup|N>>|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1070.291>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><rsub|>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>+<frac|\<rho\>-1|2<rsup|N>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
      is translation independent>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>><rsub|>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|#<around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,2<rsup|N>|}>|)><below|=|<text|[<reference|cardinality
      of a product of sets>]>>2<rsup|n\<cdot\>N>>>|<cell|2<rsup|n\<cdot\>N>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1071.291>\<forall\>N\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>=2<rsup|n\<cdot\>N>\<cdot\>\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>|)>
    </equation>

    Let now <math|R\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsup|n>> be Dyadic cube [see definition:
    <reference|measure Dyadic cubes>] then there exist a
    <math|N\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> and a <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|n>> such that
    <math|D=<around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>.
    Then we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\>D>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|[|<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|N>>,<frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have ><frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|N>>\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >0\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>-<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|z<rsub|i>+1|2<rsup|N>>-<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >0\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>-<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|N>>\<less\><frac|1|2<rsup|N>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >x<rsub|i>-<frac|z<rsub|i>|2<rsup|N>>\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x-<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\><around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>+<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1072.291>D=<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>+<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>
    </equation>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|D|)>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1072.291>]>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>+<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|\<mu\><text|
      is translation invariant>>>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1071.291>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|2<rsup|n\<cdot\>N>>>>|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|D|)>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1072.291>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>+<frac|z|2<rsup|N>>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure the Borel measure is translation invariant
      (2)>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
      R^n>]>>>|<cell|vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|0,<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|[><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<frac|1|2<rsup|N>>|)><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|2<rsup|n\<cdot\>N>>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1073.291>\<forall\>D\<in\>\<cal-D\><text| we have
      >\<mu\><around*|(|D|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|D|)>
    </equation>

    Let now <math|U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>>
    then we have either:

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|U=\<varnothing\>>>Then
      <math|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>0=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|U|)>>

      <item*|<math|U\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>Then by [theorem:
      <reference|measure Dyadic intervals and open sets>] there exist a
      pairwise disjoint <math|<around*|{|D<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-D\>>
      such that

      <\equation*>
        U=<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>
      </equation*>

      The we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|I=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1073.291>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|I=1><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|I=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>D<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|U|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>
    </description>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>U\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>><text|
      we have >\<mu\><around*|(|U|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)><around*|(|U|)>
    </equation*>

    The above, the fact that <math|\<mu\>> is a Radon measure and by
    [theorems: <reference|measure regularity and scalar product>,
    <reference|Lebesgue borel measures are Radon measures>]
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\>> is
    a Radon measure we have using [theorem: <reference|measure regularity and
    uniqueness>] that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>=\<mu\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Borel measures and linear transformations>

  We want to examine how <math|\<lambda\>> and <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>>
  behaves under a linear isomorphism. For this we must ensure that for a
  linear isomorphism <math|T> and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
  [or <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> that
  <math|\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>> [or
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>>] is well defined,
  in other words we require that <math|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
  [or <math|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>].
  As by [corollaries: <reference|continuity homemorphism between K>,
  <reference|continuity homemorphism between powers of R>] linear
  isomorphisms are homeomorphisms it is enough to prove the result for
  homeomorphisms instead of linear isomorphisms. This is done in the next
  theorem.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measurability of image of a measurable set by a homeomorphism>Let
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|\<rangle\>>> and
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|\<rangle\>>> be two
    topological spaces, <math|T:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a homeomorphism [see
    definition: <reference|continuity homeomorphism>] then
    <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>> we
    have <math|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Let

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\>=<around*|{|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>\|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>|}>
    </equation*>

    then we have, as <math|\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>>
    and <math|T<around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>,
    that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1074.291>\<varnothing\>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>> then <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>\<Rightarrow\>X\\A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>>
    and <math|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>.
    As <math|T> is a bijective hence injective we have that
    <math|T<around*|(|X\\A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|function properties (1)>]>>T<around*|(|X|)>\\T<around*|(|Y|)><below|=|T<text|
    is surjective>>Y\\T<around*|(|A|)><below|\<in\>|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>.
    This proves that <math|X\\A\<in\>A> hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1075.291>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| we have
      >X\\A\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>>
    then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> we have
    <math|A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>> so that
    <math|A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>> and
    <math|T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|(|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|)>>.
    Hence we have that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>>
    and <math|T<around*|(|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|family image and preimage>]>><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>
    which proves that <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>>.
    In other words we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1076.291>\<forall\><around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\><text|
      we have ><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    From [eqs: <reference|eq 21.1074.291>, <reference|eq 21.1075.291>,
    <reference|eq 21.1076.291>] it follows by definition that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1077.291>\<cal-A\><text| is a >\<sigma\><text|-algebra on
      >X
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|X>\<Rightarrow\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>>
    then as <math|T> is a homeomorphism, hence open, we have that
    <math|<rigid|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>>
    which proves that <math|A\<in\>\<cal-A\>>. Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1078.291>\<cal-T\><rsub|X>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation>

    Using [theorem: <reference|measure generated sigma algebra>] on [eqs:
    <reference|eq 21.1077.291>, <reference|eq 21.1078.291>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>=\<sigma\><around*|[|\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-A\>
    </equation*>

    Hence

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]><text| we
      have >A\<in\>\<cal-A\><text| so that
      >T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure create a new measure based on a measure and a
    homeomorphism>Let <math|<around*|\<langle\>|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|<around*|\<langle\>|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|\<rangle\>>> be topological
    spaces, <math|T:X\<rightarrow\>Y> a homeomorphism and
    <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
    a measure then\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|[|T|]>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-A\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      defined by >\<mu\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>
    </equation*>

    is a measure. Further if <math|\<mu\>> is locally finite then
    <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>> is also locally finite

    <\note>
      Using the previous theorem [theorem: <reference|measurability of image
      of a measurable set by a homeomorphism>]
      <math|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]>>
      so that <math|\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>> is well defined.\ 
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    First we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1079.291>\<mu\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=0
    </equation>

    Let <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>>
    be pairwise disjoint. Given <math|i,j\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> with
    <math|i\<neq\>j> and assume that <math|<rigid|T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>T<around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>\<neq\>\<varnothing\>>>,
    then there exist a <math|x\<in\>T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>T<around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>>,
    so there exist <math|a\<in\>A<rsub|i>> and <math|b\<in\>A<rsub|j>> such
    that <math|T<around*|(|a|)>=x=T<around*|(|b|)>>, as <math|T> is a
    bijection it follows that <math|a=b> so that
    <math|a\<in\>A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|j>=\<varnothing\>> a contradiction,
    hence we must have that <math|<rigid|T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>T<around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>=\<varnothing\>>>.
    Hence we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|{|T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|}><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>><below|\<subseteq\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measurability of image of a measurable set by a
      homeomorphism>]>>\<cal-B\><around*|[|Y,\<cal-T\><rsub|Y>|]><text| is
      pairwise disjoint>
    </equation*>

    So we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>A<rsub|i>|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|family image and preimage>]>>\<mu\>*<around*|(|<big|sqcup><rsub|i\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mu\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>
    </equation*>

    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1079.291>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\><around*|[|T|]><text| is a measure on
      >\<cal-B\><around*|[|X,\<cal-T\><rsub|X>|]>
    </equation*>

    Assume now that <math|\<mu\>> is locally finite. Let <math|x\<in\>X> then
    as <math|\<mu\>> is locally finite there exist a open set <math|V> such
    that <math|T<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>V> and
    <math|\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>, take
    <math|U=T<rsup|-1><around*|(|V|)>> then <math|U> is open [as <math|T> is
    continuous] and <math|x\<in\>U>, further
    <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|[><around*|(|U|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|U|)>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|V|)>|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|function preimage of image>]>>\<mu\><around*|(|V|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    In other words given <math|x\<in\>X> we found a open set <math|U> with
    <math|x\<in\>U> and <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|U|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    which proves by definition that <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>> is locally
    finite.
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure create a new measure based on a measure and a linear
    isomorphism>We have\ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>If <math|T:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is a linear
      isomorphism and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0.\<infty\>|]>>
      is a Radon measure then

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|[|T|]>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>*<around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)><text|
        is a Radon measure>
      </equation*>

      <item>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>> then if
      <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> is a linear
      isomorphism and <math|\<mu\>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>>n>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]>>
      is a Radon measure then\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|[|T|]>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)><text|
        is a Radon measure>
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    \ 

    <\enumerate>
      <item>As <math|\<mu\>> is a Radon measure it follows from [definition:
      <reference|Radon measure definition>] that <math|\<mu\>> is locally
      finite, further as <math|T:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> is a
      linear isomorphism it follows from [theorem: <reference|continuity
      homemorphism between K>] that <math|T> is a homeomorphism Applying then
      [theorem: <reference|measure create a new measure based on a measure
      and a homeomorphism>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|[|T|]>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\|||\|>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>*<around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>
      </equation*>

      is a locally finite measure. Using [theorem: <reference|measurable
      locally finiteness>] it follows then that <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>>
      is compact finite and finally by [theorem: <reference|measure Radon
      condition>] that <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>> is a Radon measure.

      <item>As <math|\<mu\>> is a Radon measure it follows from [definition:
      <reference|Radon measure definition>] that <math|\<mu\>> is locally
      finite, further as <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
      is a linear isomorphism it follows from [theorem: <reference|continuity
      homemorphism between powers of R>] that <math|T> is a homeomorphism
      Applying then [theorem: <reference|measure create a new measure based
      on a measure and a homeomorphism>] it follows that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><around*|[|T|]>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>=\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
        defined by >\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>*<around*|(|A|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>
      </equation*>

      is a locally finite measure. Using [theorem: <reference|measurable
      locally finiteness>] it follows then that <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>>
      is compact finite and finally by [theorem: <reference|measure Radon
      condition (1)>] that <math|\<mu\><around*|[|T|]>> is a Radon measure.
    </enumerate>
  </proof>

  We examine now how the measures behaves under a linear isomorphism.

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel measure of T(A)>Let
    <math|T:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> be a linear isomorphism then we
    have <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> that

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      By [theorem: <reference|continuity homemorphism between K>] <math|T> is
      a homeomorphism so that by [theorem: <reference|measurability of image
      of a measurable set by a homeomorphism>]
      <math|<rigid|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
      from which it follows that <math|\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>>
      is well defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><around*|[|T|]>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >\<lambda\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>
    </equation*>

    then by [theorem: <reference|measure create a new measure based on a
    measure and a linear isomorphism>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><around*|[|T|]><text| is a >Radon measure
    </equation*>

    Next let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>
    then we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>T<around*|(|A+x|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=T<around*|(|a+x|)><text|
      where >a\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|T<text|
      is linear>>>|<cell|y=T<around*|(|a|)>+T<around*|(|x|)><text| where
      >a\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=b+T<around*|(|x|)>
      where \ b\<in\>T<around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>T<around*|(|A|)>+T<around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that <math|T<around*|(|A+x|)>=T<around*|(|A|)>+T<around*|(|x|)>>.
    Hence we have

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A+x|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A+x|)>|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>+T<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure the Borel measure is translation invariant
      (1)>]>>\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><around*|[|T|]><text| is translation invariant>
    </equation*>

    Finally using [theorem: <reference|measure Radon and Borel measure on R>]
    we have that

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<mu\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\corollary>
    <label|measure Borel measure on R and linear isomorphism>Let
    <math|T:\<bbb-R\>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\>> be a linear isomorphism then we
    have <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>> that

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=<around*|\||T<around*|(|1|)>|\|>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)><text|
      and >T<around*|(|1|)>\<neq\>0
    </equation*>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    By [example: <reference|linear mapping on a field>] \ it follows that\ 

    <\equation*>
      T<around*|(|1|)>\<neq\>0<text| and >T=T<around*|(|1|)>\<cdot\>Id<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    </equation*>

    For <math|T<around*|(|1|)>> we have either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|0\<less\>T<around*|(|1|)>>>We have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>x\<in\><around*|[|0,1|[><text|
        such that >y=T<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>x\<in\><around*|[|0,1|[><text|
        such that >y=T<around*|(|1|)>\<cdot\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        such that >0\<leqslant\>x\<less\>1\<wedge\>y=T<around*|(|1|)>\<cdot\>x>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|0\<less\>T<around*|(|1|)>>>|<cell|\<exists\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        such that >0\<leqslant\><frac|y|T<around*|(|1|)>>\<less\>1>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>y\<in\>\<bbb-R\><text|
        such that >0\<leqslant\>y\<less\>T<around*|(|1|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|0,T<around*|(|1|)>|[>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,T<around*|(|1|)>|[>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
        R>]>>len<around*|(|<around*|[|0,T<around*|(|1|)>|[>|)>=T<around*|(|1|)>=<around*|\||T<around*|(|1|)>|\|>
      </equation*>

      in other words\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1080.292><text|If >L<text| is a linear isomorphism with
        <math|0\<less\>L<around*|(|1|)><text| then
        >\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>=<around*|\||T<around*|(|1|)>|\|>>>
      </equation>

      <item*|<math|T<around*|(|1|)>\<less\>0>>First not that <math|-Id> is a
      linear isomorphism and <math|<around*|(|-Id|)>\<circ\><around*|(|-Id|)>=Id>
      so that using [eq: <reference|measure Borel measure of T(A)>] we have\ 

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|=>|<cell|len<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|measure Borel measure of
        T(A)>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)>\<circ\><around*|(|-Id|)>|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|measure Borel measure of
        T(A)>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|]>|)>|)><rsup|2>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      which as <math|0<below|\<leqslant\>|\<lambda\><text| is a
      measure>>\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>>
      proves that\ 

      <\equation>
        <label|eq 21.1081.292>\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>=1
      </equation>

      Take <math|T<rprime|'>=<around*|(|-Id|)>\<circ\>T> then
      <math|T<rprime|'>> is a linear isomorphism and
      <math|T<rprime|'><around*|(|1|)>=<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|T<around*|(|1|)>|)>=-T<around*|(|1|)>\<gtr\>0>,
      hence using [eq: <reference|eq 21.1080.292>] we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\><around*|(|T<rprime|'><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>=<around*|\||T<rprime|'><around*|(|1|)>|\|>=<around*|\||-T<around*|(|1|)>|\|>=<around*|\||T<around*|(|1|)>|\|>
      </equation*>

      Further\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\><around*|(|T<rprime|'><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>=\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>|)><below|=|<text|[eq:
        <reference|measure Borel measure of
        T(A)>]>>\<lambda\><around*|(|<around*|(|-Id|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
        <reference|eq 21.1081.292>]>>\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>
      </equation*>

      hence we have that\ 

      <\equation*>
        \<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>=<around*|\||T<around*|(|1|)>|\|>
      </equation*>
    </description>

    So in all cases we have

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1082.292>\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>=<around*|\||T<around*|(|1|)>|\|>
    </equation>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| that
      >\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel measure of
      T(A)>]>>\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1082.292>]>>T<around*|(|1|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|measure Borel measure of T(A) (1)>Let
    <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> be a linear
    isomorphism then we have <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
    that

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      By [theorem: <reference|continuity homemorphism between powers of R>]
      <math|T> is a homeomorphism so that by [theorem:
      <reference|measurability of image of a measurable set by a
      homeomorphism>] <math|<rigid|T<around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>>
      from which it follows that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>>
      is well defined.
    </note>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]>:\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>\<rightarrow\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|]><text|
      by >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>
    </equation*>

    then by [theorem: <reference|Lebesgue borel measures are Radon
    measures>,<reference|measure create a new measure based on a measure and
    a linear isomorphism>] we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]><text| is a >Radon measure
    </equation*>

    Next let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> and
    <math|A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>> then we have

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>T<around*|(|A+x|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=T<around*|(|a+x|)><text|
      where >a\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|T<text|
      is linear>>>|<cell|y=T<around*|(|a|)>+T<around*|(|x|)><text| where
      >a\<in\>A>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y=b+T<around*|(|x|)>
      where \ b\<in\>T<around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\>T<around*|(|A|)>+T<around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that <math|T<around*|(|A+x|)>=T<around*|(|A|)>+T<around*|(|x|)>>.
    Hence we have

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A+x|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A+x|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>+T<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure the Borel measure is translation invariant
      (2)>]>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]><text| is translation invariant>
    </equation*>

    Finally using [theorem: <reference|measure Radon and Borel measure on
    R^n>] we have that

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|[|T|]><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Note that in [corollary: <reference|measure Borel measure on R and linear
  isomorphism>] we have proved that <math|\<lambda\><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=T<around*|(|1|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>>,
  in fact for <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> we have a similar result namely
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=det<around*|(|T|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><around*|(|A|)>>.
  To prove this we work in different steps:

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.578.291>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|E=<around*|{|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|n>|}>> the canonical basis
    in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> [see example: <reference|basis F^n>],
    <math|<rigid|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>> a
    linear isomorphism such that <math|\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    we have <math|\<cal-M\><around*|(|T;E,E|)><rsub|i,j>=\<delta\><rsub|i,j>\<cdot\>M<rsub|i,i>>
    where <math|0\<leqslant\>M<rsub|i,i>> then we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=det<around*|(|T|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Using the previous [theorem: <reference|measure Borel measure of T(A)
    (1)>] we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1081.291>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation>

    As <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> is a linear
    isomorphism we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1082.291>0<below|\<neq\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|determinant det(L) properties>]>>det<around*|(|T|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|determinant of matrix and linear
      transformation>]>>det<around*|(|\<cal-M\><around*|(|T;E,E|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|matrix determinant of a diagonal
      matrix>]>><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>M<rsub|i,i>
    </equation>

    From the above it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1083.291>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >0\<less\>M<rsub|i,i>
    </equation>

    Define\ 

    <\equation*>
      M\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text| by >M=<around*|(|M<rsub|1,1>,\<ldots\>,M<rsub|n,n>|)><text|
      in other words >\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text| we
      have >M<rsub|i>=M<rsub|i,i>
    </equation*>

    Using the relation between a linear transformation and its matrix we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1084.291>\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text| we have
      >T<around*|(|x|)>=\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[example: <reference|matrix
      linear map application (1)>]>><big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>\<cal-M\><around*|(|T;E,E|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>\<delta\><rsub|j,i>\<cdot\>M<rsub|i,i>\<cdot\>x<rsub|j>=M<rsub|i,i>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    Now we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|y\<in\>T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>x\<in\><around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n><text|
      such that >y=T<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|\<exists\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text|
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|0\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>1<text| and
      >y=T<around*|(|x|)>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1084.291>]>>>|<cell|\<exists\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text|
      such that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|0\<leqslant\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>1<text| and
      >y<rsub|i>=M<rsub|i,i>\<cdot\>x<rsub|i>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|0\<neq\>M<rsub|i,i>>>|<cell|<text|<math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|0\<leqslant\><frac|y<rsub|i>|M<rsub|i,i>>\<less\>1>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrowlim\><rsub|0\<less\>M<rsub|i,i>>>|<cell|<text|<math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
      <math|0\<leqslant\>y<rsub|i>\<less\>M<rsub|i,i>>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|y\<in\><around*|[|0,M|[>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1085.291>T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>=<around*|[|0,M|[>
    </equation>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|t<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1<rsup|n>|[>|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|0,M|[>|)>=vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|0,M|[>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|M<rsub|i>-0|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1082.291>]>><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>M<rsub|i,i><below||><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1082.291>>>det<around*|(|T|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \;
    </equation*>

    Substituring this result in [eq: <reference|eq 21.1081.291>] it follows
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=det<around*|(|L|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  Now to calculate the general case we first have to examine the behavior of
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n>> under a unitary operator.

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.579.291>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|U:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> be a unitary
    transformation [using the canonical inner product defined in [example:
    <reference|inner product on R^n>]] then we have that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>

    <\note>
      <label|note 21.580.291>Using [theorem: <reference|spectral unitary
      operator properties>] <math|U<rsup|-1>> is a linear isomorphism, hence
      by [theorem: <reference|continuity homemorphism between K>]
      <math|U<rsup|-1>> is a homeomorphism so that by [theorem:
      <reference|measurability of image of a measurable set by a
      homeomorphism>] <math|<rigid|U<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]>>>
      from which it follows that <math|\<lambda\><around*|(|U<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>|)>>
      is well defined.
    </note>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    First note that by [theorem: <reference|normed norm on finite dimensional
    spaces are equivalent>] all norms on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> are
    equivalent, so if <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>> is the
    canonical norm on <math|\<bbb-R\>> [see example: <reference|normed
    maximum norm R^n>] and <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>> is the
    Euclidean norm on <math|\<bbb-R\>> \ [definition: <reference|inner
    euclidean norm real numbers>] then\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1086.291>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>=\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>>
    </equation>

    As by [theorem: <reference|spectral unitary operator properties>]
    <math|U<rsup|-1>> is a isomorphism we have by [theorem:
    <reference|measure Borel measure of T(A) (1)>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1087.291>\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text|
      we have >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<kappa\>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)><text|
      where >\<kappa\>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>
    </equation>

    Take the open ball <math|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>=<around*|{|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>\<less\>1|}>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>>=\<cal-T\><rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|n>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
    then by the above we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1088.291>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>|)>=\<kappa\>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>
    </equation>

    Further we have by [theorem: <reference|spectral unitary operator
    properties>] that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1089.291>U<text| is a >linear isometry
    </equation>

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>\<less\>1>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<Leftrightarrow\>|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1089.291>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|U<around*|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>\<less\>1>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|U<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|<around*|(|U<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|U<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<in\>U<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<Leftrightarrow\>>|<cell|x\<in\>U<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that <math|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>=U<rsup|-1><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>>.
    Substituting this in [eq: <reference|eq 21.1088.291>] gives\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1090.291>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>=\<kappa\>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>>
    then we have <math|<around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>\<less\>1>. Let
    <math|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> then we have

    <\equation*>
      -x<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|complex sqrt x\<less\>sqrt
      x^2>]>><around*|\||x<rsub|i>|\|>=<sqrt|x<rsub|i><rsup|2>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theoren:
      <reference|complex root is strictly
      increasing>]>><sqrt|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i><rsup|2>>=<around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>\<less\>1
    </equation*>

    so that <math|-1\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\>1> or
    <math|x<rsub|i>\<in\><around*|[|-1,1|[>>. Hence we have proved that
    <math|x\<in\><around*|[|-1,1|[><rsup|n>> so that
    \ <math|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>\<subseteq\><around*|[|-1,1|[><rsup|n>>
    from which it follows that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|-1,1|[><rsup|n>|)><below|=|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|measure Lebesgue measure space on
    R^n>]>>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|-1,1|[>|)>=2<rsup|n>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    In other words <math|>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1091.291>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>
    </equation>

    Further if <math|x\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|0,<frac|1|<sqrt|n>>|[>>
    then <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>we have
    <math|0\<less\>x<rsub|i>\<less\><frac|1|<sqrt|n>>> so that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>=<sqrt|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>x<rsub|i><rsup|2>><below|\<leqslant\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|complex root is strictly
      increasing>]>><sqrt|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><frac|1|n>>=<sqrt|n\<cdot\><frac|1|n>>=1
    </equation*>

    which prove that <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|0,<frac|1|<sqrt|n>>|[>\<subseteq\>B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>>
    so that\ 

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>n\<cdot\><frac|1|<sqrt|n>>=vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|0,<frac|1|<sqrt|n>>|[>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><around*|[|0,<frac|1|<sqrt|n>>|[>|)>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1092.291>0\<less\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>
    </equation>

    the above together with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1091.291>] allows us to
    multiply both sides of [eq: <reference|eq 21.1090.291>] by
    <math|<frac|1|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|B<rsub|<around*|\<\|\|\>||\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>><around*|(|0,1|)>|)>>>
    to get <math|\<kappa\>=1>. Substituting this in [eq: <reference|eq
    21.1087.291>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|Lebesgue measure and unitary transformation>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|><math|U:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    be a unitary transformation [using \ the Euclidean norm on
    <math|\<bbb-R\>> [see definition: <reference|inner euclidean norm real
    numbers>]] then we have that

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text| we have
      that >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|U<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Using \ [theorem: <reference|spectral unitary operator properties>]
    <math|U<rsup|-1>> is a unitary operator, applying then [lemma:
    <reference|lemma 21.579.291>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text| we have
      >\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|U<around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|<around*|(|U<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A|)>|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.582.291>Let <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>,
    <math|E=<around*|{|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|n>|}>> the canonical basis
    for <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> [see example: <reference|basis F^n>] and
    <math|L:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> be a linear
    isomorphism then there exists two unitary operators
    <math|S,T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> and a linear
    isomorphism <math|D:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    with <math|\<forall\>i,j> <math|\<cal-M\><around*|(|D;E,E|)><rsub|i,j>=\<delta\><rsub|i,j>\<cdot\>M<rsub|i,i>\<geqslant\>0>
    such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      L=S\<circ\>D\<circ\>T
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Consider the adjoint <math|L<rsup|\<ast\>>> of <math|L> [see theorem:
    then we have

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L|)><rsup|\<ast\>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|inner adjoint and composition>]>>L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\><around*|(|L<rsup|\<star\>>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|inner adjoint of adjoint>]>>L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L
    </equation*>

    which proves that <math|L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L> is self adjoint. Using
    [theorem: <reference|spectral diagonalization of a self-adjoint operator
    (real)>] there exist unitary operator <math|U> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-M\><around*|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\><around*|(|L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L|)>\<circ\>U;E,E|)><text|
      is diagonal>
    </equation*>

    Take\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1093.291>D<rprime|'>=U<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\><around*|(|L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L|)>\<circ\>U
    </equation>

    then we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1094.291>\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >\<cal-M\><around*|(|D<rprime|'>;E,E|)><rsub|i,j>=\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<delta\><rsub|i,j>
    </equation>

    so that <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      D<rprime|'><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>\<cal-M\><around*|(|D<rprime|'>;E,E|)><rsub|j,i>\<cdot\>e<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|n>\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|j>\<cdot\>\<delta\><rsub|j,i>\<cdot\>e<rsub|j>=\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>
    </equation*>

    hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1095.291>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      >D<rprime|'><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>=\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>
    </equation>

    As <math|L> is a linear isomorphism and <math|U> is a linear isomorphism
    [see theorem: <reference|spectral unitary operator properties>] we have
    that <math|L\<circ\>U> is a linear isomorphism [see theorem:
    <reference|linear mapping composition isomorphism>] so that by [theorem:
    <reference|linear mapping injectivity and kernel>]
    <math|ker<around*|(|L\<circ\>U|)>=<around*|{|0|}>> so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1096.291>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have that >L<around*|(|U<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>=<around*|(|L\<circ\>U|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>\<neq\>0
    </equation>

    Next we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> that\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1094.291>]>>>|<cell|\<cal-M\><around*|(|D<rprime|'>;E,E|)><rsub|i,i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|inner matrix of linear mapping>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|D<rprime|'><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>,e<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|<around*|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\><around*|(|L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L|)>\<circ\>U|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>,e<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|U<rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|L<rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|L<around*|(|U<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>|)>|)>,e<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorems:
      \ <reference|inner adjoint operator>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|L<rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|L<around*|(|U<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>|)>,U<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorems:
      \ <reference|inner adjoint operator>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|L<around*|(|U<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>,L<around*|(|U<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<\|\|\>|L<around*|(|U<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|e>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<gtr\>>|<cell|0
      <around*|(|strict bigger because of <around*|[|<text|eq: <reference|eq
      21.1096.291>>|]>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1097.291>\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0
    </equation>

    Define now <math|D,D<rprime|''>\<in\>Hom<around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)>>
    by <math|D<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>=<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>>
    and <math|D<rprime|''><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>=<frac|1|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>>
    \ [see theorem: <reference|linear mapping definition by its values for
    the basis vectors>] then we have <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|D\<circ\>D<rprime|''>|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|D<around*|(|D<rprime|''><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|D<around*|(|<frac|1|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>>\<cdot\>D<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|Id<rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>\<cdot\>D<rprime|''><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|D<rprime|''><around*|(|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|D<rprime|''><around*|(|D<around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|D<rprime|''>\<circ\>D|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that by [theorem: <reference|linear mapping and basis vectors>] we
    have\ 

    <\equation*>
      D\<circ\>D<rprime|''>=Id<rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>=D<rprime|''>\<circ\>D
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1098.291>D,D<rsup|-1><text| are linear isomorphisms and
      >D<rsup|-1>=D<rprime|''>
    </equation>

    Further we have <math|\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    that

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-M\><around*|(|D<rsup|-1>;E,E|)><rsub|i,j>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|inner matrix of linear mapping>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|D<rsup|-1><around*|(|e<rsub|j>|)>,e<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\<langle\>|1/<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|j>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|j>,e<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|1/<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|j>>\<cdot\>\<delta\><rsub|i,j>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    so that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1099.291>\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text|
      we have >\<cal-M\><around*|(|D<rsup|-1>;E,E|)><rsub|i,j>=<frac|1|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|j>>>\<cdot\>\<delta\><rsub|i,j>
    </equation>

    Now <math|\<forall\>i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>> we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\><around*|(|L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)>|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|inner adjoint and composition>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|<around*|(|D<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<star\>>\<circ\>U<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|spectral diagnolizing operator is
      self-adjoint>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|D<rsup|-1>\<circ\><around*|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L<rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\>L\<circ\>U|)>\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1093.291>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|D<rsup|-1>\<circ\>D<rprime|'>\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|D<rsup|-1><around*|(|D<rprime|'><around*|(|D<rsup|-1><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|1|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>>\<cdot\><around*|(|D<rsup|-1><around*|(|D<rprime|'><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1095.291>]>>>|<cell|<frac|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>>\<cdot\><around*|(|D<rsup|-1><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<frac|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|i>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|e<rsub|i><rsub|>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|Id<rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>><around*|(|e<rsub|i>|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves by [theorem: <reference|linear mapping and basis vectors>]
    that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <text|<label|eq 18.240.291>><around*|(|L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>\<circ\><around*|(|L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)>=Id<rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    </equation*>

    Using then [theorem: <reference|spectral unitary operator (1)>] it
    follows that <math|L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>> is unitary, further as
    <math|U> is unitary we have by [theorem: <reference|spectral unitary
    operator properties>] that <math|U<rsup|-1>> is unitary. Hence if we take
    <math|S=L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>> and <math|T=U<rsup|-1>> then we
    have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1100.291>S,T<text| are unitary operators>
    </equation>

    Now

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S\<circ\>D\<circ\>T>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|L\<circ\>U\<circ\>D<rsup|-1>|)>\<circ\>D\<circ\>U<rsup|-1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|L\<circ\>U\<circ\><around*|(|D<rsup|-1>\<circ\>D|)>\<circ\>U<rsup|-1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|L\<circ\>U\<circ\>U<rsup|-1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|L>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    hence\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1101.291>S\<circ\>D\<circ\>T=L
    </equation>

    Further we have

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}><text| that
      >\<cal-M\><around*|(|D;E,E|)><rsub|i,j>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|inner matrix of linear mapping>]>><around*|\<langle\>|D<around*|(|e<rsub|j>|)>,e<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\<langle\>|<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>\<cdot\>e<rsub|j>,e<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>=<sqrt|\<lambda\><rprime|'><rsub|j>>\<cdot\>\<delta\><rsub|j,i>
    </equation*>

    which together with [eqs: <reference|eq 21.1098.291>, <reference|eq
    21.1100.291>, <reference|eq 21.1101.291>] proves the lemma.
  </proof>

  We are now ready to prove that <math|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|A|)>|)>=det<around*|(|T|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>>.

  <\theorem>
    <label|Lebesgue borel measure and linear isomorphism>Let
    <math|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>, <math|L:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>
    a linear transformation then <math|\<forall\>A\<in\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
    we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|L<around*|(|A|)>|)>=<around*|\||det<around*|(|L|)>|\|>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Take the canonical basis <math|E=<around*|(|e<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,e<rsub|n>|)>>
    then then by the previous lemma [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.582.291>]
    there exist a linear isomorphism <math|D> with
    <math|\<forall\>i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
    <math|\<cal-M\><around*|(|D;E,E|)><rsub|i,j>=\<cal-M\><around*|(|D;E,E|)><rsub|i,i>\<cdot\>\<delta\><rsub|i,j>\<gtr\>0>
    and two unitary operators <math|S,T> such that\ 

    <\equation*>
      L=S\<circ\>D\<circ\>T
    </equation*>

    Hence we have\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|L<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|S\<circ\>D\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|S<around*|(|D<around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Lebesgue measure and unitary
      transformation>]>>>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|D<around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[Lemma:
      <reference|lemma 21.578.291>]>>>|<cell|det<around*|(|D|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Lebesgue measure and unitary
      transformation>]>>>|<cell|det<around*|(|D|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|det<around*|(|D|)>\<cdot\>vol<rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|det<around*|(|D|)>\<cdot\><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|1-0|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|det<around*|(|D|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    Hence we have proved that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1102.291>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|L<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>=det<around*|(|D|)>
    </equation>

    Now\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|det<around*|(|L|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|det<around*|(|S\<circ\>D\<circ\>T|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|determinant det(L) properties>]>>>|<cell|det<around*|(|S|)>\<cdot\>det<around*|(|D|)>\<cdot\>det<around*|(|T|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<below|\<equallim\>|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|spectral determinant unitary
      transfrom>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|\<pm\>1|)>\<cdot\>det<around*|(|D|)>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<pm\>1|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|\<pm\>det<around*|(|D|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    further\ 

    <\equation*>
      det<around*|(|D|)>=\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|determinant of matrix and linear
      transformation>]>>det<around*|(|\<cal-M\><around*|(|D;E,E|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|matrix determinant of a diagonal
      matrix>]>><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>\<cal-M\><around*|(|D;E,E|)><rsub|i,i>\<gtr\>0
    </equation*>

    so that <math|det<around*|(|D|)>=<around*|\||det<around*|(|L|)>|\|>>
    which combined with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1102.291>] gives\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|L<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>=<around*|\||det<around*|(|L|)>|\|>
    </equation*>

    Finallye we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|L<around*|(|A|)>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|measure Borel measure of T(A)
      (1)>]>>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|<around*|[|0,1|[><rsup|n>|)>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>=<around*|\||det<around*|(|L|)>|\|>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A|)>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  We use the above theorem to prove that for a linear isomorphism we have\ 

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int>f
    d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
  </equation*>

  we prove this result in different steps

  <\enumerate>
    <item>For <math|f\<in\>> we have <math|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|S>f
    d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>

    <item>For non negative measurable functions we have
    <math|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|+>f
    d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>

    <item>For <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    we have that <math|f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<mu\>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>.
    </equation*>
  </enumerate>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.584.291>Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>|]>>
    and <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> a linear
    isomorphism then

    <\equation*>
      f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|T> is a linear isomorphism we have by [theorem:
    <reference|continuity homemorphism between K>] then\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1103.291>T<text| is a homeomorphism so that by [theorem:
      <reference|measurable function and continuity>] <math|T<text| is
      >\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text|-measurable>>>
    </equation>

    further we have by [theorem: <reference|determinant det(L) properties>]
    that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1104.291>det<around*|(|T|)>\<neq\>0<infix-and>det<around*|(|L<rsup|-1>|)>=<frac|1|det<around*|(|L|)>>
    </equation>

    Let <math|A\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> and take
    <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then we have for
    <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> either:\ 

    <\description>
      <item*|<math|T<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>A>>Then
      <math|x\<in\><around*|(|T<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|function inverse image preimage>]>>T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>>
      so that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>=1=\<cal-X\><rsub|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>><around*|(|x|)>>

      <item*|<math|T<around*|(|x|)>\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\A>>Then

      <\eqnarray*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x\<in\><around*|(|T<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\A|)>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function properties (1)>]>>>|<cell|<around*|(|T<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)>\\<around*|(|T<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|T<rsup|-1><text|
        is bijective>>>|<cell|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\<around*|(|T<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
        <reference|function inverse image
        preimage>]>>>|<cell|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\\T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>>>>>
      </eqnarray*>

      so that <math|<around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>=\<cal-X\><rsub|A><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>=0=\<cal-X\><rsub|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>><around*|(|x|)>>
    </description>

    hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1105.291>\<forall\>A\<subseteq\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text| we
      have >\<cal-X\><rsub|A>\<circ\>T=\<cal-X\><rsub|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A|)>>
    </equation>

    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>|]>>
    we have by [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>]
    <space|1em>that there exist a pairwise disjoint
    <math|<around*|{|A<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>
    and <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|i>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1106.291>f=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>
    </equation>

    As <math|T> is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>-measureablle
    we have that <math|<around*|{|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>.
    Further if <math|i,j\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>> with
    <math|i\<neq\>j> then <math|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)><big|cap>T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|j>|)>\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
    <reference|function function and intersection and
    union>[>>T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i><big|cap>A<rsub|i>|)>=T<rsup|-1><around*|(|\<varnothing\>|)>=\<varnothing\>>.
    Hence we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1107.291><around*|{|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|}><rsub|i\<in\><around*|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]><text|
      is pairwise disjoint>
    </equation>

    Let <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> then\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>|)><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\><around*|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|A<rsub|i>>\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1105.291>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>><around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>|)><around*|(|x|)>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1108.291>f\<circ\>T=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<cal-X\><rsub|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>
    </equation>

    Using then [theorem: <reference|simple function measurable (1)>] on [eqs:
    <reference|eq 21.1107.291>, <reference|eq 21.1108.291>] it follows that

    <\equation*>
      f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>|]>
    </equation*>

    Next\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|simple function integral>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|T<rsup|-1><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|function inverse image preimage>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|<around*|(|T<rsup|-1>|)><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|Lebesgue borel measure and linear
      isomorphism>]>>>|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>det<around*|(|T<rsup|-1>|)>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1104.291>]>>>|<cell|<frac|1|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|m>f<rsub|i>\<cdot\>\<lambda\><rsup|n><around*|(|A<rsub|i>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[definition:
      <reference|simple function integral>]>>>|<cell|<frac|1|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lemma 21.585.291>Let <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> a linear
    isomorphism then

    <\equation*>
      <rigid|f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [theorem: <reference|non negative integral alternative>]
    that there exist a <math|<around*|{|f<rsub|n>|}><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-N\>>\<subseteq\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    such that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1109.291>\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|x|)>\<Rightarrow\>\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text|>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1110.291>\<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text|
      >f<around*|(|x|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|x|)><text|
      (where the limit exist)>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1111.291><big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>><big|int><rsup|S>f
      <rsub|n>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation>

    So we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text| we have
      >\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      ><around*|(|f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<rsub|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>\<leqslant\>f<rsub|n+1><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|f<rsub|n+1>\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      \<forall\>x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n><text|
      ><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>=f<around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)><below|=|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1110.291>]>><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>>f<rsub|n><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    Hence using the Monotone Convergence theorem [see theorem:
    <reference|Monotone Convergence Theorem (1)>] it follows that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1112.291>f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation>

    Using [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.584.291>] we have\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1113.291>\<forall\>n\<in\>\<bbb-N\><text| that
      >f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>|]><text|
      and ><around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|S>f<rsub|n>
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation>

    Next\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>|<cell|<below|=|<text|[eq: <reference|eq
      21.1111.291>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1113.291>]>>>|<cell|<below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><around*|(|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|extended limit properties>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|S><around*|(|f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[theorem:
      <reference|non negative integral is a
      extension>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><below|lim|n\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f<rsub|n>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1112.291>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|+>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\theorem>
    <label|Lebesgue borel measure and linear isomorphism (n)>Let
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    and <math|T:\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<rightarrow\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> a linear
    isomorphism then

    <\equation*>
      f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    and\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    As <math|T> is a linear isomorphism it follows from [theorems:
    <reference|continuity homemorphism between K>,<reference|measurable
    function and continuity>] that <math|T> is
    <rigid|<math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>>-measurable>,
    further, as <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>\<subseteq\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>,|\<nobracket\>>>
    <math|f> is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable
    so that by [theorem: <reference|measurable function composition>] we have
    that <math|f\<circ\>T> is <math|\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>-measurable.
    Hence we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1114.291>f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-M\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation>

    As <math|f\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    we have that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1115.291><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<lambda\><rsup|n>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation>

    Now we have for <math|x\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>> that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><rsup|+><around*|(|x|)>=max<around*|(|<around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=max<around*|(|f<around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>,0|)>=f<rsup|+><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|f<rsup|+>\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><rsup|-><around*|(|x|)>=-min<around*|(|<around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>,0|)>=-min<around*|(|f<around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>,0|)>=f<rsup|-><around*|(|T<around*|(|x|)>|)>=<around*|(|f<rsup|->\<circ\>T|)><around*|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1116.291><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><rsup|+>=f<rsup|+>\<circ\>T<text|
      and ><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><rsup|->=f<rsup|->\<circ\>T
    </equation>

    As by [definition: <reference|measure integral>]
    <math|f<rsup|+>,f<rsup|->\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>>
    it follows from [lemma: <reference|lemma 21.585.291>]\ 

    <\equation*>
      f<rsup|+>\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]><text|
      and >f<rsup|->\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-M\><rsup|+><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1117.291><around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|+>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq 21.1118.291><around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|->\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation>

    so that we have\ 

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|+>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[><text|
      and ><big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|->\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>\<in\><around*|[|0,\<infty\>|[>
    </equation*>

    which together with [eq: <reference|eq 21.1114.291>] proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      f\<circ\>T\<in\>\<cal-L\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>,\<cal-B\><around*|[|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|]>,\<lambda\><rsup|n>,<wide|\<bbb-R\>|\<wide-bar\>>|]>
    </equation*>

    Finally\ 

    <\eqnarray*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>|<cell|=>|<cell|<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|+>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>-<big|int><rsup|+>f<rsup|->d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1117.291>, <reference|eq
      21.1118.291>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|+>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>-<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|->\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|+>\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>-<big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f<rsup|->\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|<text|[eq:
      <reference|eq 21.1116.291>]>>>|<cell|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><around*|(|<big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><rsup|+>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>-<big|int><rsup|+><rsup|><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)><rsup|->d\<lambda\><rsup|n>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<equallim\><rsub|definition>>|<cell|<around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>>>>>
    </eqnarray*>

    which proves that\ 

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||det<around*|(|T|)>|\|>\<cdot\><big|int><rsup|><around*|(|f\<circ\>T|)>d\<lambda\><rsup|n>=<big|int>f
      d\<lambda\><rsup|n>
    </equation*>
  </proof>

